centers development centers 30+ 3,600 3,500 in 2025 by the end of 2025 by the end of 2025 agreements signed  with startups researchers employees in the  marketing field 1,170 2,653 12 by the end of 2025 by the end of 2025 by the end of 2025 manufacturing facilities  worldwide (a) sales outlets and  showrooms worldwide Sustainable Construction  Talks organized  worldwide (b) 162,000 80 132 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 26 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (a)   By the end of 2025 - cf. “Our global footprint”, pp. 12 et 13. (b)   Cf. section 3.1.4.B, p. 63. 1.1.2 MARKETS ON A STRUCTURAL GROWTH PATH Saint-Gobain’s business is structurally driven by  underlying trends (see section 1.2.1.A, p. 39) that provide  the Group with long-term growth drivers, not only in the  residential market but also in the non-residential and  infrastructure markets. Saint-Gobain relies on a robust  organizational model based on country platforms as  management units, grouped into five major regions:  Northern Europe, Southern Europe – Middle East – Africa,  North America, Latin America, and Asia-Pacific (see “Our  global footprint,” pp.  12 and 13). With an industrial  presence in 80 countries , the Group benefits from  growth opportunities in every major region of the world. A – Western Europe In Western Europe, a recovery is expected in the  residential markets, which have seen a contraction in  recent years due to the inflation surge of 2022-2024 and  the rise in real estate rates. Building permits and housing  starts are increasing in countries that  are  important to  the Group, such as France, the  United Kingdom,  Germany, and Sweden. As of late November 2025,  building permits were up 14% year-on-year in France  (1).  The decline in activity in  recent years has exacerbated  the housing shortage and the housing crisis in Europe,  degrading property purchasing power and encouraging  governments to support housing construction . In the  United Kingdom, for example, there is a shortfall of  2.5 million homes, and the current government aims to  build 1.5  million homes between 2025 and 2029, which  represents a clear acceleration from the construction  levels of the last decade.  The winds are also favorable for building renovation. In  addition to the ongoing recovery in real estate  transactions in most European countries, which are an  important driver of renovation, energy renovation is  supported by public measures , both at the European  and national levels. In addition to European regulatory  mechanisms, such as the Energy Performance of  Buildings Directive (EPBD), many European countries  have adopted regulatory arrangements (such as the  Climate and Resilience Act in France, which prohibits the  leasing of energy-inefficient residences), subsidies, and  market mechanisms (such as energy saving certificates  in Spain). Energy renovation also offers significant  financial benefits for households, through its ability to  reduce energy bills on the one hand, and also because of  the growing influence of energy efficiency on  property  prices. In many European countries, price differentials  between the most and least energy-efficient homes  exceed 15%, even comparing homes with equivalent  structural characteristics: this is the case in the  Netherlands, for example, as well as in Germany, France,  Italy and Spain. Non-residential and infrastructure  markets are also drivers in Europe.  The need for reindustrialization, particularly in the  defense sector, and the development of data centers  represent sources of growth for new construction, while  the energy renovation of tertiary buildings, both public  and private, maintains its growth level. In the  infrastructure sector, some European governments have  ambitious development projects planned; this is notably  the case in Germany, where the new €500  billion  Infrastructure Fund will significantly increase activity in  this sector over the next 10 years. The requirements of  renovating aging infrastructure on the continent,  including bridges and tunnels, offer complementary  opportunities. Finally, the increasing effects of climate  crises on buildings, both residential and non-residential,  imply significant adaptation needs . The solutions  offered by Saint-Gobain for renovation are perfectly  adapted to these new needs. For example, protecting  buildings against heat waves involves insulating external  walls or installing solar control glass. Similarly, the  Group’s construction chemicals solutions meet the need  for buildings to be watertight, against violent rains or  floods, for example.  B – North America  In North America, the housing shortage  is a very  important factor in the structural growth  of  Saint-Gobain’s business in the residential sector. In the  US, the pace of construction in the decade following the  2008 global financial crisis did not keep up with  population growth, and the shortfall is currently  estimated at 3.7  million homes  (2), which represents  nearly 3 years’ worth of construction at the current pace.  In Canada, strong population growth over the past  decade, fueled by dynamic immigration, has also led to  severe housing shortages, exacerbating the housing  affordability crisis: an additional 5  million homes would  be needed to meet the needs and restore the housing  purchasing power of Canadian households (3).  Furthermore, the frequency of hurricanes, fires and  storms, which is unfortunately accentuated by climate  change, implies non-discretionary renovation needs ,  which represent a source of sustainable growth for the  Group’s roofing and cladding business in the region. The  increase in such climatic events due to the climate  crisis will generate significant adaptation needs in the  coming years.  Finally, the non-residential and infrastructure markets  also benefit from solid structural fundamentals. The  reindustrialization policies of successive US  governments, as well as the trend towards reshoring  industries on US soil, are driving growth in the industrial  buildings market and the rise of data centers represents  a significant source of growth. As in Europe,  infrastructure needs remain high, particularly in terms  of renovation. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 27 (1) CGEDD. (2) Freddie Mac. (3) CMCH Canada Mortgage &amp; Housing Corporation. C – Asia and fast-growing regions Emerging countries are experiencing strong population  growth, combined with rapid urbanization. The global  population will increase from 8.2 billion in 2024 to nearly  10 billion by 2080 (1), and most of this increase will take  place in emerging countries. Similarly, the urban  population is expected to grow by almost 2  billion by  2050, particularly in Asia and Africa. In addition, around  1.1 billion people currently live in informal settlements and  are still waiting for decent housing (2). Based on this data,  the World Economic Forum estimates that  new  residential construction needs  worldwide will  reach  2 billion homes over the next 80 years.  Emerging countries are also experiencing economic  development trajectories favoring strong growth of the  middle classes , whose desire for quality housing  perfectly matches Saint-Gobain’s offer. In India, the  number of households entering the middle class will  increase by an average of 16% per year by 2030. This  growth has also reached 11% in Indonesia, and 21% in  Vietnam. Urbanization will also continue in the Middle  East and Latin America : in Brazil, for example,  the  middle class is expected to grow by nearly 4%  annually over the coming years  (3). This demographic  growth and its associated urbanization imply significant  needs for the expansion of  cities, with consequential  tertiary housing and  infrastructure needs, representing  complementary sources of business for the Group. Some  countries are  starting to build new cities or ambitious  urban development projects. In Egypt, 37 new cities are  under construction, and Saudi Arabia is currently  building 14 large-scale projects to support its economic  diversification program. D – Industrial markets For its industrial customers , Saint-Gobain provides  performance solutions and meets market expectations  through its ability to innovate , its research and  development potential, its closeness to its customers,  and its use of digital technology and data analysis.  Industrial markets are expected to continue to grow  steadily over the 2025-2030 period, driven by  reindustrialization and strategic autonomy efforts in  developed countries, ramped-up military spending, and  the development of new industrial sectors associated  with “green” technologies.  The mobility market, in which Saint-Gobain is present  through a range of insulation, acoustics, and technical  glazing solutions, is undergoing expansion fueled by the  natural turnover of car fleets, the transition to electric  vehicles, and the relocation of value chains by auto  manufacturers, trends that will structurally drive the  Group’s business in the coming years. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 28 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) United Nations. (2) Idem. (3) Oxford Economics. 1.1.3 A SOLUTIONS-BASED APPROACH,  ROLLED OUT COUNTRY BY COUNTRY  Saint-Gobain relies on the support of a robust organization that is well suited to a deglobalized world, as well as  local construction markets. It is based on country platforms as managerial units, which are grouped into five major  regions: Northern Europe, Southern Europe – Middle East – Africa, North America, Latin America, and Asia-Pacific.  Within each of these regions, the country CEOs are responsible for all Saint-Gobain product lines, to provide a  comprehensive response to the most complex needs of their customers, through a solutions-based approach.  Country by country, Saint-Gobain is deploying this approach in all its markets – residential as well as non-residential  and infrastructure. A – Construction and renovation markets a. Residential In all its countries, Saint-Gobain has successfully  deployed a solutions-based approach, enabling it to  position itself as a “one-stop shop,” a leading supplier of  integrated solutions both for renovation  and new  construction of single-family houses and multi- family housing. The Group provides a comprehensive range of solutions  dedicated to the global renovation of housing,  which  help improve their energy performance, acoustic and  visual comfort, as well as air quality. In France, for  example, a customer looking to undertake a  residential  renovation project can find an integrated offering from  Saint-Gobain that covers all their needs. First of all, this  offering includes comprehensive renovation systems,  particularly external thermal insulation solutions  combining insulating materials, plasterboard, profiles,  mortars, fiberglass reinforcement, and finish coats, as  well as solar-control insulated glazing units and blow-in  insulation for attics. When combined, these solutions  help cut a residence’s energy bill by 70%, and  significantly improve its energy performance diagnosis  (EPD), raising it from class G to class C. Saint-Gobain supplements this offering with digital  solutions available through its distribution businesses,  making it easier to conduct assessments and issue price  quotes for improving building energy performance. The  Group also provides services intended to improve  productivity for craftsmen on worksites , such as  logistics and kitting solutions. Aware of the  environmental issues, Saint -Gobain also provides  worksite waste collection and sorting services, relying  on a network of 810 collection and sorting points in  France, particularly at Point.P stores. Finally, the Group  invests heavily in training professionals: each year, over  100,000 craftsmen are trained and supported in France  on energy renovation issues.  In North America, and particularly in Canada in the past  few years, the Group has expanded its offering  dedicated to lightweight construction , in order to  provide a comprehensive solution for the building  envelope, helping improve the quality of buildings and  their resilience against climate change. Saint-Gobain stands out in particular by offering  complete systems for the customer . For example, the  Group markets integrated roofing systems under the  CertainTeed brand combining waterproofing  membranes, impact-resistant ClimateFlex shingles, and  their associated accessories. These solutions help  buildings better withstand storms and guarantee roof  insurability. The Group also develops siding systems that  are highly resistant to moisture and impacts, while  providing its customers with interactive digital tools that  make it easier to choose and customize solutions.  CertainTeed’s One Precision Assemblies system is  another example that helps significantly reduce project  completion times . Pre-assembled panels featuring  windows, doors, siding, roofing, insulation, storm- proofing and vapor barrier membranes are produced in  one week. Once delivered to a modular home worksite,  they can be installed in under three weeks, compared  with five months for a construction using traditional  methods.  To replace traditional, heavy-construction methods  which devour resources, time, and labor, Saint-Gobain  has developed lightweight façade solutions which are  both ultra-high performance and easy to install . In  Brazil, for example, such solutions combine Glasroc X  board, a moisture-resistant fiberglass-reinforced  plasterboard (Placo and Adfors), glass wool insulation  (Isover), fiber cement sheets (Brasilit), as well as  fiberglass reinforcement mesh and joint tape (Adfors),  and finish coats (Quartzolit) These systems are  supplemented by technical consulting and project  monitoring services.  They make it possible to  significantly improve productivity on worksites ,  particularly in multi-family housing, respond to labor  shortage issues, reduce waste, and offer superior  performance – in terms of quality, energy efficiency, and  occupant comfort – compared with traditional methods. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 29 Example: renovation of a multi-family residential building Example: construction of a single-family house in Canada  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 30 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com Eclaz Sun double glazing  with solar control Interactive digital solution for energy  renovation diagnostics External Thermal Insulation  Composite System Roof thermal  insulation Recycling  services Interior wall  insulation Multipurpose  screeds Acoustic  ceilings Reinforced partitions Heating and plumbing  installation Siding Waterproofing  membranes Ceilings Plasterboard Metal profiles Insulation Roof shingles Roofing  membranes  b. Non-residential Saint-Gobain is accelerating the deployment of its solutions-based approach in non-residential sectors by offering  ranges of solutions dedicated to each market segment (hospitals, schools, hotels, and data centers), meeting the high  demands of its customers in those markets, particularly in terms of energy and acoustic performance, visual comfort,  and air quality. Example of solutions for lightweight construction: school As an example, Saint-Gobain provides a comprehensive  range of high-performance solutions for the  construction and renovation of educational institutions:  ceilings, plaster-based solutions and acoustic flooring  systems help significantly improve the acoustic  performance of classrooms, contributing to better  learning and teaching conditions. To support the  specification of its solutions, Saint-Gobain also offers  augmented-reality digital 