int-Gobain was founded in 1665 by Louis XIV, King of  France, under the name of “Royal Manufacture of  Mirrors”.  Compagnie de Saint-Gobain diversified into sectors such  as chemicals, glass products, and the automotive  industry, rapidly expanding into international markets,  establishing itself in the United States in 1829, Germany  in 1858, Italy in 1888 and Spain in 1905. The Group, which successfully overcame the major crises  of the century and invested heavily in research and  development, withdrew from its chemical activities and  merged with Pont-à-Mousson, created in 1856 and  specializing in cast iron pipes. In 1986, the privatization of Saint-Gobain – which had  been nationalized in 1982 – was a huge popular success:  1,500,000 shareholders subscribed to the transaction.  The acquisition of Norton in 1990, then Carborundum in  1996, enabled the Group to develop its know-how in  abrasives, plastics and ceramics.  The 2005 acquisition of British Plaster Board, the world  leader in plasterboard, combined with Saint-Gobain’s  insulation solutions, made the Group the world’s No. 1 in  interior design solutions. Saint-Gobain focused its strategy on sustainable  housing, while continuing to serve numerous industrial  markets, and pursued its expansion in emerging  countries. In late 2018, the Group launched its “Transform &amp; Grow”  plan aimed at establishing a new organization by country  and ensuring agile and value-creating portfolio  management.  In 2019, Saint-Gobain acquired Continental Building  Products, the first step in strengthening its positions  in the construction market in North America. Saint-Gobain established its corporate purpose,  “Making the world a better home”. The 2021 acquisition of Chryso made Saint-Gobain a  major player in construction chemicals. In October 2021,  Saint-Gobain launched its “Grow &amp; Impact” strategic  plan, setting out its vision to become the  worldwide  leader in light and sustainable construction.  In 2022, the Group finalized the acquisition of Kaycan – a  specialist in exterior building materials – and of GCP  Applied Technologies, a key step in establishing itself as  a leader in construction chemicals.  The following year, Saint-Gobain acquired Building  Products of Canada, a manufacturer of roofing shingles  for the residential sector, and wood fiber insulation  panels.  Saint-Gobain was certified as “Top Employer Global” for  its 2025 fiscal year, for the 11 th consecutive year. The  Group acquired CSR, a leader in construction materials in  Australia and New Zealand, as well as Bailey, a specialist  in metal frames for light construction in Canada, and  initiated the acquisitions of Fosroc, a leading global  player in construction chemicals, and Ovniver (Cemix  brand) in Mexico. These constitute new strategic steps in  establishing Saint-Gobain’s global presence in  construction chemicals, which, once the transactions are  complete, will bring its total sales in this sector to  €6.5 billion (pro forma).  In 2025, Saint-Gobain celebrates its 360th anniversary, a  figure that also represents its global reach and its circular  and comprehensive approach. In October 2025, the  Group launches its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan,  marking the beginning of a new chapter for  Saint-Gobain, based on a clear ambition: to accelerate its  profitable growth in a sustainable manner and  outperform its markets, thereby enhancing the value  created for all its stakeholders. At the end of 2025, the  Group is present in 80 countries (1). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 35 (1) Countries with active industrial presence as of Dec. 31, 2025.  1.1.6 LOOKING BACK ON THE YEAR 2025 January  Saint-Gobain certified as “Top Employer Global”  for the 10th consecutive year Saint-Gobain was certified “Top Employer Global” for the  tenth consecutive year, joining a small group of 17 global  companies recognized in 2025. This award highlights the  Group’s commitment to its 161,000 employees in  80  countries and its continued progress in diversity,  inclusion, sustainable development, and employee  development. Saint-Gobain completes the acquisition of Ovniver  in Mexico and Central America  Saint-Gobain completed the acquisition of Ovniver  Group (Cemix brand), a major player in construction  chemicals in Mexico and Central America. This strategic  transaction strengthens Saint-Gobain’s presence in  high-growth markets and strengthens its global  leadership in construction chemicals products. Saint-Gobain launches its first 100% recycled  plasterboard  Saint-Gobain announces the UK launch of its first  plasterboard entirely made from recycled materials, a  world first for the Group. Developed by British Gypsum,  this innovation represents an important step in  circular  construction and supports the Group’s commitment to  achieving carbon neutrality by 2050. February Saint-Gobain announces the launch of its first  low-carbon stone wool production line in Spain  Saint-Gobain will invest €40  million to expand and  decarbonize its insulation plant in Azuqueca, near  Madrid. The new electric furnace will replace fossil fuels,  reducing CO₂ emissions by 70% and increasing  production capacity by 40%, marking an important step  towards the Group’s goal of carbon neutrality by 2050. Saint-Gobain finalizes the acquisition of Fosroc Saint-Gobain completed the acquisition of Fosroc, a  leading construction chemicals company with a strong  presence in India, the Middle East and Asia-Pacific. This  acquisition strengthens the Group’s presence in  emerging markets and consolidates its worldwide  leadership in light and sustainable construction. March Saint-Gobain successfully carries out a €1.2 billion  double tranche bond issue  Saint-Gobain issued €1.2  billion in two tranches  –  €700 million (3 years, 2.75%) and €500 million (8 years,  3.50%) – taking advantage of favorable market  conditions to anticipate its refinancing needs. The bonds  were oversubscribed almost three times over, reflecting  investors’ confidence in the Group’s credit quality. April Saint-Gobain included in the CDP’s “List A”  For the second year in a row, Saint-Gobain is on the  CDP’s prestigious “Climate List,” which rewards its  leadership in environmental transparency and climate  action. Only 2% of the companies assessed receive this  distinction, highlighting the Group’s strong sustainability  strategy. Sustainable construction: global trends revealed by the third edition of the Saint-Gobain barometer  The third edition of the Sustainable Construction  Barometer reveals a growing global awareness and  urgency concerning sustainable construction practices.  Based on surveys conducted in 27 countries, the study  highlights the main regional differences and the need to  strengthen training and actions to accelerate the sector’s  transformation. May Saint-Gobain to launch its first low-carbon stone  wool insulation plant in the United Kingdom Saint-Gobain will build a new low-carbon stone wool  insulation plant in Melton Mowbray. Equipped with a fully  electric furnace powered by renewable energy, the plant  will produce 50,000 tonnes per year, meeting the United  Kingdom's growing demand for sustainable building  materials. June Saint-Gobain launches its world’s lowest-carbon  insulation plant in Finland  Saint-Gobain has unveiled the production of very low- carbon glass wool at its Forssa site in Finland. Fully  powered by biogas and hydropower, the plant will  reduce CO₂ emissions by 1,500 tonnes per year and  reduce the carbon footprint of its products by up to 40%. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 36 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com General Shareholders’ Meeting and Board of Directors  Meeting on June 5, 2025  Saint-Gobain shareholders reappointed Benoit Bazin as  Chief Executive Officer and approved a 5% dividend  increase, to €2.20 