. Materiality challenges have been classified  according to the contributions of the CSR roadmap. Sustainability issues were identified based on: ■ Information and knowledge gained from previous  financial years;  ■ Sector-specific reference frameworks specifying the  priority issues linked to construction business lines; ■ The specific features of the Group’s business model  and value chain; ■ Compliance with EU regulations by incorporating  the detailed challenges listed by the ESRS. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 49 The methodology and detailed results  are presented in section 3.1.3, p. 106. B – Contributions  to sustainability issues  For each of the sustainability issues identified, there are  impacts, positive or negative, risks and  opportunities,  particularly growth opportunities. Through the three axes of its CSR roadmap, Saint-Gobain  defines its ambition, including its objectives, and its  action plans to seize opportunities and contribute to  positive impacts while controlling sustainability risks and  its footprint. Growth opportunities are particularly developed under  the “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan (see section 1.2.2, p. 42)  in particular climate change adaptation solutions and the  development of circularity. A DECARBONATED HOME GROWTH AND RESILIENCE Opportunities to outperform markets  and access new markets: ■ Increased competitiveness and an attractive offering,  thanks to the proposal of low-carbon and energy-efficiency  solutions; ■ Growing demand for solutions adapted to climate change  (regulations and user demands), particularly solutions for  adapting to the effects of climate change. Opportunities related to operational  and financial control: ■ Cost reduction and availability of supplies through  industrial processes, innovation in particular, and  operational excellence. CHALLENGES Reduce the negative impact on the environment and society:  ■ Reduce greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions (scope 1, 2 and 3) contributing to climate change and biodiversity loss;  ■ Reduce GHG emissions related to the construction sector.  Adapt resources to the needs of the transition plan: ■ Risk of increased energy costs; ■ Potential need for investment in GHG reduction initiatives. APPROACH Maximize Saint-Gobain’s contribution: Proposing innovative solutions that contribute  to reducing the carbon emissions of the construction  and industrial sectors, including those linked with  building use.  Minimize Saint-Gobain’s footprint:  Achieving net zero emissions (scope 1, 2 and 3)  by 2050. ACTIONS Proposing solutions: ■ Program for sustainable and high-performance solutions;  ■ Assessing the impact of products and solutions on the  value chain. Taking action for sustainability: ■ 2030 carbon roadmap;  ■ Aligning the carbon strategy with the net zero carbon  by 2050 target. OBJECTIVES ■ Net zero emissions (scope 1, 2 and 3) by 2050; ■ Scope 1 &amp; 2 GHG emissions: reduction between 40% and 45% by 2035 (vs. 2017); ■ Scope 3 GHG emissions: 16% reduction by 2030 (vs. 2017). ESRS E1 SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT GOALS: 7; 9; 11; 12; 13 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 50 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS GROWTH AND RESILIENCE Leadership opportunities: ■ Using resources from responsible sources  and limiting the use of non-renewable resources; ■ Thanks to country platforms, creating recycling  channels for construction materials. Opportunities to outperform markets  and access new markets: ■ Growing demand and expectations for products  and systems that are eco-designed and associated  with the development of circularity. Opportunities associated with operational  and financial control: ■ Cost reduction and availability of supplies through  circularity, including local waste collection  and sorting organization; ■ Cost reduction and availability of supplies through  industrial processes and innovation, in particular  the operational excellence program. CHALLENGES  Reduce negative impacts on the environment and society:  ■ Negative consequences of pollution; ■ Pressure related to water consumption; ■ Pressure related to the consumption of extracted resources; ■ Impacts of waste generated by construction, renovation, or demolition activities; ■ Impact of activities on biodiversity (deforestation, extraction, construction). APPROACH Maximize Saint-Gobain’s contribution: Reducing adverse effects on nature by optimizing circular  flows, using more recycled or bio-sourced materials,  and improving the use of buildings. Minimize Saint-Gobain’s footprint:  Limiting pollution, especially water pollution,  and preserving natural resources and biodiversity  along the value chain.  ACTIONS Proposing solutions: ■ Program for sustainable and high-performance solutions;  ■ Innovation program for sustainability; ■ Creation of or participation in the creation of recycling  networks (waste collection and recovery). Taking action for sustainability: ■ Program on the substitution of substances of concern; ■ Application of the Forest, Timber Purchasing and Water  policies and the “Responsible Purchasing” program. OBJECTIVES ■ 80% reduction in non-recovered waste by 2030 (vs. 2017); ■ 50% reduction in water withdrawals by 2030 (vs. 2017); ■ Zero water withdrawal in areas under extremely high water stress; ■ 100% of packaging recyclable by 2030. ESRS E2 to E5 SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT GOALS: 6; 7; 9; 12; 13 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 51 A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL GROWTH AND RESILIENCE  Leadership opportunities: ■ Engaging business partners in the implementation  of sustainable strategies; ■ Positive impacts of commitments to local communities  through local anchoring and the socio-economic  development of territories; ■ Employee well-being along the value chain through fair  treatment, social dialog, inclusion, and diversity. Opportunities associated with operational  and financial control:  ■ Improving employee performance across the value chain  through opportunities and skills development; ■ Developing long-term relationships with suppliers  in the value chain to ensure their stability. Regulation and compliance:  ■ Anticipating regulations and responding to growing market  demand for greater product transparency. CHALLENGES  Reduce the negative impact on society:  ■ Exposing employees and workers in the value chain to physical and psychological problems related to health and safety,  working conditions, inequalities, and human rights;  ■ Exposing customers and end users to health and safety issues; ■ Exposing affected communities to risks of industrial accidents.  Anticipate the impacts of legal and regulatory changes and their potential effects on the Group’s reputation: ■ Inappropriate product use by customers and end users; ■ Unethical governance practices (corruption and bribery, lobbying, lack of whistleblower protection). APPROACH Maximize Saint-Gobain’s contribution: Delivering solutions with health, comfort and safety benefits  across the value chain, and supporting communities  by accelerating the transition to sustainable construction. Minimize Saint-Gobain’s footprint:  Promoting ethical conduct, diversity and inclusion, health  and safety and social dialog by engaging all stakeholders,  including suppliers. ACTIONS Proposing solutions: ■ Program for sustainable and high-performance solutions;  ■ Innovation program for sustainability. Taking action for sustainability: ■ Employee engagement and diversity: HR policy, “Ethics and  Compliance” program; ■ Health and Safety across the value chain: Responsible  Purchasing and Human Rights policies, Health and Safety  Charter; ■ Stakeholder support: Saint-Gobain Foundation,  “Build Change” program. OBJECTIVES ■ Value of TF2 by 2030: 1.4; ■ 33% female managers by 2030; ■ Employee engagement rate above benchmark (&gt;76% in 2025) each year (annual); ■ 100% coverage of the CARE program (annual); ■ Diversity index above 90% (annual). ESRS S1 to S4 / G1 SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT GOALS: 1; 3; 4; 5; 8; 9; 10; 11; 16; 17 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 52 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.2.5 SAINT-GOBAIN'S VALUE CHAIN Saint-Gobain’s value chain extends from the extraction of  raw materials to the end-user, including the end-of-life of  products. Its four-part structure is identical, regardless of  country or market. This value chain is deployed locally,  i.e., raw materials come from suppliers located in the  same region as the site that will transform them. The  range of products and solutions is defined by the  country, i.e., as close as possible to the markets and  customers it serves. This is why the country platforms are  central to the deployment of the “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic  plan. Exports of the Group’s products are  marginal and  limited to specific situations, such as the supply of pipes  for infrastructure-related projects.  Similarly, while the principle of circularity (step 4) is  universal, its deployment and implementation are strictly  local. Its results are visible only if there is  complementarity at each stage of the value chain. This  implies effective collaboration between stakeholders in  each country.  (1) Life Cycle Assessment.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 53 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 54 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com See section 3.4.6, p. 175 See sections “Markets and activities”, p. 8,  1.2.2.B and D, p. 43 and p. 45, 1.2.7.A, p. 60 See sections 1.1.2, p. 27 and 1.1.3, p. 29 See section 3.3.3.D, p. 145 The synergies between the expertise of the central  purchasing teams and the country or regional  organizations make it possible to meet the needs of the  sites, whether industrial or tertiary, in terms of  quality,  quantity, regularity and service, and competitiveness.  Group procurement is organized to combine the  Group’s operational expertise and negotiation capacity  with the specific needs of local business lines and sites.  Thus, a central team, Saint-Gobain Purchasing,  coordinates the purchasing of the major purchasing  categories. For example, coordination is in place  for  energy purchases in order to secure supply  and  control energy costs and to identify more local  sources of supply of decarbonized energy that meet  both the requirements of site supply continuity  and  energy cost competitiveness. These synergies  between the central team and the local teams  are  essential for the deployment of the “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategy, including the achievement of the financial  and non-financial objectives by 2030.  Saint-Gobain’s purchasing policy favors the establishment  of medium- and long-term contracts and partnerships  with its suppliers. Given the diversity of  materials,  products, or services purchased and the number of active  suppliers, partner companies can be large companies,  mid-sized companies, or small and medium-sized  companies. The exposure to risks of suppliers’ employees is one of  the criteria taken into account in responsible purchasing  risk mapping (see section 3.4.6, p. 175). The supply chain of industrial sites is mostly regional.  This implies the management of a large number of  suppliers. Nearly 190,000 companies supply the sites,  with nearly 20,700 account for 90% of the Group’s non- trade purchases (by purchase value).  Purchases corresponding to the offering of the trading  business lines are managed directly by the country  platforms. They are concentrated around more than  10,850 suppliers. Each brand develops its offering  according to its positioning and the expectations of the  markets in the countries in which it operates. As such, it  manages relations with mainly local suppliers.  The environmental and social impacts related to the  Group’s procurement, including the exposure risks of  employees of suppliers and subcontractors, as well as  the responsible procurement policy, risk mapping, and  action plans are described in section 3.4.6, p. 175. They consist of industrial sites including quarries,  trading or service agencies including logistics sites and  storage areas, and other tertiary sites, such as offices or  R&amp;D centers. The geographic breakdown of sites and  workforce is shown in the “Our global footprint” section  (pp. 12 and 13). Thus, solutions for the interior, solutions  for the exterior, solutions for construction sites and  construction chemicals are developed, produced, and  sold in each country in order to meet the specific needs  of the construction market and customers. The impacts  associated with industrial sites are closely monitored  from an environmental perspective due to industrial  processes and stored materials (see section 1.2.7.D.b,  p. 64 and section 5.1.1, p. 290); from a social perspective  through team-based work organization (see section  3.4.2, p.  154); and in terms of relationships with local  communities (see section 3.4.3, p. 162). In general, Saint-Gobain employees have permanent  contracts (91.8% in 2025). Depending on operational  needs, employees with fixed-term contracts (8.2% in 2025)  or temporary workers may supplement the workforce.  Some countries make marginal use of independent  contractors (fewer than 5,000 people in 2024). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 55 Each country platform has a portfolio of sustainable and  high-performance solutions (see section 1.2.7.A, p.  60)  to  meet the needs of the residential construction,  non-residential construction, infrastructure, and other  industries markets. Saint-Gobain considers a customer to  be any company or person having an influence on the  purchasing decision. Depending on the market,  customers may be segmented into three categories: ■ Direct customers: companies that acquire products or  services for use in production or construction projects  or to resell them; ■ Users: companies or persons who use the products in  their industrial process, during the implementation of  a construction or renovation project or after their  installation. ■ Specifiers: companies or persons who do not buy  or use the Group’s products and services, but whose  opinion is vital to the purchasing decision process  of  direct customers due to their technical expertise,  reputation or any other capacity (architects,  for example).  At the various stages of construction projects,  interactions with customers help determine needs and  adapt solutions. Each country is committed to  deploying solutions adapted to all channels: direct sales  or through networks of distributors or e-commerce.  Saint-Gobain’s strategy does not directly target  consumers. Some distributors, who are direct customers  of the Group, specialize in or develop business activities  linked to the “do it yourself” trend. Saint-Gobain  is  committed to adapting its offering to specific needs  in  order to support the growth of this segment.  Depending on the country, direct customers and users  can be large international companies (e.g., for large new  construction projects), trading houses, craftsmen, or sole  proprietorships. Employees of companies, whether  customers or users, work mainly on construction sites.  Their working conditions depend on the country, local  regulations, country-specific construction methods,  the climate, and the local labor market.  To develop circularity in the construction market, it is not  enough to offer recyclable products or limit waste on  construction sites, it is a question of creating new local  ecosystems. Through their leadership and proximity to  customers, country platforms can make circularity  a real growth driver. Saint-Gobain integrates the circular  economy principles into its solutions placed  on the market right from the design stage: ■ By limiting the use of non-renewable resources and  developing the reusability of products, the proportion  of recycled or biosourced materials or byproducts  incorporated into production and product packaging  (1) + (2);  ■ By reducing the proportion of final waste throughout  the entire value chain; ■ By ensuring the recyclability of the solutions placed  on the market (2); ■ By collaborating with its customers to reduce the  impact on natural resources of the construction and  building industry in particular, to extend the lifespan  of buildings and facilitate the collection of recyclable  materials when buildings are demolished (3). As part of its program to develop sustainable and  high-performance solutions (see section 1.2.7.A, p.  60),  Saint-Gobain strives to develop solutions that optimize  the use of resources. Special attention is paid to the  recyclability of products and packaging placed on the  market. Some Saint-Gobain products, such as glass and  gypsum products, can be recycled indefinitely in a  closed-loop industrial process. Industrial processes are  then adapted to replace natural raw materials with  recycled materials derived from internal or  external  collection.  Depending on the markets addressed, Saint -Gobain’s  products have a lifespan that is variable but optimized  relative to their use. Some products, such as glazing,  have theoretical lifespans of up to 30 years; others, such  as additives in the construction chemicals market,  are designed for a specific use. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 56 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.2.6 THE GROUP'S VALUE CREATION MODEL The value creation model shows the consistency of the  Group’s strategy. The “Grow &amp; Impact” (2020 – 2025)  and then "Lead &amp; Grow" (2025 – 2030) strategic plans  are aligned both with the purpose of “Making the world a  better home” and with the vision of being the leader in  light and sustainable construction. Saint-Gobain guides  its efforts on the megatrends of global demand for  construction, the fight against climate change, and  resource scarcity (see section 1.2.1, p. 39). Based on the  challenges and opportunities associated with these  megatrends, the Group has defined strategic objectives  to accelerate sustainable profitable growth and  outperform the markets (see section 1.2.2, p. 42).  As part of its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan, Saint-Gobain  confirms its vision of a construction market that it  approaches in a decentralized and local manner through  its country platforms (see section 1.2.5, p.  53), its  commitment to meeting customer expectations by  adopting a solutions-based approach (see section 1.1.3,  p.  29) and its ambition to increase its presence in the  non-residential construction and infrastructure markets  (see section 1.2.2, p. 42). To this end, the country platforms will be able to draw  on the Group’s expertise (1.2.7.F, p.  67) in terms of  industrial excellence, innovation, best commercial  practices and environmental performance.  Saint-Gobain integrates its stakeholders into its model  through mapping (see section 1.2.3, p. 46). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 57 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 58 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com We deploy our solutions-based  approach country by country  and across all sales channels  to meet our customers’  expectations and create  value for Saint-Gobain We are increasing  our presence in  the non-residential and  infrastructure markets  thanks to an expanded,  adapted, and enhanced  offering We rely on highly effective  country platforms  with strong local roots,  which catalyze our growth PRESENTING SAIN