.4 Accident frequency rate*  in 2025 see section 1.3.2.C, p. 78 91%  Proportion of employees  who took part in training  during the year see section 1.3.2.C, p. 78 30% Percentage of female  managers in 2025 see section 1.3.2.C, p. 78 * With and without lost time  for 1 million hours worked by  our employees, temporary workers,  and permanent subcontractors. PERFORMANCE SEGMENTS A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL €1,166 M  Dividends paid €21 M   Investments  in local communities €5,178 M  Portion retained  for growth €1,234 M   Taxes and duties €342 M  Interest  repayment  including IFRS16  interest€9,458 M   Salaries and  other personnel  compensation €300 M  Retirement  contributions SHARING THE VALUE CREATED €46,483 M  Sales €17,699 M Value added €29,376 M  Operational  costs/payments  to suppliers VALUE SHARING (SEE. P. 73) €592 M   Other miscel-  laneous income  1.2.7 CROSS-FUNCTIONAL STRENGTHS AND LEVERS A – Offering solutions that  combine performance  and sustainability Saint-Gobain adopts a customer-centric approach with  a view to developing complete, innovative solutions  (see “Solutions-based approach”, p.  6). In each of the  countries in which it operates, the teams offer their  customers solutions combining performance and  contribution to sustainable development, in particular  promoting the acceleration towards a decarbonized  and more circular economy.  As well as providing high-performance solutions, the  Group’s offering must enable its customers to make  progress on their own sustainable development goals,  thereby accelerating the transition of the construction,  mobility and industry sectors to a “low-carbon” economy  that consumes fewer natural resources, while improving  the health, safety, and well-being of users.  To measure its progress in this respect, since 2020  Saint-Gobain has implemented a methodology that takes  into account local markets and contexts to estimate the  share of its sales generated by solutions offering  benefits in terms of performance and sustainability. Beyond the traditional approach of meeting a specific  need with a suitable product, the aim is to offer clients  comprehensive, end-to-end support in addressing  complex challenges, modeled as a series of “use cases” – illustrations of particular scenarios such as building a  single-family home or renovating a hospital. Examples of  how these solutions are implemented in different  countries are presented in section 1.1.3, p. 29. To measure the performance of solutions , the Group  assesses the increase in economic value for the customer  based on criteria such as productivity and financial  benefits, as well as the user experience.  To measure the sustainability impacts of solutions, the  methodology relies on performance assessments of  solutions in terms of environmental or health  and well-being impacts.  The performance of a solution is assessed across the  entire value chain and for the main stakeholders  involved, right through to the end user.  In addition to measuring its overall effort, this approach –  which covers more than 90% of Saint-Gobain’s sales –  aims to align the sustainability benefit assessment  methods, and guide the work of the Group’s teams,  particularly in terms of innovation, customer relations,  sales, marketing, and the design of solutions. The share of sales generated with sustainable solutions  was 73% in 2025. a. Product innovation  and customer proximity Creating more value for its customers, strengthening its  offering of more sustainable solutions, taking full  advantage of its local and global presence and expertise,  and reducing time-to-market: these are the objectives to  which Saint-Gobain’s teams are committed.  The offering of solutions is defined by the country  platforms (see section 1.2.5, p.  53) as close as possible  to the needs of customers and local markets.  The in-depth knowledge of the markets, the proximity of  the teams with its customers (see pp. 6 and 7), the design  of a residential, non-residential, or infrastructure project  (see value chain 1.2.5, p. 53), allows the teams to propose  innovations developed locally or adapted from solutions  that have proven their worth in other countries; possibly  with the technical support and expertise of the central  teams by business line – “performance segments”. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 60 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com ECONOMIC VALUE  INCREASE IMPROVED QUALITY ENVIRONMENTAL  IMPACTS HEALTH AND  WELL-BEING IMPACTS PERFORMANCE SUSTAINABILITY ■ Productivity ■ Other financial benefits ■ Reliability ■ Creativity, adaptability in design ■ Energy efficiency and carbon footprint ■ Circularity et water resources preservation ■ Products and services that support the  environmental performance of other sectors ■ Security and safety, including professional  risks reduction on value chain ■ Ergonomics for installers ■ Interior air quality ■ Acoustic, thermal and visual comfort Regulatory and normative requirements are integrated  into the innovation process from the earliest stage of the  marketing brief, and marketing teams in each country of  commercialization ensure product compliance with  regulations. Lastly, the “World Class Manufacturing” (WCM)  industrial excellence program  (see section 1.2.7.F.a,  p. 67) ensures the deployment of best practices in terms  of quality and product compliance at industrial sites,  based on the ISO 9001 standard. Technical products are launched after sales force  training and reinforced support by product managers  and technical trainers. In several countries, they  intervene directly at worksites when installers use a  differentiating innovation for the first time, throughout  the launch phases until the sales organization reaches a  sufficient level of maturity.  In addition, a monitoring procedure incorporating every  step in the innovation process allows for the rapid  identification and consideration of potential difficulties  prompting attention to potential problems. Close  monitoring of the progress achieved and product  performance means that the process of innovation is  faster and more secure. Saint-Gobain University’s “Unicampus” program  (see  section 1.2.7.F.b, p.  70) offers five training courses  associated with the innovation process. These training  courses are primarily intended for marketing and R&amp;D  functions but also aim to raise awareness across  all functions. A product compliance training module has  also been incorporated into the program. Practical  courses combining theory, practice, and experience  sharing are offered widely to the principal offices  involved in the innovation process, such as marketing,  R&amp;D, purchasing, production, and industrial efficiency. The process for launching new products, systems and  services is verified as part of internal control (see section  1.2.7.F.a, p.  67). It is regularly updated to improve the  quality management system procedures and associated  monitoring indicators.  b. Toward more sustainable  products and solutions Saint-Gobain has incorporated an environmental, health  and safety impact verification dimension into the  innovation process since 2008 in the form of a checklist.  Since 2023, a new standard has been developed and a  tool for integrating sustainability issues into the  innovation process is available to the teams.  The main objectives of the new standard for innovation are: ■ Eliminating or limiting the presence of substances of  very high concern in new products marketed by  Saint-Gobain and used in the downstream value chain; ■ Eliminating or limiting and controlling potential health  and environmental risks during production processes  at Saint-Gobain sites. ■ select relevant sustainability indicators to measure  performance throughout the innovation process,  based on product categories and market needs. At each stage of the innovation process, this tool makes  it possible to define and monitor objectives for reducing  the environmental footprint and maximizing health and  well-being benefits in accordance with the approach to  propose efficient and sustainable solutions (see section  1.2.7.A, p. 60).  c. Informing customers  through to end users To provide clients with information on the environmental  benefits of products and solutions, Saint -Gobain is  committed to the development and  promotion of life  cycle analyses (LCAs). These analyses are useful internally to guide innovation,  and to meet growing market demand for greater  transparency. Saint-Gobain is committed to ensuring  that, by 2030, 100% of the Group’s sales, excluding  distribution activities, will be generated by products  covered by verified LCAs (life cycle analyses) or EPDs  (environmental product declarations).  As of late 2025, 57% (stable vs 2024) of sales were  generated by products covered by verified LCAs or  EPDs, either specific (produced by Saint -Gobain) or  generic (produced by professional associations). This represents more than 5.000 EPDs (2,700 in 2024)  verified EPDs published in close to 40 countries; the  Group is positioned as the world’s leading provider of  verified EPDs – by number – in the construction industry. Saint-Gobain’s customers also rely on sustainable  construction certifications, such as BREEAM and LEED,  to assess and enhance the overall performance of their  buildings. They seek products whose features help  achieve the desired levels of performance. To support country platform teams in leveraging this  opportunity, dedicated tools have been made available  for three segments – gypsum, insulation, and glass.  These include checklists to identify the potential  contributions of products to the main sustainability  standards, as well as evidence that can be provided to  customers (documents, data). These tools are  supplemented by locally adaptable presentations to  share this information with customers.  For example, the contribution of Saint-Gobain glazing is  available on the Saint -Gobain Glass website: https:// www.saint-gobain-glass.com/green-building-labelling- schemes.  According to Group’s estimates, the number of projects  supported by a sustainable construction certification is  rising in the main countries where it operates.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 61 With regard to information on users, the Group’s  products comply with current regulations, such as CE  marking in Europe, or the requirement for chemical  products to have labels and safety data sheets (SDS).  Saint-Gobain also provides non-mandatory information  specific to some of its products, such as: ■ Voluntary safety data declaration sheets for  unclassified articles or substances; ■ The declaration of the composition of components  and materials via the IMDS (International Material Data  System) database in the case of the automotive  industry; ■ Environmental Product Declarations (EPDs) for  construction products (or Environmental and  Health  Declaration Forms (FDES) in France); ■ Voluntary certifications obtained through  independent national laboratories – or, failing that,  international laboratories close to these markets –  when innovative products or systems are not covered  by international or national standards. Specific measures relating to the product compliance  program are presented in section 1.2.7.F.a, p. 67. B – Creating the conditions  for success: organization  for performance Established in June 2025 to create the conditions  required for the deployment of the “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategic plan, Saint-Gobain’s new organization is part of  a strategy aimed at strengthening profitable growth,  simplifying the structure, and asserting the Group’s  worldwide leadership in light and sustainable  construction. This development is not a break, but a  natural step that draws on the strengths of the existing  model in order to go further. It aims to  strengthen  Saint-Gobain’s ability to efficiently execute its strategic  plan by combining proximity, agility, and expertise. For several years, the multi-local model has enabled  the Group to get closer to the markets and customers.  Today, this model is being expanded : each country is  becoming fully responsible for all business lines, with  greater autonomy to coordinate commercial and  operational performance. This empowerment makes it  possible to act faster, seize opportunities in markets that  are still partially addressed, and expand Saint -Gobain’s  offering to create more value. It also promotes a  solutions-based approach, tailored to the needs of  customers, whether for key accounts, distribution  networks, or direct sales. To support this change, Saint-Gobain has created a  Group Operational Performance Department. Its role is  to guide, challenge and support countries in the  implementation of their growth plans. This Department  relies on two complementary levers. On the one hand,  the Performance Segments provide business expertise  to enrich the offering , maximize synergies and ensure  strategic consistency. On the other hand, the shared  Expertise Platforms provide innovation, digital,  purchasing and industrial and commercial excellence  solutions. This ensures that each country has  the  resources and skills to succeed. This organization also simplifies the Group’s structure.  This integration promotes strategic coherence, the  pooling of resources, and the achievement of economies  of scale, which will be reinvested in  growth. This is an  operational lever to aim for significant cost reduction.  This organization also strengthens the perception of the  consistency of offers and stimulates the culture of  collaboration within Saint-Gobain.  The expected benefits are concrete and measurable. The  Group will be able to offer a more comprehensive and  better-deployed offering, improve its operational  efficiency, strengthen its proximity to customers and act  quickly in an economic environment marked by  regionalization. This organization is designed to  accelerate profitable growth , simplify processes to  become more competitive, and assert Saint-Gobain as a  worldwide leader in light and sustainable construction  across its entire scope. This change comes at a key moment. The current  organization has reached maturity: its strengths  are known and its areas for improvement are identified.  The global context is shifting towards deglobalization,  which requires more agility and flexibility. By deepening  the multi-local model and mobilizing 100% of the Group’s  scope in the countries, Saint-Gobain is giving itself the  means to respond to these challenges and seize  opportunities. This organization is designed as a  powerful lever to execute the new “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategic plan. C – Rigorously allocating  financial resources Saint-Gobain rigorously applies its dynamic capital  allocation approach, adhering to strict criteria in terms of  value creation potential. As part of its “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategic plan, the Group plans to deploy approximately  €20 billion of capital between now and 2030, combining  growth and attractive returns for shareholders, while  maintaining a solid balance sheet with a net debt to  EBITDA ratio of between 1.5x and 2.0x. Of this total,  Saint-Gobain plans to allocate $12  billion in growth  investments  (1) over the 2025-2030 period , giving  priority to high-growth markets (North America, Asia- Pacific, growth countries, construction chemicals) as well  as consolidating its leading positions and enriching its  offering for each regional platform, country by country.  The Group also plans to increase shareholder returns to  around €8 billion: approximately €6 billion distributed in  dividends, targeting steady growth in the dividend per  share; and €2 billion in share buybacks. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 62 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) Growth investment: growth capex + net M&amp;A (acquisitions net of divestments). a. Supporting the Group’s growth  and commitments In 2025, Saint-Gobain continued its investment policy,  allocating resources to strengthen its industrial base   with 24 new lines or plants coming on stream during the  year. These investments cover all continents (Americas,  Europe, Asia-Pacific, Middle East, and Africa) and a very  large part of the Group’s activities, from plaster to  insulation, construction chemicals, mortars, and solutions  for industry and mobility. In addition, in 2025, the Group  committed €213  million in investments (CAPEX and  R&amp;D) to support its carbon roadmap, with the goal of  spending at least €100  million per year on these  expenses until 2030. b. Ensuring agile, value-creating  portfolio management Saint-Gobain is consistently pursuing a strategy to  optimize the Group’s profile in order to continue to  strengthen growth, profitability, and value creation.  From 2018 through December 31, 2025, Saint-Gobain has  made acquisitions representing approximately €7 billion  in revenue and divestments representing around  €10  billion in revenue. As part of its “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategic plan, the Group is targeting an asset rotation of  more than 20% of revenue over the 2026-2030 period. Continuing to pursue a strategy of value-creating acquisitions In 2025, Saint-Gobain completed 17 acquisitions,  generating additional sales of €862  million. These  acquisitions consolidate Saint-Gobain’s position as the  worldwide leader in light and sustainable construction  and accelerate its growth. In the construction chemicals sector, the Group  completed the acquisition of the Ovniver Group (Cemix  brand), a major player in the construction chemicals  sector in Mexico and Central America. Saint-Gobain also  completed the acquisition of Fosroc, a leading company  in construction chemicals with a strong presence in India,  the Middle East, and Asia-Pacific. This acquisition  strengthens the Group’s presence in high-growth  emerging markets and consolidates its worldwide  leadership in light and sustainable construction.  Saint-Gobain also strengthened its digital platform  dedicated to construction chemicals with the acquisition  of Maturix, a Danish company offering digital solutions  for real-time monitoring of concrete manufacturing. This  transaction expands the Group’s digital offering,  complements its VERIFI® system, and enables smarter  and more sustainable construction processes. Conducting regular reviews of the portfolio  in order to concentrate the Group’s strengths In 2025, the Group completed 6 divestments, generating  €352  million in revenue. Saint-Gobain signed an  agreement to sell Brüggemann, a German specialist in  prefabrication solutions, to the Köster Group. The Group  also finalized the sale of SFIC Belgium, a distributor of  building materials specializing in plaster, insulation, and  ceilings on the Belgian market, as well as the sale of  Tumelero, a building materials distribution chain in Brazil  with a strong presence in the south of the country. D – Creating a culture  of innovation, serving  industrial performance a. Developing a culture of innovation  Innovation is the lever on which Saint-Gobain relies to  develop new products and solutions. It is both a  condition and an accelerator of growth. Innovation  makes it possible: ■ to meet the evolving customer expectations (in  particular for more performance and sustainability) in  order to continuously adjust the Group’s offering;  ■ to stand out from its competitors;  ■ to take advantage of opportunities created by global  megatrends (see section 1.2.1, p. 39);  ■ to anticipate the rapid evolution of the role of digital  and artificial intelligence , which are transforming  Saint-Gobain’s markets;  ■ to stay ahead of increased competition;  ■ to respond effectively to the rapid growth and issues  of certain markets  (for example, the need for  increased productivity for installers, due to a growing  shortage of skilled construction and renovation  workers).  In this context, innovation is a cross-functional asset  that permeates all the Group’s activities and enables  the success of its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan   (see  section 1.2.2, p.  42). To make the most of it,  Saint-Gobain defines priorities and aims to find the right  balance between the different innovation horizons; links  innovation to the expectations of its markets,  maintaining the greatest proximity to its customers (see  above, section 1.2.7.A.a, p.  60); and tends to make  progress to reduce the time-to-market for innovative  solutions.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 63 For the Group, innovation is an open process, involving  both resources and research and development teams  present around the world (8 transversal R&amp;D centers,  3,600 researchers and 450+ patents filed in 2025) as well  as external partners such as start-ups (30+ agreements  signed in 2025 via its NOVA entity), universities,  customers and suppliers of the Group. Saint -Gobain  implements innovation through a structured, Group-wide  system, based on clear strategic priorities, a unified  Innovation Framework, and strong coordination between  central and regional teams.  Innovation is driven by  customer centricity, sustainability, and the transition of  products to complete solutions, supported by methods,  tools, and learning loops that improve success rates and  shorten time-to-market. A central Innovation  Development team maintains and deploys this  framework, while the regions gradually take responsibility  in order to maximize the impact as close as possible to  the activities. Skills are strengthened through extensive  training programs, including the network of Innovation  Catalysts – employees formally trained to stimulate  innovation locally – as well as through digital tools such  as the Innovation Project Companion, which offers  secure, personalized support. Together, these elements  create a shared mindset, harmonized practices, and  execution discipline that enable Saint-Gobain to innovate  consistently and effectively across the Group.  Saint-Gobain uses AI and digital tools to accelerate  innovation, facilitating faster experimentation, more  informed decision-making, and better access to  knowledge. Generative AI contributes to the  development of new materials, while secure internal tools  increase productivity and support teams. Together, these  technologies optimize workflows, reduce time-to-market,  and drive data-driven innovation.  b. Integrating innovation  into industrial processes  Innovation is central to Saint-Gobain’s industrial  operations. By relying on innovation the Group makes  progress in terms of operational excellence,  decarbonization, and circularity . Year after year,  investments in industrial facilities have increased  productivity and safety, reduced carbon emissions, raw  material consumption and waste production.  This modernization involves the integration of digital  technologies (in particular via IoT sensors for real-time  monitoring of energy production and consumption);  predictive maintenance through data analysis  (vibrations, temperatures, pressures) in order to reduce  unplanned shutdowns; and digital twinning to simulate  and optimize production lines. This also involves  replacing energy sources , such as biogas or  hydroelectricity, rather than fossil fuels. It also involves  automation and robotification , deploying robots for  repetitive, dangerous or delicate tasks (glass handling,  packaging) and automated quality control systems to  immediately detect defects. It is also a question of  reducing the carbon footprint , through the energy  optimization of glass furnaces (new geometries, heat  recovery); the use of alternative fuels (hydrogen,  electricity) to replace natural gas; or by integrating  waste or end-of-life products into the production  process. Saint-Gobain is also working on deploying tools  based on artificial intelligence , for example in its  automotive glazing plants – to improve product quality –  or to optimize the operation of its fiberglass furnaces, in  order to reduce costs, the quantity of resources and  energy used, and CO 2 emissions. Finally, the Group is  making progress thanks to new materials  and the  optimization of formulations, through more efficient and  sustainable materials (insulation, smart glazing), less  energy-intensive melting and transformation processes,  as well as additives and formulations that improve  quality, while accelerating rates. E – Developing a culture of trust a. Building trust with stakeholders Values shared with Saint-Gobain’s  stakeholders The Group bases its development on its corporate  purpose and draws on strong values firmly rooted in  Saint-Gobain’s culture: the Principles of Conduct and  Action. They constitute the Group’s code of ethics and  guide Saint-Gobain’s decisions. This code of ethics is the foundation of all Saint-Gobain  policies and commitments, specifically the compliance  policies, the human rights policy, the Environmental,  Health and Safety Charter, and the “Buyer” and “Supplier”  Charters of the Purchasing Department. This code of  ethics applies to all Group entities and employees, as well  as subcontractors in their work on Saint-Gobain sites, and  suppliers (see section 1.2.3.A, p. 46).  The Principles of Conduct and Action are translated into  31 languages and available on the Group’s website for  wide distribution to external as well as internal  stakeholders. During the recruitment process or at the  time of onboarding, they are communicated to each new  employee, including those with fixed-term contracts and  temporary workers, in the local language of the country.  This communication may take various forms, depending  on the country: ■ A welcome brochure with comments from the Human  Resources representative or the manager; ■ The code of ethics directly incorporated into the  employment contract or the job offer letter. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 64 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com Online training on the Principles of Conduct and Action  is available to all employees and is mandatory for new  hires. The Saint -Gobain University Management School  offers an introduction to business ethics and compliance  to allow managers to discuss these issues and share  their operational experience.  The Group trains each new manager from their first year  at the Group on the code of ethics, the fight against  corruption, and compliance with antitrust law  (“Principles of Conduct and Action, ACT and Comply”).  Participation in public debate &gt; Advocacy Saint-Gobain participates in public debates on the  strategic challenges  for its business activity and  environment. This participation takes place directly, via  professional associations, and via global, regional, or local  alliances. The Group’s advocacy activities aim to  constructively feed into discussions and bring together  value chain players and stakeholders. Inspired by its  corporate purpose “Making the world a better home ,”  Saint-Gobain regularly works on the priority themes of  decarbonization of the economy, circularity, and well- being in the construction sector (see section 1.3.2, p. 74).  Saint-Gobain and the majority of its subsidiaries belong  to professional associations representing their industry  at the national or supranational levels. For example, in  France, the Group is involved in professional associations  representing companies, such as Afep and Medef.  Saint-Gobain’s subsidiaries also cooperate with various  local associations or organizations involved in normative  or regulatory issues, or related to  environmental, social,  societal or economic issues. The subsidiaries have internal  procedures in place to ensure that their participation in  associations is recognized and referenced, and that  employees who represent them in associations are  trained in the rules of antitrust law. In 2025, Saint-Gobain  published a Lobbying Policy.  Policy Lobbying Policy Purpose Provide a framework for  responsible lobbying activities Scope of application Employees, corporate officers,  Group managers in contact with  managers holding political office,  administrations or governments Under the supervision of Director</data>
    </edge>
