 the  United Kingdom, for example, there is a shortfall of  2.5 million homes, and the current government aims to  build 1.5  million homes between 2025 and 2029, which  represents a clear acceleration from the construction  levels of the last decade.  The winds are also favorable for building renovation. In  addition to the ongoing recovery in real estate  transactions in most European countries, which are an  important driver of renovation, energy renovation is  supported by public measures , both at the European  and national levels. In addition to European regulatory  mechanisms, such as the Energy Performance of  Buildings Directive (EPBD), many European countries  have adopted regulatory arrangements (such as the  Climate and Resilience Act in France, which prohibits the  leasing of energy-inefficient residences), subsidies, and  market mechanisms (such as energy saving certificates  in Spain). Energy renovation also offers significant  financial benefits for households, through its ability to  reduce energy bills on the one hand, and also because of  the growing influence of energy efficiency on  property  prices. In many European countries, price differentials  between the most and least energy-efficient homes  exceed 15%, even comparing homes with equivalent  structural characteristics: this is the case in the  Netherlands, for example, as well as in Germany, France,  Italy and Spain. Non-residential and infrastructure  markets are also drivers in Europe.  The need for reindustrialization, particularly in the  defense sector, and the development of data centers  represent sources of growth for new construction, while  the energy renovation of tertiary buildings, both public  and private, maintains its growth level. In the  infrastructure sector, some European governments have  ambitious development projects planned; this is notably  the case in Germany, where the new €500  billion  Infrastructure Fund will significantly increase activity in  this sector over the next 10 years. The requirements of  renovating aging infrastructure on the continent,  including bridges and tunnels, offer complementary  opportunities. Finally, the increasing effects of climate  crises on buildings, both residential and non-residential,  imply significant adaptation needs . The solutions  offered by Saint-Gobain for renovation are perfectly  adapted to these new needs. For example, protecting  buildings against heat waves involves insulating external  walls or installing solar control glass. Similarly, the  Group’s construction chemicals solutions meet the need  for buildings to be watertight, against violent rains or  floods, for example.  B – North America  In North America, the housing shortage  is a very  important factor in the structural growth  of  Saint-Gobain’s business in the residential sector. In the  US, the pace of construction in the decade following the  2008 global financial crisis did not keep up with  population growth, and the shortfall is currently  estimated at 3.7  million homes  (2), which represents  nearly 3 years’ worth of construction at the current pace.  In Canada, strong population growth over the past  decade, fueled by dynamic immigration, has also led to  severe housing shortages, exacerbating the housing  affordability crisis: an additional 5  million homes would  be needed to meet the needs and restore the housing  purchasing power of Canadian households (3).  Furthermore, the frequency of hurricanes, fires and  storms, which is unfortunately accentuated by climate  change, implies non-discretionary renovation needs ,  which represent a source of sustainable growth for the  Group’s roofing and cladding business in the region. The  increase in such climatic events due to the climate  crisis will generate significant adaptation needs in the  coming years.  Finally, the non-residential and infrastructure markets  also benefit from solid structural fundamentals. The  reindustrialization policies of successive US  governments, as well as the trend towards reshoring  industries on US soil, are driving growth in the industrial  buildings market and the rise of data centers represents  a significant source of growth. As in Europe,  infrastructure needs remain high, particularly in terms  of renovation. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 27 (1) CGEDD. (2) Freddie Mac. (3) CMCH Canada Mortgage &amp; Housing Corporation. C – Asia and fast-growing regions Emerging countries are experiencing strong population  growth, combined with rapid urbanization. The global  population will increase from 8.2 billion in 2024 to nearly  10 billion by 2080 (1), and most of this increase will take  place in emerging countries. Similarly, the urban  population is expected to grow by almost 2  billion by  2050, particularly in Asia and Africa. In addition, around  1.1 billion people currently live in informal settlements and  are still waiting for decent housing (2). Based on this data,  the World Economic Forum estimates that  new  residential construction needs  worldwide will  reach  2 billion homes over the next 80 years.  Emerging countries are also experiencing economic  development trajectories favoring strong growth of the  middle classes , whose desire for quality housing  perfectly matches Saint-Gobain’s offer. In India, the  number of households entering the middle class will  increase by an average of 16% per year by 2030. This  growth has also reached 11% in Indonesia, and 21% in  Vietnam. Urbanization will also continue in the Middle  East and Latin America : in Brazil, for example,  the  middle class is expected to grow by nearly 4%  annually over the coming years  (3). This demographic  growth and its associated urbanization imply significant  needs for the expansion of  cities, with consequential  tertiary housing and  infrastructure needs, representing  complementary sources of business for the Group. Some  countries are  starting to build new cities or ambitious  urban development projects. In Egypt, 37 new cities are  under construction, and Saudi Arabia is currently  building 14 large-scale projects to support its economic  diversification program. D – Industrial markets For its industrial customers , Saint-Gobain provides  performance solutions and meets market expectations  through its ability to innovate , its research and  development potential, its closeness to its customers,  and its use of digital technology and data analysis.  Industrial markets are expected to continue to grow  steadily over the 2025-2030 period, driven by  reindustrialization and strategic autonomy efforts in  developed countries, ramped-up military spending, and  the development of new industrial sectors associated  with “green” technologies.  The mobility market, in which Saint-Gobain is present  through a range of insulation, acoustics, and technical  glazing solutions, is undergoing expansion fueled by the  natural turnover of car fleets, the transition to electric  vehicles, and the relocation of value chains by auto  manufacturers, trends that will structurally drive the  Group’s business in the coming years. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 28 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) United Nations. (2) Idem. (3) Oxford Economics. 1.1.3 A SOLUTIONS-BASED APPROACH,  ROLLED OUT COUNTRY BY COUNTRY  Saint-Gobain relies on the support of a robust organization that is well suited to a deglobalized world, as well as  local construction markets. It is based on country platforms as managerial units, which are grouped into five major  regions: Northern Europe, Southern Europe – Middle East – Africa, North America, Latin America, and Asia-Pacific.  Within each of these regions, the country CEOs are responsible for all Saint-Gobain product lines, to provide a  comprehensive response to the most complex needs of their customers, through a solutions-based approach.  Country by country, Saint-Gobain is deploying this approach in all its markets – residential as well as non-residential  and infrastructure. A – Construction and renovation markets a. Residential In all its countries, Saint-Gobain has successfully  deployed a solutions-based approach, enabling it to  position itself as a “one-stop shop,” a leading supplier of  integrated solutions both for renovation  and new  construction of single-family houses and multi- family housing. The Group provides a comprehensive range of solutions  dedicated to the global renovation of housing,  which  help improve their energy performance, acoustic and  visual comfort, as well as air quality. In France, for  example, a customer looking to undertake a  residential  renovation project can find an integrated offering from  Saint-Gobain that covers all their needs. First of all, this  offering includes comprehensive renovation systems,  particularly external thermal insulation solutions  combining insulating materials, plasterboard, profiles,  mortars, fiberglass reinforcement, and finish coats, as  well as solar-control insulated glazing units and blow-in  insulation for attics. When combined, these solutions  help cut a residence’s energy bill by 70%, and  significantly improve its energy performance diagnosis  (EPD), raising it from class G to class C. Saint-Gobain supplements this offering with digital  solutions available through its distribution businesses,  making it easier to conduct assessments and issue price  quotes for improving building energy performance. The  Group also provides services intended to improve  productivity for craftsmen on worksites , such as  logistics and kitting solutions. Aware of the  environmental issues, Saint -Gobain also provides  worksite waste collection and sorting services, relying  on a network of 810 collection and sorting points in  France, particularly at Point.P stores. Finally, the Group  invests heavily in training professionals: each year, over  100,000 craftsmen are trained and supported in France  on energy renovation issues.  In North America, and particularly in Canada in the past  few years, the Group has expanded its offering  dedicated to lightweight construction , in order to  provide a comprehensive solution for the building  envelope, helping improve the quality of buildings and  their resilience against climate change. Saint-Gobain stands out in particular by offering  complete systems for the customer . For example, the  Group markets integrated roofing systems under the  CertainTeed brand combining waterproofing  membranes, impact-resistant ClimateFlex shingles, and  their associated accessories. These solutions help  buildings better withstand storms and guarantee roof  insurability. The Group also develops siding systems that  are highly resistant to moisture and impacts, while  providing its customers with interactive digital tools that  make it easier to choose and customize solutions.  CertainTeed’s One Precision Assemblies system is  another example that helps significantly reduce project  completion times . Pre-assembled panels featuring  windows, doors, siding, roofing, insulation, storm- proofing and vapor barrier membranes are produced in  one week. Once delivered to a modular home worksite,  they can be installed in under three weeks, compared  with five months for a construction using traditional  methods.  To replace traditional, heavy-construction methods  which devour resources, time, and labor, Saint-Gobain  has developed lightweight façade solutions which are  both ultra-high performance and easy to install . In  Brazil, for example, such solutions combine Glasroc X  board, a moisture-resistant fiberglass-reinforced  plasterboard (Placo and Adfors), glass wool insulation  (Isover), fiber cement sheets (Brasilit), as well as  fiberglass reinforcement mesh and joint tape (Adfors),  and finish coats (Quartzolit) These systems are  supplemented by technical consulting and project  monitoring services.  They make it possible to  significantly improve productivity on worksites ,  particularly in multi-family housing, respond to labor  shortage issues, reduce waste, and offer superior  performance – in terms of quality, energy efficiency, and  occupant comfort – compared with traditional methods. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 29 Example: renovation of a multi-family residential building Example: construction of a single-family house in Canada  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 30 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com Eclaz Sun double glazing  with solar control Interactive digital solution for energy  renovation diagnostics External Thermal Insulation  Composite System Roof thermal  insulation Recycling  services Interior wall  insulation Multipurpose  screeds Acoustic  ceilings Reinforced partitions Heating and plumbing  installation Siding Waterproofing  membranes Ceilings Plasterboard Metal profiles Insulation Roof shingles Roofing  membranes  b. Non-residential Saint-Gobain is accelerating the deployment of its solutions-based approach in non-residential sectors by offering  ranges of solutions dedicated to each market segment (hospitals, schools, hotels, and data centers), meeting the high  demands of its customers in those markets, particularly in terms of energy and acoustic performance, visual comfort,  and air quality. Example of solutions for lightweight construction: school As an example, Saint-Gobain provides a comprehensive  range of high-performance solutions for the  construction and renovation of educational institutions:  ceilings, plaster-based solutions and acoustic flooring  systems help significantly improve the acoustic  performance of classrooms, contributing to better  learning and teaching conditions. To support the  specification of its solutions, Saint-Gobain also offers  augmented-reality digital tools through its Ecophone  acoustic ceilings brand, making it possible to test these  solutions in various acoustic environments recreating  classrooms. In another innovation, plasterboard and  ceilings equipped with Activ’Air technology help absorb  formaldehyde from the ambient air, thus helping  students concentrate better. The Eclaz glazing line  makes it possible to optimize interior comfort, with the  Sun solar-control line for façades that are heavily  exposed to sunlight, or alternatively, the Lumi range of  ultra-transparent glazing, maximizing natural light for  darker rooms. Finally, all of Saint-Gobain’s solutions for  energy renovation , such as its ventilated façade  systems and external thermal insulation systems, make  it possible to reduce heating expenses by 70% in  educational institutions and other public buildings.  Another example: Saint-Gobain is now recognized in  Europe and North America as one of the leaders  – in  terms of supplying construction materials – for data  centers. Saint-Gobain provides a comprehensive,  dedicated range of technical, innovative solutions for  meeting the sector’s strict requirements concerning  thermal performance, fire protection, and productivity.  On this note, there are a few examples we can mention,  such as stone wool-based sandwich panels which are  insulating, fire-resistant, and quick to install, its cool-roof  solutions, making it possible to reduce the temperature  of the building, its ceiling solutions that can support very  heavy loads, its range of solutions for  low-carbon  concrete, its special anti-static flooring, and its fire- resistant glazing.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 31 Customized kitting  service Acoustic  partitions Facade insulation Fire protection  glazing Self-leveling  screeds Suspended ceilings Low-carbon  delivery Solar control glazing  Example: construction of an office building c. Infrastructure Over the past five years, Saint-Gobain has built a place for  itself as a top global leader in construction chemicals. The  Group now offers solutions to meet the needs of  worksites and structures, particularly mortars, concrete  admixtures, cement additives, waterproofing solutions for  buildings and infrastructure, flooring, and façades,  concrete repair solutions, adhesives, joint tape and  adhesive systems. This offering is based on leading brands  like Weber, Chryso, Fosroc, and GCP. Construction  chemicals are essential for the infrastructure market –  bridges, tunnels, airports, hydroelectric power plants and  wind farms – a potentially significant growth driver.   Thanks to a broad range of technical products, the Group  is able to cover the various critical parts of any type  of infrastructure. In this context, Saint-Gobain offers, among others, highly  technical waterproofing solutions for bridges and  tunnels, concrete admixtures and cement additives that  help improve the mechanical performance and durability  of structures, as well as putties and anchoring systems  for fastening and connecting concrete structural  elements. The Group is also developing innovative digital  solutions, like Verifi and Maturix, which are used  to monitor concrete properties in real time.  Additionally, Saint-Gobain offers a comprehensive range  of ductile cast iron pipes  and accessories for water  transport and distribution through the PAM brand, and  with the Adfors brand, it designs fiberglass and technical  textiles used for the repair and reinforcement of roads  and airport runways. Thanks to this integrated, innovative offering,  Saint-Gobain sustainably supports infrastructure  development and modernization,  while meeting  increasing requirements concerning performance,  reliability, and durability. As an example, on the large  airport construction sites in high-growth countries like  Vietnam, Saint-Gobain stands out as the supplier of  integrated solutions  that address both the building  aspect – fire-resistant glazing and partitions, acoustic  ceiling and partition solutions, lightweight ventilated  façade systems, solar-control glazing, waterproofing  systems for flat roofs, or technical flooring for heavy- traffic areas – and the infrastructure aspect –  fiberglass for reinforcing runways and taxiways,  concrete admixtures, waterproofing solutions for  bridges and underground passages, and fireproofing  for steel structures.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 32 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com Ventilation duct  insulation “Cool roof”  waterproofing Highly energy-efficient  glazing Electrochromic  insulating glass Fire-resistant glass  partitions High-security glass Glass wool insulation Mortars for floor  covering Acoustic ceilings Perforated  plasterboard Low-carbon concrete Example: Saint-Gobain transport infrastructure solutions  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 33 Injections  for concrete repair Structural bonding Solutions for  low-carbon concrete Solar control glazing Impact-resistant  partitions Acoustic  plasterboard Fire protection  for structures Structural reinforcement  of taxiways Flooring Waterproofing solutions  for bridge decks In-transit concrete  quality control Protective coatings  for concrete Repair mortars for concrete  finishing and grouting Fiberglass  reinforcement veil Structural sealing  for concrete works Pipes  B – Industrial markets Country by country, Saint-Gobain deploys this same  solutions-based approach over its industrial application  lines. In a context marked by the energy transition,  decarbonization, and the search for performance, the  Group’s solutions make it possible to support the major  industrial players in their transformation. Saint-Gobain  is positioned as a key technological partner, offering high  value-added materials and systems. The Group offers a  comprehensive range of solutions for industry and  mobility, including technical and refractory ceramics for  glass furnaces, metallurgy, chemicals, and industrial  processes; abrasive solutions for cutting, polishing, and  assembly in complex environments; high-performance  polymers and films , offering thermal, chemical, and  dielectric properties; and glazing for mobility , for  automotive, rail and aerospace vehicles, with advanced  features (safety, comfort, design, connectivity). These  products are designed to meet the performance,  reliability and sustainability requirements of industrial  and transport markets. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 34 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.1.4 AN INSPIRING PURPOSE With its corporate purpose, “Making the World a Better Home”, the Group  took on its ambition to improve everyone’s lives by making the planet a fairer,  more inclusive, more harmonious, healthier and sustainable living space. Through its business model, generally, and with its solutions, specifically,  Saint-Gobain has a tangible impact on the life of every individual and on their  living, working, healthcare, educational and mobility environments. The  Group’s corporate purpose is the link between the infinitely small unit of each  person’s living space and the infinitely large one of our shared home: the  planet. It is fully aligned with Saint-Gobain’s vision – to be the worldwide  leader in light and sustainable construction – and with its strategic plan,  “Lead &amp; Grow” (see p. 16 as well as section 1.2.2, p. 42). Our corporate purpose sets the course for our shared future. It looks like us. It  represents a call to action, and is based on the values that guide us, the  Principles of Conduct and Action (see section 1.2.7.E.a, p.  64). This is the  profound ambition of our corporate purpose: to act every day to make the  world a more beautiful and more sustainable place in which to live.  1.1.5 360 YEARS OF HISTORY Saint-Gobain was founded in 1665 by Louis XIV, King of  France, under the name of “Royal Manufacture of  Mirrors”.  Compagnie de Saint-Gobain diversified into sectors such  as chemicals, glass products, and the automotive  industry, rapidly expanding into international markets,  establishing itself in the United States in 1829, Germany  in 1858, Italy in 1888 and Spain in 1905. The Group, which successfully overcame the major crises  of the century and invested heavily in research and  development, withdrew from its chemical activities and  merged with Pont-à-Mousson, created in 1856 and  specializing in cast iron pipes. In 1986, the privatization of Saint-Gobain – which had  been nationalized in 1982 – was a huge popular success:  1,500,000 shareholders subscribed to the transaction.  The acquisition of Norton in 1990, then Carborundum in  1996, enabled the Group to develop its know-how in  abrasives, plastics and ceramics.  The 2005 acquisition of British Plaster Board, the world  leader in plasterboard, combined with Saint-Gobain’s  insulation solutions, made the Group the world’s No. 1 in  interior design solutions. Saint-Gobain focused its strategy on sustainable  housing, while continuing to serve numerous industrial  markets, and pursued its expansion in emerging  countries. In late 2018, the Group launched its “Transform &amp; Grow”  plan aimed at establishing a new organization by country  and ensuring agile and value-creating portfolio  management.  In 2019, Saint-Gobain acquired Continental Building  Products, the first step in strengthening its positions  in the construction market in North America. Saint-Gobain established its corporate purpose,  “Making the world a better home”. The 2021 acquisition of Chryso made Saint-Gobain a  major player in construction chemicals. In October 2021,  Saint-Gobain launched its “Grow &amp; Impact” strategic  plan, setting out its vision to become the  worldwide  leader in light and sustainable construction.  In 2022, the Group finalized the acquisition of Kaycan – a  specialist in exterior building materials – and of GCP  Applied Technologies, a key step in establishing itself as  a leader in construction chemicals.  The following year, Saint-Gobain acquired Building  Products of Canada, a manufacturer of roofing shingles  for the residential sector, and wood fiber insulation  panels.  Saint-Gobain was certified as “Top Employer Global” for  its 2025 fiscal year, for the 11 th consecutive year. The  Group acquired CSR, a leader in construction materials in  Australia and New Zealand, as well as Bailey, a specialist  in metal frames for light construction in Canada, and  initiated the acquisitions of Fosroc, a leading global  player in construction chemicals, and Ovniver (Cemix  brand) in Mexico. These constitute new strategic steps in  establishing Saint-Gobain’s global presence in  construction chemicals, which, once the transactions are  complete, will bring its total sales in this sector to  €6.5 billion (pro forma).  In 2025, Saint-Gobain celebrates its 360th anniversary, a  figure that also represents its global reach and its circular  and comprehensive approach. In October 2025, the  Group launches its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan,  marking the beginning of a new chapter for  Saint-Gobain, based on a clear ambition: to accelerate its  profitable growth in a sustainable manner and  outperform its markets, thereby enhancing the value  created for all its stakeholders. At the end of 2025, the  Group is present in 80 countries (1). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 35 (1) Countries with active industrial presence as of Dec. 31, 2025.  1.1.6 LOOKING BACK ON THE YEAR 2025 January  Saint-Gobain certified as “Top Employer Global”  for the 10th consecutive year Saint-Gobain was certified “Top Employer Global” for the  tenth consecutive year, joining a small group of 17 global  companies recognized in 2025. This award highlights the  Group’s commitment to its 161,000 employees in  80  countries and its continued progress in diversity,  inclusion, sustainable development, and employee  development. Saint-Gobain completes the acquisition of Ovniver  in Mexico and Central America  Saint-Gobain completed the acquisition of Ovniver  Group (Cemix brand), a major player in construction  chemicals in Mexico and Central America. This strategic  transaction strengthens Saint-Gobain’s presence in  high-growth markets and strengthens its global  leadership in construction chemicals products. Saint-Gobain launches its first 100% recycled  plasterboard  Saint-Gobain announces the UK launch of its first  plasterboard entirely made from recycled materials, a  world first for the Group. Developed by British Gypsum,  this innovation represents an important step in  circular  construction and supports the Group’s commitment to  achieving carbon neutrality by 2050. February Saint-Gobain announces the launch of its first  low-carbon stone wool production line in Spain  Saint-Gobain will invest €40  million to expand and  decarbonize its insulation plant in Azuqueca, near  Madrid. The new electric furnace will replace fossil fuels,  reducing CO₂ emissions by 70% and increasing  production capacity by 40%, marking an important step  towards the Group’s goal of carbon neutrality by 2050. Saint-Gobain finalizes the acquisition of Fosroc Saint-Gobain completed the acquisition of Fosroc, a  leading construction chemicals company with a strong  presence in India, the Middle East and Asia-Pacific. This  acquisition strengthens the Group’s presence in  emerging markets and consolidates its worldwide  leadership in light and sustainable construction. March Saint-Gobain successfully carries out a €1.2 billion  double tranche bond issue  Saint-Gobain issued €1.2  billion in two tranches  –  €700 million (3 years, 2.75%) and €500 million (8 years,  3.50%) – taking advantage of favorable market  conditions to anticipate its refinancing needs. The bonds  were oversubscribed almost three times over, reflecting  investors’ confidence in the Group’s credit quality. April Saint-Gobain included in the CDP’s “List A”  For the second year in a row, Saint-Gobain is on the  CDP’s prestigious “Climate List,” which rewards its  leadership in environmental transparency and climate  action. Only 2% of the companies assessed receive this  distinction, highlighting the Group’s strong sustainability  strategy. Sustainable construction: global trends revealed by the third edition of the Saint-Gobain barometer  The third edition of the Sustainable Construction  Barometer reveals a growing global awareness and  urgency concerning sustainable construction practices.  Based on surveys conducted in 27 countries, the study  highlights the main regional differences and the need to  strengthen training and actions to accelerate the sector’s  transformation. May Saint-Gobain to launch its first low-carbon stone  wool insulation plant in the United Kingdom Saint-Gobain will build a new low-carbon stone wool  insulation plant in Melton Mowbray. Equipped with a fully  electric furnace powered by renewable energy, the plant  will produce 50,000 tonnes per year, meeting the United  Kingdom's growing demand for sustainable building  materials. June Saint-Gobain launches its world’s lowest-carbon  insulation plant in Finland  Saint-Gobain has unveiled the production of very low- carbon glass wool at its Forssa site in Finland. Fully  powered by biogas and hydropower, the plant will  reduce CO₂ emissions by 1,500 tonnes per year and  reduce the carbon footprint of its products by up to 40%. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 36 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com General Shareholders’ Meeting and Board of Directors  Meeting on June 5, 2025  Saint-Gobain shareholders reappointed Benoit Bazin as  Chief Executive Officer and approved a 5% dividend  increase, to €2.20 per share. Three new independent  Directors were appointed, reinforcing the diversity and  expertise of the Board of Directors: Maya Hari, a  Singaporean national, a partner at Modern Manifesto,  Antoine de Saint-Affrique, a French national, Chief  Executive Officer of Danone, and Hans Sohlström, a  Finnish national, Chairman and Chief Executive Officer of  Stora Enso. Strengthening of the local model and changes  to the Executive Committee Effective July 1, 2025 Saint-Gobain is strengthening its decentralized  organization to empower local leaders and accelerate  growth at the national level. A renewed Executive  Committee, led by Chief Executive Officer Benoit Bazin  and new Chief Operating Officer Patrick Dupin, will lead  the next phase of profitable growth to be presented at  the Capital Markets Day in October 2025. Saint-Gobain strengthens its digital platform  dedicated to construction chemicals,  with the acquisition of Maturix Saint-Gobain acquired Maturix, a Danish company  offering real-time concrete monitoring solutions. This  transaction expands the Group’s digital offering,  supplements its VERIFI® system, and enables smarter  and more sustainable construction processes. July Saint-Gobain sells Brüggemann Saint-Gobain signed an agreement to sell Brüggemann, a  German specialist in prefabrication solutions, to the  Köster group. This transaction is part of  the  Group’s  strategy to optimize its business portfolio. Saint-Gobain strengthens its construction chemicals  platform with three complementary acquisitions Saint-Gobain strengthened its construction chemicals  business with the acquisitions of Interstar Materials  (North America), Isoltech (Italy), and Soquimic (Peru).  These acquisitions expand its portfolio of sustainable  solutions and support its global growth, in line with its  “Grow &amp; Impact” strategy. August CSR enters into agreement for sale of major real estate  asset in Australia  CSR, an affiliate of Saint-Gobain, reached an agreement  to sell its Badgerys Creek property, near Sydney, for  AUD575  million (€320  million). Including previous  transactions, AUD900  million of CSR real estate assets  have now been monetized since its 2024 acquisition. September Saint-Gobain strengthens its organization  in the field of construction chemicals in Germany  Saint-Gobain is reorganizing its construction chemicals  business in Germany to strengthen its synergies and  market leadership. The Weber dry mortar business will  be integrated into the Franken Maxit joint venture,  creating one of the largest dry mortar manufacturers in  the country. Saint-Gobain unveils the first plasterboard plant  powered by 100% renewable electricity  in North America  Saint-Gobain’s CertainTeed plant in Sainte-Catherine,  near Montreal, has become the first zero-carbon  plasterboard plant in North America. Fully powered by  hydroelectricity, the plant has increased its capacity by  40% while reducing its energy consumption by 30% and  its CO₂ emissions by 44,000 tonnes per year. October Saint-Gobain enters into exclusive negotiations  for the sale of SFIC Belgium  Saint-Gobain is in exclusive talks with Ponsardin  Industries for the sale of SFIC Belgium, its building  materials distribution subsidiary. This sale, planned for  late 2025, is part of the Group’s portfolio optimization  strategy. Saint-Gobain signs an agreement for the sale  of the Tumelero retail network in Brazil  Saint-Gobain signed a definitive agreement with the  Brazilian group GG10, owner of the G-Haus brand, for the  sale of Tumelero, a retail chain specializing in construction  materials, with a strong presence in southern Brazil. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 37 New “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan: Saint-Gobain  leverages its leadership to accelerate  its profitable growth After the success of its previous plan (“Grow &amp; Impact”),  Saint-Gobain, the worldwide leader in light and  sustainable construction, launches its new strategic plan,  “Lead &amp; Grow”. With #1 positions in every region and its  country-based operating model, well-adapted to its local  markets and a deglobalized world, the Group is raising  its profitable growth trajectory, targeting  outperformance thanks to an unrivalled suite of  solutions: a strategy focused on growth and value  creation; an ambitious financial trajectory over the  2026-2030 period; and attractive shareholder returns. Saint-Gobain completes the divestment  of two distribution businesses: SFIC Belgium  and Tumelero in Brazil Saint-Gobain finalized its divestment of SFIC Belgium, a  specialist construction materials distribution business for  plasterboard, insulation and ceilings in the Belgian  market, on November 3, 2025. In addition, Saint-Gobain  finalized its divestment of Tumelero, a retail chain  specializing in construction materials with a strong  presence in southern Brazil, on December 1, 2025. Saint-Gobain cancels 4.2 million shares On December 3, 2025, Saint-Gobain cancelled 4,243,098  treasury shares purchased on the market. Following this  operation, the total number of shares composing the  capital is 495  million and the number of shares  outstanding is now 493 million compared to 497 million  at the end of December, 2024. The Group allocated  €402 million to buy back its own shares (net of employee  share creation) in 2025. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 38 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.2 Strategy and value creation 1.2.1 SEIZING OPPORTUNITIES GENERATED BY MEGATRENDS Saint-Gobain’s corporate purpose, “Making the World a  Better Home”, is directly linked to three megatrends:  global construction demand (in the residential,  non-residential and infrastructure markets) due to  urbanization, population growth, and social and  economic challenges; climate change (due to greenhouse  gas emissions), generating great unpredictability, as well  as more frequent extreme weather events; and the  scarcity of natural resources, reflected in the depletion of  non-renewable resources.  These three global megatrends generate key challenges  that the Group considers to be of importance (i.e., likely  to influence its performance) to its business model, and  which generate immense opportunities for Saint-Gobain:  population growth and urbanization, the aspiration of  the middle classes for quality buildings, energy  renovation, relocating business activities in the context  of deglobalization, adapting buildings and infrastructure  to climate change, and, lastly, the development of  circularity. In response to these major, closely related challenges,  Saint-Gobain promotes a series of relevant responses  throughout its value chain , involving its suppliers and  customers, as well as its competitors, and public and  regulatory authorities. These responses include  regulatory measures (e.g., promoting a stable legal and  financial framework focused on sustainable construction),  procurement measures (e.g., marketing reusable and  recyclable products), design measures (e.g., designing  buildings capable of withstanding more frequent extreme  weather events) and operational measures   (e.g., improving the recovery of materials when buildings  are demolished). The “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan   notably enables Saint-Gobain to contribute to these  responses. Therefore, it aligns directly with global  megatrends, the challenges that arise from them, and  the market opportunities they generate. A – Megatrends a. Global demand for construction The challenge  The urban population is expected to grow by almost  2  billion by 2050, particularly in Asia and Africa. In  addition, around 1.1  billion people currently live in  informal settlements and are still waiting for decent  housing  (1). Based on this data, the World Economic  Forum estimates that new residential construction  needs worldwide will reach 2 billion homes over the next  80 years, a sustainable source of  growth for all of the  Group’s solutions. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 39 (1) United Nations. MEGATRENDS GLOBAL DEMAND FOR  CONSTRUCTION CHALLENGES AND  OPPORTUNITIES (residential, non-residential, infrastructure) due to urbanization, population growth and social and economic challenges Growth and urbanization of global population INCREASING THE VOLUME OF NEW CONSTRUCTION Aspiration for quality buildings and infrastructure: BUILDING SAFE, DECENT, HEALTHY, COMFORTABLE, AND AFFORDABLE  BUILDINGS CLIMATE CHANGE greenhouse gas emissions, unpredictability,  extreme weather events SCARCITY OF  NATURAL RESOURCES depletion of non-renewable resources Energy renovation: ACCELERATE THE VOLUME OF RENOVATION AND REALLOCATION  OF EXISTING BUILDINGS TO INCREASE THE ENERGY PERFORMANCE  OF BUILDINGS AND REDUCE CARBON EMISSIONS Adapting to climate change: PROVIDING SOLUTIONS THAT INCREASE THE RESILIENCE  OF BUILDINGS AND INFRASTRUCTURE Resource scarcity and circularity: BUILDING AND RENOVATING USING FEWER RESOURCES De-globalization and relocation:  BUILDING LOCAL INDUSTRIES AND INFRASTRUCTURE DRAWING  ON SAINT-GOBAIN’S “LOCAL FOR LOCAL” MODEL  Although new construction needs are particularly acute  in emerging countries, developed countries are also  affected. Housing shortages can be seen in several  developed countries, including the United States,  Canada, the United Kingdom and Germany. The  deterioration in housing affordability over the past  decade implies, in particular, the need for quality but  affordable housing , an equation that lightweight  construction makes it possible to solve by reducing the  use of resources and construction time, thereby helping  to reduce the total cost of the building. Finally, urbanization and these global housing needs are  accompanied by requirements for local public services,  and more generally for improving the quality of life in  cities, thanks to quality service buildings  and  infrastructure.  How does this affect  the construction industry?  The sector is directly affected by all these challenges,  from building and renovating existing residential and  non-residential buildings, to building and renovating  infrastructure. The objective is, in particular, to increase  productivity in the building sector in order to meet this  quantitative challenge. b. Climate change  The challenge  Since the pre-industrial era (around 1850-1900), the  world’s average surface temperature has increased by  around 1.34  °C to 1.41  °C, with 2024 being the hottest  year on record, at 1.55  °C above the benchmark. It is  scientifically established that greenhouse gas emissions,  generated by human activity, are primarily responsible  for this increase in average temperatures. The  construction industry is particularly concerned , as it  accounts for nearly 34% of annual CO 2 emissions  worldwide. These emissions are generated primarily  by the operation of the buildings (around 75%), together  with the production and transport of materials  and construction equipment (around 25%). In addition, rising temperatures are accompanied by  extreme weather events such as heat waves, heavy rainfall  and floods, droughts, rires and hurricanes, which are  becoming more frequent and severe. Between 3 and  3.6 billion people live in areas that are highly vulnerable to  extreme weather events, including severe water stress and  drought. In 2024, 393 recorded disasters affected  167.2 million people, exceeding the annual average of 371  disasters recorded between 2004 and 2023. How does this affect  the construction industry?  Reducing the carbon footprint of construction requires  changes in construction standards: the more systematic  use of renovation over demolition-reconstruction, the  widespread use of lightweight construction methods  that reduce the use of cement and concrete, the  decarbonization of these two materials, and finally,  energy renovation that reduces the energy consumption  of buildings. Saint-Gobain offers solutions for all these  techniques and is therefore perfectly positioned on the  underlying trend of construction decarbonization. Furthermore, the need to adapt all residential and non- residential buildings to climate change creates immense  needs that the construction industry must face. The aim  is both to build new buildings that are resilient to climate  change, and to adapt existing buildings to exceptional  weather conditions, in order to ensure safety and  comfort for residents and to minimize losses and repair  and reconstruction costs, in a context where insurance  companies are reducing their coverage of natural  disasters. The Group’s solutions are particularly suited to  these needs, whether it involves strengthening the  insulation of buildings, installing solar-control glazing to  protect against heat waves, or protecting buildings using  waterproofing membranes. c. Scarcity of natural resources The challenge  Resource scarcity refers to the growing gap between  demand for essential natural resources (such as fresh  water, minerals, land, and energy) and the planet’s ability  to regenerate or provide them sustainably. This  imbalance is intensifying as population, consumption,  and environmental pressures converge.  Global water demand is growing fast: it is expected to  exceed supply by 40% and increase by another 20-30%  after 2040, driven by urbanization, agriculture, and  energy needs. At the same time, global commodities  extraction has tripled over the past 50 years and is  expected to almost double by 2050, exposing economies  to the risks of shortages and high price volatility.  How does this affect  the construction industry?  The construction industry accounts for 37% of solid  waste and nearly 50% of natural resource consumption  worldwide, leading to the extraction of 100 billion tonnes  of raw materials per year. Two resources are particularly  consumed by construction: sand, of  which 50  billion  tonnes are consumed per year, and water, 15% of which  is consumed by construction. Circularity ( limiting the  consumption of resources, extending product life, and  encouraging product recycling, taking into account  their entire life cycle) offers a way forward, but scaling  up requires overcoming major obstacles in  terms of  design, regulations and logistics. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 40 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com B – Challenges and opportunities a. Population growth and urbanization:  increasing the volume  of new construction The increase in the global population and the  acceleration of urbanization (see section 1.2.1.A.a  above) means accelerating both the production of  new buildings and the renovation and reallocation  of  existing buildings, in order to provide a sufficient  volume of adequate housing. The construction industry  faces an unprecedented challenge: meeting the  explosive demand for housing caused by population  growth, urbanization, and climate change, while  addressing labor shortages , stagnant productivity ,  and sustainability imperatives . This twofold issue  requires a fundamental transformation of traditional  construction and renovation methods towards  industrialized and  automated approaches. From this  point of view, Saint-Gobain’s lightweight construction  solutions, which  increase productivity, are perfectly  suited to these challenges. b. Aspiration for quality housing:  building safe, dignified, healthy,  comfortable, and affordable buildings This aspiration overlaps with two developments: on the  one hand, the middle classes are expanding in emerging  countries, as illustrated by the 16% average annual  growth of the Indian middle class by 2030; on the other  hand, the acceleration required in construction to meet  quantitative housing needs must not sacrifice quality: it  is a question of building safe, healthy, comfortable and  affordable housing.  The benefits of building comfort on occupant quality of  life have been demonstrated; for example, the thermal  and acoustic comfort of a classroom has beneficial  effects on student learning. Saint-Gobain offers  lightweight construction solutions that improve thermal  and acoustic comfort, as well as the air quality and light  of buildings.  Furthermore, in view of deteriorating housing  affordability over the past decade, in both emerging and  developed countries, the issue of affordability has also  become more important. Saint-Gobain lightweight  construction solutions optimize the cost of building  construction, thanks to productivity gains estimated at  between 20% and 70% during the construction phase, as  well as the recurring cost of building occupancy   (by  reducing energy bills); at the same time, these  solutions improve the economic value of buildings for  their owners: not only are rental yields improved, but real  estate prices increasingly reflect housing energy  efficiency. In many European countries, real estate price  differentials between the most and least energy-efficient  homes exceed 15%, even comparing homes with  equivalent structural characteristics: this is the case in  the Netherlands, for example, as well as in Germany,  France, Italy and Spain.  c. Adapting buildings and infrastructure  to climate change: providing solutions  that increase the resilience  of buildings and infrastructure The built environment faces unprecedented  vulnerabilities related to the climate crisis (see section  1.2.1.A.b above). The acceleration of extreme weather  events, such as heatwaves, fires, floods, and hurricanes,  requires cities, buildings, and infrastructure to adapt to  a new climatic situation. These adaptation efforts must  cover both new construction and existing buildings and  must be adapted to the specific nature of climate  change in each region of the world.  Saint-Gobain’s offering, in particular in the insulation of  partitions and roofs, glazing, shock-resistant shingles and  waterproofing and fire protection membranes, makes it  possible to meet the challenges of this necessary  adaptation. Furthermore, the Group’s organization by  country is particularly adapted to the need to determine  local solutions for adapting buildings.  d. Energy renovation of buildings:  accelerating the volume of renovation  and reallocation of existing buildings  to increase their energy performance  and reduce carbon emissions Achieving carbon neutrality by 2050 requires the  complete decarbonization of the construction industry,  addressing not only emissions during the construction  phase, but also during the use phase of buildings. These  represent around three quarters of the building’s carbon  emissions and can be drastically reduced by energy  renovation. To  date, only 5% of buildings are carbon  neutral, and  annual renovation rates remain low.  Saint-Gobain’s solutions are particularly suited to energy  renovation, whether for glazing, plasterboard solutions,  or insulation for façades and roofs. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 41 e. Relocation of activities  as part of deglobalization After decades of globalization and internationalization of  industrial value chains, companies have faced multiple  industrial and logistical challenges since the Covid  pandemic, including value chain disruptions  during  lockdowns, multiple climate hazards that have affected  global trade routes (e.g., the Panama Canal affected by  drought), and regional conflicts, e.g., in the Red Sea.  Moreover, growing global geopolitical tensions are also  affecting trade and encouraging central governments to  increase their strategic autonomy. All of these  developments are encouraging companies and central  governments to implement policies of reindustrialization  and the local development of strategic industries ,  including defense and the data centers necessary for the  artificial intelligence revolution. The needs for industrial  buildings are therefore increasing considerably in the  Group’s various countries. Saint-Gobain’s offering also targets these non-residential  segments. In the data center sector, for example,  solutions for technical insulation, waterproofing  membranes, technical ceilings, low-carbon admixtures,  and technical screeds meet the precise requirements of  manufacturers. f. Resource scarcity and circularity:  building and renovating,  using fewer resources Resource scarcity (see section 1.2.1.A.c above) involves  building and renovating while optimizing the use of raw  materials and promoting the modularity and reallocation  of buildings. As construction consumes 40% of the  world’s raw materials and generates one -third of the  world’s waste, circularity is both an environmental  imperative and a strategic sovereignty issue .  Saint-Gobain’s offering responds to this challenge. The  reduction in the use of raw materials can reach 50% in  lightweight construction methods: the substitution of  heavy materials and solid façades with wood, metal or  concrete structures naturally reduces the natural  resources used; in addition, site waste is significantly  reduced by the use of pre-fabricated and pre-assembled  modules; and lastly, lightweight construction facilitates  the deconstruction of buildings at the end of their life  and the re-use of construction materials, to the extent  that buildings are transformed into a “materials bank”,  which will ultimately lead to a drastic reduction in the  raw materials consumed by the construction industry. 1.2.2 DEFINING STRATEGIC OBJECTIVES The “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan marks the beginning of  a new chapter for Saint-Gobain, based on a clear  ambition: to sustainably accelerate profitable growth  and outperform its markets , thereby increasing the  value created for all its stakeholders. Over the  2026-2030 period, the Group is targeting “mid -single  digit” average annual sales growth (1), outperforming its  markets by 1 to 2 points, an EBTIDA margin of between  15% and 18%, ROCE above 13%, and a free cash flow  conversion ratio of more than 50%. Anchored in the Group’s long-term vision and corporate  purpose – “Making the World a Better Home” – this plan  is based on a proven model, local for local, and  recognized leadership in light and sustainable  construction, whether in mature or fast-growing markets.  The megatrends that are shaping the construction  industry today are creating an opportunity-rich  environment. A global population that continues to  grow and urbanize, the expansion of the middle class in  high-growth markets, the housing crisis in fully  developed economies, the growing need for local  infrastructure, and the climate emergency are  profoundly transforming demand. These developments  reinforce the need for efficient building and  infrastructure solutions that improve the daily lives of  occupants, while being resource-efficient, low-carbon,  and adapted to extreme weather conditions . In each  region, these trends are reflected in a specific way: in  Europe, energy renovation is becoming an imperative; in  North America, housing shortages and resilience to  climate change are structuring the market; in Asia and  emerging economies, urbanization and infrastructure  are generating increasing construction needs. To meet these challenges, Saint-Gobain stands out with  a unique offering of solutions covering the entire  building envelope and infrastructure, deployed on a  country-by-country basis, which constitutes a decisive  competitive advantage because it precisely meets the  complex needs of the Group’s customers. These solutions  make it possible to address all the construction markets  – residential as well as non-residential and infrastructure –  offering Saint-Gobain a vast addressable market for  sustainable, profitable growth. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 42 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) In local currencies, excluding any major economic slowdown and/or significant divestment. A – Strengthening a sustainable,  profitable growth profile The “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan aims to create  a sustainable, profitable growth profile for the Group. Continuing the geographical  rebalancing of the Group’s activities First, Saint-Gobain intends to fully capture the gradual  recovery in Europe, with the objective of growing by an  average of 3% to 5% per year (1). At the same time,  Saint-Gobain is pursuing a clear strategy of strengthening  its presence in higher-growth regions, including North  America, Asia, and other high-growth markets.  Accounting for around half of the Group’s sales, these  regions represent key drivers of future growth. The  Group aims to achieve, in the long term, nearly 60% of  global sales in these regions. This trajectory is based on  an average growth rate of 5%  to 8% (1), supported by  demographic dynamics, infrastructure demand, and the  upscaling of construction standards.  Broadening the coverage of all construction markets The “Lead &amp; Grow” plan also aims to significantly expand  the Group’s presence in all construction markets, and  more specifically in the non-residential and  infrastructure segments. Historically more focused on  residential, Saint-Gobain now enjoys a solid foundation  for acceleration in these new markets, which are still  under-penetrated, thanks to the expansion of its offering,  its mastery of complex technical solutions, and the  driving force of construction chemicals activities. In this  segment, which is a prime gateway to infrastructure  projects, the objective is to achieve over €9  billion in  sales by 2030, through a combination of capital  expenditure, product innovation and targeted external  growth operations.  Enhancing the Group’s profile in light and  sustainable construction More broadly, Saint -Gobain continuously enhances its  business profile by supplementing, in a logical sequence,  country by country, all its product lines – interior,  exterior, and construction chemicals solutions. Thus, the  Group is strengthening its ability to offer integrated  solutions adapted to the needs of its customers, and  which create value for the Group. The strategic plan is accompanied by active, selective  management of the business portfolio (see section  1.2.7, p. 60). Saint-Gobain plans to increase its scope by  about 20% between now and 2030, by combining  targeted acquisitions and disciplined disposals. Each  transaction is assessed according to strict criteria:  consolidation of leadership positions, increased  exposure to high growth countries, strengthening of  construction chemicals activities, creation of financial  value, with ROCE above 13%. B – Accelerating profitable growth  thanks to the solutions-based  approach  The roll-out of the “Lead &amp; Grow” strategy relies on a  central lever: accelerating the development of solutions.  For several years now, Saint-Gobain has undergone a  profound transformation of its model, moving from a  product portfolio to an integrated logic of solutions  capable of fully meeting customer expectations . This  approach, which was successfully initiated in the  previous plan, is being considerably strengthened under  “Lead &amp; Grow”. Meeting customers’ needs,  creating value for Saint-Gobain The Group’s solutions-based approach is above all a  response to, or even an anticipation of the needs of its  customers, who are facing increasing complexity on  construction sites and have greater needs for  productivity, performance and sustainability. The major  construction players are looking for integrated  approaches, capable of providing guarantees of quality  and performance with a single point of contact to  support them on their project. Saint-Gobain has a unique  breadth and depth of range, including the broadest low- carbon offering on the market, capable of addressing the  growing green building market. The Group thus stands  out on the market by providing a  comprehensive  response to its customers’ issues. The deployment of solutions increases the value created  for customers and, as a result, the Group’s financial  performance, through three main levers: ■ Cross-selling across the entire Saint-Gobain portfolio,  which enables the Group to increase its sales with its  customers and thus outperform its competitors; ■ The sale of innovative, differentiated, high value- added solutions  – such as complete EnveoVent  lightweight façade systems – that increase the  profit margin; ■ The services and specified sales that make it possible  to enhance the sold product mix on large projects  towards more value-added solutions. Profitable growth opportunities,  country by country The expansion of the solutions-based approach is  grounded in a rigorous, repeatable methodology,  deployed locally. This implementation is structured in  two ways: the gradual expansion of the offering in each  country, and the comprehensive coverage of all sales  channels. Country by country, Saint-Gobain is systematically  expanding and enhancing its offering to gain market  share across the entire connected suite of the Group’s  products, covering the entire building envelope and  infrastructure. This model, for example, has been  deployed in India over the last 10 years. Saint-Gobain has  grown from two product lines to a full range of  innovative solutions with high value-added, with leading  positions in each category, and strong growth in sales  and margins (see “Our strategy”, p. 19). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 43 (1) In local currencies, excluding any major economic slowdown and/or significant divestment. At the same time, the Group deploys its solutions across  all sales channels, from professional trading to individual  customers, from direct sales on major projects to digital  sales. This multi-channel coverage strengthens the  distribution of solutions, facilitates the commercial  relationship, and helps to increase market share in each  segment. To ensure the strategy’s effectiveness, Saint-Gobain  closely monitors, country by country, a set of indicators  related to the deployment of solutions: cross-selling  rates, share of value-added products and growth in  specified sales. Finally, the solutions-based approach also applies to  industrial markets . In these segments, the Group’s  activities are already characterized by a high level of  technical expertise, a high proportion of specified sales,  and a significant contribution to the Group’s  performance. The innovation resulting from these  businesses – whether coated glass in mobility, polymers  for construction chemicals, or ceramics for industrial  decarbonization – benefits all of the Group’s activities. C – Accelerated penetration  of non-residential and  infrastructure markets With €15  billion of sales already generated in the  non-residential and infrastructure segments – in a total  addressable market of nearly €250  billion –  Saint-Gobain intends to significantly increase  its penetration in these markets. This ambition is based  on a clear strategy: relying on highly technical “hero”  products, capable of opening access to complex  projects, and drawing on integrated offers adapted to  the requirements of these segments. Differentiating technical products,  real entry points for large  non-residential projects Saint-Gobain is able to differentiate itself in  non-residential projects thanks to its highly technical  solutions, essential to building performance, and which  act as strategic gateways for large-scale projects.  “X-Ray Protection” anti-radiation plasterboards, sold  under the Placo® brand, are one particularly illustrative  example: lighter, recyclable, simpler to install and  bringing real health benefits, compared with traditional  lead-based solutions, these boards have made it  possible to meet the technical and environmental  requirements of the Nantes University Hospital project,  in which Saint -Gobain sold a total of 26 solutions  (cf. section "Markets and activities", p. 9. Saint-Gobain is implementing this same approach in  the data center market, a fast-growing market  characterized by high needs for thermal performance,  fire protection and reliability guarantees. With a  comprehensive range of dedicated partitioning systems  – technical ceilings, floors, waterproofing membranes,  insulation, etc. – Saint-Gobain is now identified as one of  the leaders in this segment, used by  a significant  proportion of operators in Europe and North America (1). Construction chemicals  as a growth driver in infrastructure In just a few years, Saint-Gobain has built a global  leadership position in construction chemicals, with no  less than 43 new lines and plants built since 2021  and 37 acquisitions in this market, including Chryso, GCP,  Fosroc, and Cemix. This leadership plays a decisive role  in the Group’s ability to grow in the infrastructure  market, enabling it to address segments such as tunnels,  bridges, viaducts, energy networks, and hydro  structures, with high-performance solutions,  specification teams, and the combination of expertise  from several strong, globally recognized brands, offering  an integrated approach to the customer. A dedicated approach to capture the full  potential of under-penetrated markets To support its growth ambition in the non-residential  and infrastructure segments, Saint-Gobain has set up  a dedicated organization structured around  comprehensive offerings by market segment (data  centers, hospitals, schools, transport infrastructure,  etc.). It relies on specification and technical support  teams, with strong expertise in building sciences,  capable of supporting architects, design offices,  engineers, and project owners in the design and  execution of projects. This approach is also accompanied by a strengthened  model for managing key accounts, making it possible to  monitor major projects throughout their duration,  coordinate the Group’s brands, and propose coherent  and optimized solutions. The new Long Thanh airport in  Vietnam perfectly illustrates this method: by  mobilizing  15 complementary solutions from different Group  entities, Saint-Gobain has demonstrated its ability to  address the most demanding projects in a coherent,  integrated manner (cf. “Our strategy”, p. 18). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 44 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) Source: Morgan Stanley, July 2025. Lastly, Saint-Gobain’s lead in digital innovation is a key  competitive advantage in non-residential and  infrastructure projects by providing value to the Group’s  customers. In R&amp;D, artificial intelligence  allows  Saint-Gobain to develop new products quicker and thus  accelerate their marketing. Artificial intelligence also  improves the Group’s sales performance, for example by  automating the large -scale analysis of tenders.  Saint-Gobain also deploys a complete suite of digital  tools for its customers, for example in construction  chemicals with its Verifi and Maturix ranges, which  improve real-time monitoring of concrete and increase  product quality and productivity on construction sites. Based on these fundamentals – comprehensive offering,  technical expertise, dedicated organization, advanced  digital capabilities – Saint-Gobain aims to achieve mid to  high-single digit annual sales growth  (1) in the  non-residential and infrastructure markets. The Group  already has a solid presence in many applications,  in-depth knowledge of needs by country, and targeted  development plans to capture identified pockets  of growth. D – An organization structured  around country platforms  to catalyze growth To fully execute its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategy, Saint-Gobain  relies on a particularly robust organizational model   based on country platforms. Built gradually over the past  few years and proven in various economic contexts, this  model is now a major asset for capturing growth  opportunities, enabling rapid execution, and ensuring  consistency of decisions as close to the markets  as possible. These platforms, deeply rooted in their local ecosystem,  combine three essential dimensions to seize growth  opportunities: daily proximity to customers and teams,  access to the Group’s global expertise, and strong  autonomy to manage their resources, adapt their  priorities, and deploy the most relevant solutions at the  local level. This combination makes them real  commercial machines, capable of simultaneously  activating all product lines and sales channels. A bolt-on, proactive acquisition strategy Country platforms play a decisive role in the Group’s  acquisition momentum, which is one of the structuring  levers of the “Lead &amp; Grow” plan. They are often the  ones who initiate discussions with targets. Their  proximity to local players, their in-depth knowledge of  the markets, and their established reputation facilitate  bilateral discussions with family businesses or specialized  players. Over the past five years, 80% of acquisitions  made by Saint-Gobain have been concluded bilaterally,  sometimes after several years of dialog and trust  building. The country platforms are then responsible for  operational integration, talent retention, and generating  synergies. Recent successes – for example in India,  where the margin rate of insulation activities has  increased significantly in three years thanks to rigorous  integration – demonstrate the relevance of this model. Operational excellence supported  by the Group’s expertise Saint-Gobain’s country platforms ensure that they  deliver the best operational performance possible,  drawing on the Group’s expertise, whether in terms of  industrial excellence, innovation with the regional R&amp;D  centers, good sales practices in key account  management, customer loyalty or training programs, or  monitoring the deployment of Saint-Gobain’s solutions- based approach. This approach enables them to increase  their competitiveness, by accelerating and optimizing  execution. Environmental excellence is at the heart of the  performance model of the country platforms, which are  working to reduce the carbon footprint of their  operations, increase their share of recycled materials,  reduce their water consumption, and thus deploy  sustainable solutions for Saint-Gobain’s customers that  give the Group a leading competitive advantage. The progress made by Saint-Gobain in terms of  decarbonization and circularity is recognized by all  stakeholders. They help to increase the confidence of  customers, investors, and public partners, and contribute  to the Group’s sustainable growth. This environmental  excellence is now seen as a differentiating attribute  of Saint-Gobain in construction markets. A culture of performance  and accountability Lastly, the performance of the country platforms is based  on a culture of high standards and  accountability  embodied by their leaders. Nearly 90% of country  platform CEOs are native to the country they manage,  which guarantees a thorough understanding of local  issues and enhances the quality of operational leadership. This performance culture is supported by an incentive  scheme that is closely aligned with value creation.  Managers manage their P&amp;L independently, with specific  targets in terms of growth, margins, ROCE, and CSR. The  Group’s 3,000 key managers also benefit from  a  long- term incentive system directly indexed to  financial  performance, share-price trends and  environmental  objectives. This framework helps to unite the teams around a high  level of commitment and a strong collective ambition,  essential to ensuring the long-term success of the  “Lead &amp; Grow” plan. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 45 (1) In local currencies, excluding any major economic slowdown and/or significant divestment. 1.2.3 STAKEHOLDERS A – Principles of dialog with stakeholders Saint-Gobain considers the interests of all its  stakeholders when determining its long-term strategy.  First of all, this involves building a dialog, and also  ensuring that this  dialog is transparent and based on  mutual trust. Factors such as the size of the Group, its  global dimension, and the variety of its business lines  mean that dialog must, above all, be organized in a  decentralized manner, with each operational entity being  responsible for conducting it within its own scope. Saint-Gobain has mapped out its ecosystem, identifying  and grouping its stakeholders by category. For each  category, a Group position is responsible for organizing  the reporting of information on its expectations, at the  local or global levels, and producing a summary of them.  Thus, dialog is established with stakeholders on a regular  basis at both the Group and local levels. Each  stakeholder’s preferred dialog method has also been  noted. This dialog enables us to consider all the  expectations of Saint-Gobain’s stakeholders. In June 2025, the Group’s organization changed to  further accentuate coordination by country (see sections  1.2.2.D, p.  45 and 1.2.7.B, p.  62). Consequently, each  country is in charge of the dialog with its local  stakeholders and deploys a specific action plan to meet  expectations.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 46 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 47 KEY STAKEHOLDERS DIALOG MODALITY Customers: direct customers,  users and s