int-Gobain was founded in 1665 by Louis XIV, King of  France, under the name of “Royal Manufacture of  Mirrors”.  Compagnie de Saint-Gobain diversified into sectors such  as chemicals, glass products, and the automotive  industry, rapidly expanding into international markets,  establishing itself in the United States in 1829, Germany  in 1858, Italy in 1888 and Spain in 1905. The Group, which successfully overcame the major crises  of the century and invested heavily in research and  development, withdrew from its chemical activities and  merged with Pont-à-Mousson, created in 1856 and  specializing in cast iron pipes. In 1986, the privatization of Saint-Gobain – which had  been nationalized in 1982 – was a huge popular success:  1,500,000 shareholders subscribed to the transaction.  The acquisition of Norton in 1990, then Carborundum in  1996, enabled the Group to develop its know-how in  abrasives, plastics and ceramics.  The 2005 acquisition of British Plaster Board, the world  leader in plasterboard, combined with Saint-Gobain’s  insulation solutions, made the Group the world’s No. 1 in  interior design solutions. Saint-Gobain focused its strategy on sustainable  housing, while continuing to serve numerous industrial  markets, and pursued its expansion in emerging  countries. In late 2018, the Group launched its “Transform &amp; Grow”  plan aimed at establishing a new organization by country  and ensuring agile and value-creating portfolio  management.  In 2019, Saint-Gobain acquired Continental Building  Products, the first step in strengthening its positions  in the construction market in North America. Saint-Gobain established its corporate purpose,  “Making the world a better home”. The 2021 acquisition of Chryso made Saint-Gobain a  major player in construction chemicals. In October 2021,  Saint-Gobain launched its “Grow &amp; Impact” strategic  plan, setting out its vision to become the  worldwide  leader in light and sustainable construction.  In 2022, the Group finalized the acquisition of Kaycan – a  specialist in exterior building materials – and of GCP  Applied Technologies, a key step in establishing itself as  a leader in construction chemicals.  The following year, Saint-Gobain acquired Building  Products of Canada, a manufacturer of roofing shingles  for the residential sector, and wood fiber insulation  panels.  Saint-Gobain was certified as “Top Employer Global” for  its 2025 fiscal year, for the 11 th consecutive year. The  Group acquired CSR, a leader in construction materials in  Australia and New Zealand, as well as Bailey, a specialist  in metal frames for light construction in Canada, and  initiated the acquisitions of Fosroc, a leading global  player in construction chemicals, and Ovniver (Cemix  brand) in Mexico. These constitute new strategic steps in  establishing Saint-Gobain’s global presence in  construction chemicals, which, once the transactions are  complete, will bring its total sales in this sector to  €6.5 billion (pro forma).  In 2025, Saint-Gobain celebrates its 360th anniversary, a  figure that also represents its global reach and its circular  and comprehensive approach. In October 2025, the  Group launches its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan,  marking the beginning of a new chapter for  Saint-Gobain, based on a clear ambition: to accelerate its  profitable growth in a sustainable manner and  outperform its markets, thereby enhancing the value  created for all its stakeholders. At the end of 2025, the  Group is present in 80 countries (1). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 35 (1) Countries with active industrial presence as of Dec. 31, 2025.  1.1.6 LOOKING BACK ON THE YEAR 2025 January  Saint-Gobain certified as “Top Employer Global”  for the 10th consecutive year Saint-Gobain was certified “Top Employer Global” for the  tenth consecutive year, joining a small group of 17 global  companies recognized in 2025. This award hi