nt  to sell its Badgerys Creek property, near Sydney, for  AUD575  million (€320  million). Including previous  transactions, AUD900  million of CSR real estate assets  have now been monetized since its 2024 acquisition. September Saint-Gobain strengthens its organization  in the field of construction chemicals in Germany  Saint-Gobain is reorganizing its construction chemicals  business in Germany to strengthen its synergies and  market leadership. The Weber dry mortar business will  be integrated into the Franken Maxit joint venture,  creating one of the largest dry mortar manufacturers in  the country. Saint-Gobain unveils the first plasterboard plant  powered by 100% renewable electricity  in North America  Saint-Gobain’s CertainTeed plant in Sainte-Catherine,  near Montreal, has become the first zero-carbon  plasterboard plant in North America. Fully powered by  hydroelectricity, the plant has increased its capacity by  40% while reducing its energy consumption by 30% and  its CO₂ emissions by 44,000 tonnes per year. October Saint-Gobain enters into exclusive negotiations  for the sale of SFIC Belgium  Saint-Gobain is in exclusive talks with Ponsardin  Industries for the sale of SFIC Belgium, its building  materials distribution subsidiary. This sale, planned for  late 2025, is part of the Group’s portfolio optimization  strategy. Saint-Gobain signs an agreement for the sale  of the Tumelero retail network in Brazil  Saint-Gobain signed a definitive agreement with the  Brazilian group GG10, owner of the G-Haus brand, for the  sale of Tumelero, a retail chain specializing in construction  materials, with a strong presence in southern Brazil. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 37 New “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan: Saint-Gobain  leverages its leadership to accelerate  its profitable growth After the success of its previous plan (“Grow &amp; Impact”),  Saint-Gobain, the worldwide leader in light and  sustainable construction, launches its new strategic plan,  “Lead &amp; Grow”. With #1 positions in every region and its  country-based operating model, well-adapted to its local  markets and a deglobalized world, the Group is raising  its profitable growth trajectory, targeting  outperformance thanks to an unrivalled suite of  solutions: a strategy focused on growth and value  creation; an ambitious financial trajectory over the  2026-2030 period; and attractive shareholder returns. Saint-Gobain completes the divestment  of two distribution businesses: SFIC Belgium  and Tumelero in Brazil Saint-Gobain finalized its divestment of SFIC Belgium, a  specialist construction materials distribution business for  plasterboard, insulation and ceilings in the Belgian  market, on November 3, 2025. In addition, Saint-Gobain  finalized its divestment of Tumelero, a retail chain  specializing in construction materials with a strong  presence in southern Brazil, on December 1, 2025. Saint-Gobain cancels 4.2 million shares On December 3, 2025, Saint-Gobain cancelled 4,243,098  treasury shares purchased on the market. Following this  operation, the total number of shares composing the  capital is 495  million and the number of shares  outstanding is now 493 million compared to 497 million  at the end of December, 2024. The Group allocated  €402 million to buy back its own shares (net of employee  share creation) in 2025. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 38 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.2 Strategy and value creation 1.2.1 SEIZING OPPORTUNITIES GENERATED BY MEGATRENDS Saint-Gobain’s corporate purpose, “Making the World a  Better Home”, is directly linked to three megatrends:  global construction demand (in the residential,  non-residential and infrastructure markets) due to  urbanization, population growth, and social and  economic challenges; climate change (due to greenhouse  gas emissions), generating great unpredictability, as well  as more frequent extreme weather events; and the  scarcity of natural resources, reflected in the depletion of  non-renewable resources.  These three global megatrends generate key challenges  that the Group considers to be of importance (i.e., likely  to influence its performance) to its business model, and  which generate immense opportunities for Saint-Gobain:  population growth and urbanization, the aspiration of  the middle classes for quality buildings, energy  renovation, relocating business activities in the context  of deglobalization, adapting buildings and infrastructure  to climate change, and, lastly, the development of  circularity. In response to these major, closely related challenges,  Saint-Gobain promotes a series of relevant responses  throughout its value chain , involving its suppliers and  customers, as well as its competitors, and public and  regulatory authorities. These responses include  regulatory measures (e.g., promoting a stable legal and  financial framework focused on sustainable construction),  procurement measures (e.g., marketing reusable and  recyclable products), design measures (e.g., designing  buildings capable of withstanding more frequent extreme  weather events) and operational measures   (e.g., improving the recovery of materials when buildings  are demolished). The “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan   notably enables Saint-Gobain to contribute to these  responses. Therefore, it aligns directly with global  megatrends, the challenges that arise from them, and  the market opportunities they generate. A – Megatrends a. Global demand for construction The challenge  The urban population is expected to grow by almost  2  billion by 2050, particularly in Asia and Africa. In  addition, around 1.1  billion people currently live in  informal settlements and are still waiting for decent  housing  (1). Based on this data, the World Economic  Forum estimates that new residential construction  needs worldwide will reach 2 billion homes over the next  80 years, a sustainable source of  growth for all of the  Group’s solutions. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 39 (1) United Nations. MEGATRENDS GLOBAL DEMAND FOR  CONSTRUCTION CHALLENGES AND  OPPORTUNITIES (residential, non-residential, infrastructure) due to urbanization, population growth and social and economic challenges Growth and urbanization of global population INCREASING THE VOLUME OF NEW CONSTRUCTION Aspiration for quality buildings and infrastructure: BUILDING SAFE, DECENT, HEALTHY, COMFORTABLE, AND AFFORDABLE  BUILDINGS CLIMATE CHANGE greenhouse gas emissions, unpredictability,  extreme weather events SCARCITY OF  NATURAL RESOURCES depletion of non-renewable resources Energy renovation: ACCELERATE THE VOLUME OF RENOVATION AND REALLOCATION  OF EXISTING BUILDINGS TO INCREASE THE ENERGY PERFORMANCE  OF BUILDINGS AND REDUCE CARBON EMISSIONS Adapting to climate change: PROVIDING SOLUTIONS THAT INCREASE THE RESILIENCE  OF BUILDINGS AND INFRASTRUCTURE Resource scarcity and circularity: BUILDING AND RENOVATING USING FEWER RESOURCES De-globalization and relocation:  BUILDING LOCAL INDUSTRIES AND INFRASTRUCTURE DRAWING  ON SAINT-GOBAIN’S “LOCAL FOR LOCAL” MODEL  Although new construction needs are particularly acute  in emerging countries, developed countries are also  affected. Housing shortages can be seen in several  developed countries, including the United States,  Canada, the United Kingdom and Germany. The  deterioration in housing affordability over the past  decade implies, in particular, the need for quality but  affordable housing , an equation that lightweight  construction makes it possible to solve by reducing the  use of resources and construction time, thereby helping  to reduce the total cost of the building. Finally, urbanization and these global housing needs are  accompanied by requirements for local public services,  and more generally for improving the quality of life in  cities, thanks to quality service buildings  and  infrastructure.  How does this affect  the construction industry?  The sector is directly affected by all these challenges,  from building and renovating existing residential and  non-residential buildings, to building and renovating  infrastructure. The objective is, in particular, to increase  productivity in the building sector in order to meet this  quantitative challenge. b. Climate change  The challenge  Since the pre-industrial era (around 1850-1900), the  world’s average surface temperature has increased by  around 1.34  °C to 1.41  °C, with 2024 being the hottest  year on record, at 1.55  °C above the benchmark. It is  scientifically established that greenhouse gas emissions,  generated by human activity, are primarily responsible  for this increase in average temperatures. The  construction industry is particularly concerned , as it  accounts for nearly 34% of annual CO 2 emissions  worldwide. These emissions are generated primarily  by the operation of the buildings (around 75%), together  with the production and transport of materials  and construction equipment (around 25%). In addition, rising temperatures are accompanied by  extreme weather events such as heat waves, heavy rainfall  and floods, droughts, rires and hurricanes, which are  becoming more frequent and severe. Between 3 and  3.6 billion people live in areas that are highly vulnerable to  extreme weather events, including severe water stress and  drought. In 2024, 393 recorded disasters affected  167.2 million people, exceeding the annual average of 371  disasters recorded between 2004 and 2023. How does this affect  the construction industry?  Reducing the carbon footprint of construction requires  changes in construction standards: the more systematic  use of renovation over demolition-reconstruction, the  widespread use of lightweight construction methods  that reduce the use of cement and concrete, the  decarbonization of these two materials, and finally,  energy renovation that reduces the energy consumption  of buildings. Saint-Gobain offers solutions for all these  techniques and is therefore perfectly positioned on the  underlying trend of construction decarbonization. Furthermore, the need to adapt all residential and non- residential buildings to climate change creates immense  needs that the construction industry must face. The aim  is both to build new buildings that are resilient to climate  change, and to adapt existing buildings to exceptional  weather conditions, in order to ensure safety and  comfort for residents and to minimize losses and repair  and reconstruction costs, in a context where insurance  companies are reducing their coverage of natural  disasters. The Group’s solutions are particularly suited to  these needs, whether it involves strengthening the  insulation of buildings, installing solar-control glazing to  protect against heat waves, or protecting buildings using  waterproofing membranes. c. Scarcity of natural resources The challenge  Resource scarcity refers to the growing gap between  demand for essential natural resources (such as fresh  water, minerals, land, and energy) and the planet’s ability  to regenerate or provide them sustainably. This  imbalance is intensifying as population, consumption,  and environmental pressures converge.  Global water demand is growing fast: it is expected to  exceed supply by 40% and increase by another 20-30%  after 2040, driven by urbanization, agriculture, and  energy needs. At the same time, global commodities  extraction has tripled over the past 50 years and is  expected to almost double by 2050, exposing economies  to the risks of shortages and high price volatility.  How does this affect  the construction industry?  The construction industry accounts for 37% of solid  waste and nearly 50% of natural resource consumption  worldwide, leading to the extraction of 100 billion tonnes  of raw materials per year. Two resources are particularly  consumed by construction: sand, of  which 50  billion  tonnes are consumed per year, and water, 15% of which  is consumed by construction. Circularity ( limiting the  consumption of resources, extending product life, and  encouraging product recycling, taking into account  their entire life cycle) offers a way forward, but scaling  up requires overcoming major obstacles in  terms of  design, regulations and logistics. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 40 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com B – Challenges and opportunities a. Population growth and urbanization:  increasing the volume  of new construction The increase in the global population and the  acceleration of urbanization (see section 1.2.1.A.a  above) means accelerating both the production of  new buildings and the renovation and reallocation  of  existing buildings, in order to provide a sufficient  volume of adequate housing. The construction industry  faces an unprecedented challenge: meeting the  explosive demand for housing caused by population  growth, urbanization, and climate change, while  addressing labor shortages , stagnant productivity ,  and sustainability imperatives . This twofold issue  requires a fundamental transformation of traditional  construction and renovation methods towards  industrialized and  automated approaches. From this  point of view, Saint-Gobain’s lightweight construction  solutions, which  increase productivity, are perfectly  suited to these challenges. b. Aspiration for quality housing:  building safe, dignified, healthy,  comfortable, and affordable buildings This aspiration overlaps with two developments: on the  one hand, the middle classes are expanding in emerging  countries, as illustrated by the 16% average annual  growth of the Indian middle class by 2030; on the other  hand, the acceleration required in construction to meet  quantitative housing needs must not sacrifice quality: it  is a question of building safe, healthy, comfortable and  affordable housing.  The benefits of building comfort on occupant quality of  life have been demonstrated; for example, the thermal  and acoustic comfort of a classroom has beneficial  effects on student learning. Saint-Gobain offers  lightweight construction solutions that improve thermal  and acoustic comfort, as well as the air quality and light  of buildings.  Furthermore, in view of deteriorating housing  affordability over the past decade, in both emerging and  developed countries, the issue of affordability has also  become more important. Saint-Gobain lightweight  construction solutions optimize the cost of building  construction, thanks to productivity gains estimated at  between 20% and 70% during the construction phase, as  well as the recurring cost of building occupancy   (by  reducing energy bills); at the same time, these  solutions improve the economic value of buildings for  their owners: not only are rental yields improved, but real  estate prices increasingly reflect housing energy  efficiency. In many European countries, real estate price  differentials between the most and least energy-efficient  homes exceed 15%, even comparing homes with  equivalent structural characteristics: this is the case in  the Netherlands, for example, as well as in Germany,  France, Italy and Spain.  c. Adapting buildings and infrastructure  to climate change: providing solutions  that increase the resilience  of buildings and infrastructure The built environment faces unprecedented  vulnerabilities related to the climate crisis (see section  1.2.1.A.b above). The acceleration of extreme weather  events, such as heatwaves, fires, floods, and hurricanes,  requires cities, buildings, and infrastructure to adapt to  a new climatic situation. These adaptation efforts must  cover both new construction and existing buildings and  must be adapted to the specific nature of climate  change in each region of the world.  Saint-Gobain’s offering, in particular in the insulation of  partitions and roofs, glazing, shock-resistant shingles and  waterproofing and fire protection membranes, makes it  possible to meet the challenges of this necessary  adaptation. Furthermore, the Group’s organization by  country is particularly adapted to the need to determine  local solutions for adapting buildings.  d. Energy renovation of buildings:  accelerating the volume of renovation  and reallocation of existing buildings  to increase their energy performance  and reduce carbon emissions Achieving carbon neutrality by 2050 requires the  complete decarbonization of the construction industry,  addressing not only emissions during the construction  phase, but also during the use phase of buildings. These  represent around three quarters of the building’s carbon  emissions and can be drastically reduced by energy  renovation. To  date, only 5% of buildings are carbon  neutral, and  annual renovation rates remain low.  Saint-Gobain’s solutions are particularly suited to energy  renovation, whether for glazing, plasterboard solutions,  or insulation for façades and roofs. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 41 e. Relocation of activities  as part of deglobalization After decades of globalization and internationalization of  industrial value chains, companies have faced multiple  industrial and logistical challenges since the Covid  pandemic, including value chain disruptions  during  lockdowns, multiple climate hazards that have affected  global trade routes (e.g., the Panama Canal affected by  drought), and regional conflicts, e.g., in the Red Sea.  Moreover, growing global geopolitical tensions are also  affecting trade and encouraging central governments to  increase their strategic autonomy. All of these  developments are encouraging companies and central  governments to implement policies of reindustrialization  and the local development of strategic industries ,  including defense and the data centers necessary for the  artificial intelligence revolution. The needs for industrial  buildings are therefore increasing considerably in the  Group’s various countries. Saint-Gobain’s offering also targets these non-residential  segments. In the data center sector, for example,  solutions for technical insulation, waterproofing  membranes, technical ceilings, low-carbon admixtures,  and technical screeds meet the precise requirements of  manufacturers. f. Resource scarcity and circularity:  building and renovating,  using fewer resources Resource scarcity (see section 1.2.1.A.c above) involves  building and renovating while optimizing the use of raw  materials and promoting the modularity and reallocation  of buildings. As construction consumes 40% of the  world’s raw materials and generates one -third of the  world’s waste, circularity is both an environmental  imperative and a strategic sovereignty issue .  Saint-Gobain’s offering responds to this challenge. The  reduction in the use of raw materials can reach 50% in  lightweight construction methods: the substitution of  heavy materials and solid façades with wood, metal or  concrete structures naturally reduces the natural  resources used; in addition, site waste is significantly  reduced by the use of pre-fabricated and pre-assembled  modules; and lastly, lightweight construction facilitates  the deconstruction of buildings at the end of their life  and the re-use of construction materials, to the extent  that buildings are transformed into a “materials bank”,  which will ultimately lead to a drastic reduction in the  raw materials consumed by the construction industry. 1.2.2 DEFINING STRATEGIC OBJECTIVES The “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan marks the beginning of  a new chapter for Saint-Gobain, based on a clear  ambition: to sustainably accelerate profitable growth  and outperform its markets , thereby increasing the  value created for all its stakeholders. Over the  2026-2030 period, the Group is targeting “mid -single  digit” average annual sales growth (1), outperforming its  markets by 1 to 2 points, an EBTIDA margin of between  15% and 18%, ROCE above 13%, and a free cash flow  conversion ratio of more than 50%. Anchored in the Group’s long-term vision and corporate  purpose – “Making the World a Better Home” – this plan  is based on a proven model, local for local, and  recognized leadership in light and sustainable  construction, whether in mature or fast-growing markets.  The megatrends that are shaping the construction  industry today are creating an opportunity-rich  environment. A global population that continues to  grow and urbanize, the expansion of the middle class in  high-growth markets, the housing crisis in fully  developed economies, the growing need for local  infrastructure, and the climate emergency are  profoundly transforming demand. These developments  reinforce the need for efficient building and  infrastructure solutions that improve the daily lives of  occupants, while being resource-efficient, low-carbon,  and adapted to extreme weather conditions . In each  region, these trends are reflected in a specific way: in  Europe, energy renovation is becoming an imperative; in  North America, housing shortages and resilience to  climate change are structuring the market; in Asia and  emerging economies, urbanization and infrastructure  are generating increasing construction needs. To meet these challenges, Saint-Gobain stands out with  a unique offering of solutions covering the entire  building envelope and infrastructure, deployed on a  country-by-country basis, which constitutes a decisive  competitive advantage because it precisely meets the  complex needs of the Group’s customers. These solutions  make it possible to address all the construction markets  – residential as well as non-residential and infrastructure –  offering Saint-Gobain a vast addressable market for  sustainable, profitable growth. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 42 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) In local currencies, excluding any major economic slowdown and/or significant divestment. A – Strengthening a sustainable,  profitable growth profile The “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan aims to create  a sustainable, profitable growth profile for the Group. Continuing the geographical  rebalancing of the Group’s activities First, Saint-Gobain intends to fully capture the gradual  recovery in Europe, with the objective of growing by an  average of 3% to 5% per year (1). At the same time,  Saint-Gobain is pursuing a clear strategy of strengthening  its presence in higher-growth regions, including North  America, Asia, and other high-growth markets.  Accounting for around half of the Group’s sales, these  regions represent key drivers of future growth. The  Group aims to achieve, in the long term, nearly 60% of  global sales in these regions. This trajectory is based on  an average growth rate of 5%  to 8% (1), supported by  demographic dynamics, infrastructure demand, and the  upscaling of construction standards.  Broadening the coverage of all construction markets The “Lead &amp; Grow” plan also aims to significantly expand  the Group’s presence in all construction markets, and  more specifically in the non-residential and  infrastructure segments. Historically more focused on  residential, Saint-Gobain now enjoys a solid foundation  for acceleration in these new markets, which are still  under-penetrated, thanks to the expansion of its offering,  its mastery of complex technical solutions, and the  driving force of construction chemicals activities. In this  segment, which is a prime gateway to infrastructure  projects, the objective is to achieve over €9  billion in  sales by 2030, through a combination of capital  expenditure, product innovation and targeted external  growth operations.  Enhancing the Group’s profile in light and  sustainable construction More broadly, Saint -Gobain continuously enhances its  business profile by supplementing, in a logical sequence,  country by country, all its product lines – interior,  exterior, and construction chemicals solutions. Thus, the  Group is strengthening its ability to offer integrated  solutions adapted to the needs of its customers, and  which create value for the Group. The strategic plan is accompanied by active, selective  management of the business portfolio (see section  1.2.7, p. 60). Saint-Gobain plans to increase its scope by  about 20% between now and 2030, by combining  targeted acquisitions and disciplined disposals. Each  transaction is assessed according to strict criteria:  consolidation of leadership positions, increased  exposure to high growth countries, strengthening of  construction chemicals activities, creation of financial  value, with ROCE above 13%. B – Accelerating profitable growth  thanks to the solutions-based  approach  The roll-out of the “Lead &amp; Grow” strategy relies on a  central lever: accelerating the development of solutions.  For several years now, Saint-Gobain has undergone a  profound transformation of its model, moving from a  product portfolio to an integrated logic of solutions  capable of fully meeting customer expectations . This  approach, which was successfully initiated in the  previous plan, is being considerably strengthened under  “Lead &amp; Grow”. Meeting customers’ needs,  creating value for Saint-Gobain The Group’s solutions-based approach is above all a  response to, or even an anticipation of the needs of its  customers, who are facing increasing complexity on  construction sites and have greater needs for  productivity, performance and sustainability. The major  construction players are looking for integrated  approaches, capable of providing guarantees of quality  and performance with a single point of contact to  support them on their project. Saint-Gobain has a unique  breadth and depth of range, including the broadest low- carbon offering on the market, capable of addressing the  growing green building market. The Group thus stands  out on the market by providing a  comprehensive  response to its customers’ issues. The deployment of solutions increases the value created  for customers and, as a result, the Group’s financial  performance, through three main levers: ■ Cross-selling across the entire Saint-Gobain portfolio,  which enables the Group to increase its sales with its  customers and thus outperform its competitors; ■ The sale of innovative, differentiated, high value- added solutions  – such as complete EnveoVent  lightweight façade systems – that increase the  profit margin; ■ The services and specified sales that make it possible  to enhance the sold product mix on large projects  towards more value-added solutions. Profitable growth opportunities,  country by country The expansion of the solutions-based approach is  grounded in a rigorous, repeatable methodology,  deployed locally. This implementation is structured in  two ways: the gradual expansion of the offering in each  country, and the comprehensive coverage of all sales  channels. Country by country, Saint-Gobain is systematically  expanding and enhancing its offering to gain market  share across the entire connected suite of the Group’s  products, covering the entire building envelope and  infrastructure. This model, for example, has been  deployed in India over the last 10 years. Saint-Gobain has  grown from two product lines to a full range of  innovative solutions with high value-added, with leading  positions in each category, and strong growth in sales  and margins (see “Our strategy”, p. 19). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 43 (1) In local currencies, excluding any major economic slowdown and/or significant divestment. At the same time, the Group deploys its solutions across  all sales channels, from professional trading to individual  customers, from direct sales on major projects to digital  sales. This multi-channel coverage strengthens the  distribution of solutions, facilitates the commercial  relationship, and helps to increase market share in each  segment. To ensure the strategy’s effectiveness, Saint-Gobain  closely monitors, country by country, a set of indicators  related to the deployment of solutions: cross-selling  rates, share of value-added products and growth in  specified sales. Finally, the solutions-based approach also applies to  industrial markets . In these segments, the Group’s  activities are already characterized by a high level of  technical expertise, a high proportion of specified sales,  and a significant contribution to the Group’s  performance. The innovation resulting from these  businesses – whether coated glass in mobility, polymers  for construction chemicals, or ceramics for industrial  decarbonization – benefits all of the Group’s activities. C – Accelerated penetration  of non-residential and  infrastructure markets With €15  billion of sales already generated in the  non-residential and infrastructure segments – in a total  addressable market of nearly €250  billion –  Saint-Gobain intends to significantly increase  its penetration in these markets. This ambition is based  on a clear strategy: relying on highly technical “hero”  products, capable of opening access to complex  projects, and drawing on integrated offers adapted to  the requirements of these segments. Differentiating technical products,  real entry points for large  non-residential projects Saint-Gobain is able to differentiate itself in  non-residential projects thanks to its highly technical  solutions, essential to building performance, and which  act as strategic gateways for large-scale projects.  “X-Ray Protection” anti-radiation plasterboards, sold  under the Placo® brand, are one particularly illustrative  example: lighter, recyclable, simpler to install and  bringing real health benefits, compared with traditional  lead-based solutions, these boards have made it  possible to meet the technical and environmental  requirements of the Nantes University Hospital project,  in which Saint -Gobain sold a total of 26 solutions  (cf. section "Markets and activities", p. 9. Saint-Gobain is implementing this same approach in  the data center market, a fast-growing market  characterized by high needs for thermal performance,  fire protection and reliability guarantees. With a  comprehensive range of dedicated partitioning systems  – technical ceilings, floors, waterproofing membranes,  insulation, etc. – Saint-Gobain is now identified as one of  the leaders in this segment, used by  a significant  proportion of operators in Europe and North America (1). Construction chemicals  as a growth driver in infrastructure In just a few years, Saint-Gobain has built a global  leadership position in construction chemicals, with no  less than 43 new lines and plants built since 2021  and 37 acquisitions in this market, including Chryso, GCP,  Fosroc, and Cemix. This leadership plays a decisive role  in the Group’s ability to grow in the infrastructure  market, enabling it to address segments such as tunnels,  bridges, viaducts, energy networks, and hydro  structures, with high-performance solutions,  specification teams, and the combination of expertise  from several strong, globally recognized brands, offering  an integrated approach to the customer. A dedicated approach to capture the full  potential of under-penetrated markets To support its growth ambition in the non-residential  and infrastructure segments, Saint-Gobain has set up  a dedicated organization structured around  comprehensive offerings by market segment (data  centers, hospitals, schools, transport infrastructure,  etc.). It relies on specification and technical support  teams, with strong expertise in building sciences,  capable of supporting architects, design offices,  engineers, and project owners in the design and  execution of projects. This approach is also accompanied by a strengthened  model for managing key accounts, making it possible to  monitor major projects throughout their duration,  coordinate the Group’s brands, and propose coherent  and optimized solutions. The new Long Thanh airport in  Vietnam perfectly illustrates this method: by  mobilizing  15 complementary solutions from different Group  entities, Saint-Gobain has demonstrated its ability to  address the most demanding projects in a coherent,  integrated manner (cf. “Our strategy”, p. 18). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 44 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) Source: Morgan Stanley, July 2025. Lastly, Saint-Gobain’s lead in digital innovation is a key  competitive advantage in non-residential and  infrastructure projects by providing value to the Group’s  customers. In R&amp;D, artificial intelligence  allows  Saint-Gobain to develop new products quicker and thus  accelerate their marketing. Artificial intelligence also  improves the Group’s sales performance, for example by  automating the large -scale analysis of tenders.  Saint-Gobain also deploys a complete suite of digital  tools for its customers, for example in construction  chemicals with its Verifi and Maturix ranges, which  improve real-time monitoring of concrete and increase  product quality and productivity on construction sites. Based on these fundamentals – comprehensive offering,  technical expertise, dedicated organization, advanced  digital capabilities – Saint-Gobain aims to achieve mid to  high-single digit annual sales growth  (1) in the  non-residential and infrastructure markets. The Group  already has a solid presence in many applications,  in-depth knowledge of needs by country, and targeted  development plans to capture identified pockets  of growth. D – An organization structured  around country platforms  to catalyze growth To fully execute its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategy, Saint-Gobain  relies on a particularly robust organizational model   based on country platforms. Built gradually over the past  few years and proven in various economic contexts, this  model is now a major asset for capturing growth  opportunities, enabling rapid execution, and ensuring  consistency of decisions as close to the markets  as possible. These platforms, deeply rooted in their local ecosystem,  combine three essential dimensions to seize growth  opportunities: daily proximity to customers and teams,  access to the Group’s global expertise, and strong  autonomy to manage their resources, adapt their  priorities, and deploy the most relevant solutions at the  local level. This combination makes them real  commercial machines, capable of simultaneously  activating all product lines and sales channels. A bolt-on, proactive acquisition strategy Country platforms play a decisive role in the Group’s  acquisition momentum, which is one of the structuring  levers of the “Lead &amp; Grow” plan. They are often the  ones who initiate discussions with targets. Their  proximity to local players, their in-depth knowledge of  the markets, and their established reputation facilitate  bilateral discussions with family businesses or specialized  players. Over the past five years, 80% of acquisitions  made by Saint-Gobain have been concluded bilaterally,  sometimes after several years of dialog and trust  building. The country platforms are then responsible for  operational integration, talent retention, and generating  synergies. Recent successes – for example in India,  where the margin rate of insulation activities has  increased significantly in three years thanks to rigorous  integration – demonstrate the relevance of this model. Operational excellence supported  by the Group’s expertise Saint-Gobain’s country platforms ensure that they  deliver the best operational performance possible,  drawing on the Group’s expertise, whether in terms of  industrial excellence, innovation with the regional R&amp;D  centers, good sales practices in key account  management, customer loyalty or training programs, or  monitoring the deployment of Saint-Gobain’s solutions- based approach. This approach enables them to increase  their competitiveness, by accelerating and optimizing  execution. Environmental excellence is at the heart of the  performance model of the country platforms, which are  working to reduce the carbon footprint of their  operations, increase their share of recycled materials,  reduce their water consumption, and thus deploy  sustainable solutions for Saint-Gobain’s customers that  give the Group a leading competitive advantage. The progress made by Saint-Gobain in terms of  decarbonization and circularity is recognized by all  stakeholders. They help to increase the confidence of  customers, investors, and public partners, and contribute  to the Group’s sustainable growth. This environmental  excellence is now seen as a differentiating attribute  of Saint-Gobain in construction markets. A culture of performance  and accountability Lastly, the performance of the country platforms is based  on a culture of high standards and  accountability  embodied by their leaders. Nearly 90% of country  platform CEOs are native to the country they manage,  which guarantees a thorough understanding of local  issues and enhances the quality of operational leadership. This performance culture is supported by an incentive  scheme that is closely aligned with value creation.  Managers manage their P&amp;L independently, with specific  targets in terms of growth, margins, ROCE, and CSR. The  Group’s 3,000 key managers also benefit from  a  long- term incentive system directly indexed to  financial  performance, share-price trends and  environmental  objectives. This framework helps to unite the teams around a high  level of commitment and a strong collective ambition,  essential to ensuring the long-term success of the  “Lead &amp; Grow” plan. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 45 (1) In local currencies, excluding any major economic slowdown and/or significant divestment. 1.2.3 STAKEHOLDERS A – Principles of dialog with stakeholders Saint-Gobain considers the interests of all its  stakeholders when determining its long-term strategy.  First of all, this involves building a dialog, and also  ensuring that this  dialog is transparent and based on  mutual trust. Factors such as the size of the Group, its  global dimension, and the variety of its business lines  mean that dialog must, above all, be organized in a  decentralized manner, with each operational entity being  responsible for conducting it within its own scope. Saint-Gobain has mapped out its ecosystem, identifying  and grouping its stakeholders by category. For each  category, a Group position is responsible for organizing  the reporting of information on its expectations, at the  local or global levels, and producing a summary of them.  Thus, dialog is established with stakeholders on a regular  basis at both the Group and local levels. Each  stakeholder’s preferred dialog method has also been  noted. This dialog enables us to consider all the  expectations of Saint-Gobain’s stakeholders. In June 2025, the Group’s organization changed to  further accentuate coordination by country (see sections  1.2.2.D, p.  45 and 1.2.7.B, p.  62). Consequently, each  country is in charge of the dialog with its local  stakeholders and deploys a specific action plan to meet  expectations.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 46 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 47 KEY STAKEHOLDERS DIALOG MODALITY Customers: direct customers,  users and s