ated before prevention, management and mitigation  measures or with a likelihood of occurrence assessed as low) Initiatives Location in the value chain:                    Level of priority:             S1 Own workforce Health and safety Employees’ physical and  psychological health / negative  impact Risk of deterioration of employees’ physical and psychological  health due to exposition to work-related risks (example: accidents,  arduous work). Protecting employ ees’ health and  safety (cf. section 3.4.2.A.c,  p. 156)    Work-related rights and equity Training and skills development /  opportunity &amp; positive impact Employees’ training and skills development foster their  engagement. They also represent opportunities for the employees to grow  professionally and develop their competencies.  Developing  human capital  (cf. section 1.2.7.F.b,  p. 70)  Engaging working  conditions  (cf. section 3.4.2.A,  p. 155)    Working conditions / positive  impact Fostering social dialog and encouraging employees to speak freely  and use their right of association and right to bargain, as well as  promoting fair compensation and a culture of diversity and  inclusion, enable the creation of fair working conditions.   Employees’ work-related rights /  negative impact Potential violation of employees’ work-related rights (child labor,  forced labor…), including risks of exposure to discrimination  or harassment, exposes employees to unfair or violent treatment. Respect human  rights in the  Group’s operations  (cf. section 3.4.5,  p. 172)   A COMMITTED GROUP SUSTAINABILITY STAKES INTEGRATED INTO STRATEGY 110 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com S2 Workers in the value chain Health and safety Workers’ physical and  psychological health / negative  impact Risk of deterioration of the value chain workers’ physical  and psychological health due to exposition to work-related risks  (example: accidents, arduous work). “Responsible  purchasing”  program  (cf. section 3.4.6,  p. 175)     Work-related rights and equity Workers’ work-related rights  and working conditions / negative  impact Potential violation of employees’ work-related rights (child labor,  forced labor, etc.), including risks of exposure to discrimination  or harassment, exposes employees to unfair or violent treatment. The working conditions include access to a fair compensation.  “Responsible  purchasing”  program (cf. section 3.4.6,  p. 175)      S3 Affected communities Communities’ socio-economic development and security Local anchoring and socio- economic development of  territories / positive impact The Group’s markets are highly local, and the organization  is structured around country platforms with support for the local  economy and employment through purchases and actions of local  operations (direct, indirect and induced jobs). Country platforms  (cf. sections 1.2.5  and 1.2.2.D, p. 53  and p. 45)      Security of local communities  (environment, health and safety) /  negative impact The presence of industrial sites potentially exposes local  communities to severe industrial accidents. Risk prevention  program  (cf. section 1.2.7.F.a,  p. 67) EHS management  system (cf. section  1.2.7.F.a, p. 67)    S4 Consumers and end-users Health, safety and quality of products Quality and safety of solutions  and products / negative impact  &amp; risk Potential deterioration of the health, safety and security  of consumers and end-users due to their composition  (chemicals, materials, etc.) or their inappropriate  and dangerous use.  This includes the reputational and legal risks in the event  of inappropriate use of products by customers and end-users. Towards more  sustainable  products and  solutions  (cf. section 3.2.B.b,  p. 119) Product quality  and compliance  program  (cf. section 1.2.7.F.a,  p. 67)    Sales practices and customer information Transparency of information  on products / opportunity Growth opportunity linked to anticipating