wers among  own activities and value chain operations.     Other ethical practices / negative  impact &amp; risk Potential risks related to non-compliance with business restrictions,  penalties and sanctions, or anti-competitive behavior. This includes  potential risks related to lobbying practices related to energy and  ecological transition.  ■   Governance A COMMITTED GROUP SUSTAINABILITY STAKES INTEGRATED INTO STRATEGY 112 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 3.1.4 SUSTAINABILITY STAKES INTEGRATED INTO STRATEGY A – Governance of sustainability stakes A COMMITTED GROUP SUSTAINABILITY STAKES INTEGRATED INTO STRATEGY   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 113 BOARD OF DIRECTORS ■ Examines and establishes the Group’s strategic  orientations annually and ensures their  implementation, taking into consideration the social  and environmental challenges of its activity ■ Determines, on the proposal of the General  Management, the multi-annual ESG strategic  orientations  ■ Reviews results annually (climate or ESG) EXECUTIVE COMMITTEE ■ Defines strategies in line with the Group’s  commitments and stakeholder expectations ■ Ensures implementation methods CORPORATE SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITY COMMITTEE  OF THE BOARD OF DIRECTORS ■ Reviews the Group’s CSR strategy, commitments  and roadmap ■ Examines the double materiality matrix in connection  with the annual review of non-financial risks conducted  by the Audit and Risk Committee ■ Reviews sustainability information and results ■ Reviews, in conjunction with the Nomination  and Remuneration Committee, the criteria for CSR  performance metrics in the long-term compensation  and variable compensation of corporate officers ■ Monitors the non-financial ratings  and sustainability expectations of rating agencies  THE BOARD’S AUDIT AND RISK COMMITTEE ■ Ensures the sustainability information preparation  process is monitored  ■ Ensures the internal control and risk management  systems are effective ■ Monitors the performance of sustainability information  certification exercises  ■ Examines non-financial risks  ■ Designs the methods for implementing  strategies including short-, medium-  and long-term action plans ■ Proposes and instructs prospective elements  in the updating of action plans CORPORATE SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITY COMMITTEE  OF THE EXECUTIVE COMMITTEE ■ Oversees the incorporation of ESG stakes  into the strategy  ■ Coordinates the CSR roadmap ■ Ratifies the objectives SENIOR VICE PRESIDENT, HUMAN RESOURCES  AND CORPORATE SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITY ■ Identifies sustainability stakes by ensuring  the relevance of the methodology followed  (materiality analysis) ■ Develops and maintains the ESG risk management  methodology and updates the maps ■ Ensures compliance and relevance of ESG  reporting including procedures, benchmarks and internal control ■ Coordinates the CSR roadmap CORPORATE SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITY DEPARTMENT The control of sustainability information is the  responsibility of the Corporate Social Responsibility  Department, with the support of the functional  Departments. Each functional Department (e.g. Human  Resources, Legal Affairs, Purchasing, Environment,  Security and Health) with the support of country or  Business Unit organizations, is responsible for producing,  consolidating, analyzing and reporting social,  environmental and governance-related data. Internal  procedures, including internal control procedures, are  updated and widely communicated via an information  tool that compiles the procedures related to financial  and non-financial information. For each of the material stakes, action plans are  prepared, drawing on the expertise of the functional  departments. They are deployed in the countries under  the responsibility of local managers.  The Group has not reported any current financial impact  related to the risks and opportunities of sustainability  stakes. Specific items related to combating climate  change are presented in the note to the consolidated  financial statements 3 “Climate issues” (see section 7,  p. 350). B – CSR strategy For Saint-Gobain, sustainable growth is conceived within  its ecosystem, in other words, taking into account the  interests of all its stakeholders. It is therefore a question  of pursuing a growth trajectory that integrates financial  performance and shareholder value objectives as well as  the Group’s Corporate Social Responsibility.  Saint-Gobain has a perfect alignment between its  corporate purpose, “Making the world a better home”, its  vision, to be the leader in light and sustainable  construction, and its strategic plan, “Grow &amp; Impact”,  from 2021 to 2025, and “Lead &amp; Grow”, since  October 2025. Stakeholders’ expectations and the impact of operations  (see  section 1.2.3.B, p.  48) are thus translated into  sustainability stakes incorporated into the Group’s  strategy. Saint-Gobain has growth opportunities linked  to short-, medium- and long-term sustainability stakes.  These include in particular opportunities related  to  adaptation to climate change and the development  of circularity.  Its CSR roadmap is based on two levers of action  systematically implemented for each of the sustainability  stakes: ■ Deploy high-performance and sustainable solutions  that bring benefits to stakeholders while maximizing  its contribution to environmental impact, health or  well-being; ■ Work towards sustainability by minimizing the  adverse environmental and social effects of its  operations across its value chain and with  communities potentially affected. Saint-Gobain makes use of its code of ethics, the  Principles of Conduct and Action, responsible business  practices (see section 1.2.7.E.a, p.  64) and the values  shared with its partners to make decisions that maximize  its positive impact and minimize the adverse effects on  the environment, human beings and local communities,  including residents in the vicinity of Group sites.  In addition to, and as part of, the “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategic plan, Saint-Gobain is committed to accelerating  the transition of the construction sector towards a more  sustainable model. This transition requires the collective  commitment of all stakeholders – professionals,  institutions and citizens – both at the global level and in  each of the countries where the Group operates. This is why Saint-Gobain launched the Sustainable  Construction Observatory in 2023, positioning itself as a  benchmark company, both a pioneer and a driving force,  to accelerate sustainable construction worldwide by  bringing together all stakeholders.  A barometer of sustainable construction, based on a  quantitative study, was conducted in 22 countries  among professionals, students, elected officials, and  members of associations. While all respondents agreed  that implementing sustainable construction is a priority,  the understanding of sustainable construction often  remains limited to environmental issues: the energy  efficiency of buildings or the carbon footprint of  construction. The concepts of resilience and occupant  comfort are often still considered secondary.  In 2025, an Action Paper was published. This document  is the result of collaboration between international  leaders and experts. It proposes 13 priority actions and a  framework for reflection to accelerate the transformation  towards a more sustainable construction model. It draws  on the analyses and discussions conducted by the  Sustainable Construction Observatory since its creation. Beyond this barometer and publications, events are  organized to bring stakeholders together on topics  related to sustainable construction. In 2025, “Sustainable  Construction Talks” were held, notably in Belém during  COP 30 to present the Sustainable Construction  Observatory’s Action Paper and to discuss collective  mobilization and priority actions to address the climate  crisis; in Venice for the Architecture Biennale on  reconciling creativity and sustainability; in Cape Town to  share the challenges of inclusion and just transition  during World Environment Day; and in Davos during the  World Economic Forum in January 2026 to highlight the  power and opportunity of resilience and adaptation in  sustainable construction. This approach is accompanied by the creation of an  information platform on sustainable construction  challenges, “Constructing a sustainable future by  Saint-Gobain”, to share the opinions or analyses of  stakeholder representatives and theme-based  presentations including innovative solutions and inspiring  projects ( https://www.constructing-sustainable- future.com/). These levers of action are implemented with the  objective of contributing to three long-term ambitions: ■ “a decarbonated home”, encompassing all the  initiatives implemented for the Group’s contribution to  the decarbonization of economies and reducing  global carbon emissions on a trajectory aligned with  the Paris Agreement; A COMMITTED GROUP SUSTAINABILITY STAKES INTEGRATED INTO STRATEGY 114 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com ■ “more performance with less”, representing actions in  favor of an economy that is more frugal in its use of  resources, raw materials and water, avoiding waste  throughout the value chain of Saint -Gobain’s  operations and favoring reuse or recycling;  ■ “a better living for all”, aiming to create a safe,  engaging working environment for Group employees  and those of its partners operating in its value chain  and to contribute to the development of local  communities, based on Saint-Gobain’s code of ethics,  the Principles of Conduct and Action. Internal and external stakeholders are involved in these  action plans as part of an ongoing dialog at both country  organization and Group support function levels (see  section 1.2.3, p. 46). Medium-term objectives are set to monitor Saint-Gobain’s  ESG performance. As part of the implementation of the  “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan, the dashboard of CSR  objectives for 2030 was reviewed (see section 3.2.4.A,  p. 129). Saint-Gobain’s CSR strategy and the corresponding  objectives have been presented to and approved by the  Board of Directors and its CSR Committee. a. Maximize the contribution  to sustainability stakes  The dual materiality analysis identified material  opportunities for the Group, whether financial or impact- related, in the short, medium or long term (see section  3.1.3 B, p. 108).  By 2030, the main challenges and opportunities under  the “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan are linked to  sustainability stakes (see section 1.2.1, p.  39). Growth  opportunities lie in solutions for adapting buildings and  infrastructure to climate change and developing  circularity.  Country platforms are an ideal form of organization for  specifying and adapting solutions to the expectations  and needs of customers who are specific to a given  country and rolling them out in local markets that are  involved in all the stages of the execution of a  construction project, from design, to operation and even  use (see value chain in section 1.2.5, p. 53) The solution-based approach makes it possible to  anticipate the needs of customers through in-depth  knowledge of their expectations and technical expertise  in interior solutions, exterior solutions, construction  chemicals and services including trade and service  agencies. (see section Solutions-based approach and  Markets and activities, pp. 6 to 9) The Group’s approach to developing high-performing  and sustainable solutions is described in section 1.2.7.A,  p.  60. The integration of sustainability considerations  into the innovation process (see section 1.2.7.A.b, p. 61) is  systematic. The impact and footprint of the solutions  developed are assessed throughout their entire lifecycle  and for their main stakeholders involved across the  entire value chain, up to the end user. The share of revenue generated with solutions assessed  as sustainable according to the Group’s methodology  published is 73% in 2025. This revenue includes  operations which are not assessed under European  Regulation 2020/852 Taxonomy Regulation (see section  3.6, p. 200), such as trade and services activities, as well  as impacts and benefits not integrated into the  regulation such as resources and circular economy or  other benefits related to health, safety and comfort  which will potentially be eligible for the social taxonomy  in the future. b. Minimize the Group’s footprint The reduction of the environmental footprint of its  operations, responsible management of its value chain,  and the implementation of its HR policy (see section  3.4.2, p.  154) are the pillars of the Group’s objective to  minimize its operational footprint. To achieve this, Saint-Gobain relies on the efficiency of  its operational excellence program (see section 1.2.7.F.a,  p. 67). To accelerate the reduction of its environmental  footprint, the Group has identified a list of priority  industrial sites called “Focus Groups.” For each theme,  these “Focus Group” sites represent approximately 80%  of the impact. Of Saint-Gobain’s 1,100 industrial sites, the  number of “Focus Group” sites remains limited, fewer  than 200 whatever the topic. The follow-up of the implementation of the  environmental impact management system for  operations and the ones for health and safety is  accompanied by performance monitoring, in particular  the recording of events related to health and safety,  especially accidents or exposure to health risks and  those having an impact on the environment such as  accidental pollution. Saint-Gobain has thus implemented a system for  monitoring environmental incidents based on a  classification that takes into account the nature of the  accident, the type of accidental impact (air, water, or soil  pollution, nuisances, particularly those related to odors  or noise, or any other environmental impact with  consequences for nature or local residents), its intensity,  and its consequences. This approach also relies on the principle of sharing best  practices between sites. For many years, “EHS Awards”  have been organized. Each site can present an  innovative project recently implemented that has  reduced the site’s environmental footprint or improved  health and safety. In 2025, the 35th edition of the “EHS Awards” received 115  projects, which were evaluated by an international jury  composed of Saint-Gobain employees and external  experts. Four main criteria were considered: the project’s  impact (risk reduction, energy savings, reputation,  improvement of EHS culture, replicability, etc.), project  management (participation of staff or external  stakeholders such as associations, cross-site collaboration,  etc.), innovation taking into account the local context, and  communication about the project, both internally and  externally. Fourteen winners were recognized. A COMMITTED GROUP SUSTAINABILITY STAKES INTEGRATED INTO STRATEGY   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 115 3.2 A decarbonated home 3.2.1 GOVERNANCE AND STRATEGY Faced with the challenge of climate change and the risks related to the increase in average temperatures,  Saint-Gobain aims to contribute to the decarbonization of the construction sector, which accounts for nearly 40% of  global CO2 emissions. In September 2019, Saint-Gobain responded to a call for action issued by a broad coalition of  business leaders, civil society representatives and United Nations leaders to help limit the rise in global temperature to  1.5 °C above pre-industrial levels.  In concrete terms, the objective is to bring the Group’s customers solutions to reduce their carbon emissions or to  facilitate their adaptation to climate change while reducing the carbon impact of its operations across the value chain  and thus achieving its goal of net zero emissions by 2050.  A – Governance A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 116 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com The “Carbon” roadmap (see section 3.2.4, p. 129) corresponds to Saint-Gobain’s commitment to “net zero  emissions” by 2050. As a result, Saint-Gobain will reduce its CO2 emissions as much as possible in scope 1, 2  and 3 by 2050 and will implement measures to absorb all the residual emissions. The success of the  “net zero emissions” objective is based on intermediate objectives for 2030 and 2035 and on several levers  of action. The roadmap is coordinated by the Sustainable Development Department.  The Senior Vice President, Human Resources and Corporate Social  Responsibility is in charge of corporate social responsibility. She oversees  the incorporation of ESG challenges into strategy, coordinates the CSR roadmap,  and ratifies the objectives. The Sustainable Development department manages climate-related challenges,  which represent both a risk and an opportunity for the Group. The organizational chart for governance of CSR challenges by the Board  of Directors and the Executive Committee is defined in section 3.1.4.A, p. 113. THE “CARBON” ROADMAP At Group level: ■ the Strategy Department supports  Saint-Gobain’s ambition to be a leader  in sustainability by including sustainable  development as a key pillar of the Group’s  growth and differentiation strategy.  It monitors and implements the internal  carbon price, including updating its level; ■ the Innovation Department coordinates  innovation efforts in processes,  both industrial and related to product design; ■ the Department of Technology and Industrial  Performance supervises coordination  of industrial excellence programs, including  the “World Class Manufacturing” program  and the CAPEX committed; ■ the Purchasing Department ensures  mobilization across the supply chain,  in particular the commitment of suppliers  to the “net zero emissions” ambition  in their operations.  At country level: ■ roadmaps are drawn up by the countries  so that they can define their short-  and medium-term priorities and objectives,  in line with Saint-Gobain’s, and draw up  their own action plans; ■ detailed roadmaps are developed for each  industrial process and supplemented by action  plans designed and deployed by each country  based on knowledge of local markets  and regulatory contexts; ■ each site defines its own roadmap  for the WCM program, in line with international  reference frameworks, standards specific  to its business, the expectations  of its customers, and the improvement  objectives that have been set. Each industrial  department and each Business Unit  is responsible for defining, leading  and monitoring the results of the sites  within its scope. A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 117 The identification, assessment and management of risks and opportunities related  to the effects of climate change is part of the existing procedures for controlling  the Group’s potential risks and developing growth opportunities.  However, the climate is not identified as a risk factor for Saint-Gobain as it does  not have significant financial materiality for the Group. There are many opportunities  related to the effects of climate change, particularly adaptation to climate change.  The Sustainable Development Department coordinates the work on the risks  and opportunities related to climate change. At Group level: ■ the Strategy department oversees the development  of climate scenarios, thereby integrating climate risk  management into the Group’s medium- and long-term  strategy; ■ the Audit and Internal Control department establishes  and maintains the financial risk management methodology  and updates the financial risk maps; ■ the Risk and Insurance department defines the Group’s  prevention and insurance policy, including climate risks; ■ the Environment department participates in assessing  the gross and net risks associated with the effects  of climate change; ■ the Sustainable Construction department, in collaboration  with the Departments of the Performance segment,  coordinates the identification and development of offers  related to opportunities;  ■ the CSR department ensures the consistency of risk  and opportunity assessments in accordance with the  transparency requirements expressed by stakeholders, in particular regulatory requirements and international  standards; ■ the Finance department assesses the financial impact  of climate-change risks and opportunities on the Group,  based on identified medium- and long-term scenarios. At country level: The countries are  included in the risk  identification and  management process.  A digital mapping tool  is made available  to the sites to enable  them to identify their  exposure to risks  and prioritize their  action plans. Specific action plans  related to  opportunities are  developed based  on country exposure  and market maturity. RISK AND OPPORTUNITY MANAGEMENT The Sustainable and High-Performance Solutions offering  (section 1.2.7.A, p. 60) is central to the Group’s strategy.  The aim is to focus on identifying proposals for solutions  that combine sustainability and performance, and measure  the benefits for stakeholders, in particular customers.  The benefits are assessed using a standardized method.  The offering is deployed by the country platforms  with the support of the Departments  of the Performance segments.  At Group level: ■ the departments of the  Performance segments  facilitate and accelerate  Saint-Gobain’s innovation  process, based on meeting  customer expectations.  The teams specializing  in innovation and  management ensure  the consistency and  relevance of use cases.  ■ the Sustainable  Construction department  coordinates  the measurement  of estimated benefits; ■ the CSR department  ensures that the solutions  proposed by the Group  are consistent with  objectives and action  plans in operational terms.  It also manages  performance follow-up. At country level: ■ tools and  information are  made available to  countries to guide  them in developing  a strategy adapted  to their market  context; ■ discussions  between the  marketing and  sales teams serve  to pinpoint cross- functional issues  for which  Saint-Gobain can  provide innovative  and competitive  solutions using  local and global  synergies. SUSTAINABLE AND HIGH-PERFORMANCE SOLUTIONS The Corporate Social Responsibility Department’s role is defined in section 3.1.4.A, p. 113. B – Strategy Buildings and construction account for nearly 40% of  annual CO2 emissions worldwide (1). Two thirds of the  sector’s carbon impact comes from the operation of  buildings, while the remaining one third comes from the  emissions contained in building products in particular.  The transition of buildings and the construction industry  to carbon neutrality is therefore vital to the fight against  climate change (see section 1.3.2.B, p. 76). Saint-Gobain’s  objective is to contribute to a fair and sustainable  transition towards a low-carbon economy. The  implementation and results of this strategy are  incorporated into scenarios that limit global warming to  below a 1.5 °C rise compared with the pre-industrial era,  so that they are compatible with the Paris Agreement.  Saint-Gobain is thus seeking to decouple the growth of its  sales and financial performance from its carbon impact.  In response to climate risks, taking action as closely as  possible to the areas concerned (countries, regions, etc.)  makes it possible to make use of resilient local  ecosystems that are more favorable to the development  of a “low-carbon” economy. Country platforms (see  section 1.2.5, p.  53) are an ideal organization in this  context and their ability to initiate local partnerships is an  asset both in risk management and in the development  of growth opportunities. In addition, Saint -Gobain’s organizational structure is  based on a unique combination of local and global,  which makes it possible to benefit from new  opportunities that meet local needs while being part of a  worldwide dynamic, including in terms of energy- efficient building renovation and the development of  light construction (see  section 1.2.2.A, p.  43) and in  anticipating the effects of climate change.  Policy Climate policy Purpose Set the Group’s strategic climate  guidelines Scope of application All entities Under the supervision of Senior Vice President, Human  Resources and Corporate Social  Responsibility Link to the policy Publication 2025  (replacing the current Energy  and Climate policy) a. Working with stakeholders  for a just transition The fight against climate change requires the  cooperation of all stakeholders as part of a demanding  international framework. Saint-Gobain is committed to  implementing rapid action plans based on a common  goal of limiting the rise in temperature to 1.5 °C. To that  end, the Group has developed programs to involve its  employees, the entire construction sector, public entities  and NGOs. Enhancing employee commitment As part of a just transition, the transformation of  Saint-Gobain’s industrial processes under the  implementation of its “Carbon 2030” roadmap (see  section 3.2.4, p. 129) does not result in any major change  in the business lines or organization of industrial sites.  The training programs set up by the Group already  anticipate this transition (see section 1.2.7.A.a, p. 60), in  particular those aiming to: ■ ensure efficiency and operational excellence;  ■ accelerate the digital transformation in operations,  business models and skills. In addition, Saint-Gobain is seeking to inform and train  its employees on the challenges of climate change for  the planet and for human beings.  To meet this broad objective, it has provided employees  with access to a set of e-learning training courses called  the “Climate Academy”, regardless of their level of skills  on the subject or their role at the company. The Climate  Academy has been designed in two stages. In the first  stage, employees learn about climate change impacts  and challenges through modules of no more than five  minutes. In the second, the Climate Academy presents  eight themes describing the various means of acting to  protect the environment and contribute to carbon  neutrality in 2050. In addition to these online training  courses, “Climate Fresco” workshops are organized  around the world, with more than 75% of employees  having been trained in the climate fresco at end-2025.  To engage all its employees on the road to the  contribution to carbon neutrality by 2050, and to  contribute to achieving the objective of reducing the  Group’s CO2 emissions between now and 2035, in April  2021, Saint-Gobain launched an internal “Carbon Fund”.  First implemented in a pilot region, Northern Europe, it  aims to accelerate the reduction of non-industrial CO 2  emissions through the daily actions of employees and  targeted investments in sites. The areas covered by  these investments are mainly related to the sustainable  mobility of employees, renewable energies and the  improvement of comfort and energy efficiency at  Saint-Gobain sites. These projects proposed and  selected by employees mainly concern their working  environment. Organizational methods, thematic choices  and priorities are defined by local organizations. Engage the transition  of the construction sector The 2025 sustainable construction barometer, published  as part of the sustainable construction observatory,  shows significant global progress in knowledge and  understanding of the concept of sustainable construction.  Among the challenges, issues related to climate change  (energy efficiency, carbon neutrality, and adaptation to  climate hazards in particular) are particularly recognized  by all stakeholders. Resilience to climate hazards has  thus seen significant progress since the previous edition.  A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 118 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) “Global Status Report for Buildings and Construction”, 2022, p. 42. While solutions to reduce carbon emissions in the  construction sector are available, the building value  chain remains fragmented, complicating the  implementation of a fair and carbon-free transition.  Saint-Gobain’s “local for local” model and country  platforms offer concrete opportunities to strengthen  collaboration between stakeholders. Train customers locally, inform the end user Saint-Gobain provides training on energy efficiency and  reducing the environmental impact of buildings, notably  to materials trading companies, which play a key role in  supporting craftsmen, thereby facilitating the marketing  and use of sustainable products.  In France, the Point P network has supported craftsmen  in obtaining EGR qualification since 2023. Beyond  Building Distribution, training structures are offered by  country. They are open to craftsmen, installers,  architects, and other actors in the construction sector.  Opportunities for training, via e-learning or face-to-face,  are offered. Depending on the country and the products,  trainers can travel to construction sites, such as “the  caravans” in Morocco. As part of a just transition, renovation plans to improve  the energy efficiency of buildings have an undeniable  effect not only on carbon emissions, but also on end- users’ energy bills, which is more important for people in  fragile or financially precarious situations. The “Build  Change” program launched in 2022 identifies and  promotes actions taken by countries in favor of social  housing. The Saint -Gobain Foundation also supports  employee initiatives to promote access to decent  housing for excluded people (see section 3.4.3, p. 120).  Contribute to the implementation  of ambitious policy frameworks  with public and non-profit entities Saint-Gobain works in collaboration with public  stakeholders on climate issues. Through professional  organizations, the Group supports the work of the  European Commission, particularly on issues relating to  the Green Deal, including the circular economy and the  framework for decarbonizing the economy.  Saint-Gobain is committed to guiding the construction  sector on a low-carbon trajectory by actively supporting  sustainable construction and energy efficiency:  ■ the Group is a founding member of the steering  committee of the Global Alliance for Buildings and  Construction (GABC), which is behind the “Buildings  Breakthrough” initiative aimed at making resilient and  low-emission buildings the norm by 2030;  ■ Saint-Gobain is fully committed as a non-state player  to the implementation of the Paris Agreement and has  participated with its partners in subsequent  Conferences of the Parties (COPs) on climate change,  including the COP30 in Belém in November 2025.  Saint-Gobain actively contributed to the development  of the “Buildings Breakthrough” and to the  organization in Belém of the first inter-ministerial  meeting on building and the climate; ■ Saint-Gobain is also a member of the WBCSD  Steering Committee on the City and Building and  helped organize the first World Building and Climate  Forum in 2024, at which the Chaillot Declaration was  published and supported by more than 60 states;  ■ The global network of Green Building Councils  (GBCs), a key partner, offers a fast path for the  deployment of sustainable construction technologies  and the dissemination of best practices, particularly  via education for and collaboration with market  players. The Group is a member of more than half of  the world’s local GBCs, a partner of the European  GBC Network (ERN) and a member of their corporate  advisory; ■ Saint-Gobain is part of the Energy Transition  Commission (ETC), which is working to accelerate the  shift to a low-carbon energy system enabling robust  economic growth while limiting global warming  to 1.5 °C. ■ Saint-Gobain is a partner of the Institute of  Sustainable Development and International Relations  (ISDIR) and the Institute of Climate Economics (I4CE),  two think tanks at the heart of the development of  ambitious climate policies. b. Solutions to reduce the carbon impact  of the construction sector and support  its adaptation Saint-Gobain’s solutions help to reduce the negative  climate impact of the construction sector and adapt  buildings to the effects of climate change. The Group is  focusing its action plans on the following areas: ■ offering low-carbon solutions by reducing emissions  in Group operations (scope  1 and 2) and emissions  related to its value chain (scope  3) to reduce the  carbon content of buildings. (see section 3.2.4.B,  p. 129); ■ offering solutions for decarbonizing industrial  processes and manufactured products, such as the  admixtures developed by Construction Chemicals to  reduce the carbon impact of cement and concrete for  buildings and infrastructure alike; ■ offering solutions that provide benefits during the  building operation phase (use phase): the design,  production and distribution of high-performance  solutions with a positive contribution to the  environment, i.e., solutions that promote energy  efficiency and reduce CO 2 emissions during the use  phase of buildings.  A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 119 Solutions to improve the energy performance of  buildings help to reduce both the negative impact of  buildings and construction on the environment and their  occupants’ energy bills, while also enhancing occupant  well-being. In addition, the solutions developed by  Saint-Gobain help reduce energy demand, both during  peak periods and on average, for heating and electricity,  lower energy costs for industries and households, reduce  associated carbon emissions, and optimize investments  in energy production and transportation infrastructure.  They therefore play an important role in the fight against  climate change, because by reducing energy demand  they decrease the quantity of greenhouse gases emitted.  Since 2015, Saint -Gobain regularly updates the  methodology used to calculate the quantity of  greenhouse gases avoided thanks to the use of its own  solutions in order to: ■ comply with the most recent and recognized  international recommendations for the calculation of  avoided greenhouse gas emissions; ■ extend the geographic coverage of the assessment; ■ update the operating conditions of the buildings  taken into account, such as the heating systems, the  distribution of energy used and the associated  emission factors, and the efforts made to renovate the  stocks. Saint-Gobain has estimated that some 1, 043  million  tonnes of emissions have been avoided thanks to the  solutions produced and sold in one year in 2023  throughout their entire life cycle. This research was  conducted for the residential and non-residential  buildings segment and for technical insulation activities  (insulation in an industrial environment or insulation of  ducts and pipes).  The evaluation of avoided emissions is assessed based  on the benefits provided by the installation of  Saint-Gobain solutions compared with an installation  meeting the standard or regulation. When current  construction standards are demanding in terms of  reducing buildings’ impact and are therefore aligned with  the principles of more sustainable construction, the  emissions avoided by the solutions installed by  Saint-Gobain are low or even zero. In this case, it is the  emissions of the entire construction sector that are being  reduced. The gradual reduction in the amount of  emissions avoided related to the products sold is a  favorable sign of the transition of the construction sector,  to which the Group is committed (see section 1.2.1, p. 39).  The updated methodology is available to all stakeholders  on the Saint-Gobain website. This calculation has been  reviewed by an independent third party. The assessment  is based on collecting country-by-country information  from marketing teams, in particular on the condition of  the building stock, the standards in force for new  construction, and the use of recognized external sources,  in particular for evaluating the impact of the country’s  existing energy mix. c. Achieve “net zero emissions”  (scope 1, 2 and 3) by 2050 Ambitious interim targets to support growth  Saint-Gobain set its first decarbonization targets in the  early 2010s and ever since has incorporated plans to  reduce its carbon footprint. In 2019, the Group set a  target of net zero emissions by 2050, validated by the  Science Based Targets initiative and aligned with the  Paris Agreement and a 1.5 °C trajectory. The action plan for reducing carbon emissions (scope 1, 2  and 3) is divided into three periods: ■ 2020 to 2030: Saint-Gobain is rolling out its carbon  roadmap, with an action plan and targets for the end  of 2030 (see section 3.2.4.A.a, p. 130). This roadmap  has two ambitions: – reduce (in absolute terms) CO2e emissions in  scope  1 and 2 by 33% and in scope  3 by 16%  between 2017 and 2030, and do so without using  carbon offsetting or sequestration measures; – innovate and test industrial processes to achieve  net zero emissions in the three scopes. ■ 2025 to 2035 (medium term): when launching its  “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan, Saint-Gobain published  a new objective to reduce scope 1 and 2 emissions in  the medium term. Given the uncertainties of changes  in valuation standards for scope  1, 2 and 3, and the  extension by SBTi of the validity of its objectives to  2030, the Group has chosen to propose a new  objective that will be revised if necessary: a reduction  of between 40% and 45% in scope 1 and 2 emissions  between 2017 and 2035, in absolute value; ■ 2035 to 2050 (long term): the roadmap will be  adapted on the basis of the results obtained during  the previous period. The innovations identified when  implementing the action plans will be rolled out.  Carbon capture projects may be activated at the end  of the period to supplement the transformation  measures up to a maximum of 10% of emissions  (scope 1, 2 and 3). The Group’s action plan does not, at this stage, include  carbon credits or offsetting, or avoided emissions. To support this carbon roadmap, Saint -Gobain has  committed to investing at least €100  million a year in  CAPEX and R&amp;D over the period 2020 to 2030. Saint-Gobain’s operations, in particular its industrial  assets (asset base replaced over a period of 20 years),  but also the solutions that the Group puts on the market,  will not generate significant locked-in CO 2 emissions  by 2050.  A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 120 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com Integrating carbon impact  into operational decision-making tools  to accelerate the transition Since 2016, Saint-Gobain has used an internal carbon price  to assess the current or potential impact that a regulatory  carbon price would have on all of Saint -Gobain’s  operations, identify growth opportunities, refocusing  capital expenditure towards manufacturing and R&amp;D, and  prioritizing actions to reduce CO2 emissions. The objective  of the internal carbon price is consequently to influence  investment decisions. The Group has set two levels of  internal carbon prices. The first internal carbon price level  per tonne is applied to capital expenditure above,  investments associated with a change in energy source,  energy investments on an existing or greenfield site. The  second carbon price level is used for R&amp;D investment in  breakthrough technology. The second carbon price level  is used for R&amp;D investments in technological disruptions.  This price level has proved its relevance in supporting  low-carbon R&amp;D projects. The internal carbon price levels are regularly updated in  line with market trends and are applicable to all  countries in which the Group operates. At end-2025, the  prices applicable were €100 per tonne of CO2 for CAPEX  and €200 per tonne for R&amp;D projects. In addition, a  specific approach applicable to significant acquisitions  has been defined. It includes any effort required to  ensure that the carbon impact of this acquisition is  compatible with Saint -Gobain’s direct and indirect  emissions target trajectory. The carbon impact is also integrated into the processes  for establishing annual budgets or strategic plans.  Information on the carbon impact of planned investments  is available to technical teams at sites and to the industrial  departments of each business. Technical innovations such  as investments in more mature technologies are identified  according to their cost, technical maturity and potential  for reducing CO2 emissions.  Scope  1 and scope  2 emissions are automatically  measured monthly for nearly 90% of the Group’s impact.  This reporting is based on computerized management  systems (ERP). The management controllers are  responsible for updating the information used to  calculate scope 1, such as consumption of raw material  or energy. PPA (Purchase Power Agreement) contracts  are updated by the purchasing teams and integrated  into the reporting tools for monitoring scope 2. In addition, the Board’s CSR Committee ensures that  Corporate Social Responsibility issues are taken into  consideration when defining Saint-Gobain’s strategy and  in its implementation (see organizational chart and for a  description of the Corporate Social Responsibility  Committee’s remit and activities in section 3.1.4.A, p. 113).  Considering the challenge of climate change as strategic  for the Group, the reduction of CO 2 emissions is a  performance metric included within the short- and long- term compensation plans for Group executives (see  section 4.2.3, p. 275 and section 4.2.4.B, p. 276).  Lastly, between 2022 and 2024, financing tools linked  to  ESG performance criteria such as the Sustainability  Link Bond, the Sustainability Link Loan and Green Bond  were issued. A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 121 3.2.2 RISKS, OPPORTUNITIES AND SOLUTIONS Saint-Gobain is committed to understanding and  anticipating the impacts of climate change on its business  activities. The “Risks and opportunities” section reflects  the Group’s proactive approach to climate risk analysis.  The analysis is based on three future climate scenarios,  which identify relevant risks for Saint-Gobain, such as  extreme weather events and new environmental  regulations. At the same time, Saint-Gobain is exploring  the opportunities that may arise from these  developments, particularly in terms of technological  innovation, low-carbon offerings and solutions for  adapting to climate change. This approach helps to  prepare the Group for an uncertain future while actively  contributing to the transition to a more sustainable world. A – Analysis of climate scenarios for 2050 For strategic planning purposes, Saint-Gobain has built three qualitative “climate scenarios” that incorporate political,  technological, economic and societal assumptions. Each scenario is associated with an increase in average  temperature of between 1.5 °C and 4.5 °C before the end of the century and is based on the work of the IPCC.  Indicators Wind of Change The Show Might Go On Highway to Climate Hell GENERAL CONTEXT Increase in temperatures in 2100  compared with the pre-industrial  era (1850–1900) 1.5-1.7 °C 2.1-3.5 °C 4.0-4.5 °C Peak global carbon dioxide emissions Around 2020 Around 2050 Around 2100 Achievement of the “Global zero  carbon” objective Mid-2050 After 2100 Not in the near future Global CO2 emissions per year in 2050  (in 2024: 34 GtCO2) 5 GtCO2/year 30 GtCO2/year 60 GtCO2/year International cooperation model  Multilateralism  and cooperation Digital arms race, lack  of cooperation Regional rivalry  and isolationism MACROECONOMIC CONTEXT Global population (2024 = 8.1 billion) 9.5 billion 9.5 billion 9.5 billion Urbanization rate  (2024 = 58%) 68% 68% 68% CONSTRUCTION NEEDS Sea-level rise  (in 2100 compared with 1986–2005)  +0.4 meters  +0.6 meters  +0.8 meters Occurrence of droughts relative  to 2025 (once in a decade) x 2 x 3 x 4.1 Proportion of intense tropical cyclones  (%) (in 2100 compared with 2025) +10% +15% +30% ENERGY EFFICIENCY OF BUILDINGS Change in energy consumption  of buildings  (in 2050 compared with 2024) -21% +35% +57% Change in net CO2 emissions  of buildings  (in 2050 compared with 2024) -98% -10% WIND OF CHANGE SCENARIO SUSTAINABLE CITY Model  of existing  city Amsterdam, Valencia, Copenhagen, Dubai. Size Medium-sized city – generally between 750,000 and 3 million inhabitants. The city center is densely populated. Mobility Well-established public transit system, increasing investments in active modes of mobility (walking, cycling) and  shared roads. Digital platforms optimize traffic flows and reduce congestion. A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 122 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com METHOD OF CONSTRUCTION ■ Collective buildings are designed for near-zero energy performance. ■ Household appliances are shared and efficient while excess heat is shared between connected buildings. ■ Apartments are easy to reconfigure and refurbish to optimize space and reuse. ■ Builders favor natural lighting, passive cooling and solar protection. ■ To improve their resilience, the buildings are equipped with green walls and roofs, wastewater and rainwater harvesting  systems, reflective materials, and the foundations are adapted to heat waves and clay shrinkage. ■ The use of bio-sourced, reused and recycled materials is becoming widespread to mitigate the effects of climate change.  Likewise, energy self-production, waste sorting and composting systems are installed: innovations (particularly 3D printing,  AI assistance) are thus used to optimize energy efficiency. ■ The installation of smart meters and sensors makes it possible to monitor consumption and automatically regulate lighting,  ventilation, cooling and heating. ■ Real estate markets favor low-carbon, high-performance assets, and the infrastructure sector is sacrificing productivity gains  in favor of a lighter environmental footprint. THE SHOW MIGHT GO ON SCENARIO SUSTAINABLE CITY Model of existing city Nairobi, Buenos Aires, Beijing and some other Asian cities. Size Rapid growth due to intensive industrialization. Mobility Public transit does not cover the entire city and does not make it  possible to optimize travel. Most people continue to travel by car. METHOD OF CONSTRUCTION ■ Two models emerge: firstly, the European model, and secondly, the US model. ■ In Europe, the majority of the population lives in office buildings built in 2000 and converted into residential buildings in  2030. The individual apartments are small, but benefit from large shared spaces (workspaces, fitness, high-tech kitchens).  The appliances shared in the kitchens and laundry rooms, which are gradually being introduced into a circular economy  system, are highly efficient and consume little energy. The roof is equipped with photovoltaic panels, heat pumps are used  for heating, and insulation has been reinforced with recycled materials. A centralized monitoring system automatically  adjusts heating, lighting, ventilation, and cooling. ■ In the United States, the majority of the population lives in apartment buildings and private condominiums in the suburbs of a  large city, in buildings dating from the 1970s. Only a minority of individual apartments have been renovated. Most devices are  programmable, but none are shared. Energy consumption is not systematically monitored. However, facilities have been put in  place to increase the energy efficiency of buildings and mitigate the effects of climate change on residents’ comfort: the  electrification of heating is slowly developing, wall and roof insulation has been improved, double-glazed windows have been  installed, and passive cooling systems have been put in place. ■ With greater exposure to natural disasters, cities are taking reactive measures, but green infrastructure is being introduced late.  HIGHWAY TO CLIMATE HELL SCENARIO SUSTAINABLE CITY Model of existing city London, Rio de Janeiro, some US cities, Lagos, Lima, New Cairo. Size These immense cities occupy a vast territory and are home to  3  to 5  million inhabitants. In less developed countries, these  growing cities are surrounded by large slums. Mobility The majority of the population lives in the suburbs, and driving is  the most used mode of transportation thanks to the extent of the  road network. METHOD OF CONSTRUCTION ■ Subsidized, affordable but low-quality housing is built to contain the endless expansion of slums in emerging countries. Poor  quality is often accompanied by raised living spaces, a lack of daylight, thermal discomfort, high levels of noise pollution and  inadequate ventilation of fresh air.  ■ Emergency renovations in response to natural disasters or the increasing use of air conditioning, which has become the only  recourse to global warming in a self-perpetuating process, do not ensure systemic resilience. Basic insulation solutions are  still incompatible with the affordability required for such “slum sanitation” approaches.  ■ Access to basic services (wastewater, drinking water, electricity, sanitation, waste disposal) is an important step towards  achieving the fundamental objectives of human development. Recycling, reuse and the circular economy can only be  considered when these basic services have been fully realized or when resource scarcity forces innovation, around 2050. A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 123 B – Risks and opportunities  across the value chain The identification and assessment of risks and  opportunities related to climate change is an integral  part of Saint -Gobain’s global risk management and  innovation processes.  Saint-Gobain distinguishes between two types of risk: ■ physical risks, which are the direct consequences of  climate change, such as floods or heat waves, which  can damage infrastructure and affect human health;  ■ transition risks, which are related to the economic and  regulatory changes required to move to a low-carbon  economy, such as the closure of coal-fired power  plants and the increase in carbon taxes.  The major risks linked to climate disruption identified on  the basis of the sixth IPCC report are the increased  frequency and/or intensity of potentially destructive  events, resource scarcity, and the increase in global  temperatures.  The Group has identified five strategic risks and five  strategic opportunities related to climate change. Each  risk and opportunity affects each segment of  Saint-Gobain’s value chain differently, from raw material  extraction to end-of-life.  The analysis of exposure to transition risks was carried  out based on the 1.5 °C scenario, Wind of Change. The  risks analyzed by Saint-Gobain are political and legal,  technological, reputational, and market risks. The  analysis covered the entire value chain. There are  significant disparities between regions, linked in  particular to the varying pace of development of  ambitious policies and regulatory frameworks, local  investment to ensure the availability of carbon-free  energy at a competitive price, and the capacity to  develop circular material flows within a given territory.  The Group’s opportunities are linked both to the effects  of climate events and to the adaptation of buildings and  infrastructure.  The Risk and Insurance Department continuously  assesses the risks to which the Group’s sites are exposed  worldwide and, in particular, the risks related to the  effects of climate change (see section 3.2.3, p. 127).  In order to evaluate the financial impact of these risks and  opportunities, Saint-Gobain conducted a further study  with an external firm to identify its exposure to physical  risks related to the impact of climate change (floods,  forest fires, cyclones, storms, drought and heat stress), as  well as earthquakes, on its operations. Exposure and  vulnerability to climate issues were analyzed for assets at  approximately 500 of the largest industrial and logistics  sites (covering more than 80% of the Group’s sales and  net book value of its assets), using three scenarios based  on the work of the IPCC: SSP1-2.6 – Wind of Change;  SSP2-4.5 – The Show Might Go On; SSP5-8.5 – Highway  to Climate Hell; and three time horizons: 2030 (medium  term), 2040 and 2050 (long term).  This study served to assess the financial impact for each  site and for the Group as a whole, after factoring in the  following impacts: ■ direct impacts: the immediate effects of climate  change on sites, infrastructure, and living and working  conditions; ■ indirect impacts: the consequences of climate change  on business activity and the financial statements, and  the impact on the workforce, including business  interruptions. Heat stress, floods and storms would represent the bulk  of the estimated risks, which would mainly arise not as a  result of direct damage but from business interruption.  The impact would be more significant in Asia and India,  while Europe would not be materially affected.  Potential adaptation measures were then examined to  reduce the vulnerability of the sites, taking into account  their cost, feasibility and ease of implementation. This  study confirms that the risks identified do not present  significant financial materiality for the Group on its own  operations, including in the most extreme scenario and  by 2050 (scenario SSP5-8.5 – Highway to Climate Hell)  (see Note 3 Climate issues in chapter 7). This study was supplemented in 2024 by a comparable  analysis of 51 Australian sites of the CSR Australia entity.  The risks identified during this additional analysis also  appear to be insignificant for CSR and Saint -Gobain  overall. The update of the entirety of this study  conducted in 2024 confirmed the conclusions of the  initial 2023 study. In parallel with this physical risk assessment for its assets,  the Group has begun analyzing the growth opportunities  for its solutions resulting from the impact of climate  change in several regions.  Saint-Gobain’s solutions are designed to limit their  impact on the climate. The Group’s analysis of  opportunities focused on three risks in four strategic  countries, covering 38% of its revenue and focusing on  the construction and renovation markets. Heat waves  were analyzed in France and Brazil, floods in India, and  cyclones in the United States, according to the three  IPCC scenarios: SSP1-2.6 – Wind of Change; SSP2-4.5 –  The Show Might Go On; and SSP5-8.5 – Highway to  Climate Hell.  The tables below show how the opportunities and risks  identified by Saint -Gobain impact each stage of the  value chain while being part of global market dynamics  and meeting the expectations of end users. This  approach has also been aligned with TCFD  recommendations. The risks identified do not present  significant financial materiality for the Group, even over  the long-term, worst-case scenario based on current  knowledge. The Group takes these climate challenges fully into  account, incorporating them into its financial statements  and their short-, medium- and long-term projections  (economic forecasts, energy mix, discount rate, etc. (see  Note 3 Climate issues in chapter 7). A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 124 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com Physical  risks Acute  physical Increase in  the intensity  and frequency  of extreme  events  (cyclones,  floods) ■ Ensuring the safety  of workers throughout  the supply chain; ■ Risk of disruptions  in the supply of raw  materials; ■ Decrease in production  capacity due to  damage and losses; ■ Limit losses and  additional delays. Highway  to  Climate  Hell LT + + + Adaptation  plan (see section  3.2.3, p. 127) Chronic  physical Sea-level rise,  increase  in average  temperatures,  change in  precipitation  regime ■ Ensuring the safety  of workers throughout  the supply chain; ■ Risk of disruptions  in the supply of raw  materials; ■ Decrease in production  capacity due to  damage and losses; ■ Limit losses and  additional delays. LT + + + Adaptation  plan (see section  3.2.3, p. 127) Transition  risk Political  and legal Legal  proceedings  and climate  disputes ■ Threat to the  company’s reputation; ■ Exposure to claims  for damages resulting  in financial costs. Wind of  Change ST +++ Adaptation  plan (see section  3.2.3, p. 127) Legal risks  (see section  5.1.4, p. 295) Technolo- gical Transition  to low-carbon  products and  technologies ■ Availability and costs  of raw materials  and production; ■ Increase in R&amp;D  spending; ■ Risk of loss of  competitiveness. Wind of  Change MT +++ +++ + Innovation  (see sections  1.2.7.A.b,  p. 60 and  2.2.2.B, p. 96) Market Increase in  the price of raw  materials and  energy ■ Costs of raw materials  and production; ■ Increase in R&amp;D  spending; ■ Demand for reduction  of emissions related  to products and  operations. MT +++ +++ + Note 10.1.3  Market risks Short Medium Long  terms Priority on the  value chain* Risk  management  strategy  and growth  opportunitiesType Title Impact description Scenario A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 125 Opportu- nities Resource  use Recycling  and reducing  dependency  on raw  materials ■ Replacement of  extracted raw materials  with recycled materials; ■ Treatment of waste  to transform it into  potential new raw  materials; ■ Increased demand  for products  containing recycled  materials; ■ Development of local  recycling channels  in response to the  scarcity of resources. Wind of  Change ST ++ ++ +++ ++ Resources  and the  circular  economy  (see section  3.3.3, p. 143) Goods  and  services Development  of solutions  to meet  customers’  adaptation  and resilience  needs ■ Taking into account  local climate  specificities in  the development  of sustainable  construction solutions; ■ Cooperation with local  actors to improve  the resilience and  adaptability of cities  in relation to their  exposure to physical  risks; ■ Development  of new materials  and construction  techniques for better  energy efficiency. ST + + ++ Sustainable  solutions  (see section  3.2.1.B.b,  p.119) and  transition  plan/ adaptation  plan Goods  and  services Development/ expansion  of low-carbon  products ■ Development of a  low-carbon solutions  offering; ■ Design of products  whose use has little  impact, in particular  energy efficiency  solutions. ST +++ +++ +++ ++ Sustainable  solutions  (see section  3.2.1.B.b,  p.119) and  transition  plan Political  and legal Regulatory  changes in  the composition  of products ■ Substitution of the raw  materials currently  used; ■ Demand for  transparency and  choice of more  sustainable solutions; ■ Implementation of new  compositions. ST + + ++ Adaptation  plan (see section  3.2.3, p. 127) Climate  change  mitigation  transition  plan  (see section  3.2.4, p. 129) Reputa- tional Perception  in civil  society ■ Increasing stakeholder  attention and  responsiveness on  climate change issues,  including the use  of fossil fuels; ■ Growing impact  of public opinion. ST ++ ++ Adaptation  plan (see section  3.2.3, p. 127) Short Medium Long  terms Priority on the  value chain* Risk  management  strategy  and growth  opportunitiesType Title Impact description Scenario *  Very low ( ), Low (+), Moderate (++), High (+++) A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 126 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 3.2.3 ADAPTATION PLAN A – Adaptation to physical risks In addition to developing an offering linked to market  adaptation needs, Saint -Gobain is preparing its  operations for the risks associated with climate change  and is developing: ■ programs to adapt its operations, particularly its  industrial sites, to climatic events (see section 1.2.7.F.a  “Industrial and distribution risk prevention policy”,  p. 67); ■ programs to adapt working conditions to rising  temperatures; ■ adaptation programs linked to the potential impact of  climate change on the supply chain. The “World Class Manufacturing” (WCM) industrial  excellence program (see section 1.2.7.F.a, p. 67) ensures  the implementation of best practices in terms of  methods and tools at industrial sites and facilitates the  implementation of adaptation measures. In 2022, a specific study of the exposure of the main  sites was conducted. These risks are managed by the  sites concerned, which draw up and implement action  plans to reduce their risk exposure, updating business  continuity plans to take account of climatic risks. The  technical and industrial performance department, in  particular the industrial and R&amp;D departments of each  activity, coordinates actions involving changes to  industrial processes or plant design. However, as  specified in paragraph 3.2.2.B, the risks identified do not  present significant financial materiality for the Group  (even over the long-term, in the worst-case scenario  based on current knowledge).  Saint-Gobain is implementing concrete measures to  respond to physical risks at its sites. For example, the  new furnaces installed on the sites are designed to  better take into account the risk of flooding. All  Saint-Gobain industrial sites exceed local seismic  standards. To mitigate the risk of drought, particularly in  water-stressed areas, Saint-Gobain is working to adapt  its most water-intensive sites to operate them in a closed  loop and potentially invest in rainwater capture systems.  These measures are accompanied by training programs  for site teams that enable the rapid implementation of  preventive measures in the event of climate events such  as rising water levels or floods.  The HR and Environment, Health and Safety (EHS) teams  have worked on prevention tools to anticipate the  deterioration of working conditions during heat waves  and periods of intense cold.  B – Adaptation to transition risks All of Saint-Gobain’s activities are compatible with  climate transition and the goal of achieving net zero  emissions by 2025. a. Political and legal At the local level, monitoring and compliance programs  have been implemented in the countries coordinated by  the EHS Department, while the Legal Department  monitors new environmental regulations. In addition, the  marketing teams ensure that the Group’s products  comply with local environmental regulations. Similarly,  the risks of climate change disputes are assessed as part  of the existing risk management process. Saint -Gobain  monitors changes in climate reporting requirements in  order to meet the expectations of its stakeholders,  particularly regulatory requirements and investor  expectations. b. Technological The Group anticipates the technological risk linked to the  substitution of existing products with low-carbon  options by investing in the development of disruptive  technologies and eco-innovative solutions to meet the  expectations of its customers. Two internal carbon prices  were set up in 2016 to support the viability of  Saint-Gobain’s projects and strategy (see section  3.2.1.B.c, p. 120).  c. Market Saint-Gobain anticipates the risk of raw materials  scarcity by actively promoting the transition towards a  circular economy (see section 3.3.3, p.  143) and by  reducing its water consumption (see section 3.3.2,  p.  140). The substitution of non-renewable virgin raw  materials with renewable or recycled raw materials, the  extension of the lifespan or use of Saint-Gobain’s  products or systems, and the reduction of the intensity  of materials are at the heart of the Group’s innovation  process and enable it to ensure the competitiveness of  its solutions while anticipating changes in the  preferences of its end users and legislation. Saint-Gobain is also working to optimize and reduce its  energy consumption through such means as investing in  digital tools to adjust energy consumption as closely as  possible to needs. A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 127 Anticipation of the increase in the cost of high-emission  products, which would follow the tightening of  regulations and the change in preferences expected by  end users and customers, encourages Saint -Gobain to  support the development of low-carbon materials and  methods throughout its value chain. In addition to its  efforts to contribute to carbon neutrality in its  operations, the Group promotes renewable energies  and develops solutions to improve the energy efficiency  of its customers to enable them to meet the climate  and  environmental challenges they face (see section  3.2.2.B, p. 124).  Lastly, the Purchasing Department factors the risk of an  increase in the price of raw materials or of energy and  greenhouse gas emissions into its purchasing strategies.  As part of its Responsible Purchasing program,  Saint-Gobain relies on a diversity of suppliers and supply  sources to reduce the risk of transportation difficulties  and supply chain disruptions. d. Reputational Mindful of the impact of a bad reputation on  environmental matters, the Group fully integrates  reputational risk into its risk management policy.  Saint-Gobain is demonstrating its commitment to the  climate through its climate policy and concrete targets  for reducing its CO2 emissions and energy consumption.  Lastly, the needs and risks related to access to water  for  local populations are also taken into account in its  action plans. C – Opportunities related  to adaptation  Regardless of the predictive scenarios on the effects of  climate change (see section 3.2.2.A, p.  122), the  combined effects of temperature rises and climate  events (droughts, floods, fires, storms, etc.) will have a  strong impact on construction market growth: ■ destruction of homes caused by the increase in  climatic phenomena, resulting in increased demand  for reconstruction in the geographical areas affected; ■ buildings damaged by drought and shrinkage in clay  soils, for example in France the percentage of  buildings reckoned to be exposed or highly exposed  to risks varies from 35% to 78% for temperature  increases between 1.8 °C and 4.4 °C (source I4CE). At the same time, construction methods will have to  change to incorporate new constraints to preserve the  value of buildings and/or to ensure the comfort of  building users, both residential and non-residential, in  response to rising temperatures and the growing  number of days of heatwave.  Saint-Gobain is thus orienting its offering towards  sustainable construction markets. Saint-Gobain’s portfolio  of businesses and expertise in insulation, materials  reinforcement with construction chemicals, and building  science, ensures that it is particularly well positioned to  meet the adaptation needs of construction markets. By  leveraging existing expertise and building on its culture  of innovation (see section 1.2.7.D.a, p. 63), Saint-Gobain’s  range of solutions can be extended to meet the perils  associated with the effects of climate change, in  particular adapting housing to rising temperatures or  protecting buildings from storms and flooding. Thanks to its strong exposure to the renovation market,  the Group is ideally situated to play a decisive role in the  national and European “green recovery” plans for the  energy transition, which should support Saint -Gobain’s  structural growth. Saint-Gobain’s portfolio of expertise  and solutions provides it with a particularly favorable  positioning in the face of the need to adapt construction  markets, and resource scarcity. The program to develop  the range of sustainable and high-performance solutions  also includes a component on improving customer  productivity. The organization structure based on country platforms  (see section 1.2.5, p.  53) makes it possible to develop  solutions that meet the specific needs of the region  concerned and to be part of local building resilience  programs. Specific offers for adapting buildings are being  developed in many countries, for example, in the US  CertainTeed has partnered with the Insurance Institute  for Business &amp; Home Safety (IBHS) to offer roofing  solutions that meet their FORTIFIED™ Roof standard.  FORTIFIED™ Roof is a nationally recognized standard  designed to strengthen homes against extreme weather  conditions, including high winds, hail, and heavy rain. It  goes beyond typical building codes and offers a unified  system of products and installation methods that have  been proven to increase the resilience of homes and  reduce potential damage.  In some parts of the world, rising temperatures combined  with high humidity levels will have a significant effect on  working conditions at construction sites, which may even  lead to a shutdown of construction sites for an increasing  number of days. Saint -Gobain’s customers will have to  change their methods and find productivity drivers. Since  its launch in 2021, the program to develop the range of  sustainable and high-performance solutions (see  section  1.2.7.A, p.  60) has included a customer  productivity improvement component through  digitalization service proposals designed to improve  anticipation of worksite organization or prefabrication,  optimizing assembly time on the worksite. A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 128 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 3.2.4 TRANSITION PLAN  A – 2030 to 2050 trajectory Saint-Gobain’s medium- and long-term growth ambitions  require a reduction in the carbon intensity of its  operations and solutions. The Group has set a target of  net zero emissions by 2050 in scope 1, 2 and 3, validated  by the Science Based Targets initiative (SBTi) and  aligned with the Paris Agreement and a 1.5 °C trajectory. In 2020, SBTi validated Saint -Gobain’s 2030 target:  8.9 million tonnes of CO2 in scope 1 and 2 in 2030, i.e., a  33% reduction in absolute terms between 2017 and 2030,  and a 16% reduction in scope 3 over the same period.  While the Group has rotated its business portfolio, the  cumulative effects on the original baseline are not  significant, at under 5%. The Group has invested in new  business activities, mainly in the construction chemicals  sector, and has sold trading and glass processing  activities. Saint-Gobain has also made acquisitions to  step up growth in new regions, investing in the  Australian company CSR in 2024 as well as in Canada, in  Kaycan, Bailey and BP Canada.  In addition, Saint-Gobain has implemented a set of  indicators to monitor its performance in terms of climate  change. Emissions reduction performance is therefore an  operational performance indicator in the same way as  financial performance indicators. By the end of 2025, the Group had reduced its emissions  in scope 1 and 2 by 35% since 2017. Assessing scope 3 emissions remains a challenge for all  companies due to methodological changes, the  complexity of value chains, and disparities in the quality  of data provided by partners. Since 2021, Saint-Gobain  has implemented a program to improve the quality of  data collected and expand the scope and categories of  scope 3 emissions considered.  This is why the update of Saint-Gobain’s scope  3  emissions assessment in 2021 was accompanied by a  more specific assessment of emission factors and a more  granular analysis of the impacts on the three most  significant categories, representing more than 80% of  total scope 3 emissions. The calculation was updated in  2023 to more accurately reflect the impact of end-of-life  products and based on more recent emission factors.  The methodology used to measure Saint-Gobain’s  scope  3 emissions is available in the resource center  section of its website ( https://www.saint-gobain.com/ en/corporate-responsibility/resource-center). A database has also been created to track the emission  factors of materials and products purchased by the  Group. The ideal and most reliable approach is to obtain  a life cycle analysis from suppliers for the materials and  products they have delivered to the Group. If this  information is not available, the assessment is based on  values from recognized databases, such as Gabi or  EcoInvent. This collective effort to improve data results in an  automatic increase in the Group’s scope  3 emissions  assessment, but it is an essential prerequisite for  ensuring the effectiveness of the collective action plans  implemented.  At the end of 2025, scope 3 emissions were estimated at  23.4 million tonnes for the scope considered in the 2030  target validated by SBTi (24.4 million tonnes at the end  of 2023), as detailed in section 3.2.4.A, p.  129. This  increase is largely due to improved coverage of  estimates, particularly the inclusion of end-of-life  emissions from products. The scope 3 reduction target between 2017 and 2030 is  measured on a reduced scope in accordance with the  methodology validated by SBTi. Nevertheless,  Saint-Gobain assesses all categories and action plans are  implemented across all of its operations. scope  3  reporting tables for all categories and for the scope of  the target validated by SBTi are available at section  3.5.3, p. 197. The scope  3 impact of Saint -Gobain’s operations is  concentrated on the three most significant categories:  ■ purchases of raw materials (category 1) and energy  (category 3);  ■ transport and logistics (categories 4 and 9);  ■ purchases related to trading operations (category 1).  Each country is responsible for its own roadmap  incorporating emissions from scope  1, 2 and 3. Each  year’s financial budgets include an assessment of carbon  emission reductions based on planned investments. The  carbon roadmap is supported by a CAPEX and R&amp;D  investment plan of at least €100  million per year until  2030. In 2025, €213 million had been invested in capital  expenditure and R&amp;D related to the carbon roadmap.  Under European Taxonomy Regulation 2020/852, a  limited number of the Group’s activities have been  assessed. As a result, only 16.4% of its revenue is eligible  in 2025. Furthermore, the methods used to calculate  indicators, particularly Capex and Opex as defined by  the regulation, differ from the operational rules for  monitoring the carbon roadmap. The Capex amounts  published in accordance with the European Taxonomy  Regulation are available in section 3.6, p. 200. The carbon roadmap, and the action plans in general,  addressing the commitment to achieve net zero carbon  emissions by 2050 are regularly monitored by the  governance bodies (cf. section 3.2.1.A, p. 116). A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 129 B – Scope 1 and 2 carbon emissions  and decarbonization levers The 2030 roadmap relies on four main levers to reduce  scope 1 and 2 emissions: ■ actions on products  (A): product optimization and  eco-design, including recycling efforts and the  integration of recycled materials;  ■ industrial excellence  (B): industrial process  improvements and productivity efforts; ■ innovation  (C): new technologies and new  compositions; ■ the use of carbon-free energies (D). The quantification  of the importance of each of these levers in achieving  the 2030 target is estimated and excludes the effect  of production volume.  The CO₂ emissions reduction target is calculated by  absolute value and not by intensity. In addition,  variations in production volume to adapt to market  demand, and changes in scope  related to acquisitions  and disposals, have a significant impact on the  measurement of progress towards the 2030 target.  The success of this roadmap requires the involvement of  all employees and a change of mentality. Carbon  reduction objectives are included in all operational  processes: the WCM program for ongoing productivity  efforts, energy purchases that include securing supplies  of carbon-free energies (renewable electricity or biogas,  for example), and increased use of recycled materials,  which involves the development of collection channels in  all countries or the evolution of products through eco- design (such as lightening and optimizing packaging to  reduce the impact of transport). The future availability of decarbonized and economically  viable energy in sufficient quantities is an indispensable  factor in achieving zero net emissions by 2050. However,  access to this energy remains uncertain owing to  challenges in the production, distribution and growing  demand for low-carbon energy. Energy transport and  the connection of sites also remain key factors to be  included in the roadmap, certainly since the security of  supply is not fully guaranteed. 2017 2025 Volume Optimization  &amp; eco-design Process &amp;  Performance  Improvement Innovation Decarbonated  Energy  Purchase 2030 Growth 2035 The levers identified by Saint-Gobain to achieve its scope 1 and 2 decarbonization targets are detailed in the table  below. The percentage corresponds to the contribution of each lever to achieving the 2030 target (excluding scope  and volume effects). This assessment remains an estimate.  A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 130 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com - 33% A B C D 13.4 Mt 8.7 Mt 9 Mt 8 Mt  Name  of lever Position in supply chain Description  of action Contribution  to the  objective Structuring  assumptions A – Product  optimization  and eco-design Purchases of raw materials Operations Development and optimization  of the solution portfolio (lighter products,  optimized packaging, integration  of recycled materials) 15% Excluding  volume and  scope effects Application case: ■ BANTAM program (weight of plasterboard reduced by up to 20%); ■ SLIMWOOL program (optimized weight of glass wool for equivalent performance). B – Improving  processes and  performance Operations Improving the productivity and energy  efficiency of Saint-Gobain’s industrial  processes 30% Excluding  volume and  scope effects Application case: ■ ISO 50001 certification for 26.5% of Saint-Gobain sites; ■ Deployment of digital tools and energy audits to improve and better control industrial performance. C – Innovation Research and development Research and development of sustainable  and efficient solutions for new decarbonized  industrial processes (low-carbon glass,  hydrogen) 5% Excluding  volume and  scope effects Application case: ■ First low-carbon gypsum plants under investment in Norway and Canada; ■ Oraé® low-carbon glass offer (40% reduction compared with the European market average) and Lanaé® recyclable glass  wool gradually rolled out. D – Purchase  of decarbonated  energy Operations Transition to energy supply from  decarbonized sources 50% Excluding  volume and  scope effects Application case: ■ Action plans for site procurement and development of renewable energy projects; ■ Launch in March 2024 of very low-carbon production at three US façade cladding production sites. C – Scope 3 emissions and  decarbonization levers The deployment of the 2030 carbon roadmap also  includes the scope 3 reduction target. The first step is to  improve the identification and measurement of impacts,  while raising awareness among internal players, in  particular buyers and logistics experts, and external  partners, primarily suppliers.  Actions are taken in each of the 15 categories according  to the materiality of their impact. Three categories are crucial to achieving the reduction  target: ■ category 1: purchased goods and services; ■ categories 4 and 9: transport. While scope  3 decarbonization levers are clearly  identified, the Group’s ability to reduce its scope  3  emissions largely depends on the ability of the Group’s  partners to decarbonize their own operations.  The action plan addresses three main areas: ■ product reformulation, which will contribute  approximately 30% to achieving the target; ■ innovation and logistics performance, which will  contribute approximately 10% to achieving the target. ■ the decarbonization of purchases through supplier-led  initiatives, which will contribute approximately 60% to  achieving the target. These figures exclude any changes in methodology,  changes in scope, improvements in information systems  and the quality of information about emission factors.  SBTi commitment  and presentation of scope 3  Category 11 of scope  3, “Use of products sold”, is not  material for Saint-Gobain according to SBTi criteria and  therefore does not need to be reported. The main  components of this category are the use of boilers and  windshields sold by the Group. For each of these activities, the Group’s direct influence  on reducing this category is limited and does not prevent  the transition of these industries. Nevertheless, Mobility  Business Unit teams are developing specific products for  electric vehicles and are involved in their customers’  action plans for sustainable mobility. Similarly, the  purchasing teams of Saint-Gobain’s distribution  businesses work with their suppliers to reduce the carbon  impact of heating during the use phase of buildings. The  brands are also contributing to efforts to transform the  construction industry by training trade professionals and  informing users (see section 3.4.3.B, p. 163). A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 131  Other scope 3 categories The impact of category 11, on the use of sold products, is  concentrated on two operations: the sale of automotive  glazing and the sale of air-conditioning and heating  equipment by the heating equipment building trading  brands.  With regard to category 3 (fuel and energy-related  emissions not included in scope  1 and 2), efforts to  reduce fossil fuel consumption and promote the use of  decarbonated energy sources will have a positive impact  on the reduction of scope 3 emissions. Regarding category 6 on business travel, the travel  policy was updated in 2023 to: ■ check and significantly reduce the number of trips,  including international trips; ■ systematically review the advisability of postponing or  switching seminars and other meetings into virtual  meetings; ■ travel by train is mandatory for all journeys of less  than 4 hours.  Methodology, Volume Saint-Gobain expects an increase in scope 3 emissions as  a result of the increase in production, and also as a result  of actions to improve the reliability of the scope  3  assessment carried out with suppliers. Five principles are implemented by the purchasing  teams and environmental experts:  ■ a digital tool available to teams at local level and by  category to enable them to estimate scope  3  emissions;  ■ communication kits to share the challenges and  targets of the fight against climate change with  suppliers; ■ the collection of information by suppliers on their  emissions on the basis of life cycle analyses verified  by independent third parties; ■ the integration of the maturity of suppliers regarding  their climate commitment into the overall assessment  of their performance;  ■ the creation of joint action plans with suppliers to  reduce their carbon impact. This systematization will continue to make scope  3  assessments more reliable. It also structures action plans  to accelerate the reduction of emissions.  Best practices have been identified and shared with all  Group buyers. 2017 2025 Methodology,  Volume Reformulation Decarbonization  of the supply  chain Innovation &amp;  logistics  performance 2030 The levers identified by Saint-Gobain to achieve its scope 3 decarbonization targets are detailed in the table below.  The percentage corresponds to the contribution of each lever to achieving the 2030 target (excluding scope and  volume effects). This assessment remains an estimate.  A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 132 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com - 16% 17.4 Mt 23.4 Mt 14,6 Mt A B C  Name  of lever Position in supply  chain Description  of action Contribution  to the  objective Structuring assumptions E –  Reformulation  of products Operations Reformulation of products  aimed at substituting the  highest-impact raw materials,  either with recycled materials  or with new low-carbon  compositions 30% Excluding any changes in methodology  and scope, improvements in information  systems, and the quality of information  on emission factors Application case: ■ Integration of recycled glass in glass wool production (see section 3.3.3.C.b, p. 144). F – Logistics  optimization The entire  value chain      Optimization of logistics  upstream and downstream  of the value chain 10% Excluding any changes in methodology  and scope, improvements in information  systems, and the quality of information  on emission factors Application case: ■ Choice of multimodal and lower-emission modes of transport in partnership with the Group’s logistics providers. G –  Decarbonization  of purchasing Purchases of  raw materials Decarbonization of purchases  through measures carried out  by suppliers (exogenous  driver) 60% Excluding any changes in methodology  and scope, improvements in information  systems, and the quality of information  on emission factors Application case: ■ Assessment questionnaires and on-site audits of the CSR performance of suppliers according to risk level; ■ Specific action plans on critical raw materials such as sand, wood and high-risk minerals (see section 3.4.6, p. 175). D – The objectives beyond 2030 a. Objective 2035 In October 2025, for Capital Markets Day and the launch  of its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan, Saint-Gobain  announced new targets for 2035, with an absolute  reduction in scope  1 and 2 emissions of between 40%  and 45% (2017 basis). This objective was determined by taking into account  the Group’s growth objectives, the effects of measures  and investments linked to the “Carbon 2030” roadmap,  and the uncertainties linked to the availability of low- carbon and competitive energy in the countries where  the Group operates.  This objective was set on a voluntary basis. SBTi  extended the validity of scope  1 and 2 and scope  3  objectives through to 2030. A process to approve new  objectives beyond 2030 has been initiated as part of the  revision of methodologies, which impacts trajectory  calculations in particular.  At the same time, the standards and methodologies for  assessing scope  3 are being revised by the reference  bodies. In addition, pending an assessment of the effect  of these changes, Saint-Gobain is moving ahead with its  in-depth work to improve data quality and the reliability  of the methodology implemented.  b. Objective 2050 A new roadmap for 2050 will be established following  the “Carbon 2030” roadmap. It will incorporate the  technical advances identified thanks to the innovation  efforts made over the 2020-2030 period. While in the  2020-2030 period, innovation efforts will not produce  much by way of tangible results in terms of reducing  scope 1 and 2 emissions, their implementation will be the  most significant contributor to reducing emissions in the  2030-2050 period.  The development of a circular economy in all countries  where the Group is present and is also a determining  factor in achieving the reduction target for scope  1, 2  and 3. The availability of decarbonized and economically viable  energy in sufficient quantities will be an indispensable  factor in achieving zero net emissions by 2050. The associated financial resources will also be assessed. A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 133 3.2.5 2025 PERFORMANCE (CLIMATE) OBJECTIVES Deadline 2025 data 2024 data (2) 2023 data Baseline -40 to-45% in scope 1 and 2 GHG emissions  between 2017 and 2035 2035  -35.1%  -36.8 %  -34.3 % 8.7 Mt 8.5 Mt 8.8 Mt 13.4 Mt  -16% in scope 3 GHG emissions between 2017  and 2030 (1) 2030  +34.8 %  +36.5 %  +40.8 % 23.4 Mt 23.7 Mt 24.4 Mt 17.4 Mt - 15% in energy consumption between 2010  and 2025 (at iso-production) 2025  -5.9 %  -5.6 %  -6.7 % - 20% in scope 1 and 2 CO2e emissions  between 2010 and 2025 (at iso-production) 2025  -35.4 %  -32.9 %  -30.8 % 100% of revenue generated with products  covered by verified LCA or EPD, excluding  distribution activity 2030  57.4 %  57.7 %  53.5 % (1) Methodology and scope of calculation for scope 3 aligned with the Science Based Targets 2030 (SBTi) initiative. Scope has been expanded compared to 2017 thanks to improved data collection. (2) Data for scope 1 and 2 in 2024 exclude recent acquisitions. 2024 CO2e is estimated at 8.9 Mt, including the full-year effect of acquisitions. The 2010-2025 targets for iso-production were  established in 2014, prior to the implementation of the  “Transform &amp; Grow” and “Grow &amp; Impact” strategic plans.  Given the rotation of the asset portfolio and changes in  the geographical footprint, they no longer reflect the  Group’s performance. As a result, medium-term targets  in absolute terms have been gradually introduced.  Nevertheless, by the end of 2025, most of the targets set  had been achieved:  ■ CO2 emissions and energy: exceeded CO2e emission  reduction targets for scope  1 and 2 (-35% vs. -20%)  thanks to the use of carbon-free energy, offsetting the  shortfall in energy consumption reduction targets  (-6% vs. -15%). The Group has significantly changed its  energy mix, favoring the transition from high-carbon  energies such as fuel oil to lower-carbon energies  such as natural gas and decarbonized energies such  as decarbonized electricity. The choice of energy type  has an impact on the consumption measured for the  same use. ■ Other emissions: Sox and Nox emission reduction  targets have been significantly exceeded. The dust  emission reduction target, although regularly  exceeded in recent years, has not been achieved in  2025. ■ Water discharges: the effect of changes in the asset  portfolio is particularly significant for this indicator.  The reduction achieved remains substantial, even if it  is far from the target of -48% vs. -80%. ■ Waste: with a reduction of 47%, the result achieved is  close to the target of 50%. A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 134 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com GHG EMISSIONS 2025 2024 (2) 2023 ESRS GRI SDG Market based Gross direct GHG emissions (scope 1) 7,907,492 t 7,628,642 t 7,738,985 t E1-6 305-1 12; 13 Change in direct GHG emissions (scope 1) 278,850 t -110,343 t -657,341 t E1-6 305-5 12; 13  3.66%  -1.43%  -7.83 % E1-6 305-5 12; 13 Percentage of direct GHG emissions (scope 1)  from regulated emission trading schemes  EU-ETS  35.9%  34.4% E1-6 305-5 12; 13 Gross indirect GHG emissions (scope 2) -  market-based 802,233 t 856,282 t 1,088,365 t E1-6 305-5 12; 13 Change in indirect GHG emissions (scope 2) -  market based -54,049 t -232,083 t -317,677 t E1-6 305-5 12; 13 Percentage of indirect GHG emissions (scope  2) from contractual instruments  69.7%  68.2% E1-6 305-5 12; 13 GHG emissions (scope 1+2) - market based 8,709,725 t 8,484,924 t 8,827,350 t E1-6 305-1 &amp; 2 12; 13 Change in total GHG emissions (scope 1+2) -  market based 224,801 t -342,426 t -975,018 t E1-6 305-5 12; 13 Other indirect GHG emissions (scope 3) -  Group (1) 78,371,616 t 79,403,867 t 81,859,656 t E1-6 30