 t 49,437 t E5-5 306-3 9; 12; 13 Percentage of waste directed to disposal  (inc. energy valorization)  33.3%  35.6% E5-5 306-3 9; 12; 13 Packaging Rate of recyclable packaging  91.3%  87.0%  94.6% E5-5 301-1 9; 12; 13 BIODIVERSITY 2025 2024 2023 ESRS DR GRI SDG Percentage of the Group’s active quarries  with a biodiversity management plan  35.0%  28.0%  27.0% E4 304-1 15 Group quarries in protected areas 15 20 E4 304-1 15 Number of industrial sites in protected  areas 45 56 E4 304-1 15 A COMMITTED GROUP MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 151 3.4 A better living for all 3.4.1 GOVERNANCE AND STRATEGY A – Governance A COMMITTED GROUP A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL 152 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com The Senior Vice President, Human Resources and Corporate Social  Responsibility is in charge of corporate social responsibility. She  oversees the incorporation of ESG issues into strategy, coordinates  the CSR roadmap, and ratifies the objectives The Human Resources Committee includes the directors  of the support functions (Social Affairs, Training, Group Talent  and Executive Career Management) and the main human resources  directors of the regions (see section 3.4.2, p. 154). Countries  ■ implement the Group’s policies and major commitments; ■ implement locally social dialog, the salary policy, and action plans  linked to the human resources policy. The organizational chart for governance of CSR issues  by the Board of Directors and the Executive Committee  is defined in section 3.1.4.A, p. 113. The Corporate Social Responsibility Department’s role  is defined in section 3.1.4.A, p. 113. The Health Department and the Industrial Safety and Hygiene Department  ■ lead the management of health and safety issues; ■ develop standards and protocols for medical surveillance  to guarantee a high level of protection and medical monitoring; ■ provide support and expertise in analyzing employees’ exposure  to risks.  Countries ■ implement the Group’s policies and major commitments; ■ implement locally social dialog, the salary policy, and action plans  linked to the human resources policy. A COMMITTED GROUP A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 153 The Purchasing Department, and in particular the “Responsible  Purchasing” Department, leads the “Responsible Purchasing” program  (see section 3.4.6, p. 175). The Responsible Purchasing Committee is chaired by the Chief  Financial Officer and led by the Director of Responsible Purchasing.  The Senior Vice President, Human Resources and Corporate Social  Responsibility, the Executive Vice President, Technology and Industrial  Performance, are present, as well as representatives from countries. The countries implement the action plans of the “Responsible  Purchasing” program. The Ethics and Compliance Department: ■ promotes the Principles of Conduct and Action; ■ designs and implements ethics and compliance programs, in  particular those relating to compliance with competition law, the  fight against corruption, economic sanctions and export controls,  and the protection of personal data; ■ designs and implements human rights programs in collaboration  with regional and country human resources departments, the  responsible purchasing department and the corporate social  responsibility department; ■ identifies and assesses risks, and proposes policies and procedures; ■ coordinates investigations linked to the Speak-Up professional  whistle-blowing system The Ethics and Compliance Network: ■ assists in risk identification and assessment; ■ disseminates policies and procedures. The countries: ■ implement ethics and compliance policies; ■ deploy the related programs. The Group Chief Operating Officer oversees the Group’s Purchasing  policy including the “Responsible Purchasing” program  The Corporate Secretary supervises ethics and compliance issues B – Strategy The Principles of Conduct and Action (section 1.2.7.E.a,  p. 64) are the Group’s benchmark for business conduct,  in particular the four Principles of Action that govern the  decision-making of all managers and employees in the  performance of their duties. The five Principles of  Conduct are the fundamental values that unite all  employees.  This Saint-Gobain code of ethics is underpinned by the  applicable conventions of the International Labor  Organization (ILO), the International Bill of Human  Rights, the Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises of the  Organization for Economic Co-operation and  Development (OECD), and the OECD Anti-Bribery  Convention.  The implementation of the Principles of Conduct and  Action takes the form of specific commitments and  policies applicable to all entities and employees in their  work for Saint-Gobain, including dialog with stakeholders.  In particular, the signing of the supplier policy by the  partners shows their commitment to the Group’s values  and their desire to develop medium- and long-term  partnerships. Suppliers and subcontractors commit not  only to ethical business practices but also to respect for  their employees’ rights, workplace health and safety  compliance, and respect for individuals.  Whether industrial sites, logistics platforms or builders’  merchants, Saint-Gobain’s operations (see value chain  diagram, section 1.2.5, p. 53) directly or indirectly impact  the lives of individuals and communities.  In particular, industrial sites, and the sites of Group  customers or suppliers, present exposure to specific risks  for individuals and communities owing to the raw  materials used and stored, the industrial processes  followed, or the circulation of goods, raw materials or  finished products. Risk prevention and accident  monitoring measures are deployed wherever the Group  operates (see section 1.2.7.F.a, p. 67). 3.4.2 CREATE A HEALTHY, SAFE AND ENGAGING  WORK ENVIRONMENT Saint-Gobain’s Human Resources (HR) policy ensures the  provision of an environment that is conducive to the  employee’s professional and personal growth and  balances job-related performance with their well-being.  This policy requires the compulsory buy-in of all  employees for the Group’s values as expressed in its  code of ethics, the Principles of Conduct and Action.  Saint-Gobain’s Human Resources policy must allow  for  the rapid adaptation of the organization and, in  particular, careful management of changes in skills  requirements, support for employees in the face of  major transformations, as well as attraction and retention  of talent. To do so, it incorporates two dimensions: ■ global coordination tasked with defining a common  framework for all Group employees, incorporating  ethical values, respect for human rights, the  deployment of managerial principles based on trust,  empowerment and collaboration, and the offering  of training programs to better handle major cultural  and market transformations, the establishment of  ambitious and demanding health and safety  objectives, social protection, diversity or any other  subject that brings about decent working  conditions for all; ■ local implementation, by the HR teams in charge of  social dialog, wage policy, local adaptation and  implementation of action plans serving to achieve  Saint-Gobain’s objectives following a philosophy of  subsidiarity and close proximity. The new organization structure implemented in  July  2025 further strengthens this balance between  the global and local dimensions.  Each country implements Saint- Gobain’s policies and  major commitments (health, safety, diversity, etc.) by  adapting the action plans, stages of attainment and  tools to specific factors in the country (regulatory,  cultural, etc.), without compromising on the expected  performance level or the Group’s values. HR policies  are disseminated by the global HR network. The local  teams have guides to support their deployment, and  special training sessions are organized by the HR  Academy. Practical guides and training provided by  the global support teams are available for the  following topics in particular: social dialog, diversity,  remuneration and benefits, talent management,  recruitment and integration, and mobility. The Human Resources Committee meets every month  under the presidency of the Senior Vice President,  Human Resources and Corporate Social Responsibility. It  brings together the directors of the HR support  functions, in particular the Director of Social Affairs and  the Director of Group Talent and Executive Career  Management, and the HR directors of the various  regions. These meetings ensure the regular monitoring of  local and global action plans and the analysis of key  HR indicators. In addition, when companies are acquired, coordination  takes place in order to align the HR policies of the new  entities with the Group’s principles. Newly integrated  entities are monitored centrally to ensure that the  policies applied correspond to those disseminated by  the Group and applied within the network. A COMMITTED GROUP A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL 154 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com The success of this policy and the quality of social dialog  are measured each year in a survey of all employees (see  section 1.2.7.E.b, p.  66). The proportion of employees  responding to the survey (88%, or 127,311 people in 2025),  their satisfaction with working conditions (for 85% of  respondents, working conditions are good), and their  confidence in both the strategy and its implementation  (88% of respondents are confident about the Group’s  future), are signs of a strong commitment that reinforces  the Group’s choice of a balance between local and global  dimensions (see section 1.2.7.B, p. 62).  Saint-Gobain also submits its human resources practices  each year to the Top Employers Institute, an independent  organization that evaluates human resources and ethical  performance on the basis of an evaluation questionnaire  followed by audits of practices. For the 11th year in a row,  the Group was ranked among the 17 companies  recognized worldwide. Saint-Gobain is also recognized as  a “Top Employer” locally in some 40 countries. A – Engaging working conditions a. Wage policy and employee benefits Wage policies are set by the countries in line with market  conditions. In most of the countries in which  Saint-Gobain operates, minimum wages are set either  within the legal framework or by negotiating collective  bargaining agreements.  A general Framework for Fair Wages sets out common  rules and principles to ensure fair remuneration for  Saint-Gobain employees. It has been circulated within  the HR network and is published on the Group’s website. Framework Framework for Fair Wages Purpose Set common rules and principles  to ensure fair remuneration Scope of application All employees Under the supervision of Senior Vice President, Human  Resources and Corporate Social  Responsibility Link to the framework Framework for Fair Wages In an uncertain economic climate in some countries,  salary negotiations have taken into account the impact  of inflation on the lowest salaries. Saint-Gobain continued its work on decent wages. Given  the disparity in local regulations, particularly with regard  to the quantification and characterization of hours of  absence, comparisons between the fixed annual salary  received by an employee (sometimes including hours of  absence) and an annualized decent wage threshold for  full-time work can sometimes be complicated,  depending on the country. In 2025, 99.8% of the Group’s  employees received fixed compensation above the  decent wage.  In addition to the fixed salary, additional elements of  remuneration, such as individual and group bonuses or  employee benefits, are defined by the countries.  Saint-Gobain also seeks to offer its employees social  guarantees enabling them to protect themselves against  the uncertainties of life. Launched on January  1, 2020,  “CARE by Saint-Gobain” is a social protection program  for all Group employees and their families. The coverage  is defined to meet basic daily healthcare needs but also  to support key moments of family life: ■ daily medical monitoring of families and access to  care, by covering health costs (doctor visits or basic  hospitalization) at a rate of at least 80%; ■ the birth of a child, including adoption procedures, by  paying at least 14 weeks of maternity leave with full  pay and three days for the partner leave with full pay; ■ fatality, by providing the family with financial capital  representing at least one year of the employee’s  salary. The program has been rolled out country by country.  Parenthood measures were rolled out in 2020, and all  Saint-Gobain employees currently enjoy minimum  guaranteed parental leave at their full salaries.  Since December 2022, 100% of Saint-Gobain employees  and their families have enjoyed the full benefits of the  “CARE by Saint -Gobain” program. Newly acquired  entities have three years to finalize the roll-out of the  program. Lastly, the majority of the Group’s employees benefit  from locally defined programs that guarantee them a  pension at the end of their career (99% in 2022). At the same time, employee shareholding offers  employees the option to become shareholders under  preferential conditions, either directly as individuals or  via involvement in a dedicated collective fund. The  Group Savings Plan (PEG) enables them to acquire  Saint-Gobain shares at a discount and, in some countries,  an additional amount. In France, to encourage a team  spirit and to associate each person with the success of  the Group, Saint -Gobain favors the conclusion of  collective incentive agreements. In 2025, employee  shareholding programs were offered in 57 (53 in 2024)  countries. In all, 92% (94% in 2024) of Saint -Gobain  employees have access to these programs and, in certain  countries, retirees are also eligible. In addition to these  programs, Saint -Gobain has set up long-term  compensation plans including, depending on the year,  stock options, performance shares and performance  units (see section 4.2.4, p. 276). A COMMITTED GROUP A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 155 b. Social dialog Saint-Gobain has made the quality of social dialog an  essential criterion for the performance of its HR policy.  The principle of consultation and negotiation, either  directly with employees or through their representatives,  is universal, with the aim of translating this dialog into  collective agreements. Freedom of association is one of  the values embedded in the Group’s code of ethics and  respect for it is a prerequisite for quality social dialog. Because social dialog must provide concrete answers to  the questions of working conditions, the specific  expectations of employees, and the deployment of HR  action plans, it is mainly carried out at local level in  accordance with Saint-Gobain’s HR policy (see section  1.2.7.E.b, p.  66). This is why Saint -Gobain favors local  agreements. Executives in each country meet with  employee representatives periodically to discuss  strategy and local issues. More than 3,201 (3,120 in 2024)  agreements signed with employee representative bodies  are thus active, of which 26% (25.2% in 2024) include  criteria related to employee health and safety.  Saint-Gobain has not put in place any worldwide  agreements at this time.  The various discussions held locally have made it  possible – in some subsidiaries – to develop work  flexibility, in particular to meet employees’ demands for  a better balance between their personal and professional  life and their family needs. Even though Saint-Gobain encourages social dialog at a  local level, Group coordination exists. The Chief Executive  Officer of Saint -Gobain meets with the central union  coordinators several times a year and chairs the Group  Works Council in France at least twice a year. At the  European level, he chairs the Convention for Social  Dialog, which brings together 70 union representatives  from 28 European countries annually. With the aid of an  independent expert, this Convention makes it possible to  supplement national dialog by addressing subjects of  common interest such as safety, corporate social  responsibility and the trend of employment on European  sites. These subjects are raised in particular by the  members of the Select Committee, which acts as  spokesman for the Convention, who benefit from specific  training to perform their role and are brought together  by the Director of Social Affairs at least four times a year.  Listening attentively to employees is essential to the  success of social dialog. The employee satisfaction  survey conducted in 2025 (see section 1.2.7.E.b, p.  66)  shows that 85% of respondents consider their working  conditions to be good and 84% find their work/life  balance satisfactory.  In an uncertain economic context, the Group is  committed, as far as possible, to implementing solutions  to safeguard employment and to making job cuts only as  a last resort. The aim is initially to reorganize to deal with  situations on a temporary basis, as in the case of  temporary lay-off, or to favor internal occupational  mobility agreements, which, associated with incentive  measures, make it possible to maintain jobs at  Saint-Gobain. When restructuring is inevitable, the  employees affected by workforce adjustments benefit  from personalized support programs, which may result in  training associated with retraining, assistance for  geographic mobility or support for the execution of a  personal project, such as the creation of a business. When companies are acquired, coordination is set up to  align the HR policy of the new entities with the Group’s  principles. c. Protecting employees’ health  and safety  The health and safety of employees are absolute  priorities, and Saint -Gobain makes them central to its  corporate culture. It is important for everyone to play an  active role in their own health and safety, and those of all  their colleagues. Employee health  In terms of health and well-being, Saint-Gobain adopted  a policy in 2013 and updated it in early 2022. The policy  sets out the guidelines for action to protect the health  and promote the well-being of the Group’s employees,  customers and suppliers, users of its products, as well  as  residents living near its sites. The action plans  focused  on external stakeholders are described in  section 3.4.1.B, p. 154. Policy Health Policy Purpose Establish health and safety  requirements for Saint-Gobain’s  employees, its customers  and users of its products  and solutions, and its neighbors  and communities around  the Group’s sites Scope of application All entities Under the supervision of Senior Vice President, Human  Resources and Corporate Social  Responsibility Link to the policy Health Policy The employee health risk management system covers  all  employees regardless of the type of contract,  temporary workers and subcontractors on site  (see section 3.1.4.B, p. 114). To ensure the same level of protection and medical care  for all its employees worldwide, the Group has  established mandatory standards, medical monitoring  protocols and recommendations for health and industrial  hygiene. These are supplemented by specific standards  for certain operations, guides and tools for deployment. All Saint -Gobain sites worldwide must implement the  health policy. The actions implemented by each site are  prioritized on the basis of risk assessments in  accordance with the “risk assessment and control”  standard. A Health Indicator for Occupational Exposure  (HICE) is monitored at Group level, allowing  performance to be monitored in terms of the risks of  exposure to noise and chemical agents.  A COMMITTED GROUP A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL 156 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com Countries develop prevention and health promotion  programs for employees. In Brazil, for example, the “Bem  me quero” program consists of actions to raise  awareness of chronic diseases, cancer prevention  campaigns, and support for young mothers, as well as  more individual actions to combat sedentary lifestyles  and advice for healthier eating. This program also  focuses on mental health and psychological support. In  the United States, the “Livewell” program supports  employees regarding their physical and mental health  and also helps control healthcare costs. Lastly, the  “#jeprendssoindemoi” program in France harnesses  digital tools and videos to raise employee awareness,  without making them feel guilty, of physical and mental  well-being.  The “CARE by Saint-Gobain” program (see section  3.4.2.A.a, p.  155) provides access to care and social  protection for all employees and their families. Recommendations concerning the organization of first  aid for cardiac arrest and equipping entities with  automatic external defibrillators have been circulated. &gt; Measuring exposure to health risks  Saint-Gobain has defined a risk control indicator for the  health of its staff relating to its operations in line with its  industrial health and hygiene standards.  Following the HICE (Health Indicator for Chronic  Exposure) indicator targeting noise exposure “HICE  Noise”, created in 2017, a new indicator dedicated to  exposure to chemical agents has been calculated since  early 2023, “HICE Chemicals”. This health indicator is  applied to all sites affected by exposure to noise and/or  chemical agents. It corresponds to the degree of  potential exposure to these health hazards and aims to  promote the prioritization of actions to reduce risks of  exposure for employees on the sites. The monitoring of indicators is presented to the Group  Executive Committee on an annual basis. At the end of  2025, the HICE Noise indicator stood at 10.5, which  means that, on average, at each site, 10.5% of the work  situations of employees and permanent subcontractors  are subject to potential noise exposure. PPE is not taken  into account in this figure. HICE Noise was equal to  12.9 in 2024. The “HICE Chemicals” indicator also tracks the progress  that remains to be made in assessing and reducing  chemical exposures at each site. HICE is the percentage  of potential exposure to hazardous substances that  should be reduced. In 2025, 50% of sites had their risk  assessment validated by a trained person in accordance  with the Saint -Gobain requirements listed in the “Risk  assessment and management” standard. For these sites,  the HICE indicator was 15, meaning that on average, on  each site, 15% of the work situations of employees and  permanent subcontractors are potentially exposed to  chemical substances. PPE is not taken into account in  this figure. For the remaining 51% of sites that need to  start or improve their risk assessment, a default  maximum exposure of 100% is considered. &gt; Risks associated with hazardous  substances and products Saint-Gobain is committed to reducing and controlling  chemical risks (hazardous substances and products,  dust). Three further tools have been developed to  support the sites in managing chemical risks: ■ the internal standard and its implementation guide on  the assessment and control of the risk linked to  chemical agents provides guidance to industrial sites  on how to conduct a periodic assessment of exposure  to hazardous substances according to precise  minimum rules. It is supplemented by risk management  guides (ventilation and personal protective equipment,  examples of chemical management and storage  procedures, activity-specific product inventories, etc.)  as well as training kits; ■ the SBASE database provides a list of chemical  substances and their classification, according to their  level of danger on the basis of an internal  classification system. This database is updated on an  ongoing basis by internal and external experts in  response to changes in the classification of the  various regulatory frameworks, such as REACH in  Europe; ■ inventory tools enable each industrial site to prepare  and document its own inventory of the chemical  substances and products used and also potentially  generated during industrial production processes.  The  results of the inventories are consolidated in  a  single tool called “SAFHEAR” in order to produce  and document the results of the exposure  assessments by site. The inventory of products and substances used by  Saint-Gobain entities is a continuous improvement  process. Newly acquired entities are supported so as to  align their practices with those of the Group. At the end  of 2025, 97% of sites updated their inventory, and 50%  completed and validated their risk assessment. Saint-Gobain actively monitors updates to the European  list of candidate substances for the authorization or  subject to authorization or restriction and anticipates the  deadlines for authorization of substances, in order to  fulfill its substitution and communication obligations with  its customers. In countries outside the European Union, subject to other  chemical regulations such as the US “Toxic Substance  Control Act,” the Environmental Protection Act and the  Chemicals Management Plan in Canada, or “China  REACH” in China, the Group applies the regulations in  force and monitors their evolution. This cross-functional control of the management of  chemical substances and products is also part of the  product innovation and stakeholder information  processes, particularly for consumers. A COMMITTED GROUP A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 157 &gt; Managing the risks  of musculoskeletal disorders A standard for assessing factors that may contribute to  the occurrence of musculoskeletal disorders and a grid  for detecting ergonomic factors were developed in 2021.  The ergonomics standard lists the risk factors to be  assessed and specifies the method for assessing these  risks, in particular on the basis of international standards  (for example, EN ISO 14738:2008 and ISO 11228-3). The  detection checklist is an easy-to-use tool that lists the  criteria to be assessed. It aims to identify improvements  to be made to a work situation through a before/ after rating. The Group has a set of information and precautions as to  the use of exoskeletons. A dedicated platform brings  together the results of the work and recommendations  of the INRS, the French national research and safety  institute, and the IFA (the institute for occupational  health and safety of the German social insurance body  covering accidents) to assess the use of exoskeletons for  the prevention of musculoskeletal disorders (MSDs).  &gt; Monitoring occupational illnesses  Occupational illnesses recognized by the health  authorities of each country are recorded in the  EHS report. The processes for declaring and recognizing  occupational diseases are not harmonized at a global  level and therefore depend on regional legislation and  processes in which the Group cannot interfere. It should  be noted that the most serious occupational illnesses are  linked to past exposure, often dating back some time,  and that knowledge about it is of little use for prevention,  even if each case reported is examined to this end.  Details of the reporting procedures are presented in the  note on methodology (see section 3.7.1.B, p. 206). Well-being at work Saint-Gobain attaches fundamental importance not just  to the physical health but to the mental health of its  employees, as outlined in the Group’s new health policy.  The Mental WellBeing (MWB) program has been rolled  out in all countries. It is structured around six steps:  ■ employee training in mental health: the Mental Health  Academy is an e-learning program made available to  managers and employees. The program consists of  11 short and independent videos covering topics such  as the role of the manager and the employee,  detection, measurement and factors in psychological  well-being, action plans, the challenges and the  Group’s ambition. The detection of early warning  signs of mental health problems is facilitated so that  the commensurate resources can be provided. The  Mental Health Academy encourages employees to  take care of themselves and managers to take care of  their teams by adopting good practices and teaching  them to recognize the early signs of trouble; ■ prevention and the application of best practices:  managers have access to an interactive application to  optimize the psychological well-being of their teams.  Each manager can build and monitor a tailored  program and exchange it with other managers or share  relevant best practices drawn from their experience.  There are six action areas: management practices,  change management, interpersonal environment,  physical working environment, work/life balance, and  personal well-being skills. This interactive portal is  above all aimed at primary prevention practices and  proposes best practices, collects those identified by  teams worldwide and provides key information so that  each team can implement them. This approach is  linked to the “human resources” pillar of the “WCM”  excellence program (see section 1.2.7.F.a, p. 67); ■ assessment of well-being: the annual  me@Saint-Gobain survey (see section 1.2.7.E.b, p. 66)  includes specific questions serving to measure  psychological well-being at the team level. Managers  have access to their team’s results; ■ an alert system: via the Speak-up system (see section  3.4.5.C, p. 174), employees can report a situation that  they consider critical in their work and which affects  their mental health; ■ psychological support: to support them in any  difficulties they may encounter at work and outside  work, around 80% of employees can benefit from  psychological support provided by the country in  complete confidentiality. This individual care can be  provided by on-site psychologists, specialized  external firms, or in-house medical teams; ■ the monitoring of a well-being indicator: each year,  the results of the me@saint-Gobain survey include the  establishment of a Group well-being index. In 2025,  this index came out 85%, reflecting a working  environment that is highly favorable to well-being  at work. Workplace safety  Saint-Gobain ensures that all employees on its sites,  including temporary workers and subcontractors, have  safe working conditions and environments by  identifying, reducing and controlling risks. To support newly-acquired sites in their integration  process, a checklist of the requirements to be met within  six months, then two years, is made available to EHS  managers and project managers in charge of integration.  Requirements may be stricter depending on the level of  EHS maturity of the site to be integrated. Each  integrated site is required to reach at least “beginner”  level when evaluating its management system with the  Saint-Gobain Mini-ISA audit checklist within two years of  the integration period.  A COMMITTED GROUP A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL 158 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com Saint-Gobain’s safety commitment applies not just to its  employees and temporary staff but to on-site  subcontractors. A training and awareness-raising course  on specific risks related to the presence of on-site  subcontractors is offered to the teams of the sites. The  buyer network is also involved in training and informing  subcontractors on the security standards to be put in  place and respected.  Over the last three years, a special program on the  control of work situations that could lead to a serious or  even fatal accident has been deployed at the sites.  Entitled “Severity first”, the program takes the form of  e-learning courses and structures safety-related  communication.  The four pillars of this approach are:  ■ compliance with the golden rules to be drafted with  staff corresponding as closely as possible to local  realities;  ■ the reporting of potentially serious injuries, i.e.  accidental events that did not have serious  consequences but could have had them under similar  conditions; ■ compliance with the standards of the Group or an  entity, if the latter has specific standards; ■ the roll-out of communication campaigns focused on  two topics, “Dare to intervene” and “Power to stop”,  which gives each employee the right to stop a task if  they consider it dangerous. In 2025, the reporting of potentially serious injuries was  made mandatory. The information collected makes it  possible to anticipate potentially serious events and is  shared widely across the organization, leading, where  necessary, to the creation or updating of security  standards.  80% of the entities reported no occupational accidents,  compared with 78% in 2024. The “Millionaires Club”  comprises the most exemplary Group sites in terms of  safety, with 1 million hours worked or five years without  any accidents involving lost time for any of the  individuals present on a site (employees, temporary  workers, subcontractors, visitors, etc.). At the end of  2025, a total of 244 sites were in the “Millionaires Club,”  compared with 233 at the end of 2024. The Club  highlights the entities achieving the best results and  demonstrating that the objective of zero work-related  accidents is possible. In 2025, 88 (92 in 2024) of  “Millionaires Club” sites were “Silver Millionaires” (10 years  without a lost-time accident) and 70 (62 in 2024) were  “Gold Millionaires” (15 years without a lost-time accident).  At the highest level, the management has demonstrated  its involvement in the development of a culture of safety  within the Group. All the meetings of the Board of  Directors and the Executive Committee include a safety  performance review. Operational management is  responsible everywhere and guarantees all aspects of  safety: objectives, action plans and performance  measurement. Unfortunately, in 2025, the Group reported five fatal  work-related accidents, though the number of fatal  accidents on site has been steadily reduced over the  past 15 years to reach zero fatal accidents by 2024. A  reminder was sent to all Group managers to remain  vigilant. This message was relayed at the highest level  during a live chat dedicated to these events and hosted  by Benoit Bazin, Chairman and CEO of Saint-Gobain. In the same year as these dramatic events, the TRAR  (total recordable accident rate with and without lost  time – employees, temporary workers and permanent  subcontractors) remained unchanged in 2025 relative to  2024 at a low level of 1.4. Since 2017, the TRAR has been  halved. To strengthen the control of critical situations on a  lasting basis and at all levels of the organization, the  Group has decided to further strengthen the monitoring  of the number of High Consequences Injuries (HCIs),  applying from the beginning of 2026. HCIs are events  having resulted in serious consequences both in terms of  injury severity and recovery time, including fatal  accidents. The concept of HCI adds a human dimension  related to the management of the consequences of the  accident for the victim to the monitoring of safety at  work. In addition, the EHS management system has been  strengthened for 2026, both in terms of training systems  and site audit processes.  For many years, an occupational safety performance  indicator has been included in individual or collective  variable remuneration schemes. HCI-related  performance will be included in the performance  evaluation criteria for 2026.  All these measures are consistent with Saint-Gobain’s  determination to make the lasting absence of a fatal  accident a priority objective. A COMMITTED GROUP A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 159 A COMMITTED GROUP A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL 160 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com B – Diversity and inclusion  Diversifying its teams ensures that Saint-Gobain is in tune  with the world around it, understands its challenges, and  is enriched by different skills and experience, while  developing its capacity to innovate. To meet its diversity  and inclusion targets, the Group is working to create an  environment conducive to fairness and equality, crucial to  employees’ professional growth, while fostering training  and the cohesion of high-performance operational teams.  The main drivers of this strategy are managers that lead  by example and the policy of equal treatment in  recruitment, vocational training and remuneration.  Everywhere it operates, Saint -Gobain undertakes to  promote inclusion and diversity in all its forms: gender,  nationalities, training, career paths, generational diversity,  disabilities and ethnic and social origins. In addition, employees are asked a specific question on  diversity and inclusion as part of the annual  “me@Saint-Gobain” survey. In 2025, more than 88% of  employees said that people were respected in the  company regardless of their profile (gender, age, origin,  sexual orientation, etc.). The Group is committed to the themes of diversity and  inclusion. The requirements to be followed are defined at  the global level, while action plans to support this vision  are rolled out locally. An overall diversity indicator  incorporating diversity of gender, nationality and  professional experience is monitored annually. It is a  performance component of Saint -Gobain’s long-term  remuneration plans for managers (see section 4.2.4,  p. 276). The Group is committed to maintaining a diversity  index above 90% at all times. This remained the case in  2025, with a score of 92.8% (see section 3.4.4, p. 164). The increase in the gender diversity of teams is based on  a recruitment policy and on action plans for occupational  promotion, equal pay, training and work/life balance. In  2025, the gender diversity target was updated for the  period 2025 to 2030 and set at 33% of female  executives in 2030. This target is in line with previous  targets (25% target achieved in 2020 and 30% in 2025). The assessment of pay gaps between women and men  in equivalent positions is carried out systematically. In  2025, women receive a base salary that is 4% lower than  that of men. The ratios for average pay gaps among  Group employees are measured and published in section  3.4.4, p. 164. All diversity objectives (see section 3.4.4, p.  164) are  monitored quarterly by the Executive Committee. They  have been broken down by country and sector and are  included in the performance criteria determining the  annual variable compensation of senior executives. At  the Group level, each monthly meeting of the Human  Resources Committee includes a section dedicated to  women, with the aim of boosting career opportunities. These objectives were strengthened in 2020 to promote  the access of women to the management bodies of  Saint-Gobain: ■ 30% of women in the Group Executive Committee in  2025; ■ an average of 30% of women in the Executive  Committees of Business Units in 2025. These objectives were achieved in 2025. With seven  women out of 16 members, representing 44% of the  total, the Group’s Executive Committee is now close to  gender parity.  In terms of training, an e-learning entitled “Gender  Balance Awareness”, helps raise awareness and  therefore combat unconscious biases related to diversity  in recruitment, teamwork, and career planning. Programs  to raise team awareness of diversity are also being  rolled out.  Lastly, in 2020, the parenting component of the “CARE  by Saint-Gobain” social protection program (see section  3.4.2.A.a, p. 155) was rolled out in all the countries where  the Group operates, with a guaranteed minimum of  14 weeks of maternity leave at full pay. Saint-Gobain has been a signatory to the Women’s  Empowerment Principles (WEPs) since 2021, confirming  its commitment to gender equality. Defined by the  United Nations Global Compact, the WEPs are a set of  principles that aim to help companies promote gender  equality and women’s empowerment in the workplace. Likewise, the insertion and retention of people with  disabilities are important subjects for Saint-Gobain. First  of all, disability awareness-raising and training initiatives  are in place in the various countries. Several subsidiaries  offer workshops in partnership with specialized bodies.  The Group also implements recruitment policies in  partnership with various specialist agencies, applying a  strict non-discrimination policy. For example, in France,  several entities are in contact with ESATs (bodies  promoting the inclusion of people with disabilities in  society and the workplace) to support them in their  efforts. One of Saint -Gobain’s objectives is to make  everyday life easier for employees with disabilities by  adapting workstations and hours. To develop a culture of inclusion, Saint-Gobain University  has provided an e-learning program for managers on  inclusive management. Saint -Gobain has also  contributed, along with other major French companies,  to the production of a glossary in French and English  which provides definitions and explanations of the main  terms and concepts relating to diversity and inclusion.  Each region or country establishes a diagnosis which  then enables it to set priorities and local plans. Potential  types of discrimination are identified, and appropriate  programs are put in place. Gender, disability, and ethnic  and social origins are taken into consideration in the  assessment. A COMMITTED GROUP A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 161 To encourage pluridisciplinarity and the diversity of  nationalities, the emphasis is on the valuing of diverse  career paths in the areas of expertise (marketing,  research and development, etc.) and on equal  opportunities for local profiles. In terms of generational  diversity, Saint-Gobain ensures that the age pyramid of  its employees is balanced, giving young people as well  as older employees their full place. Particular attention is  paid to integration programs for young people. Since 2024, the DEI Lab Accelerator, a centralized  working group, has been working to accelerate diversity,  equity, and inclusion (DEI) initiatives. This laboratory  brings together managers who are committed to these  issues. Its objectives are to define new ambitions and  test innovative practices in DEI, engage top  management and obtain an overview of diversity across  continents, promote actions that foster DEI culture, and  coordinate these initiatives at the Group level.  Among the initiatives undertaken are the rollout of the  DEI Initiatives World Map tool, which lists more than 120  DEI actions across five continents, and the organization  of best practice sharing sessions to limit cognitive bias.  Recruitment practices are evolving, with greater  emphasis being placed on diversity and work-life  balance. The DEI Lab Accelerator’s actions help attract  and retain talent, increase innovation and creativity, and  reduce bias in favor of more inclusive behaviors. 3.4.3 COMMUNITY ENGAGEMENT The communities affected by Saint-Gobain’s operations  can be divided into three categories: ■ local communities that are geographically close to  Saint-Gobain sites, including residents in the vicinity of  industrial sites; ■ fragile populations, including those living in poverty,  migrants and refugees, women, and other minorities;  ■ indigenous populations (see section 3.4.5.A, p. 172).  Dialog with local stakeholders is ensured by the country  platforms (see section 1.2.5, p. 53). Communities near the  Group’s industrial sites are exposed to specific risks  associated with the safety of the facilities, in particular  the risks of accidental pollution or industrial accidents.  The risk prevention program is described in section 1.2.7  Industrial and distribution risk prevention policy, p. 60.  Each country is responsible for its own community  support program. In order to consolidate commitments  in line with the Group’s strategy, the “Build Change”  program identifies actions aimed at: ■ support access to decent housing; ■ train a new generation of responsible builders; ■ work towards sustainable and responsible territories This program encourages young people, especially the  most disadvantaged, to join the construction industry,  which in many countries is facing labor shortages. Similarly, there is a chronic lack of decent housing for the  most disadvantaged populations. Improving access to  insulated housing also allows these vulnerable  populations to reduce their energy bills and improve  their comfort. With regard to citizen engagement in local democracy,  the Group does not have reporting systems in place that  enable it to determine the political commitments of its  employees. It has not applied for the “local democracy  partner employer” label. In France, Saint-Gobain  has supported the national military reserve policy since  2017 and renewed its partnership with the National  Guard in 2025.  A – Supporting vulnerable  populations Saint-Gobain’s organization is decentralized and each  entity defines its own programs and initiatives for  vulnerable populations. Depending on circumstances,  these initiatives can be grouped into local Foundations  such as in the United States and India, or engagement  programs, as in Brazil and South Africa.  At central level, the mission of the Saint -Gobain  Foundation is to help finance solidarity initiatives  sponsored by Group employees and associations. These  philanthropic projects must be aimed at disadvantaged  populations and focus on: ■ the professional integration of people in difficulty; ■ the construction, improvement or renovation, in the  general interest, of living spaces for people in  precarious situations, contributing to sustainable  construction. The Saint-Gobain Foundation provides direct financial  support to selected projects, thanks to a €7  million  endowment for the years 2025, 2026, and 2027. At the  same time, employee sponsors facilitate access  associations’ to additional partnerships involving the  donation of materials, access to technical expertise,  particularly as regards energy efficiency, and the  organization of volunteer work. In 2025, 44 projects in  13  countries where Saint-Gobain operates were  supported by the Saint-Gobain Foundation. A COMMITTED GROUP A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL 162 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com Some countries have also set up Foundations to support  local initiatives, particularly in the USA, India and Poland.  In India, the Saint -Gobain India Foundation supports  more than 9,500 young girls in targeted education  programs. Saint-Gobain has also developed programs to  accompany young people from rural areas. In emergency situations, Saint -Gobain’s local teams  mobilize to support the populations affected. For  example, since the beginning of the conflict,  Saint-Gobain teams in Poland have continued to mobilize  to help Ukrainians.  Countries are also developing programs for local  populations in local communities. In 2025, Saint-Gobain,  both centrally and in the countries, gave more than  €21  million, in the form of financial donations or  donations of materials. B – Training for the construction  business lines to accelerate  the transition to sustainable  construction  Training programs for Saint-Gobain’s customers’  employees are developed in most countries where the  Group operates. In France, a complete training catalog,  via e -learning or face-to-face, is available for all  customers of Saint-Gobain. The Isover, Placo® and Weber  teams offer programs led by experts trained in teaching  methods. Some of the courses are certifiable. In Brazil, a  complete offer is deployed. In some countries, the  Group’s teams travel to worksites. This is the case for  Weber teams in France and certain training courses in  Morocco. Saint-Gobain PAM has also set up a “customer school”  offering a range of training courses on the water and  wastewater business lines. These courses can be given  on site.  Saint-Gobain UK &amp; Ireland supports Barnardo’s  YouthBuild Academy, which aims to train unemployed  young people in building trades at a time when the  construction industry is facing labor shortages. Efforts are being made in South Africa to overcome the  shortage of skills in roofing and partition installation  through the “Saint -Gobain YouthBuild Academy.” This  training program, launched in 2003 and financially  supported since 2016 by YouthBuild International, aims  to share the know-how of Group employees with  unemployed young people from disadvantaged  communities, through a combination of theoretical  courses and the achievement of a local renovation  project. The “Saint -Gobain YouthBuild Academy” is  currently the only training provider accredited by CETA  (Construction Education Training Authority) to issue the  National Certificate: Ceiling &amp; Partition Installation  NQF  3. The Group supports the sustainability of youth  employment while contributing to the dynamism of its  business sector. Since its creation, about 1,000 people  have benefited from this program. Since November 2021, Saint -Gobain in Morocco has  developed its own academy with the same objective of  training young people in the building trades and in  particular in the evolution of techniques related to  sustainable construction. Qualifying training courses are  provided at three training centers.  In France, since 2022, Saint -Gobain has developed a  network of schools that train qualified workers for jobs in  the construction sector, where personnel supply is short.  The specialized brands have formed partnerships with  apprentice training centers to develop professional  training courses, such as the roofing school for roofers  set up by Asturienne, the builder’s school set up by  Point.P, the thermal engineering school, 19 °C, set up by  CEDEO, and the CARRE school opened by SFIC in 2024.  Training programs are deployed region by region. By the  end of 2025, over 115 classes were open and over  1,000  apprentices recruited. The target is to train  2,500 craftsmen by 2028.  Several professional training programs are on offer in  India. For example, the Gyproc Academy has trained  more than 1,150 young people since its creation in 2013,  with a placement rate of over 90%. For many years, Saint-Gobain has been supporting craft  trades by partnering Worldskills France, the trades  competition that enables young professionals from all  over the world to showcase their skills. For the 2025  edition, in line with the actions taken to raise awareness,  promote and train future generations of roofers, masons  and plumbers with the Ecole du Toit, L’école des  Bâtisseurs and 19° L’École du Génie Climatique  respectively, the Asturienne, POINT.P and CEDEO  builders’ merchants, like Placo®, have supported building  trades competitors by donating equipment to enable  them to compete in the best possible conditions.  A COMMITTED GROUP A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 163 3.4.4 2025 PERFORMANCE (SOCIAL) OBJECTIVES Deadline 2025 data 2024 data 2023 data Baseline Decrease TRAR to 1.4 own workforce  and sub-contractors (baseline 2017) (1) 2030 1.4 1.4 1.3 2.6 100% of industrial sites covered  by a chemical inventory (2) 2025  97.3 %  91.0 %  87.0 % Exceed employee engagement rate  benchmark every year (&gt;76% in 2025) Annual  83.0 %  84.0 %  83.0 %  76%  100% coverage of the CARE program Annual  100.0 %  100.0 %  100.0 %  —%  33% of women managers by 2030  (30% by 2025) 2030  29.6 %  28.6 %  28.0 %  30%  25% of women senior managers 2025  27.0 %  26.8 %  24.8 %  25%  40% of women managers hired 2025  33.8 %  34.8 %  33.1 %  40%  Maintain diversity index above 90% Annual  92.8 %  91.3 %  91.0 %  90%  100% of countries have a community  assistance program 2025  100.0 %  95.8 %  91.7 % (1) Total recordable accident rate, with and without lost time, per  million hours worked for employees, temporary workers and permanent  subcontractors. (2) See methodology for perimeter details (section 3.7.1.A, p. 147). A COMMITTED GROUP A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL 164 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com HEALTH - SAFETY 2025 2024 2023 ESRS-DR GRI SDG Number of recordable work-related accidents  in own workforce 529 479 439 S1-14 403-9 3; 8 Number of recordable work-related accidents  (employees) 440 395 352 S1-14 403-9 3; 8 Number of recordable work-related accidents  (non-employees) 89 84 87 S1-14 403-9 3; 8 Number of fatal incidents connected with the  work of Saint-Gobain employees 3 0 0 S1-14 403-9 3; 8 Number of fatal incidents connected with the  work of Saint-Gobain non-employees 2 0 2 S1-14 403-9 3; 8 of which connected to sub-contractors 2 0 2 S1-14 403-9 3; 8 of which connected to temporary workers 0 0 0 S1-14 403-9 3; 8 of which connected to third parties 0 0 0 S1-14 403-9 3; 8 Group accident frequency rate (TRAR (1))  (employees, temporary workers and  permanent subcontractors) 1.4 1.4 1.3 S1-14 403-9 3; 8 Group accident frequency rate (TRAR (1))  (employees) 1.5 1.4 1.3 S1-14 403-9 3; 8 Group accident frequency rate (TRAR (1))  (temporary workers &amp; permanent  subcontractors) 1.1 1.1 1.2 S1-14 403-9 3; 8 Total recordable accident rate with lost time of  more than 24 hours (employees, temporary  workers and permanent subcontractors) 0.9 0.8 0.8 S1-14 403-9 3; 8 Accident rate with lost time of more than 24  hours employees 0.9 0.8 S1-14 403-9 3; 8 Accident rate w