university training • Forums in schools • Support for youth development MARKET EMPLOYEES INVESTORS REGULAR  AUTHORITIES AND  PUBLIC AFFAIRS  PARTNERS LOCAL  COMMUNITIES CIVIL SOCIETY B – Incorporating stakeholders’ interests and opinions Dialog with stakeholders is therefore carried out on an ongoing basis at various levels of the Saint-Gobain  organization. The Group’s activities and the deployment of its strategy are mainly local. For this reason, country  platforms (see section 1.2.5, p. 53) play an essential role in the quality of this dialog.  ORGANIZATION OF STAKEHOLDER DIALOG AND SUMMARY OF THEIR INTERESTS AND POINTS OF VIEW: Main stakeholders Dialog and coordination  by countries  (Coordination) Summary  produced by the  functional divisions Interests  and points of view Market Customer: direct  customer, users,  and specifiers The Group’s upstream  and downstream value  chains are mainly local.  Group Operational  Performance Department Sustainable growth Improve productivity,  attractiveness for  the workforce  and incorporate  sustainability issues Trust and reliability Suppliers and  subcontractors, partners Employees Employees (full-time,  part-time), temporary  workers, employee  representatives,  apprentices, interns,  secondary and vocational  education The human resources  organization of each  country is in charge  of social dialog.  The Group HR  Department Working conditions Opportunities Development  and training Investors Shareholders: employees,  institutional and  individual shareholders.  SRI (Socially Responsible  Investment), rating and  ranking agencies Dialog with investors is  conducted only at Group  level. The financial  communications team  is responsible for this. Finance Department Results: financial  performance  and sustainability Trust: transparency  and meeting  commitments Reputation Regulatory  authorities and  public-sector  business  partners Governments, regulators,  intergovernmental  entities, international  organizations (UN, ILO,  etc.), inter-professional  associations, Green  Building Councils The dialog is facilitated  by the country teams  according to an  organization adapted  to local specificities, and  the need for technical  expertise. Support teams  can reinforce local teams  on strategic issues  such as sustainable  construction.  Institutional Affairs  Department  Compliance with  commitments Value sharing:  responsibility  and sustainability  (combating climate  change) Local  communities Local populations  and communities close  to sites The Senior Management  of the country is in  charge of dialog with  local communities and  in particular residents  in the vicinity of sites. Regional management Safety Trust: respecting values Solidarity Civil society NGOs, foundations,  professional associations,  universities, schools,  media Each country determines  the primary contacts in  its teams based on the  subject. In general, the  contacts are incorporated  into HR teams. Communications, HR,  CSR and Sustainable  Development  Departments Clarity in its  commitments  transparency on actions  and achievements Reliability in partnerships PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 48 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.2.4 SUSTAINABILITY CHALLENGES INTEGRATED  INTO STRATEGY A – The materiality of  sustainability challenges Since 2015, Saint-Gobain has regularly carried out  “double materiality” assessments to identify and  prioritize the impacts that are most significant for both  the company and its stakeholders, and  those  with a  major financial impact. The update of the double materiality analysis carried out  in 2024 is based on the ESRS (European Sustainability  Reporting Standards). This change in methodology  has not led to any major changes in the list of material  challenges for Saint-Gobain.  The matrix is a representation made based on the results  of the double materiality analysis (see section 3.1.3,  p.  106). Materiality challenges have been classified  according to the contributions of the CSR roadmap. Sustainability issues were identified based on: ■ Information and knowledge gained from previous  financial years;  ■ Sector-specific reference frameworks specifying the  priority issues linked to construction business lines; ■ The specific features of the Group’s business model  and value chain; ■ Compliance with EU regulations by incorporating  the detailed challenges listed by the ESRS. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 49 The methodology and detailed results  are presented in section 3.1.3, p. 106. B – Contributions  to sustainability issues  For each of the sustainability issues identified, there are  impacts, positive or negative, risks and  opportunities,  particularly growth opportunities. Through the three axes of its CSR roadmap, Saint-Gobain  defines its ambition, including its objectives, and its  action plans to seize opportunities and contribute to  positive impacts while controlling sustainability risks and  its footprint. Growth opportunities are particularly developed under  the “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan (see section 1.2.2, p. 42)  in particular climate change adaptation solutions and the  development of circularity. A DECARBONATED HOME GROWTH AND RESILIENCE Opportunities to outperform markets  and access new markets: ■ Increased competitiveness and an attractive offering,  thanks to the proposal of low-carbon and energy-efficiency  solutions; ■ Growing demand for solutions adapted to climate change  (regulations and user demands), particularly solutions for  adapting to the effects of climate change. Opportunities related to operational  and financial control: ■ Cost reduction and availability of supplies through  industrial processes, innovation in particular, and  operational excellence. CHALLENGES Reduce the negative impact on the environment and society:  ■ Reduce greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions (scope 1, 2 and 3) contributing to climate change and biodiversity loss;  ■ Reduce GHG emissions related to the construction sector.  Adapt resources to the needs of the transition plan: ■ Risk of increased energy costs; ■ Potential need for investment in GHG reduction initiatives. APPROACH Maximize Saint-Gobain’s contribution: Proposing innovative solutions that contribute  to reducing the carbon emissions of the construction  and industrial sectors, including those linked with  building use.  Minimize Saint-Gobain’s footprint:  Achieving net zero emissions (scope 1, 2 and 3)  by 2050. ACTIONS Proposing solutions: ■ Program for sustainable and high-performance solutions;  ■ Assessing the impact of products and solutions on the  value chain. Taking action for sustainability: ■ 2030 carbon roadmap;  ■ Aligning the carbon strategy with the net zero carbon  by 2050 target. OBJECTIVES ■ Net zero emissions (scope 1, 2 and 3) by 2050; ■ Scope 1 &amp; 2 GHG emissions: reduction between 40% and 45% by 2035 (vs. 2017); ■ Scope 3 GHG emissions: 16% reduction by 2030 (vs. 2017). ESRS E1 SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT GOALS: 7; 9; 11; 12; 13 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 50 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS GROWTH AND RESILIENCE Leadership opportunities: ■ Using resources from responsible sources  and limiting the use of non-renewable resources; ■ Thanks to country platforms, creating recycling  channels for construction materials. Opportunities to outperform markets  and access new markets: ■ Growing demand and expectations for products  and systems that are eco-designed and associated  with the development of circularity. Opportunities associated with operational  and financial control: ■ Cost reduction and availability of supplies through  circularity, including local waste collection  and sorting organization; ■ Cost reduction and availability of supplies through  industrial processes and innovation, in particular  the operational excellence program. CHALLENGES  Reduce negative impacts on the environment and society:  ■ Negative consequences of pollution; ■ Pressure related to water consumption; ■ Pressure related to the consumption of extracted resources; ■ Impacts of waste generated by construction, renovation, or demolition activities; ■ Impact of activities on biodiversity (deforestation, extraction, construction). APPROACH Maximize Saint-Gobain’s contribution: Reducing adverse effects on nature by optimizing circular  flows, using more recycled or bio-sourced materials,  and improving the use of buildings. Minimize Saint-Gobain’s footprint:  Limiting pollution, especially water pollution,  and preserving natural resources and biodiversity  along the value chain.  ACTIONS Proposing solutions: ■ Program for sustainable and high-performance solutions;  ■ Innovation program for sustainability; ■ Creation of or participation in the creation of recycling  networks (waste collection and recovery). Taking action for sustainability: ■ Program on the substitution of substances of concern; ■ Application of the Forest, Timber Purchasing and Water  policies and the “Responsible Purchasing” program. OBJECTIVES ■ 80% reduction in non-recovered waste by 2030 (vs. 2017); ■ 50% reduction in water withdrawals by 2030 (vs. 2017); ■ Zero water withdrawal in areas under extremely high water stress; ■ 100% of packaging recyclable by 2030. ESRS E2 to E5 SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT GOALS: 6; 7; 9; 12; 13 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 51 A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL GROWTH AND RESILIENCE  Leadership opportunities: ■ Engaging business partners in the implementation  of sustainable strategies; ■ Positive impacts of commitments to local communities  through local anchoring and the socio-economic  development of territories; ■ Employee well-being along the value chain through fair  treatment, social dialog, inclusion, and diversity. Opportunities associated with operational  and financial control:  ■ Improving employee performance across the value chain  through opportunities and skills development; ■ Developing long-term relationships with suppliers  in the value chain to ensure their stability. Regulation and compliance:  ■ Anticipating regulations and responding to growing market  demand for greater product transparency. CHALLENGES  Reduce the negative impact on society:  ■ Exposing employees and workers in the value chain to physical and psychological problems related to health and safety,  working conditions, inequalities, and human rights;  ■ Exposing customers and end users to health and safety issues; ■ Exposing affected communities to risks of industrial accidents.  Anticipate the impacts of legal and regulatory changes and their potential effects on the Group’s reputation: ■ Inappropriate product use by customers and end users; ■ Unethical governance practices (corruption and bribery, lobbying, lack of whistleblower protection). APPROACH Maximize Saint-Gobain’s contribution: Delivering solutions with health, comfort and safety benefits  across the value chain, and supporting communities  by accelerating the transition to sustainable construction. Minimize Saint-Gobain’s footprint:  Promoting ethical conduct, diversity and inclusion, health  and safety and social dialog by engaging all stakeholders,  including suppliers. ACTIONS Proposing solutions: ■ Program for sustainable and high-performance solutions;  ■ Innovation program for sustainability. Taking action for sustainability: ■ Employee engagement and diversity: HR policy, “Ethics and  Compliance” program; ■ Health and Safety across the value chain: Responsible  Purchasing and Human Rights policies, Health and Safety  Charter; ■ Stakeholder support: Saint-Gobain Foundation,  “Build Change” program. OBJECTIVES ■ Value of TF2 by 2030: 1.4; ■ 33% female managers by 2030; ■ Employee engagement rate above benchmark (&gt;76% in 2025) each year (annual); ■ 100% coverage of the CARE program (annual); ■ Diversity index above 90% (annual). ESRS S1 to S4 / G1 SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT GOALS: 1; 3; 4; 5; 8; 9; 10; 11; 16; 17 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 52 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.2.5 SAINT-GOBAIN'S VALUE CHAIN Saint-Gobain’s value chain extends from the extraction of  raw materials to the end-user, including the end-of-life of  products. Its four-part structure is identical, regardless of  country or market. This value chain is deployed locally,  i.e., raw materials come from suppliers located in the  same region as the site that will transform them. The  range of products and solutions is defined by the  country, i.e., as close as possible to the markets and  customers it serves. This is why the country platforms are  central to the deployment of the “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic  plan. Exports of the Group’s products are  marginal and  limited to specific situations, such as the supply of pipes  for infrastructure-related projects.  Similarly, while the principle of circularity (step 4) is  universal, its deployment and implementation are strictly  local. Its results are visible only if there is  complementarity at each stage of the value chain. This  implies effective collaboration between stakeholders in  each country.  (1) Life Cycle Assessment.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 53 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 54 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com See section 3.4.6, p. 175 See sections “Markets and activities”, p. 8,  1.2.2.B and D, p. 43 and p. 45, 1.2.7.A, p. 60 See sections 1.1.2, p. 27 and 1.1.3, p. 29 See section 3.3.3.D, p. 145 The synergies between the expertise of the central  purchasing teams and the country or regional  organizations make it possible to meet the needs of the  sites, whether industrial or tertiary, in terms of  quality,  quantity, regularity and service, and competitiveness.  Group procurement is organized to combine the  Group’s operational expertise and negotiation capacity  with the specific needs of local business lines and sites.  Thus, a central team, Saint-Gobain Purchasing,  coordinates the purchasing of the major purchasing  categories. For example, coordination is in place  for  energy purchases in order to secure supply  and  control energy costs and to identify more local  sources of supply of decarbonized energy that meet  both the requirements of site supply continuity  and  energy cost competitiveness. These synergies  between the central team and the local teams  are  essential for the deployment of the “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategy, including the achievement of the financial  and non-financial objectives by 2030.  Saint-Gobain’s purchasing policy favors the establishment  of medium- and long-term contracts and partnerships  with its suppliers. Given the diversity of  materials,  products, or services purchased and the number of active  suppliers, partner companies can be large companies,  mid-sized companies, or small and medium-sized  companies. The exposure to risks of suppliers’ employees is one of  the criteria taken into account in responsible purchasing  risk mapping (see section 3.4.6, p. 175). The supply chain of industrial sites is mostly regional.  This implies the management of a large number of  suppliers. Nearly 190,000 companies supply the sites,  with nearly 20,700 account for 90% of the Group’s non- trade purchases (by purchase value).  Purchases corresponding to the offering of the trading  business lines are managed directly by the country  platforms. They are concentrated around more than  10,850 suppliers. Each brand develops its offering  according to its positioning and the expectations of the  markets in the countries in which it operates. As such, it  manages relations with mainly local suppliers.  The environmental and social impacts related to the  Group’s procurement, including the exposure risks of  employees of suppliers and subcontractors, as well as  the responsible procurement policy, risk mapping, and  action plans are described in section 3.4.6, p. 175. They consist of industrial sites including quarries,  trading or service agencies including logistics sites and  storage areas, and other tertiary sites, such as offices or  R&amp;D centers. The geographic breakdown of sites and  workforce is shown in the “Our global footprint” section  (pp. 12 and 13). Thus, solutions for the interior, solutions  for the exterior, solutions for construction sites and  construction chemicals are developed, produced, and  sold in each country in order to meet the specific needs  of the construction market and customers. The impacts  associated with industrial sites are closely monitored  from an environmental perspective due to industrial  processes and stored materials (see section 1.2.7.D.b,  p. 64 and section 5.1.1, p. 290); from a social perspective  through team-based work organization (see section  3.4.2, p.  154); and in terms of relationships with local  communities (see section 3.4.3, p. 162). In general, Saint-Gobain employees have permanent  contracts (91.8% in 2025). Depending on operational  needs, employees with fixed-term contracts (8.2% in 2025)  or temporary workers may supplement the workforce.  Some countries make marginal use of independent  contractors (fewer than 5,000 people in 2024). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 55 Each country platform has a portfolio of sustainable and  high-performance solutions (see section 1.2.7.A, p.  60)  to  meet the needs of the residential construction,  non-residential construction, infrastructure, and other  industries markets. Saint-Gobain considers a customer to  be any company or person having an influence on the  purchasing decision. Depending on the market,  customers may be segmented into three categories: ■ Direct customers: companies that acquire products or  services for use in production or construction projects  or to resell them; ■ Users: companies or persons who use the products in  their industrial process, during the implementation of  a construction or renovation project or after their  installation. ■ Specifiers: companies or persons who do not buy  or use the Group’s products and services, but whose  opinion is vital to the purchasing decision process  of  direct customers due to their technical expertise,  reputation or any other capacity (architects,  for example).  At the various stages of construction projects,  interactions with customers help determine needs and  adapt solutions. Each country is committed to  deploying solutions adapted to all channels: direct sales  or through networks of distributors or e-commerce.  Saint-Gobain’s strategy does not directly target  consumers. Some distributors, who are direct customers  of the Group, specialize in or develop business activities  linked to the “do it yourself” trend. Saint-Gobain  is  committed to adapting its offering to specific needs  in  order to support the growth of this segment.  Depending on the country, direct customers and users  can be large international companies (e.g., for large new  construction projects), trading houses, craftsmen, or sole  proprietorships. Employees of companies, whether  customers or users, work mainly on construction sites.  Their working conditions depend on the country, local  regulations, country-specific construction methods,  the climate, and the local labor market.  To develop circularity in the construction market, it is not  enough to offer recyclable products or limit waste on  construction sites, it is a question of creating new local  ecosystems. Through their leadership and proximity to  customers, country platforms can make circularity  a real growth driver. Saint-Gobain integrates the circular  economy principles into its solutions placed  on the market right from the design stage: ■ By limiting the use of non-renewable resources and  developing the reusability of products, the proportion  of recycled or biosourced materials or byproducts  incorporated into production and product packaging  (1) + (2);  ■ By reducing the proportion of final waste throughout  the entire value chain; ■ By ensuring the recyclability of the solutions placed  on the market (2); ■ By collaborating with its customers to reduce the  impact on natural resources of the construction and  building industry in particular, to extend the lifespan  of buildings and facilitate the collection of recyclable  materials when buildings are demolished (3). As part of its program to develop sustainable and  high-performance solutions (see section 1.2.7.A, p.  60),  Saint-Gobain strives to develop solutions that optimize  the use of resources. Special attention is paid to the  recyclability of products and packaging placed on the  market. Some Saint-Gobain products, such as glass and  gypsum products, can be recycled indefinitely in a  closed-loop industrial process. Industrial processes are  then adapted to replace natural raw materials with  recycled materials derived from internal or  external  collection.  Depending on the markets addressed, Saint -Gobain’s  products have a lifespan that is variable but optimized  relative to their use. Some products, such as glazing,  have theoretical lifespans of up to 30 years; others, such  as additives in the construction chemicals market,  are designed for a specific use. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 56 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.2.6 THE GROUP'S VALUE CREATION MODEL The value creation model shows the consistency of the  Group’s strategy. The “Grow &amp; Impact” (2020 – 2025)  and then "Lead &amp; Grow" (2025 – 2030) strategic plans  are aligned both with the purpose of “Making the world a  better home” and with the vision of being the leader in  light and sustainable construction. Saint-Gobain guides  its efforts on the megatrends of global demand for  construction, the fight against climate change, and  resource scarcity (see section 1.2.1, p. 39). Based on the  challenges and opportunities associated with these  megatrends, the Group has defined strategic objectives  to accelerate sustainable profitable growth and  outperform the markets (see section 1.2.2, p. 42).  As part of its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan, Saint-Gobain  confirms its vision of a construction market that it  approaches in a decentralized and local manner through  its country platforms (see section 1.2.5, p.  53), its  commitment to meeting customer expectations by  adopting a solutions-based approach (see section 1.1.3,  p.  29) and its ambition to increase its presence in the  non-residential construction and infrastructure markets  (see section 1.2.2, p. 42). To this end, the country platforms will be able to draw  on the Group’s expertise (1.2.7.F, p.  67) in terms of  industrial excellence, innovation, best commercial  practices and environmental performance.  Saint-Gobain integrates its stakeholders into its model  through mapping (see section 1.2.3, p. 46). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 57 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 58 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com We deploy our solutions-based  approach country by country  and across all sales channels  to meet our customers’  expectations and create  value for Saint-Gobain We are increasing  our presence in  the non-residential and  infrastructure markets  thanks to an expanded,  adapted, and enhanced  offering We rely on highly effective  country platforms  with strong local roots,  which catalyze our growth PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 59 A CULTURE OF TRUST  WITH STAKEHOLDERS FINANCIAL STRENGTH FOSTERING GROWTH COUNTRY PLATFORMS (CF. PP. 12 AND 13) OPERATIONAL EXCELLENCE  Our values: the Principles of Conduct and Action 83% employee  engagement index  see section 1.2.7.E.b, p. 66 100% of partners are  signatories to the charter  see section 3.4.6, p. 175  2,653 sales outlets / showrooms 90% of country CEOs are  native to their region 1,170 manufacturing facilities 8 regional R&amp;D centers  Marketing and business expertise Brand name Product Innovation €3,752 M Free cash flow see section 2.1, p. 82 €213 M In CAPEX and R&amp;D  in 2025 for the  “Carbon 2030” roadmap see section 3.2, p. 116€3,309 M Current net income see section 2.1, p. 82 3,606 researchers  see section 1.2.7.D.a, p. 63 1.21 kg CO2e /€  Scope 1 and 2 carbon  intensity in 2025  (in € of EBITDA), representing  a 58% decrease since 2017 –  see section 3.2, p. 116 450+  patents filed in 2025 see section 1.2.7.D.a, p. 63 93% of employees feel health  and safety is a priority  on their team see section 3.4.2.A, p. 155 17 PPAs active in 2025 see section 3.7.1.B.a, p. 206 53 Mt of materials consumed  (estimation on a restricted  perimeter –  see section 3.3, p. 138 30+ investment or collaboration agreements signed in 2025 with start-ups see section 1.2.7.D.a, p. 63 2026-2030: SAINT-GOBAIN IS AIMING FOR (SEE. P. 17) A DECARBONATED HOME MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS MID-SINGLE-DIGIT: Average sales growth 15-18% EBITDA  margin &gt;50% Free cash flow  conversion ratio  &gt;13% ROCE -35% CO2 emissions over scope 1  and 2 between 2017 and  2024 in absolute value  see section 1.3.2.A, p. 74 +35% scope 3 emissions  between 2017 and 2024,  in absolute value see section 1.3.2.A, p. 74 -27% waste not recovered  between 2017 and 2025  in absolute value see section 1.3.2.B, p. 76  -26% Reduction in water  withdrawals between  2017 and 2025 see section 1.3.2.B, p. 76 1.4 Accident frequency rate*  in 2025 see section 1.3.2.C, p. 78 91%  Proportion of employees  who took part in training  during the year see section 1.3.2.C, p. 78 30% Percentage of female  managers in 2025 see section 1.3.2.C, p. 78 * With and without lost time  for 1 million hours worked by  our employees, temporary workers,  and permanent subcontractors. PERFORMANCE SEGMENTS A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL €1,166 M  Dividends paid €21 M   Investments  in local communities €5,178 M  Portion retained  for growth €1,234 M   Taxes and duties €342 M  Interest  repayment  including IFRS16  interest€9,458 M   Salaries and  other personnel  compensation €300 M  Retirement  contributions SHARING THE VALUE CREATED €46,483 M  Sales €17,699 M Value added €29,376 M  Operational  costs/payments  to suppliers VALUE SHARING (SEE. P. 73) €592 M   Other miscel-  laneous income  1.2.7 CROSS-FUNCTIONAL STRENGTHS AND LEVERS A – Offering solutions that  combine performance  and sustainability Saint-Gobain adopts a customer-centric approach with  a view to developing complete, innovative solutions  (see “Solutions-based approach”, p.  6). In each of the  countries in which it operates, the teams offer their  customers solutions combining performance and  contribution to sustainable development, in particular  promoting the acceleration towards a decarbonized  and more circular economy.  As well as providing high-performance solutions, the  Group’s offering must enable its customers to make  progress on their own sustainable development goals,  thereby accelerating the transition of the construction,  mobility and industry sectors to a “low-carbon” economy  that consumes fewer natural resources, while improving  the health, safety, and well-being of users.  To measure its progress in this respect, since 2020  Saint-Gobain has implemented a methodology that takes  into account local markets and contexts to estimate the  share of its sales generated by solutions offering  benefits in terms of performance and sustainability. Beyond the traditional approach of meeting a specific  need with a suitable product, the aim is to offer clients  comprehensive, end-to-end support in addressing  complex challenges, modeled as a series of “use cases” – illustrations of particular scenarios such as building a  single-family home or renovating a hospital. Examples of  how these solutions are implemented in different  countries are presented in section 1.1.3, p. 29. To measure the performance of solutions , the Group  assesses the increase in economic value for the customer  based on criteria such as productivity and financial  benefits, as well as the user experience.  To measure the sustainability impacts of solutions, the  methodology relies on performance assessments of  solutions in terms of environmental or health  and well-being impacts.  The performance of a solution is assessed across the  entire value chain and for the main stakeholders  involved, right through to the end user.  In addition to measuring its overall effort, this approach –  which covers more than 90% of Saint-Gobain’s sales –  aims to align the sustainability benefit assessment  methods, and guide the work of the Group’s teams,  particularly in terms of innovation, customer relations,  sales, marketing, and the design of solutions. The share of sales generated with sustainable solutions  was 73% in 2025. a. Product innovation  and customer proximity Creating more value for its customers, strengthening its  offering of more sustainable solutions, taking full  advantage of its local and global presence and expertise,  and reducing time-to-market: these are the objectives to  which Saint-Gobain’s teams are committed.  The offering of solutions is defined by the country  platforms (see section 1.2.5, p.  53) as close as possible  to the needs of customers and local markets.  The in-depth knowledge of the markets, the proximity of  the teams with its customers (see pp. 6 and 7), the design  of a residential, non-residential, or infrastructure project  (see value chain 1.2.5, p. 53), allows the teams to propose  innovations developed locally or adapted from solutions  that have proven their worth in other countries; possibly  with the technical support and expertise of the central  teams by business line – “performance segments”. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 60 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com ECONOMIC VALUE  INCREASE IMPROVED QUALITY ENVIRONMENTAL  IMPACTS HEALTH AND  WELL-BEING IMPACTS PERFORMANCE SUSTAINABILITY ■ Productivity ■ Other financial benefits ■ Reliability ■ Creativity, adaptability in design ■ Energy efficiency and carbon footprint ■ Circularity et water resources preservation ■ Products and services that support the  environmental performance of other sectors ■ Security and safety, including professional  risks reduction on value chain ■ Ergonomics for installers ■ Interior air quality ■ Acoustic, thermal and visual comfort Regulatory and normative requirements are integrated  into the innovation process from the earliest stage of the  marketing brief, and marketing teams in each country of  commercialization ensure product compliance with  regulations. Lastly, the “World Class Manufacturing” (WCM)  industrial excellence program  (see section 1.2.7.F.a,  p. 67) ensures the deployment of best practices in terms  of quality and product compliance at industrial sites,  based on the ISO 9001 standard. Technical products are launched after sales force  training and reinforced support by product managers  and technical trainers. In several countries, they  intervene directly at worksites when installers use a  differentiating innovation for the first time, throughout  the launch phases until the sales organization reaches a  sufficient level of maturity.  In addition, a monitoring procedure incorporating every  step in the innovation process allows for the rapid  identification and consideration of potential difficulties  prompting attention to potential problems. Close  monitoring of the progress achieved and product  performance means that the process of innovation is  faster and more secure. Saint-Gobain University’s “Unicampus” program  (see  section 1.2.7.F.b, p.  70) offers five training courses  associated with the innovation process. These training  courses are primarily intended for marketing and R&amp;D  functions but also aim to raise awareness across  all functions. A product compliance training module has  also been incorporated into the program. Practical  courses combining theory, practice, and experience  sharing are offered widely to the principal offices  involved in the innovation process, such as marketing,  R&amp;D, purchasing, production, and industrial efficiency. The process for launching new products, systems and  services is verified as part of internal control (see section  1.2.7.F.a, p.  67). It is regularly updated to improve the  quality management system procedures and associated  monitoring indicators.  b. Toward more sustainable  products and solutions Saint-Gobain has incorporated an environmental, health  and safety impact verification dimension into the  innovation process since 2008 in the form of a checklist.  Since 2023, a new standard has been developed and a  tool for integrating sustainability issues into the  innovation process is available to the teams.  The main objectives of the new standard for innovation are: ■ Eliminating or limiting the presence of substances of  very high concern in new products marketed by  Saint-Gobain and used in the downstream value chain; ■ Eliminating or limiting and controlling potential health  and environmental risks during production processes  at Saint-Gobain sites. ■ select relevant sustainability indicators to measure  performance throughout the innovation process,  based on product categories and market needs. At each stage of the innovation process, this tool makes  it possible to define and monitor objectives for reducing  the environmental footprint and maximizing health and  well-being benefits in accordance with the approach to  propose efficient and sustainable solutions (see section  1.2.7.A, p. 60).  c. Informing customers  through to end users To provide clients with information on the environmental  benefits of products and solutions, Saint -Gobain is  committed to the development and  promotion of life  cycle analyses (LCAs). These analyses are useful internally to guide innovation,  and to meet growing market demand for greater  transparency. Saint-Gobain is committed to ensuring  that, by 2030, 100% of the Group’s sales, excluding  distribution activities, will be generated by products  covered by verified LCAs (life cycle analyses) or EPDs  (environmental product declarations).  As of late 2025, 57% (stable vs 2024) of sales were  generated by products covered by verified LCAs or  EPDs, either specific (produced by Saint -Gobain) or  generic (produced by professional associations). This represents more than 5.000 EPDs (2,700 in 2024)  verified EPDs published in close to 40 countries; the  Group is positioned as the world’s leading provider of  verified EPDs – by number – in the construction industry. Saint-Gobain’s customers also rely on sustainable  construction certifications, such as BREEAM and LEED,  to assess and enhance the overall performance of their  buildings. They seek products whose features help  achieve the desired levels of performance. To support country platform teams in leveraging this  opportunity, dedicated tools have been made available  for three segments – gypsum, insulation, and glass.  These include checklists to identify the potential  contributions of products to the main sustainability  standards, as well as evidence that can be provided to  customers (documents, data). These tools are  supplemented by locally adaptable presentations to  share this information with customers.  For example, the contribution of Saint-Gobain glazing is  available on the Saint -Gobain Glass website: https:// www.saint-gobain-glass.com/green-building-labelling- schemes.  According to Group’s estimates, the number of projects  supported by a sustainable construction certification is  rising in the main countries where it operates.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 61 With regard to information on users, the Group’s  products comply with current regulations, such as CE  marking in Europe, or the requirement for chemical  products to have labels and safety data sheets (SDS).  Saint-Gobain also provides non-mandatory information  specific to some of its products, such as: ■ Voluntary safety data declaration sheets for  unclassified articles or substances; ■ The declaration of the composition of components  and materials via the IMDS (International Material Data  System) database in the case of the automotive  industry; ■ Environmental Product Declarations (EPDs) for  construction products (or Environmental and  Health  Declaration Forms (FDES) in France); ■ Voluntary certifications obtained through  independent national laboratories – or, failing that,  international laboratories close to these markets –  when innovative products or systems are not covered  by international or national standards. Specific measures relating to the product compliance  program are presented in section 1.2.7.F.a, p. 67. B – Creating the conditions  for success: organization  for performance Established in June 2025 to create the conditions  required for the deployment of the “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategic plan, Saint-Gobain’s new organization is part of  a strategy aimed at strengthening profitable growth,  simplifying the structure, and asserting the Group’s  worldwide leadership in light and sustainable  construction. This development is not a break, but a  natural step that draws on the strengths of the existing  model in order to go further. It aims to  strengthen  Saint-Gobain’s ability to efficiently execute its strategic  plan by combining proximity, agility, and expertise. For several years, the multi-local model has enabled  the Group to get closer to the markets and customers.  Today, this model is being expanded : each country is  becoming fully responsible for all business lines, with  greater autonomy to coordinate commercial and  operational performance. This empowerment makes it  possible to act faster, seize opportunities in markets that  are still partially addressed, and expand Saint -Gobain’s  offering to create more value. It also promotes a  solutions-based approach, tailored to the needs of  customers, whether for key accounts, distribution  networks, or direct sales. To support this change, Saint-Gobain has created a  Group Operational Performance Department. Its role is  to guide, challenge and support countries in the  implementation of their growth plans. This Department  relies on two complementary levers. On the one hand,  the Performance Segments provide business expertise  to enrich the offering , maximize synergies and ensure  strategic consistency. On the other hand, the shared  Expertise Platforms provide innovation, digital,  purchasing and industrial and commercial excellence  solutions. This ensures that each country has  the  resources and skills to succeed. This organization also simplifies the Group’s structure.  This integration promotes strategic coherence, the  pooling of resources, and the achievement of economies  of scale, which will be reinvested in  growth. This is an  operational lever to aim for significant cost reduction.  This organization also strengthens the perception of the  consistency of offers and stimulates the culture of  collaboration within Saint-Gobain.  The expected benefits are concrete and measurable. The  Group will be able to offer a more comprehensive and  better-deployed offering, improve its operational  efficiency, strengthen its proximity to customers and act  quickly in an economic environment marked by  regionalization. This organization is designed to  accelerate profitable growth , simplify processes to  become more competitive, and assert Saint-Gobain as a  worldwide leader in light and sustainable construction  across its entire scope. This change comes at a key moment. The current  organization has reached maturity: its strengths  are known and its areas for improvement are identified.  The global context is shifting towards deglobalization,  which requires more agility and flexibility. By deepening  the multi-local model and mobilizing 100% of the Group’s  scope in the countries, Saint-Gobain is giving itself the  means to respond to these challenges and seize  opportunities. This organization is designed as a  powerful lever to execute the new “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategic plan. C – Rigorously allocating  financial resources Saint-Gobain rigorously applies its dynamic capital  allocation approach, adhering to strict criteria in terms of  value creation potential. As part of its “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategic plan, the Group plans to deploy approximately  €20 billion of capital between now and 2030, combining  growth and attractive returns for shareholders, while  maintaining a solid balance sheet with a net debt to  EBITDA ratio of between 1.5x and 2.0x. Of this total,  Saint-Gobain plans to allocate $12  billion in growth  investments  (1) over the 2025-2030 period , giving  priority to high-growth markets (North America, Asia- Pacific, growth countries, construction chemicals) as well  as consolidating its leading positions and enriching its  offering for each regional platform, country by country.  The Group also plans to increase shareholder returns to  around €8 billion: approximately €6 billion distributed in  dividends, targeting steady growth in the dividend per  share; and €2 billion in share buybacks. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 62 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) Growth investment: growth capex + net M&amp;A (acquisitions net of divestments). a. Supporting the Group’s growth  and commitments In 2025, Saint-Gobain continued its investment policy,  allocating resources to strengthen its industrial base   with 24 new lines or plants coming on stream during the  year. These investments cover all continents (Americas,  Europe, Asia-Pacific, Middle East, and Africa) and a very  large part of the Group’s activities, from plaster to  insulation, construction chemicals, mortars, and solutions  for industry and mobility. In addition, in 2025, the Group  committed €213  million in investments (CAPEX and  R&amp;D) to support its carbon roadmap, with the goal of  spending at least €100  million per year on these  expenses until 2030. b. Ensuring agile, value-creating  portfolio management Saint-Gobain is consistently pursuing a strategy to  optimize the Group’s profile in order to continue to  strengthen growth, profitability, and value creation.  From 2018 through December 31, 2025, Saint-Gobain has  made acquisitions representing approximately €7 billion  in revenue and divestments representing around  €10  billion in revenue. As part of its “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategic plan, the Group is targeting an asset rotation of  more than 20% of revenue over the 2026-2030 period. Continuing to pursue a strategy of value-creating acquisitions In 2025, Saint-Gobain completed 17 acquisitions,  generating additional sales of €862  million. These  acquisitions consolidate Saint-Gobain’s position as the  worldwide leader in light and sustainable construction  and accelerate its growth. In the construction chemicals sector, the Group  completed the acquisition of the Ovniver Group (Cemix  brand), a major player in the construction chemicals  sector in Mexico and Central America. Saint-Gobain also  completed the acquisition of Fosroc, a leading company  in construction chemicals with a strong presence in India,  the Middle East, and Asia-Pacific. This acquisition  strengthens the Group’s presence in high-growth  emerging markets and consolidates its worldwide  leadership in light and sustainable construction.  Saint-Gobain also strengthened its digital platform  dedicated to construction chemicals with the acquisition  of Maturix, a Danish company offering digital solutions  for real-time monitoring of concrete manufacturing. This  transaction expands the Group’s digital offering,  complements its VERIFI® system, and enables smarter  and more sustainable construction processes. Conducting regular reviews of the portfolio  in order to concentrate the Group’s strengths In 2025, the Group completed 6 divestments, generating  €352  million in revenue. Saint-Gobain signed an  agreement to sell Brüggemann, a German specialist in  prefabrication solutions, to the Köster Group. The Group  also finalized the sale of SFIC Belgium, a distributor of  building materials specializing in plaster, insulation, and  ceilings on the Belgian market, as well as the sale of  Tumelero, a building materials distribution chain in Brazil  with a strong presence in the south of the country. D – Creating a culture  of innovation, serving  industrial performance a. Developing a culture of innovation  Innovation is the lever on which Saint-Gobain relies to  develop new products and solutions. It is both a  condition and an accelerator of growth. Innovation  makes it possible: ■ to meet the evolving customer expectations (in  particular for more performance and sustainability) in  order to continuously adjust the Group’s offering;  ■ to stand out from its competitors;  ■ to take advantage of opportunities created by global  megatrends (see section 1.2.1, p. 39);  ■ to anticipate the rapid evolution of the role of digital  and artificial intelligence , which are transforming  Saint-Gobain’s markets;  ■ to stay ahead of increased competition;  ■ to respond effectively to the rapid growth and issues  of certain markets  (for example, the need for  increased productivity for installers, due to a growing  shortage of skilled construction and renovation  workers).  In this context, innovation is a cross-functional asset  that permeates all the Group’s activities and enables  the success of its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan   (see  section 1.2.2, p.  42). To make the most of it,  Saint-Gobain defines priorities and aims to find the right  balance between the different innovation horizons; links  innovation to the expectations of its markets,  maintaining the greatest proximity to its customers (see  above, section 1.2.7.A.a, p.  60); and tends to make  progress to reduce the time-to-market for innovative  solutions.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 63 For the Group, innovation is an open process, involving  both resources and research and development teams  present around the world (8 transversal R&amp;D centers,  3,600 researchers and 450+ patents filed in 2025) as well  as external partners such as start-ups (30+ agreements  signed in 2025 via its NOVA entity), universities,  customers and suppliers of the Group. Saint -Gobain  implements innovation through a structured, Group-wide  system, based on clear strategic priorities, a unified  Innovation Framework, and strong coordination between  central and regional teams.  Innovation is driven by  customer centricity, sustainability, and the transition of  products to complete solutions, supported by methods,  tools, and learning loops that improve success rates and  shorten time-to-market. A central Innovation  Development team maintains and deploys this  framework, while the regions gradually take responsibility  in order to maximize the impact as close as possible to  the activities. Skills are strengthened through extensive  training programs, including the network of Innovation  Catalysts – employees formally trained to stimulate  innovation locally – as well as through digital tools such  as the Innovation Project Companion, which offers  secure, personalized support. Together, these elements  create a shared mindset, harmonized practices, and  execution discipline that enable Saint-Gobain to innovate  consistently and effectively across the Group.  Saint-Gobain uses AI and digital tools to accelerate  innovation, facilitating faster experimentation, more  informed decision-making, and better access to  knowledge. Generative AI contributes to the  development of new materials, while secure internal tools  increase productivity and support teams. Together, these  technologies optimize workflows, reduce time-to-market,  and drive data-driven innovation.  b. Integrating innovation  into industrial processes  Innovation is central to Saint-Gobain’s industrial  operations. By relying on innovation the Group makes  progress in terms of operational excellence,  decarbonization, and circularity . Year after year,  investments in industrial facilities have increased  productivity and safety, reduced carbon emissions, raw  material consumption and waste production.  This modernization involves the integration of digital  technologies (in particular via IoT sensors for real-time  monitoring of energy production and consumption);  predictive maintenance through data analysis  (vibrations, temperatures, pressures) in order to reduce  unplanned shutdowns; and digital twinning to simulate  and optimize production lines. This also involves  replacing energy sources , such as biogas or  hydroelectricity, rather than fossil fuels. It also involves  automation and robotification , deploying robots for  repetitive, dangerous or delicate tasks (glass handling,  packaging) and automated quality control systems to  immediately detect defects. It is also a question of  reducing the carbon footprint , through the energy  optimization of glass furnaces (new geometries, heat  recovery); the use of alternative fuels (hydrogen,  electricity) to replace natural gas; or by integrating  waste or end-of-life products into the production  process. Saint-Gobain is also working on deploying tools  based on artificial intelligence , for example in its  automotive glazing plants – to improve product quality –  or to optimize the operation of its fiberglass furnaces, in  order to reduce costs, the quantity of resources and  energy used, and CO 2 emissions. Finally, the Group is  making progress thanks to new materials  and the  optimization of formulations, through more efficient and  sustainable materials (insulation, smart glazing), less  energy-intensive melting and transformation processes,  as well as additives and formulations that improve  quality, while accelerating rates. E – Developing a culture of trust a. Building trust with stakeholders Values shared with Saint-Gobain’s  stakeholders The Group bases its development on its corporate  purpose and draws on strong values firmly rooted in  Saint-Gobain’s culture: the Principles of Conduct and  Action. They constitute the Group’s code of ethics and  guide Saint-Gobain’s decisions. This code of ethics is the foundation of all Saint-Gobain  policies and commitments, specifically the compliance  policies, the human rights policy, the Environmental,  Health and Safety Charter, and the “Buyer” and “Supplier”  Charters of the Purchasing Department. This code of  ethics applies to all Group entities and employees, as well  as subcontractors in their work on Saint-Gobain sites, and  suppliers (see section 1.2.3.A, p. 46).  The Principles of Conduct and Action are translated into  31 languages and available on the Group’s website for  wide distribution to external as well as internal  stakeholders. During the recruitment process or at the  time of onboarding, they are communicated to each new  employee, including those with fixed-term contracts and  temporary workers, in the local language of the country.  This communication may take various forms, depending  on the country: ■ A welcome brochure with comments from the Human  Resources representative or the manager; ■ The code of ethics directly incorporated into the  employment contract or the job offer letter. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 64 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com Online training on the Principles of Conduct and Action  is available to all employees and is mandatory for new  hires. The Saint -Gobain University Management School  offers an introduction to business ethics and compliance  to allow managers to discuss these issues and share  their operational experience.  The Group trains each new manager from their first year  at the Group on the code of ethics, the fight against  corruption, and compliance with antitrust law  (“Principles of Conduct and Action, ACT and Comply”).  Participation in public debate &gt; Advocacy Saint-Gobain participates in public debates on the  strategic challenges  for its business activity and  environment. This participation takes place directly, via  professional associations, and via global, regional, or local  alliances. The Group’s advocacy activities aim to  constructively feed into discussions and bring together  value chain players and stakeholders. Inspired by its  corporate purpose “Making the world a better home ,”  Saint-Gobain regularly works on the priority themes of  decarbonization of the economy, circularity, and well- being in the construction sector (see section 1.3.2, p. 74).  Saint-Gobain and the majority of its subsidiaries belong  to professional associations representing their industry  at the national or supranational levels. For example, in  France, the Group is involved in professional associations  representing companies, such as Afep and Medef.  Saint-Gobain’s subsidiaries also cooperate with various  local associations or organizations involved in normative  or regulatory issues, or related to  environmental, social,  societal or economic issues. The subsidiaries have internal  procedures in place to ensure that their participation in  associations is recognized and referenced, and that  employees who represent them in associations are  trained in the rules of antitrust law. In 2025, Saint-Gobain  published a Lobbying Policy.  Policy Lobbying Policy Purpose Provide a framework for  responsible lobbying activities Scope of application Employees, corporate officers,  Group managers in contact with  managers holding political office,  administrations or governments Under the supervision of Director of Institutional Affairs Link to the policy Lobbying Policy Saint-Gobain ensures that its advocacy initiatives are  aligned with its commitment to contribute to carbon  neutrality by 2050. Its actions and positions therefore  take into account the ambition to limit the rise  in temperatures to below 1.5 °C.  The Group ensures the proper coordination of the  positions taken locally and that these institutional  commitments are well-known and respected by  the  countries. Saint -Gobain complies with the  transparency obligations applicable to relations between  companies and public authorities in all the  countries  where it operates. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 65 &gt; Multi-stakeholder partnerships Saint-Gobain has been a member of the United Nations  Global Compact since 2003 and regularly reports on its  progress in the areas covered by the Compact.  Saint-Gobain also encourages its partners, particularly its  suppliers, to commit to the Global Compact’s ten  principles. Likewise, the Group pays special attention to the United  Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), so as to  make progress in evaluating the real impacts that  companies can have in working towards a fairer, more  sustainable world. These goals also make possible new  multi-stakeholder collaborations, in which companies are  engaged locally and globally.  Finally, the Group is a member of the forum of committed  companies with the organization Transparency  International France. In this respect, Saint -Gobain has  committed to rejecting and combating corruption, in all  its forms. The Group undertakes to make its best efforts  to implement a solid prevention mechanism, inspired by  the current best practices of the business world.  A list of the Group’s main partnerships and investments is  available on the website (https://www.saint-gobain.com/ en/corporate-responsibility/our-responsibility/our- commitments-partnerships-and-recognitions). The Group refrains from financing any political party. b. Committed employees Building a culture of trust,  responsibility and collaboration Saint-Gobain’s new organization, aiming to empower  countries and at the local level, entails building trust, as a  condition for the effective delegation of authority and  fostering cross-functional collaboration between all the  Group’s functions and business lines at the global level.  The Trust, Empowerment and Collaboration (TEC)  approach represents a significant change in corporate  culture within the Group , which primarily concerns  managerial culture: the aim is to establish, by default,  the  principle of trust and thus move from a vertical,  traditional structure, to an open, learning organization,  leaving the freedom of action at the local management  level in the 80 countries (1) where Saint-Gobain operates.  It also responds to the underlying trends affecting the  world in which the Group operates, and in particular to  the disintermediation brought about by new  technologies, which makes managerial methods obsolete  and promotes profound changes in the relationship  between individuals and work. The successive lockdowns  associated with Covid-19, the development of  teleworking, and the adaptation of working hours at the  production sites have called for greater autonomy on  the part of employees and required them to be more  flexible and adaptable. In this respect, the TEC approach  is a way of aligning Saint-Gobain’s strategy with changes  in the expectations of employees and strongly supports  the development of its employer brand. The TEC culture also helps to make the innovation  process more efficient, to align it with customer needs  by fostering collaboration between countries and  business lines (for example, to provide an offering of  complete solutions to Saint-Gobain’s customers) and  between functions (for example, between researchers,  marketing specialists and technicians).  To implement this approach on the ground, the Group is  relying increasingly on its training program (see section  1.2.7.F.b, p. 70) and on approaches such as collective and  individual coaching, made accessible to a greater  number of employees, as well as the gradual roll-out of  360° assessments. The development of feedback is  encouraged to strengthen collaboration and trust, both  at the Group level (for example, via the annual  me@Saint-Gobain survey), as well as in project  management, and at the individual level (by developing  the ability to request and receive feedback). Each manager is therefore encouraged to promote  the TEC culture within their scope and monitor progress  on its implementation.  Employee satisfaction Increasing employees’ involvement in a context of  change, both generational and technological, is an  essential challenge for the Group. To meet this challenge,  the Group places managerial attitude and involvement at  the heart of this approach: managers motivate and  develop the loyalty of employees by giving more  meaning to their everyday work and by favoring a spirit  of initiative. Since 2019, a unique tool to measure employee  commitment has been developed. It makes it possible to  conduct surveys targeted to particular populations and/ or themes, for example, such as the specific  responsiveness of managers during transformation  phases. This platform is also used to conduct the annual  global survey sent to all employees: “me@Saint-Gobain”.  Employees are asked questions covering the seven main  focus areas of the HR policy and the current  transformation programs: ■ Team: a positive and collaborative working environment; ■ Actor: an organization that values employees; ■ Talent: an HR organization focused on upskilling; ■ Management: a participatory management style; ■ Inspiration: a clear focus on the future; ■ CSR: a responsible company; ■ Innovation, excellence, and customer focus. With a participation rate of 88% in 2025,  127,311  employees took part. In particular, 85% of them  would recommend Saint-Gobain as a good place to work. The engagement index reached a score of 83%.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 66 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) This figure refers to countries with active industrial presence. This index groups the results into four themes: ■ Pride in working for Saint-Gobain; ■ Short-term retention capacity; ■ Recommendation; ■ The feeling of being appreciated and valued for their  work. In accordance with the HR organization, each country is  able to obtain results for its own scope and is  responsible for circulating the results and initiating  dialog with employees to define action plans to work  on areas requiring improvement. F – Deploying an expertise  platform a. Ensuring the Group’s operational  performance The "WCM" Program for industrial excellence Saint-Gobain’s “World Class Manufacturing” (WCM)  program, deployed at industrial sites, combines the  standardization of methods, tools, and best practices   with the modularity required to adapt to a wide variety  of industrial processes and site sizes. It is based on  continuous performance improvement methods such as  Lean, Six Sigma, TPM (Total Productive Maintenance),  and 5S. This program defines the logic, the objective of  rigor, and the actions to achieve continuous  improvements in terms of quality, performance, and  sustainability by integrating a high level of service and  customer satisfaction.  The “WCM” program is based on certification procedures  such as ISO 9001 for quality, ISO 14001 and 50001 for the  environment, ILO OSH 2001 and ISO 45001 for health and  safety. This is a program of cultural transformation of  sites, aimed at involving as many people as possible in  improving performance. Digital tools are made available  to help this transformation. Its management, monitoring  its execution, and improving the performance of the sites  are managed across the businesses in coordination with  the Management of the sites. The deployment of the “WCM” program at a site is  adapted to its priorities, size, strengths, and weaknesses  and its objectives in terms of financial and environmental  performance, quality, and customer satisfaction. Thus,  each site establishes its own roadmap, in accordance  with international guidelines, standards specific to its  business line, the expectations of its customers and the  improvement objectives set. A “WCM” expertise network  has been set up to better disseminate the program,  optimize resources by country, region, or business line,  and share expertise on each of the program pillars. A  central team heads up the WCM network, trains the  teams, creates and applies the program standards,  circulates the tools, and provides site-specific assistance  on request. They rely on local teams to increase the  maturity of the sites. Saint-Gobain’s “WCM” program is based on eight pillars,  each one representing an area of excellence. The foundations of the program define the methods  and tools: ■ Analyzing losses to determine the priority of actions; ■ Resolving problems with logic, rigor and precision for  lasting improvement; ■ Involving and engaging employees in a proactive  approach; ■ Improving standards to make progress easier to  deploy and more robust over time. Achieving industrial excellence is a demanding process  that requires gradual, methodical and constant  implementation. To support the sites in this process,  training programs are rolled out during sessions led  either by the central teams or by local trainers. These  trainers are trained and certified by the central team. In  2025, more than 2,200 employees were trained. The  methods developed under the “people development”  pillar to ensure the management of  Saint -Gobain’s  technical skills by adapting training programs, employee  career paths, and, when necessary, the search for external  skills. This pillar also plays a vital role in strengthening the  TEC culture (see section 1.2.7.E.b, p. 66).  The benefits in terms of competitiveness, improvement  of customer service, stability of performance, and  employee commitment can be measured at each stage  of the site path. Stringency levels have been established  to set principles shared by the entire organization. Each  of these levels corresponds both to a performance  objective set by the business and to a maturity milestone  in the approach. Audits verify the robustness of the  actions implemented and the sustainability of the results  obtained. As of late 2025, 45 “Foundations” sites,  181 sites were certified “Bronze”, 60 “Silver” and 6 “Gold”. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 67 The “WCM” program accelerates the digital  transformation of the sites , a significant reduction in  production costs, while minimizing risks in terms of  health, safety, environmental, and industrial risks. The  “Quality”, “Industrial Performance” and “Environment”  pillars contribute significantly to reducing Saint-Gobain’s  environmental footprint by reducing production waste  and water consumption and by improving energy  efficiency. The “WCM” program and its extension to the  supply chain represent a change of culture and  management system, with the aim of bringing a high  level of service to customers, better competitiveness,  and greater efficiency, while continuously improving the  health and safety of all Group employees and also of its  partners. It fosters and promotes employee commitment  and mobility. Finally, it contributes to the success of the  digital transformation of plants and to the adoption  of its methods and tools by user. In the Industry 5.0 era, the Group’s operational  performance is based on the ability of Saint-Gobain’s  sites to integrate digital technology across the entire  value chain. To meet this challenge, a strategic program  has been launched to accelerate the digital  transformation of all the Group’s sites. Its goal is to  increase operational efficiency, reduce costs, and drive  product innovation through a unified governance  framework and state-of-the-art technology assets. This  initiative is part of a global ambition, in line with the  “Lead &amp; Grow” strategy: to make each site, by 2030, a  model of performance and sustainable development on  a global scale, based on an integrated, lean, and resilient  digital ecosystem capable of supporting competitiveness  and operational excellence. To achieve this ambition, it is essential to establish  a  Group baseline to assess the digital maturity of each  site and incorporate it into the existing WCM program.  The objective assessment of this digital maturity on five  levels makes it possible to develop personalized  digitalization roadmaps, taking into account the specific  needs and potential of each site, in order to support  them step by step in their progress. Saint-Gobain is embarking on a bold transformation to  reinvent the future of its operations. By combining  innovation and standardization, Saint-Gobain’s initial  priorities focus on key areas: streamlining solutions,  consolidating data platforms, expanding IoT  connectivity, integrating AI (classic, generative, agentic),  and deploying computer vision and digital twinning.  These levers will allow the Group to rethink its processes  and increase operational excellence to a new level, but  also to continuously improve the performance of its  products in terms of  quality, reliability, and efficiency.  This ambition is also driven by a Digital Transformation  Accelerator, which ensures strategic consistency and  catalyzes synergies. Training programs organized by technical business line  ensure the management and updating of the skills  required to offer customers high-performance,  competitive, and innovative products and services. These  programs are generally developed and implemented by  training teams organized by business line: glass, gypsum,  insulation, mortars, pipe, etc. The Glass Technical  Academy thus incorporated all glass manufacturing  training, as well as technical glass transformation training  for construction and automotive applications. Designed  and delivered by around 70 experts, the training sessions  provide in-depth technical content, practical exercises,  and site visits. This constitution of a center of excellence  around the businesses enables the transfer of knowledge,  sharing of best practices, and the alignment of methods  within the business lines. Optimizing the supply chain Ensuring optimized management of the supply chain,  from supplier to customer, is an essential part of both  industrial efficiency and customer service. By helping  optimize inventory levels, supply chain efficiency is also a  crucial factor in cash management. Lastly, the Supply  Chain makes it possible to develop innovation in logistics  services, the shared management of products, and their  sales forecasts (Sales &amp; Operations Planning), which are  levers of profitable growth in Saint-Gobain’s existing and  future markets of Saint-Gobain. All of these missions  make the supply chain a major contributor to the  company’s sustainable development. The Group’s supply chains may be local or global  and are managed by the entities’ internal organizations.  Global coordination and a team of experts allow  common logistics optimization tools, dedicated training,  and customized supply chain improvement programs to  be provided. These teams of experts also work directly in  the countries with the support of the general  management bodies to raise awareness of all functions,  identify potential for improvement and draw up action  plans to be implemented by the country teams.  Depending on the maturity of local supply chains,  actions may involve optimizing the management of  product lines by the marketing teams, implementing  collaborative processes, developing the customer culture  at the sites, adapting planning (customer forecasts such  as plant scheduling) or optimizing transport. The  objective is to maximize customer satisfaction while  optimizing financial and environmental impacts. Saint-Gobain has also developed “Supply Chain 4.0”  digital programs to optimize distribution networks,  measure transport CO₂ emissions, and then take action to  reduce them, and reduce the lead times in order or  procurement processes. Whether it is for its purchases or  customer delivery, the Group has programs for the  optimization of processes, routes, and vehicle loading  as well as more efficient travel planning by coordinating  inbound and outbound transport to prevent empty miles. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 68 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com The supply chain support teams also work with the  internal data, AI, digital, marketing, purchasing, and  finance teams to test, implement and improve innovative  solutions or choose market tools so as to best meet the  needs of the entities.  In competitive markets, the Group’s supply chains work  constantly to optimize, innovate, and strike the best  balance between delivery times and reliability, costs,  inventories, and carbon footprint. This is in order to best  meet the growing expectations of Saint-Gobain’s  customers, and to serve the objectives of its “Lead &amp;  Grow” strategic plan. The impact of these scope  3 reduction efforts  (categories 4 and 9) is described in section 3.2.4.C, p. 131.  Quality and the product compliance program The Group’s Quality Excellence Department supports  countries and local teams in order to boost customer  satisfaction by improving the quality culture and Group  product compliance and reducing the costs stemming  from poor quality.  It relies on the ISO  9001 or IATF  16494 certification  standards, the “Quality &amp; Process Control” pillar of the  “WCM” program, and the “Internal Quality Control”  guidelines (see section 5.2.5, p. 313). Training programs  on the tools relating to product quality and compliance  are also organized as part of “UniCampus” (see section  1.2.7.F.b, p. 70) or via e-learning courses available on the  Group’s training platform. The purpose of the product compliance program is to  challenge and improve organizations’ processes in order  to ensure that products: ■ comply with applicable regulations and product  standards; ■ meet the customer promise. The customer promise includes the functionalities or  performances announced and communicated to the  customer.  Product compliance is a fundamental requirement .  Procedures and processes are adapted in step with  changes in the organization of the Group, which  supplemented the measures already in place by  launching a program in 2021 to further strengthen the  culture of quality and resilience of organizations  concerning product compliance. The program was  designed to remind countries and local teams of the key  principles and processes related to product quality and  compliance throughout the product life cycle, from  design to production, marketing, use, and end of life. This program includes four steps: ■ Awareness campaigns at all Group levels; ■ The definition of a roadmap by local management  teams; ■ An assessment of the effectiveness of organizations  and processes in the countries for each business line; ■ The definition and implementation of continuous  improvement plans where necessary. A network of local facilitators and supports comprising  over 200 people at end-2025 has been set up and  trained to support local teams in the implementation of  the program and ensure the sharing of best practices,  thus fostering the continuous improvement of  procedures and processes.  Country and regional CEOs demonstrate their  commitment to promoting and implementing the  program through the annual self-assessment process  and job offer letters implemented at the Group level by  internal audit.  Lastly, the smooth running of the program is monitored  by a committee created for this purpose consisting of  two members of the Group’s General Management, and  the results are presented regularly to the members of the  Executive Committee. The Group’s internal control teams  also assess the effective implementation of the program  through the general audit process and  annual targeted  audit campaigns. Industrial and distribution risk prevention The aim of the industrial and distribution risk prevention  policy is to reduce the likelihood of accidental events,  minimize their severity if  they do occur, and preserve  the continuity of business operations. This policy applies  to all Saint-Gobain sites. Defined and led by the Risk and Insurance Department,  this policy is rolled out within the organization to the sites  by a network of  prevention coordinators. The Group’s  reference framework is a risk prevention manual. It  includes the applicable standards, procedures, and  technical rules, methods for identifying and reducing risks,  including natural risks, and the drafting of emergency and  business continuity plans. This  manual may be shared  with the relevant stakeholders, such as permanent on-site  subcontractors. The risk assessment tool is used by all  operational sites and provides an objective assessment of  the protection and prevention level. It takes into account  both human factors (organization, procedures,  communication, training, etc.) and physical facilities. This  means that each site is able to identify its areas for  improvement, develop action plans including investments,  and use this tool to measure progress. The tool is  accompanied by periodic site visits by the risk prevention  engineering team: a total of 387 sites were visited in 2025.  A total of 119 sites have the HPR (Highly Protected Risk)  label, 47% of which are priority sites. The Group is rolling out business continuity plans for  each site according to the risk assessment and the  vulnerability of processes to unforeseen interruptions  including an anticipation of the impacts of an accident, so  as to limit its effects. The aim is to fully or partially ensure  customer service and recover operational capabilities  as quickly as possible. The subcontractors working on the  sites are involved in the process and must commit to a  prevention plan. In 2025, 21 sites were visited by the  prevention engineering team, specifically to verify the  quality of their continuity plans with regard to the  Group’s guidelines in this area. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 69 Saint-Gobain constantly adapts its risk management  system to better anticipate the potential risks associated  with the effects of climate change. Action plans to  further increase prevention, particularly in the face of  natural disaster risks, are being rolled out. In particular,  they include changes in the design of certain sites to  reduce, where necessary, the impacts of these natural  events, especially flooding. In 2025, 14 at-risk sites were  visited to specifically check their exposure to  natural  disasters, especially flooding. Environmental management system,  industrial hygiene, occupational health  and safety (EHS) The EHS management system is based on an internal  reference framework that describes the process for  identifying and managing environmental and  occupational health and safety impacts and risks to be  implemented by each of the Saint-Gobain sites. The  description of this system is available in chapter 5 “Risks  and Control” in section 5.2.5, p. 313. Each Group site conducts its own assessment of  environmental, health and safety risks  under the Site  Manager’s responsibility. To do this, a standardized  method has been deployed and is regularly updated.  Training courses are organized for site teams, and a  digital application has been developed incorporating risk  analysis, assessment, and management actions. This tool  facilitates the deployment, reliability and monitoring of  risk management action plans at all the Group’s sites. Each site has tools to facilitate the continuous  improvement of its performance by reducing negative  impacts and anticipating risks: ■ Thematic policies accompanied by standards  and application guides; ■ Thematic training and webinars on the main EHS  issues; ■ Tools for monitoring action plans and reporting  to drive performance improvement. A support team of experts, in the regions and centrally,  support the sites in this process. The action plans of the sites are part of the “WCM”  approach (see section 1.2.7.F.a, p.  67), in particular the  “Environment and risk prevention” and “Health &amp; Safety”  pillars.  Audits of the environmental, health, and safety  management system are conducted by the functional  division concerned. These audits are conducted as a  priority at sites with ISO or equivalent certification. In  addition to such audits, a version of the audit  methodology has been developed for smaller sites  for which ISO certification is less suitable. Lastly, a training matrix specifies the training courses on  managing environmental, health, and safety risks for the  teams according to the position held. It is a particularly  relevant tool to use to define employee training courses. b. Developing human capital Saint-Gobain’s development depends first and foremost  on the quality and expertise of the women and men who  comprise the Group. This is reflected first and foremost in  the attention paid to recruiting the best talents in all  specialties, including engineers, digital &amp; data scientists,  sales and marketing staff, product managers, supply  chain, and sustainable development specialists. Beyond  the recruitment process and the attention paid to  its  brand as an employer, it is Saint -Gobain’s collective  ability to train, nurture, and constantly develop these  talents that will enable it to stand out. With digital  technology and refined data processing, career paths can  be more easily individualized and the HR support policy  customized (see section 1.2.7.E.b, p. 66). Training provided by Saint -Gobain must ensure the  employability and success of all employees throughout  their working lives. The objective is to facilitate access to  training through formats that correspond to their needs  and expectations. Training is part of the Group’s major transformations, as  it provides support for employees and availability of the  skills necessary for the success of operational teams. Particular emphasis is placed on the skills that are  the most critical for the future of the Group and that will  therefore strengthen the employability of  employees in  the medium and long term. The training policy is structured as follows: ■ Supporting the transformation of leadership based on  trust, empowerment and collaboration, and  promoting ways of working that generate greater  efficiency and flexibility. This includes managerial  training and talent development; ■ Fostering growth and focus on customers: sales and  marketing performance, product innovation, services,  and business models; ■ Ensuring efficiency and operational excellence:  industrial performance, and programs dedicated to  Saint-Gobain’s major support functions (finance, HR,  purchasing, marketing, etc.) in order to improve and  adapt skills; ■ Implementing mandatory training (on compliance,  ethics, cybersecurity, etc.). All programs are in digital or hybrid format (in-person  and digital) and the majority of in-person programs are  regional, in order to maximize inclusion for all the  employees worldwide, and to reduce the number  of  business trips and minimize the CO 2 emissions and  carbon footprint. This applies in particular to the  “Unicampus” program, the objective of which is to  enable the  dissemination of a common culture of  operational excellence focused on the expectations  and  needs of  customers. To that end, the training  sessions focus mainly on marketing, sales, and customer  services or  innovation. The only global in-person  programs are  reserved for employees identified as  having high potential. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 70 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com The level of satisfaction measured in 2025 was strong  and improved compared with 2024: ■ 91% of employees confirm that they are satisfied with  their training (“I feel adequately trained to do my  work”); ■ 82% of employees are satisfied with the training and  development proposals (“I have opportunities for  learning and development within my company”); ■ 88% of employees believe they have improved their  knowledge and skills (“I feel I have improved my skills  and abilities”); On the other hand, “Learning” was ranked third best,  with a score of 97.32% in the Top Employer certification,  after “Ethics &amp; Integrity” and “Purpose &amp; Value”. New programs and modules were created in 2025 to  support the Group’s growth strategy in the areas of  Commercial Excellence, Customer Focus, and Value  Creation. These programs aim in particular to increase  skills in the areas of Key Account Management and  Specification Sales. Unicampus programs continued to be  updated and enhanced to accelerate the development of  skills in these same areas.  The Group has launched two online webinars to teach  the basics of artificial intelligence: ■ Understanding Generative AI; ■ Using Generative AI Everyday.  In total, nearly 11,000 employees have been trained,  and these two training courses will be recorded to make  them accessible to everyone at any time. Workshops  are also held on request according to specific needs. c. Striving for commercial excellence Striving for commercial excellence is a priority for  Saint-Gobain, and is one of the priority objectives of  its  “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan (see section 1.2.2.C,  p.  42). The idea is to foster a winning mindset in the  Group’s customer approach, to be the best commercially  and  operationally on every line in Saint -Gobain’s  portfolio, leveraging innovation and digital platforms.  The aim is to outperform the Group’s competitors and  underlying end markets. For several years, Saint-Gobain has developed a  structured customer approach, with an organization that  places knowledge and listening to customers at the heart  of its strategy. This requirement translates into an  approach based on detailed segmentation of its markets  and customization of its offerings, in a “go-to-partner”  logic. The Group provides its teams with effective  methods and tools to strengthen their operational  efficiency over the long term. It also makes a special  effort to intervene as early as possible in the value chain,  in particular by recommending comprehensive solutions  and working closely with architects and specifiers. This  integrated approach enables Saint-Gobain to consistently  outperform its competitors across all of its markets. Identifying customer expectations and needs makes it  possible to improve the existing product and service  offering, drive innovation (see section 1.2.7.A.a, p. 60), and  adapt sales approaches to generate customer loyalty. The development of this corporate culture – in which  every employee focuses squarely on making customers’  every lives easier – is one of the fundamental levers for  generating sustainable growth. Knowledge of current and future market expectations is  essential in order to achieve Saint-Gobain’s medium- and  long-term targets. For this reason, the satisfaction of its  customers is a priority for the Group, whose objective is  to be an organization focused on their expectations.  To measure customer satisfaction, several practices have  been extended to all Group operations: ■ On the one hand, the provision of tools and  methodologies to detect the main points of  satisfaction and dissatisfaction among actual and  potential customers, both direct and indirect, and to  determine the net promoter score, the only measure  common to all; this refined measure will eventually  make all customers the Group’s primary specifiers; ■ On the other hand, the regular deployment of  operational action plans to visibly improve the  customer experience throughout the customer  journey.  Thus, customer satisfaction measurements are carried  out on a regular basis for all operations. In the field, the brands are increasingly collecting  feedback from customers on an interpersonal and  transactional level, with the dissemination, internally and  across functions, of best practices in the area. These data  are cross-referenced with the context of the relationship,  as well as with the type of customers, in order to assess  the degree of satisfaction throughout their journey, i.e.,  across the entire chain of interactions between them and  the Group’s brands. In this context, the satisfaction  questionnaires already implemented have shown that  compliance and punctuality are  the  most frequently  cited negative points. Every piece of customer feedback  is useful in improving the  quality of Saint -Gobain’s  performance in concrete, operational ways. Expertise developed at the central level enables local  teams to accelerate the implementation of best practices  while adapting them to the specificities of their markets,  thus creating an ability to know and respond rapidly to  customer needs which, among other things, enables  Saint-Gobain to deliver commercial excellence and  outperform markets. The aim is to generate growth by creating the conditions  that facilitate the experience and satisfaction of  customers. In addition to tools, it is essential to involve all  employees in this approach. A network of “Customer  Experience Champions” facilitates the deployment of  this customer culture at the local level. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 71 To go even further, Saint-Gobain invests in tools, training  and programs to help its employees better understand  its customers’ needs. The aim is to implement a  widespread culture of commercial excellence and a spirit  of conquest, covering various aspects of the customer  relationship, from devising offers and  setting prices to  digitalizing the customer journey, analyzing margins, and  seizing opportunities specific to certain markets, such as  energy renovation. The aim is to develop a corporate  culture in which the obsession of each employee is to  make the day-to-day lives of  customers easier and  create the conditions for greater proximity, so as to  detect all the customer’s needs and  respond to them  through offers that mobilize the  Group’s full range of  expertise. One of the Group’s areas of excellence and  progress is training, in order to  build a growing  community of customer experience experts, through  training in “customer experience” methods and concepts  through the “Unicampus” program. In addition, a  platform called “Pulse” has been created to centralize  and make available to Saint -Gobain countries and  entities methodologies and tools for  managing the  customer experience, such as  “personas”, i.e., the  definition of groups of customers whose behaviors and  preferences are merged into  a  single predictive model  and interview guides to  encourage each employee to  develop a culture of customer feedback. All the aspects of this approach have the virtue of  positioning Saint-Gobain as a provider of complete  solutions to its customers , and constitute a powerful  lever for development, differentiation and growth  for the Group.  Looking beyond the measurement of satisfaction in the  strictest sense, all these systems create the basis for a  co-development approach; based on customer opinions,  a shared innovation process can be implemented. Actions aimed at specifiers are also carried out. In  construction markets, architects play a key role in the  choice of solutions chosen by direct customers. In  addition, local and central teams work regularly with  them to analyze existing products, identify avenues for  innovation, and develop construction techniques. For  nearly 20 years, Saint -Gobain has organized an  architecture competition for students, the aim being to  develop a project that incorporates the principles of  sustainable construction. Every year, Saint-Gobain selects  a country with an architectural challenge and works  closely with the local municipality to develop a task that  will respond to real social and cultural issues. Students  from universities around the world submit their best ideas  to compete and win one of the prizes in this international  competition. The 2025 competition was organized in  close collaboration with the city of Villefontaine, France,  Les Grands Ateliers, the village of Chimilin, and the  Alliance HQE-GBC. The challenge was to develop a  residential area (for students and visiting professors) near  Les Grands Ateliers, and to renovate a former school  building in Chimilin into a multipurpose building for local  associations and organizations. More than 1,300 students  from over 200 universities in 33 countries submitted  projects. Thirty-three projects were selected for the final  phase and the awarding of five prizes. To meet the specific expectations of specifiers   (architects, project owners, or project managers),  dedicated teams are set up in numerous countries.  They  support customers and facilitate the optimization  of construction projects. These teams also participate in  the development of technical Business Units such as the  “Façades” Business Units or pre-manufacturing. This  collaboration, essential to generating innovation and  developing more sustainable, high-performance  products, solutions and construction methods, is built  right from the start of architects’ training. To help the Group stand out more and increase the value  provided on the market, Saint-Gobain aims to adopt a  one-stop-shop approach enabling them to find all the  solutions they need in one place. This approach simplifies  the customer journey and improves their  overall  experience. In this respect, Saint-Gobain’s organization,  which places the priority on the local level, constitutes a  high-value operational driver, as it enables greater agility  and in-depth knowledge of each region, along with its  regulatory context and economic fabric. The  adjustments made in the summer of 2025 to  Saint-Gobain’s organization reinforce this dynamic,  placing all business lines under the responsibility of the  country and regional CEOs and setting up specialized  central teams to support them.  The Group also uses artificial intelligence to improve its  sales performance, for example by automating the  large-scale analysis of tenders. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 72 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.3 Sharing created value and contributions 1.3.1 SHARING THE VALUE CREATED PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SHARING CREATED VALUE AND CONTRIBUTIONS SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 73 €46,483 M €29,376 M SALES OPERATIONAL COSTS –  PAYMENTS TO SUPPLIERS €9,458 M €5,178 M €1,234 M Salaries and other  personnel compensation:  53.44% Taxes and  duties: 6.97% Share retained for  growth: 29.26% €300 M Retirement  contributions: 1.70% €342 M Interest repayment  including IFRS16  interest: 1.93% €1,166 M Dividends paid: 6.59% €21 M Investments in local  communities: 0.12% ADDED VALUE €17,699 M Other miscellaneous  income: €592 M 1.3.2 SAINT-GOBAIN'S CONTRIBUTIONS Saint-Gobain’s CSR strategy (cf.  section 3.1.4 B, p.  114)  relies on two levers for action: ■ deploying high-performant and sustainable solutions  that deliver benefits to stakeholders by maximizing  its contribution in terms of environmental impact,  health, and well-being; ■ mobilizing for sustainability by minimizing the  environmental and social footprint of its operations  across its value chain and with potentially affected  local communities. A – A decarbonated home Combating climate change and advancing the transition  toward a decarbonated world require the collective  mobilization and collaboration of all stakeholders:  customers and suppliers, citizens and employees,  governments and major institutions. Through solutions  that help reduce the construction sector’s environmental  footprint and its commitment to achieving net-zero  emissions by 2050, Saint-Gobain is making a significant  contribution to the fight against global warming. Solutions to reduce the carbon  impact of construction Saint-Gobain is deploying a global strategy aimed at  accelerating the reduction of the construction sector’s  carbon footprint throughout the life cycle of buildings  and infrastructure by developing a growing range of  sustainable solutions. This involves designing and  distributing solutions that are low in carbon emissions  during production, but that also facilitate energy  efficiency during the use phase (reducing heating, air  conditioning, and energy consumption requirements).  In addition to the greenhouse gas emissions avoided by  integrating these solutions into buildings, they also  reduce energy consumption and associated costs for  occupants, as well as reducing overall energy demand  both during peak periods and on average.  These solutions reduce CO₂ emissions, optimize the use  of natural resources, and improve the comfort, safety  and quality of life of occupants. Adaptation and resilience solutions Through its adaptation solutions, Saint-Gobain also  contributes to the resilience of its customers and end  users by responding to the growing needs of  construction markets facing more intense climatic  phenomena.  Thanks to the diversity of its businesses and expertise, its  partnerships and innovations, Saint-Gobain offers  solutions to address the dangers associated with the  effects of climate change (droughts, floods, fires, storms  and rising temperatures). Country platforms play an essential role in offering  adaptation solutions specific to the effects of climate  change in their territory. In this way, in countries  experiencing an increase in the number of heatwave  days, demand is shifting towards insulation solutions  and, more broadly, changes in building design to better  protect occupants from the effects of the sun. For  countries more exposed to the risk of storms, solutions  to reinforce roofs in particular can be offered. In addition to the need to adapt buildings and  infrastructure, Saint-Gobain’s approach also takes into  account the potential impact of climatic phenomena on  its customers’ construction sites. Solutions to improve  productivity or prefabrication can be proposed to better  organize construction sites and improve productivity. 2030 roadmap towards  net zero emissions in 2050 Saint-Gobain is committed to a long-term trajectory  aimed at achieving net zero emissions by 2050,  structured around ambitious intermediate milestones.  This roadmap relies on several complementary levers to  reduce scope  1 and 2 emissions: actions on products  (eco-design, weight reduction, composition, etc.),  industrial excellence (productivity, energy efficiency,  etc.), innovation (creating sites of zero scope  1 and 2  emissions in particular), and decarbonated energy  (electricity or other decarbonated energy sources).  Through its effective actions on these four levers, the  Group has reduced its emissions by 35% in 2025  compared to 2017, in line with its target of -33%  emissions in 2030 compared to 2017, in a context where  Saint-Gobain is developing a growth strategy for 2030  with the rollout of its “Lead &amp; Grow” plan. In this context, a new target has been set and the Group  is now aiming for a reduction of -40% to -45% by 2035. For scope  3, decarbonization is progressing more  gradually, as it involves a large ecosystem of  stakeholders. The most significant categories are  purchasing and transportation, with three key levers for  reduction: reformulating product compositions,  improving logistics performance, and decarbonizing  purchases linked to actions taken directly by suppliers. Saint-Gobain has also implemented a plan to adapt its  industrial sites, which strengthens the Group’s resilience  to the physical risks associated with climate change:  adapting industrial sites and the supply chain to climate  events, but also protecting its employees from extreme  working conditions. The Group’s efforts and the quality of its actions have  once again been rewarded with its inclusion in the CDP’s  “Climate A List.” PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SHARING CREATED VALUE AND CONTRIBUTIONS 74 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) Life Cycle Assessment. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SHARING CREATED VALUE AND CONTRIBUTIONS SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 75 The categories referred to are the most significant ones. Scope 3 cat. 4  and 9: 5 Mt CO2e Scope 3 cat. 1:  10.9 Mt CO2e Scope 3 cat. 3:  5 Mt CO2e Scope 1 and 2: 8.7 Mt CO2e B – More performance with less The construction sector has a major impact on the  environment, accounting for 40% of solid waste and  almost 50% of the consumption of natural resources.  Circular models contribute to environmental protection  and the preservation of resources and nature. Proposing solutions that promote  the development of circular flows  Saint-Gobain invests in innovation and eco-design in  order to offer increasingly sustainable products ,  particularly in the field of lightweight construction. The  Group is therefore continuing its efforts in terms of both  its products and solutions, while also working with its  customers to anticipate the use and demolition phases  of residential and non-residential buildings: optimizing  formulations to incorporate more recycled or renewable  raw materials, simulating the optimal deconstruction of a  building to predict which materials can be recycled and  facilitate their recovery, increasing the modularity of  buildings to facilitate changes in use and thus extend  their occupancy time… Developing circularity drawing on the  Group’s country platforms and expertise Saint-Gobain draws on both the Group’s expertise, which  drives a global vision, know-how and operational  excellence, and on country platforms with in-depth  knowledge of markets and customers, to respond locally  to the challenges on the ground. This combination of  global expertise and local presence enables Saint-Gobain  to implement concrete solutions tailored to customer  expectations and the specific characteristics of each  market (such as distribution channels, regulations,  customer expectations, and construction market  characteristics). This is essential for developing circularity,  which by its very nature can only be achieved at the local  level. Local production, local markets and customers and  leadership, that brings stakeholders together, are all  assets that country platforms can leverage to seize  growth opportunities linked to circularity. Optimizing resources through  the creation of recycling channels To optimize the use of raw materials, Saint-Gobain takes  action on several fronts. The Group first seeks to reduce  waste generation, whether from internal industrial  processes, construction sites, or demolition. Organizing  waste recovery along the value chain, setting up  collection channels (reuse, repair, recycling, or energy  recovery), and initiating waste treatment solutions for  reuse in production are all examples of partnerships and  solutions offered by country platforms. These local  partnerships enable us to work in synergy with other  players in the construction sector to recover and  reintroduce reused or recycled materials or products,  which also requires close dialog with stakeholders. In  parallel with this hands-on management, the Group’s  expertise plays an essential role in product innovation,  the promotion of the circular economy, and R&amp;D aimed  at accelerating the development of recycling channels  and the capacity for reuse or recycling in the  construction sector. Reducing the use  of non-renewable raw materials To reduce the use of non-renewable raw materials,  Saint-Gobain is taking action by reducing the natural  resource intensity of its products through eco-design:  making products and solutions lighter, changing  formulations and substituting non-renewable materials  with recycled or renewable materials, facilitating  recyclability, etc.  Beyond this reduction, Saint-Gobain’s approach is part of  the goal to reduce the intensity of non-renewable raw  materials in the construction sector: developing  modularity that limits waste at source and facilitates  waste management, seeking to increase the lifespan of  materials such as buildings and infrastructure, or  promoting lightweight construction that halves the  amount of materials required compared to traditional  methods. These resources, which are recyclable, recycled,  renewable, biosourced, or derived from co-products, are  directly integrated into the Group’s solutions. Thanks to  this dual approach, Saint-Gobain designs solutions that  are more economical, lighter, and incorporate a  growing proportion of alternative materials , thereby  helping to preserve natural resources and reduce the  environmental impact of the construction sector. Preserving water Faced with increasing water stress and rising demand for  water, Saint-Gobain is strengthening its responsible  water management, particularly with regard to  withdrawals and discharges in areas under water stress  and taking into account the water needs of local  stakeholders. The Group has set ambitious targets,  aiming to achieve zero liquid discharge by 2030 in areas  of extremely high water risk and to minimize water  withdrawals at all its sites, particularly those located in  regions subject to water stress. As a result, 86% of the  water consumed is reused thanks to closed-loop water  reuse systems. An internal water price has been in place  since 2024 and differentiates investments made in areas  under water stress. Protecting biodiversity  and combating deforestation Saint-Gobain is committed to Act4Nature International  (commitment renewed in 2025), reaffirming its  willingness to integrate biodiversity issues into all its  activities. Saint-Gobain has made a commitment to non- deforestation and is implementing a “Forest” policy,  supplemented by two specific policies published or  updated in 2025 (Timber Policy and Wood Pulp Policy).  In 2025, the Group published its Sustainability Briefs  entitled “Forests, Our Commitment,” which presents the  new Forest Policy and the concrete actions Saint-Gobain  is implementing to preserve forest ecosystems. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SHARING CREATED VALUE AND CONTRIBUTIONS 76 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) Life Cycle Assessment. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SHARING CREATED VALUE AND CONTRIBUTIONS SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 77 C – A better living for all  Saint-Gobain bases its development on its purpose –  Making the world a better home – and on strong values.  Through its business model and, in particular, thanks to  the solutions it provides, Saint-Gobain contributes to  having a tangible positive impact on all its stakeholders,  including individuals, local communities, and the  environment. In practice, this translates into respect for  human rights, a responsible purchasing policy, and the  preservation of people’s health and safety throughout  the value chain. Well-being and comfort solutions Saint-Gobain is developing a range of solutions  designed to improve comfort, convenience, and well- being for end users. The Group’s innovations and choice  of suitable materials can help improve cleanliness,  hygiene, air quality, and comfort in living spaces while  limiting health risks. These solutions are particularly  relevant in non-residential buildings such as schools and  hospitals, where indoor environmental quality is  essential. Through this offering, Saint-Gobain provides a  concrete and sustainable response to users’ needs.  When designing products, Saint-Gobain also takes into  account the working conditions of users. Improving  ergonomics for installers and anticipating risks on  construction sites are two key aspects of Saint-Gobain’s  product innovation approach. Employee commitments  For the 11 th consecutive year, Saint-Gobain has been  awarded the “Top Employer Global” certification, which  highlights the quality of the company’s commitment to  its employees, particularly in terms of diversity, inclusion,  and development of opportunities and skills. The high  participation rate in the annual me@Saint-Gobain survey  (88%) and the high level of recommendation of the  company as a good place to work (85%) reflect a  relationship of mutual trust, based on listening and the  involvement of everyone.  In addition, the Saint-Gobain Foundation supports Group  employees in their personal commitments to local  communities. Since its creation in 2008, the Foundation  has supported more than 400 projects sponsored by  employees in 44 countries promoting social housing and  the professional integration of excluded people.  Preserving employees’ health  and security The health and safety of employees are top priorities,  and Saint-Gobain places them at the heart of its  corporate culture. While the frequency rate of accidents  with and without lost time among employees, temporary  workers and permanent subcontractors has been halved  since 2017, Saint-Gobain is strengthening its measures  for managing critical situations at all levels of the  organization.  Eliminating all workplace accidents, everywhere, for  everyone, at all times, is Saint-Gobain’s daily priority. In  this context, the Group reiterates its strong commitment  to making the long-term absence of fatal accidents a  priority objective and is rolling out numerous awareness- raising and prevention initiatives targeting work  situations that could lead to serious accidents on its  sites, for its employees as well as temporary workers and  subcontractors. Responsible purchasing Saint-Gobain is continuing the sustainable transformation  of its supply chain by strengthening the management of  risks related to natural raw materials, in particular through  its “Forest” policy. In addition, Saint-Gobain is mobilizing  its suppliers around ambitious environmental and social  requirements, supported by the development of  recycling channels, such as those for paper, which in  many countries achieve very high rates of recycled  material integration. The Group regularly conducts CSR  audits, particularly in the most sensitive regions, in  accordance with international standards, and implements  corrective action plans with suppliers when necessary. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SHARING CREATED VALUE AND CONTRIBUTIONS 78 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) Life Cycle Assessment. PRESENTIN