onized and economically viable  energy in sufficient quantities will be an indispensable  factor in achieving zero net emissions by 2050. The associated financial resources will also be assessed. A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 133 3.2.5 2025 PERFORMANCE (CLIMATE) OBJECTIVES Deadline 2025 data 2024 data (2) 2023 data Baseline -40 to-45% in scope 1 and 2 GHG emissions  between 2017 and 2035 2035  -35.1%  -36.8 %  -34.3 % 8.7 Mt 8.5 Mt 8.8 Mt 13.4 Mt  -16% in scope 3 GHG emissions between 2017  and 2030 (1) 2030  +34.8 %  +36.5 %  +40.8 % 23.4 Mt 23.7 Mt 24.4 Mt 17.4 Mt - 15% in energy consumption between 2010  and 2025 (at iso-production) 2025  -5.9 %  -5.6 %  -6.7 % - 20% in scope 1 and 2 CO2e emissions  between 2010 and 2025 (at iso-production) 2025  -35.4 %  -32.9 %  -30.8 % 100% of revenue generated with products  covered by verified LCA or EPD, excluding  distribution activity 2030  57.4 %  57.7 %  53.5 % (1) Methodology and scope of calculation for scope 3 aligned with the Science Based Targets 2030 (SBTi) initiative. Scope has been expanded compared to 2017 thanks to improved data collection. (2) Data for scope 1 and 2 in 2024 exclude recent acquisitions. 2024 CO2e is estimated at 8.9 Mt, including the full-year effect of acquisitions. The 2010-2025 targets for iso-production were  established in 2014, prior to the implementation of the  “Transform &amp; Grow” and “Grow &amp; Impact” strategic plans.  Given the rotation of the asset portfolio and changes in  the geographical footprint, they no longer reflect the  Group’s performance. As a result, medium-term targets  in absolute terms have been gradually introduced.  Nevertheless, by the end of 2025, most of the targets set  had been achieved:  ■ CO2 emissions and energy: exceeded CO2e emission  reduction targets for scope  1 and 2 (-35% vs. -20%)  thanks to the use of carbon-free energy, offsetting the  shortfall in energy consumption reduction targets  (-6% vs. -15%). The Group has significantly changed its  energy mix, favoring the transition from high-carbon  energies such as fuel oil to lower-carbon energies  such as natural gas and decarbonized energies such  as decarbonized electricity. The choice of energy type  has an impact on the consumption measured for the  same use. ■ Other emissions: Sox and Nox emission reduction  targets have been significantly exceeded. The dust  emission reduction target, although regularly  exceeded in recent years, has not been achieved in  2025. ■ Water discharges: the effect of changes in the asset  portfolio is particularly significant for this indicator.  The reduction achieved remains substantial, even if it  is far from the target of -48% vs. -80%. ■ Waste: with a reduction of 47%, the result achieved is  close to the target of 50%. A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 134 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com GHG EMISSIONS 2025 2024 (2) 2023 ESRS GRI SDG Market based Gross direct GHG emissions (scope 1) 7,907,492 t 7,628,642 t 7,738,985 t E1-6 305-1 12; 13 Change in direct GHG emissions (scope 1) 278,850 t -110,343 t -657,341 t E1-6 305-5 12; 13  3.66%  -1.43%  -7.83 % E1-6 305-5 12; 13 Percentage of direct GHG emissions (scope 1)  from regulated emission trading schemes  EU-ETS  35.9%  34.4% E1-6 305-5 12; 13 Gross indirect GHG emissions (scope 2) -  market-based 802,233 t 856,282 t 1,088,365 t E1-6 305-5 12; 13 Change in indirect GHG emissions (scope 2) -  market based -54,049 t -232,083 t -317,677 t E1-6 305-5 12; 13 Percentage of indirect GHG emissions (scope  2) from contractual instruments  69.7%  68.2% E1-6 305-5 12; 13 GHG emissions (scope 1+2) - market based 8,709,725 t 8,484,924 t 8,827,350 t E1-6 305-1 &amp; 2 12; 13 Change in total GHG emissions (scope 1+2) -  market based 224,801 t -342,426 t -975,018 t E1-6 305-5 12; 13 Other indirect GHG emissions (scope 3) -  Group (1) 78,371,616 t 79,403,867 t 81,859,656 t E1-6 30