mediation  and other clean-up costs €14.5 M €15.3 M €19.3 M E2-6 9 Capital expenditure on environmental  management measures €159.8 M €157.1 M €147.1 M E2-6 13 Provisions for environmental risks €211.6 M €225.7 M €221.0 M E2-6 13 Number of serious major Group  environmental events or accidents (1) 0 0 0 E2-6 12 Number of “Environment” certified sites  (ISO 140001 and/or EMAS) 626 593 580 E2-6 12 Proportion of “Environment” certified  sites in scope of consolidation  (ISO 140001 and/or EMAS)  72.3 %  72.0 %  71.7 % E2-6 12; 13; 15 Number of sites certified for Energy  management (ISO 50001) 168 163 155 E2-6 7; 12; 13  Proportion of sites certified for Energy  management (ISO 50001)  26.5 %  24.0 %  23.4 % E2-6 7; 12; 13  Number of quality-certified sites 801 747 648 E2-6 9; 12; 13 Of which ISO 9001 749 692 582 E2-6 9; 12; 13 Proportion of certified sites  51.1 %  52.7 %  59.5 % E2-6 9; 12; 13 Variation in production in sellable units  -0.4 %  +3.4 %  -10.6 % E2-6 13 (1) See definition section 3.1.4.B.b, p. 63. – Definition change in 2025. Data update for 2023 &amp; 2024. A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 137 3.3 More performance with less 3.3.1 GOVERNANCE AND STRATEGY The construction sector is responsible for the consumption of 50% of natural resources and generates 40% of solid  waste every year. Transforming the industry toward more sustainable building practices is essential to lessen its  impact on the environment – especially considering that 50% of the buildings that will exist in 2050 have yet to be  constructed. A – Governance A COMMITTED GROUP MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS 138 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com The Senior Vice President, Human Resources and Corporate  Social Responsibility is in charge of corporate social  responsibility. She oversees the incorporation of ESG issues  into strategy, coordinates the CSR roadmap, and ratifies  the objectives The Sustainable Development Department manages  climate-related issues, which represent both a risk  and an opportunity for the Group. The Corporate Social Responsibility Department’s role  is defined in section 3.1.4.1, p. 113. The organizational chart for governance of CSR issues  by the Board of Directors and the Executive Committee  is defined in section 3.1.4.A, p. 113. At Group level: ■ the Environment Department identifies priority sites  based on measured impacts and environmental  sensitivity, monitors the implementation of action  plans and disseminates best practices; ■ the Innovation Department coordinates innovation  efforts in processes, both industrial and related to  product design; ■ the Technology and Industrial Performance  Department oversees the Group’s industrial verticals  as well as the manufacturing digital transformation  program, the coordination of industrial excellence  programs, including the “World Class Manufacturing”  program, and capital expenditure; ■ the Purchasing Department ensures mobilization  across the supply chain, in particular the commitment  of suppliers to reduce their footprint on nature,  develop the recyclability of purchased raw materials  and the share of recycled material in raw materials. Countries contribute to the objective  of reducing the environmental footprint: ■ roadmaps are drawn up by the countries so that they  can define their short- and medium-term priorities  and objectives, in line with Saint-Gobain’s, and draw  up their own action plans; ■ detailed roadmaps are developed for each industrial  process and supplemented by action plans designed  and deployed by each country based on knowledge  of local markets and regulatory contexts; ■ each site defines its own roadmap for the WCM  program, in line with international reference  frameworks, standards specific to its business required  by customers, and the improvement objectives  that have been set. Each industrial department  and each Business Unit is responsible for defining,  leading and monitoring the results of the sites  within its scope. Reduce the environmental footprint of operations  A COMMITTED GROUP MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 139 Countries: ■ develop and market  solutions that meet  customer needs and  sustainability matters  (see section 1.2.7.A, p. 60),  in particular circularity- related service offerings; ■ develop partnerships with  stakeholders to promote the  development of lightweight,  sustainable construction; ■ promote the creation  of recycling channels.  Developing growth drivers  At Group level: ■ the Strategy Department supervises the definition and  characteristics of sustainable and high-performance solutions  and coordinates their deployment; ■ the Performance Segments facilitate and accelerate  Saint-Gobain’s innovation process, based on meeting customer  expectations. The teams specializing in innovation and  management ensure consistency and relevance of use cases and  best practices. The teams specializing in sustainable construction  ensure the coordination of the measurement of estimated  benefits; ■ the Sustainable Construction Department ensures the  coordination of initiatives and the relevance of the assessment  of the benefits of the solutions for the customer; ■ the Environment, Health and Safety Department provides  technical support and expertise on environmental issues, in  particular on the use of substances of concern or on recyclability  and recycling channels.  B – Strategy Construction markets are responding both to the need  for buildings driven by demographics and growing  urbanization in developing countries, and to the need for  renovation, the benefits of which in terms of energy  efficiency and CO2 emissions reduction are essential in  the fight against climate change, under transition or  adaptation plans alike. In this context, the number of  geographical areas exposed to water stress is increasing,  while water demand is constantly rising. The circular economy is a model that helps reduce  pressure on non-renewable natural resources. Circularity  combined with an efficient and responsible use of  resources is definitely a response to sustainable  development issues, in particular the fight against climate  change and pollution, and the preservation of  biodiversity. For example, a study published by the  World Economic Forum in December 2023 indicates that  the circularity of materials can contribute to a reduction  of around 75% in emissions linked to the materials used in  the construction of a building by 2050, while creating  significant economic value.  Developing circularity is applied closer to markets and at  country and regional level. A successful transition  towards the circular economy will make it possible to  offer solutions and services over the long term which  take into account environmental, social and societal  expectations and which balance well-being, sustainability  and performance for stakeholders. Saint-Gobain is  actively and collaboratively involved in discussions on  the evolution of construction methods towards  lightweight construction solutions that use fewer  materials for at least the same performance. In this way,  the Group is participating in the transformation of the  sector towards more sustainable construction in all the  countries where it operates. Country platforms (cf. section 1.2.5, p. 53) and the “local  for local” model (see section 1.2.2.D, p.45) make it  possible to seize growth opportunities and develop the  partnerships essential to implementing circularity:  innovation in building design to increase the lifespan and  modularity of constructions, waste collection and  treatment channels throughout the value chain to create  circular material flows, integration of these alternative  raw materials into production, and proposals for reusable  or recyclable solutions. Success depends on the ability of  local players to mobilize. Operational excellence supported by the Group’s  expertise in innovation, industrial excellence, and  environmental performance enables country platforms  to further strengthen the confidence of customers and  other partners.  3.3.2 PRESERVING WATER A – Strategy The world’s water resources are finite and depend on the  geographical area under consideration. The effects of  climate change have a significant impact on the renewal  of water resources. As a result, the number of areas with  high and very high water stress continues to grow.  Depending on the location, access to water will have to  be divided between use by populations, agriculture or  industry. According to the World Resources Institute  (WRI), some 4  billion people are already exposed to  water stress for at least one month of the year. At the  same time, demand for water is set to increase by 30%  by 2050.  Reducing water withdrawals from Saint-Gobain sites is  therefore essential to safeguarding resources,  particularly in areas identified as water stress areas, while  preserving water quality, particularly to ensure access to  drinking water for populations.  The Group’s water policy confirms its desire to minimize  the impact of its operations on water resources and  establishes four resources for action and prevention:  ■ mobilize internal and external stakeholders; ■ implement action plans based on assessed risks;  ■ measure progress; ■ anticipate changes in regulations in each country  where the Group operates.  This policy includes specific targets such as the reuse or  recycling, internally or externally, of water used in  industrial processes, and also the challenges of conflicts  of use in regions of high and very high water stress.  Saint-Gobain does its utmost to: ■ reduce water withdrawals from the natural  environment and optimize processes to minimize  water consumption; ■ reuse “process” water whenever possible; ■ recycle on-site or off-site with appropriate treatments; ■ not harm water quality by reducing and controlling  discharges into the natural environment and  preventing accidental pollution; ■ not compete with local populations for access to  drinking water. Policy Water Policy Purpose Set strategic guidelines for  sustainable water management Scope of application All entities Under the supervision of Senior Vice President, Human  Resources and Corporate Social  Responsibility Link to the policy Water Policy A COMMITTED GROUP MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS 140 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com The long-term objective is to withdraw as little water as  possible, especially from water-stressed areas, and to  aim for zero discharges to avoid generating new impacts  for other natural environments and/or for other  stakeholders.  In the medium term, Saint-Gobain has set itself the  following objectives: ■ 50% reduction in water withdrawals in absolute value  between 2017 and 2030; ■ zero liquid water discharge in areas with extremely  high water risk by 2030. The associated action plan is based on the  environmental management framework applied by each  of the Group’s industrial sites (see section 3.3.4, p. 147),  the “WCM” program (see section 1.2.7.F.a, p. 67), and the  action plans of the sites established according to the  specific risks identified and deployed under the  responsibility of the countries.  At Group level, the “Focus Sites” program (see section  3.7.1.B, p.  206) entails supporting those sites which  contribute 80% of the Group’s environmental indicator.  In 2025, 44 sites (42 in 2024) contributed 80% of  Saint-Gobain’s water discharges and 135 sites (113 in  2024) 80% of water withdrawals. Each of these focus  sites has drew up a short-, medium-, and long-term  action plan to reduce its impact. To assess the water sensitivity of the location of its sites,  the Group uses the global “Aqueduct” atlas of the WRI  organization. This atlas is based not only on qualitative  and quantitative physical risks (such as water stress or  flood risk), but also on stakeholder risk (such as access  to water). This tool enables each industrial site to assess  its water risk from “low” to “extremely high.” In 2025,  303  sites (274 in 2024) accounting for approximately  19% of Saint -Gobain’s water withdrawals and 9% of its  discharges were located in high-risk or extremely high- risk areas. An internal water price mechanism applying according  to the same model as the internal carbon price was  implemented in 2024. This internal price is set at €1/m3  for investments made in low or moderate water stress  areas and €2/m3 for investments made in high or very  high water stress areas.  B – Risks and opportunities  across the value chain The material risks and opportunities identified during the  double materiality analysis concern the risks of water  pollution linked to the use of chemicals and substances  of concern on sites and in the value chain, and the use of  water in areas experiencing water stress. These are  described in section 3.1.3.B, Environment, p. 106.  Risks related to accidental releases from industrial sites  are listed in section 5.1.1, p. 290.  Regulatory changes related to the use of these products  and substances could force the Group to accelerate its  program to replace substances of concern, including the  risk of having to make additional R&amp;D and investment  efforts. The costs of raw materials, water treatment, and  operations during composition substitutions could be  affected.  Civil society’s attention to this issue could pose a  reputational risk for the Group.  Saint-Gobain’s range of sustainable solutions includes a  program to replace substances of concern (see section  1.2.7.A.b, p.  61). The Group’s ability to anticipate  customer expectations in this area represents an  opportunity.  C – Managing water-related risks a. Control over the quality  of discharges into water The impacts of Saint-Gobain industrial sites on water  quality have three main origins: ■ water contamination following discharges from  industrial or construction sites; ■ the use of substances of concern in the value chain; ■ the use of plastics in the value chain. Most of the water discharges are from cooling plants  where the water does not come into contact with any  chemicals used in the process. Since 2024, an action plan to reassess and control water  pollution risks has been strengthened. The risk exposure  level of a site is defined by combining the risks inherent  in the industrial process deployed and the site’s  geographical location.  Each industrial Business Unit conducted an analysis of  the potential risks based on the raw materials inflows in  compositions, the frequency of contact between water  and pollutants at each stage of the process, and the  hazard of the pollutants concerned.  The risk level associated with the location of a site  depends on three criteria:  ■ the sensitivity of the natural environment surrounding  the site: – proximity to an area of biodiversity protected  under Natura 2000, – location in a zone of high or very high water stress  according to WRI; ■ whether industrial water is discharged via on-site  treatment facilities, via a wastewater or industrial  wastewater collection network, or directly into the  natural environment. The treatment resources used by the sites are  monitored. In 2025, industrial water is treated on-site  before discharge at nearly 70% of high-priority sites  (69% in 2025 vs. 72% in 2024) and at nearly 50% of  lower-priority sites. The remainder is treated via external  facilities. As a reminder, Saint-Gobain has an environmental  management system (cf. section 1.2.7.F, p. 67) deployed  across all industrial sites to identify and manage  environmental risks.  A COMMITTED GROUP MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 141 Lastly, a prioritization factor has been added based on  the pollutants present, in particular the use of substances  of concern. Reducing the presence of substances of  concern is an essential factor in ensuring the quality of  discharges. Saint-Gobain has implemented a program to  reduce the presence of hazardous substances on its sites  and in its production. These substances are identified  and a program to reduce their presence is initiated  (cf. section 3.4.2.A.c, p. 156). Concurrently, the innovation  program for Saint -Gobain’s products and solutions  (cf.  section 1.2.7.A, p.  60) incorporates the objective of  eliminating or at least limiting the presence of hazardous  substances on the sites and controlling potential risks to  health and the environment during production processes  and throughout the value chain. The Group’s contribution to the risks associated with the  presence of microplastics in water arises mainly from the  use of plastics in the construction sector. To avoid the  transformation of plastics into microplastics, the first  measure is the creation of a recycling route for plastics,  enabling materials to be collected and recycled.  In addition, Saint -Gobain has set up an action plan to  transform the packaging of its products so that this  packaging can be recycled. An objective of 100% of  recyclable packaging by the end of 2030 is supported  by action plans involving buyers, technical teams, to  adapt industrial equipment where necessary, and  marketing teams.  b. Reducing the impact  on water resources  Saint-Gobain uses water as a thermal fluid particularly for  cooling, as a washing fluid, as a raw material for the  production of mortar and gypsum-based products, and  to provide Group employees with access to drinking  water and sanitary facilities. This last use represents a  marginal share of Saint-Gobain’s water withdrawals.  For many applications, it is possible to create closed- loop systems. In this way, water withdrawals are limited  and correspond to the replacement of water evaporated  during operations. The proportion of reused water in  processes reached 85.9% in 2025. Investing in a closed- loop system is the first step towards reducing water  consumption and discharges alike. When water is used as a raw material in a process,  technical and R&amp;D teams work on optimizing consumption  and developing processes to reduce water requirements.  A new pilot project to reduce the water consumption of  the gypsum industrial process was successfully developed  in 2025, making it possible to reuse more than 80% of  water. These process improvements are being  implemented as a priority in areas where water resources  are already scarce, particularly in India.  To address the growing risks associated with access to  water resources, site continuity plans may incorporate  this dimension based on their location and the water  needs of other stakeholders, particularly the drinking  water needs of local populations and water needs for  agriculture. This applies in particular to sites in areas  exposed to water stress. Specific investments have been directed to priority  projects in the focus sites (see section 3.7.1.B, p.  206),  which account for nearly 80% of the Group’s water  withdrawals and the 303 sites located in zones of high or  very high water stress. In total, 52 projects (31 in 2024)  have been identified for 47 focus sites and 5 non-focus  sites but in areas of high or very high water stress. They  concern the detection and sealing of leaks or the  replacement of installations such as washing stations or  compressors.  In areas at high or very high risk of water stress,  rainwater recovery systems can accompany action plans  to reduce water consumption.  A COMMITTED GROUP MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS 142 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 3.3.3 RESOURCES AND CIRCULAR ECONOMY A – Strategy  The challenge of the increasing scarcity of natural  resources facing the world has many causes, all linked to  human activities, particularly urbanization and  population growth. Saint-Gobain’s aim is to promote the recirculation of  natural resources and manufactured products while  minimizing waste at every stage of the construction  value chain: upstream with suppliers, downstream in  customer operations through to the end user, and within  the Group’s own operations.  The supply chain for raw materials (natural resources,  manufactured materials or products) remains mostly  local, and includes purchases of over 500 types of  material. Over 50% of these materials are processed.  Given this complexity, Saint -Gobain relies on external  sources to assess the impacts and risks associated with  the materials it consumes, in particular the “Responsible  Purchasing” program’s risk mapping tool. Of the  materials consumed, around 60% are identified as being  at high or very high risk of becoming scarce, and less  than 5% are at very high risk.  A policy on the responsible management of resources  applies to the entire Group. The main priorities in the  implementation of this policy are the integration of  maximum reused, recycled or bio-sourced content into  its products and the maximum limitation of final waste.  Policy Circular Economy Policy Purpose Set strategic guidelines  for managing and reducing  the impact of resource use  throughout the life cycle  of the Group’s solutions Scope of application All entities Under the supervision of Senior Vice President, Human  Resources and Corporate Social  Responsibility Link to the policy Circular Economy Policy Saint-Gobain has set targets both on reducing pressure  on non-renewable raw materials and reducing final  waste. Access to data to measure the Group’s impact on  natural resources remains partial. Action plans are being  implemented to improve performance indicators.  Saint-Gobain will propose an update of its medium-term  objectives.  As regards waste, recovery at Group sites shows that the  “zero non-recovered waste” objective is attainable. In the  medium term, the Group has established the target of an  80% reduction in non-recovered waste between 2017  and 2030. This makes it possible to work first on  reducing the overall volume of waste at the sites and  then on identifying or developing recovery channels.  Action plans are implemented by each of the country  platforms according to the portfolio of products and  solutions on offer, the technical modalities of the  construction market, the maturity of stakeholders on the  subject of the circular economy, and local capacities to  create partnerships. Lastly, waste management  regulations and public policies encourage the application  of circular economy principles throughout the  construction market value chain. Technical synergies and business expertise are  fundamental to facilitating the reformulation of product  compositions and accelerating the substitution of natural  raw materials for renewable or recycled ones. These  changes potentially imply changes in industrial  processes, so the industrial and R&amp;D departments of the  business lines are at the heart of this approach. B – Managing risks  and opportunities The material risks and opportunities identified during the  double materiality analysis concerning resources and  circularity are described in section 3.1.3.B, Environment,  p. 108. While the construction sector consumes extracted and/ or non-renewable raw materials, applying circularity  principles presents opportunities for Saint-Gobain and  has positive impacts across the entire value chain. The raw materials used by the construction sector are  largely extracted and non-renewable. The negative  impacts concern both the impact on nature due to  extraction and the reserves of raw materials, which  increase the risk of resource depletion.  Reducing waste and treating it along the value chain  tends to reduce potential negative impacts such as  pollution and soil contamination from landfilling. While there are many opportunities for developing  circularity, they will only materialize through active  collaboration between all stakeholders in the sector at  the national or regional level. This is why Saint-Gobain’s  organization into country platforms and its leadership in  light and sustainable construction are assets that will  accelerate the transition to greater circularity.  A COMMITTED GROUP MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 143 C – Reducing pressure  on natural resources Saint-Gobain has updated the impact assessment on raw  materials used in the composition of products  manufactured at its industrial sites.  The assessment took into account: ■ the environmental impact, including an evaluation of  resource scarcity; ■ the Group’s dependencies based on the quantities of  raw materials consumed; ■ the inclusion of lists of critical materials as defined by  the EU, for materials used by the Group. For each of the raw materials identified, an operational  action plan was defined, cutting across the Group’s  business lines by bringing together purchasing,  marketing, R&amp;D teams and technical experts. Action plans aimed at reducing the consumption of the  most critical raw materials have been rolled out, mainly  targeting gypsum, sand, soda ash, iron ore, coke,  cement, asphalt, paper and plastics.  Critical raw materials were identified based on: ■ annual raw material consumption, identifying the most  significant in terms of mass; ■ the criticality of the raw material based on recognized  external sources, in particular the database used for  responsible purchasing risk mapping.  Saint-Gobain has two main ways to reduce pressure on  natural resources in its operations: ■ reducing products’ resource intensity; ■ replacing non-renewable raw materials with bio- sourced or recycled materials. a. Reducing natural resource intensity To reduce the natural resource intensity of its offering,  Saint-Gobain relies on operational excellence carried out  in the WCM program (see section 1.2.7.F.a, p.  67).  Performance in terms of quality and productivity  constitutes the first step in reducing industrial waste and  optimizing the use of resources. Most of the Group’s business lines are working to reduce  the resource intensity of their products by reducing the  average weight per product unit without affecting  technical performance. This is the case, for example, for  car windshields and plasterboard. Several countries have  already deployed the BANTAM program, aimed at  making plasterboard lighter by working on industrial  processes and product formulations. Some plants have  been able to reduce the weight of their standard boards  by more than 20% compared with their initial weight.  The SLIMWOOL program, rolled out worldwide since  2018, aims to optimize the weight of glass wool, while  guaranteeing the product’s thermal performance. b. Replacing natural resources with  recycled or renewable materials  or with by-products Whether substitution is developed on the basis of  closed-loop recyclable products, or by changing product  formulations and adding renewable or recycled materials,  or by-products from other businesses or markets, the  quest for maximum substitution of non-recyclable raw  materials is an element of the WCM industrial  performance improvement program (see section 1.2.7.F.a,  p. 67). The aim of this substitution is to maintain or even  improve the quality and competitiveness of products and  solutions, while reducing their carbon footprint. Thus, the  increase in the quantity of cullet (glass from recycling)  integrated into glass floats is underpinned by the  creation of a collection channel for windows or glazing  resulting from deconstruction (see section 3.3.3.D.b,  p. 146). This enables the traceability of the material and  quality control, and ensures the economic  competitiveness essential to the sustainability of the  circular economy. Similarly, in the United States, gypsum  recovery channels from renovation or demolition sites  are developing in an optimized geographical area around  industrial production sites.  Other Group products also accept the replacement of  virgin raw materials by recycled materials from other  consumption channels, recycling channels from  industries other than construction. This is the case in the  production of cast-iron pipes and ceramic grains and  powders. The production of glass wool can incorporate recycled  materials from multiple markets: glass from flat glass  recycling (window glazing or windscreens), packaging  glass recycling, and glass wool recycling from  construction or deconstruction sites.  These compositional changes, which consist in reducing  the contribution of extracted virgin materials and  replacing them with recycled materials from end-of-life  product recovery circuits, require adaptations to  industrial sites, particularly in terms of raw material  storage areas, composition workshops, and even the  industrial processes themselves. It may also be necessary  to set up ad hoc sorting and shredding facilities to  prepare secondary materials. Each country develops innovations based on its access  to new sources of recycled or biosourced materials, or  co-products. Actions concern not only products, but also  their packaging. Action plans have been deployed to  reduce packaging overall, increase the proportion of  recycled or biosourced materials in its composition, and  ensure that it is recyclable. For example, marketing  teams and buyers have been working together to  change packaging materials or their design to encourage  recycling in existing circuits. A COMMITTED GROUP MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS 144 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com The quantity of recycled material in products depends  essentially on the existence, in countries or even  territories, of efficient and sustainable collection  networks capable of supplying the sites. Even when  technical solutions exist to enable virgin raw materials to  be replaced by recycled or renewable materials, or by  co-products, increasing the proportion of recycled  materials in production depends on the availability of  these alternative materials. Efforts to increase available  volumes at an acceptable cost and quality are underway  (see section 3.3.3.D, p.  145), but the creation of supply  chains will be gradual, country by country. It is essential to reduce the content of hazardous  substances in compositions to avoid their being  disseminated or perpetuated in recycling cycles (see  section 3.4.2.A.c, p. 156). D – Optimizing the use  of natural resources Optimizing the use of natural resources means limiting  waste throughout the value chain. This translates into: ■ reducing the waste generated at every stage of the  life cycle of products and their packaging; ■ recovering waste through recycling channels, thereby  multiplying the uses to which materials can be put; ■ facilitating reuse, extending the lifespan of products  and buildings, and anticipating their end-of-life right  from the design stage. Saint-Gobain’s long-term ambition is to produce no non- recovered production waste and to maximize the  recycled, bio-sourced or co-product content of its  products and packaging. The Group believes that waste  should be considered as a strategic resource and that  secondary materials (from reuse or recycling) or by- products should be used whenever possible, as a  substitute for non-renewable virgin resources. Reducing the consumption of non-renewable primary  raw materials per functional unit produced and the  waste generated by industrial processes are the pillars of  the sustainable resource management policy. Overall synergies are possible across the businesses for  each industrial process or non-renewable raw material to  identify the deposits, material qualities or even best  technical practices and favorable technical innovations  that are conductive to optimizing the use of resources.  Cross-business working groups including the Industrial  and Technical Departments, purchases, sustainable  development experts on the collection and processing  of  recycled materials have been set up to develop  these synergies. a. Reducing waste generation  and maximizing recovery To achieve its objectives of reducing and recovering the  waste generated by its industrial and trading operations,  Saint-Gobain follows the “three Rs” principle: reduce,  reuse, and recycle.  The sites strive to reduce the waste generated during  production operations and seek to reuse production  residues in their own industrial processes. To that end,  sites may invest in waste reprocessing units and in  adapting their industrial processes to encourage the use  of recycled materials in production and to recover waste  as much as possible internally or externally. From the  recovery of paper from plasterboards before the  reintegration of plaster into the process to manufacture  new plates to the treatment of waste generated during  the production of mineral wool for recovery in the  cement or terracotta industries (tiles and bricks), the  industrial sites are contributing to the creation of  recycling channels and promoting waste recovery at  local level.  Lastly, inventory management of finished products is  also optimized to ensure a second life for unsold or non- marketable products. For example, Saint-Gobain works  in France with an association of Group retirees,  “Saint-Gobain Solidarités”, to act as a link between the  Group’s sites in France and local associations in order to  facilitate donations of materials, particularly unsold  stock. This serves to avoid the destruction of certain  stocks and facilitate their use.  Beyond their environmental impact and the waste of  resources represented by industrial waste from sites,  their recovery in recycling circuits prevents their burial  and potential environmental consequences such as soil  pollution or groundwater pollution.  Saint-Gobain generates a limited amount of hazardous  waste, 6% in 2025, of which nearly half is recovered. In  line with the Group’s health policy and in compliance  with local regulations, the management of hazardous  waste is subject to special monitoring to ensure the  health of employees, residents, customers, and users of  its products and services is protected.  Saint-Gobain strives to optimize the volume of packaging  for its products and to ensure that it is recyclable. To this  end, the Group has set itself the target of having 100% of  its packaging recyclable by 2030. In 2025, the proportion  of recyclable packaging reached 91%. A COMMITTED GROUP MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 145 b. Creating recycling channels  for construction materials  From plasterboards and insulation to glazing and asphalt  shingles, most of the construction products sold by  Saint-Gobain are recyclable and can be recycled in the  Group’s own production processes.  Saint-Gobain’s experience shows that, while techniques  for recycling and recovering construction and demolition  waste do exist, the implementation of sustainable and  competitive channels integrating waste collection,  treatment and recycling is highly dependent on the  region, country or even territory. It requires the support  of external partners, to educate customers and collect,  sort and reprocess waste, before turning it into  secondary raw materials suitable for reintroduction into  the Group’s processes.  Lastly, recycling channels will be more efficient and  competitive if buildings are designed to be more  modular and incorporate deconstruction from the design  phase. This will prevent material losses from mixtures of  materials experienced in current demolitions. Recovering waste on construction sites  and during demolition Whatever the construction products considered, for  recovery to be effective it is essential that craftsmen sort  their waste on site during construction, deconstruction  or renovation operations. Where present on a country platform, building materials  trading brands are organizing themselves to take back  construction site waste from the same types of building  materials, products and equipment they sell. In France,  construction site waste collection points for craftsmen  have been set up close to sales outlets. Various  partnerships have been set up with specialized  companies and start-ups such as Tri’n’collect to speed  up the recovery, sorting and recycling of construction  site waste. Saint-Gobain has developed a network in Europe,  Saint-Gobain Glass Recycling, to facilitate the recycling  of flat glass from dismantling or processing waste. The  aim is to qualify local companies capable of collecting  and dismantling glass products, and which are  committed to promoting the closed-loop float recycling  of end-of-life glass. Some 20 partners have already  joined the network, offering processing and recycling  services for glass products. In France, the quantities of  end-of-life glazing from buildings returned to the  Group’s glass furnaces have more than doubled every  year over the past three years. Machines have been  developed by Saint -Gobain in the United Kingdom to  automatically separate glass from the rest of the  materials making up a window. These machines are now  deployed in several European countries. In 2019, the Group launched its I-LOOP project,  supported by the European Union through its LIFE  funding program. The purpose of this project is to  contribute to the gradual recovery of glass wool waste  generated on construction, renovation or demolition  sites, waste currently most often landfilled. The initiative  aims to offer construction market firms new value chains  for glass wool recycling in France, Germany and parts of  Scandinavia, as a cost-effective alternative to landfill. Ecophon, a producer of acoustic panels and ceiling  systems, has also developed a recycling and reuse  service called “SoundCircularity” to collect and recycle  end-of-life ceilings. This service is available in Sweden,  Finland and France. The ambition is to extend its  availability across Europe. Proven recycling and recovery technologies Once collected, waste needs to be transformed into a  new raw material.  For example, plasterboards recovered from construction  sites are reprocessed to remove contaminants; the  processing is completed by shredding and separating  the paper on both sides of the board. While plaster itself  can be recycled in a closed loop and under certain  conditions without any loss of material, the presence of  paper on both sides of the board has a negative impact  on the manufacturing process and must therefore be  eliminated as far as possible. Today, some Saint-Gobain  plants achieve an average recycled content of 30%, most  of which comes from construction or demolition  plaster waste.  For flat glass, the recycling of window glass, façade glass  or automotive glass is infinitely possible as long as the  collected glass elements meet the quality requirements  of a float furnace, in particular that contaminants of all  kinds are eliminated. Integration into products is  therefore mainly dependent on the existence of efficient  and sustainable collection networks able to ensure  sorting compliant with the desired quality specifications.  Glass wool from renovation, building demolition or waste  generated during construction operations can also be  recycled for new glass wool production. A new  technology has been developed by Saint-Gobain and has  been operational since 2023 at the Chemillé site in  France. This technology is gradually being rolled out  across Europe.  The abrasives business line is also working on the  development of technologies to recycle grinding wheels  once they have reached the end of their life, with the aim  of being able to recover abrasive grains for reuse as a  replacement for virgin materials. A reprocessing unit  already exists in Brazil. A COMMITTED GROUP MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS 146 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com The Valoref Group specializes in the treatment and  recovery of waste from technical ceramics and  refractories. For example, it recovers bricks during repairs  or reconstructions of glass furnaces from Saint-Gobain or  its competitors. The waste collected is primarily recycled  in the refractory production chain. The other recovery  circuits are the manufacture of construction materials  such as mortars or tiles, and other industrial additives. c. Facilitating reuse and recycling From the design of products, solutions and services to  the benefits expected by customers and end users and  products’ end-of-life management, Saint -Gobain’s  offering must enable more recycled, renewable materials  and co-products to be included so as to reduce the  consumption of virgin raw materials, facilitate the  recycling or reuse of products at the end of their life  cycle to successfully meet market needs with a limited  impact on natural resources, and extend product  lifespans. The same approach applied to products also  applies to the packaging that accompanies them.  Action plans are implemented at Group level and then  rolled out at national level to improve the recyclability  and increase the recycled or bio-sourced content of  packaging used.  The principle of optimizing natural resources is one of  the benefits identified as part of the program to develop  the offering of sustainable, high-performance solutions  (see section 1.2.7.A, p.  60). This criterion includes both  limiting the use of exhaustible natural resources and  integrating recycled or renewable materials or co- products into product compositions. While it is difficult to extend the lifespan of construction  products, it is possible to optimize the use of buildings.  Thinking about building design and construction  methods anticipates the possibility of modular buildings  to make them more flexible and adapt their use during  their life cycle. For example, Saint -Gobain’s offerings  include the ability to dismantle and reuse certain  products, such as lightweight plasterboard partitions.  For example, the 60,000 square meters of interior  partitions in the Athletes’ Village buildings were specially  designed to be dismantled, and the majority of materials  will be reused, in order to meet the Paris 2024 Games’  objectives of being responsible and exemplary in terms  of natural resource consumption. 3.3.4 CONTROLLING THE IMPACT ON BIODIVERSITY  AND THE RISKS OF DEFORESTATION The Group’s impacts on biodiversity are associated with  the markets it serves, and in particular construction  markets. New construction generates land take,  generating a risk of biodiversity loss. These markets  continue to consume large amounts of extracted raw  materials, particularly for cement production and  quarries, modifying the natural environment and  presenting a significant risk for local ecosystems. To reduce these pressures, lightweight construction (see  section 1.2.2.A, p. 43) is an alternative as it limits the use  of extractive materials. Depending on the context,  wooden structures can replace heavy concrete, brick or  cement structures. In addition, Saint-Gobain sources raw materials likely to  have an impact on deforestation, including wood for its  trading activities, wood pulp for paper manufacturing,  and wood for its packaging (pallets, crates).  Saint-Gobain reinforced its approach and action plans  when renewing its commitments to Act4Nature.  Validated in 2025 by this independent organization,  these commitments are published on its website and  that of the Group (Act4Nature). The commitments are based in particular on the  implementation of a Forest Policy (see section 3.3.4.B,  p. 148) and on the increased maturity of the organization,  which now allows it to more effectively anticipate the  potential impacts of its activities and supply chain. A – Biodiversity Saint-Gobain is committed to protecting biodiversity, in  particularly at its high-impact sites or in areas with  remarkable biodiversity. Thanks to the experience  acquired in the field of extraction activities, the Group  now has strong internal expertise in the area. It has  adopted a biodiversity policy aimed at preserving,  restoring, enhancing and boosting biodiversity and to  this end is encouraging the involvement of all relevant  stakeholders. A COMMITTED GROUP MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 147 Policy Biodiversity Policy Purpose Set strategic guidelines  for managing and reducing  the impact of the Group’s  activities on biodiversity Scope of application All entities Under the supervision of Senior Vice President, Human  Resources and Corporate Social  Responsibility Link to the policy Biodiversity Policy A mapping study of the Group’s sites assesses their  sensitivity to ecosystems based on their proximity to  areas with high biodiversity value (IUCN, Natura 2000,  RAMSAR). This analysis identified some 100 priority sites,  mainly quarries, for the implementation of biodiversity  management plans.  In 2025, 35% of active quarries had a validated plan  (28% in 2024). By the end of 2025, 15 of the Group’s quarries and  30 active sites (excluding quarries), i.e. 45 sites in total,  will be located in protected areas.  For industrial sites, a catalog of best practices in the  area  of biodiversity was created this year to facilitate  the  implementation of biodiversity actions at the  Group’s sites.  The Group’s quarries are exploited and then restored  with the aim of preserving the environment in  compliance with local rules, by reducing visual, sound  and ecological impacts as much as possible. Of the 108  (100 in 2024) underground or open quarries operated by  the Group worldwide, 85 were in operation in 2025, and  the vast majority are for the production of gypsum  (more than 50%). A policy on the environment and  biodiversity at all Saint-Gobain’s quarries and mines was  published in 2019, capitalizing on the experience  acquired over many years in gypsum.  Charter Mining and Quarrying  Environmental Charter Purpose Define mining and quarrying  management rules in order to  reduce the environmental impact  of the Group’s activities Scope of application All the Group’s mines  and quarries B – Combating deforestation To manage deforestation risks, Saint -Gobain has  implemented a Forest Policy applicable to all Group  entities and is committed to non-deforestation. This  policy is based on the principles recommended by  recognized international institutions in this area.  This general policy is supplemented by two specific  policies: ■ a timber purchasing policy in place since the early  2000s and updated in 2025; ■ a paper and wood pulp purchasing policy published  in 2025.  Policy Forest Policy Purpose Adopt a definition  of deforestation and establish  rules for purchasing raw  materials likely to have  an impact on forests Scope of application All entities Under the supervision of Senior Vice President, Human  Resources and Corporate Social  Responsibility Link to the policy Publication 2025 Saint-Gobain published a brochure entitled “Forest, our  commitment” in October 2025. The brochure specifies  that the Group purchases most of its timber in Europe  (93% in 2024, and 95% in 2025). The actions taken to control the risks associated with  timber purchases are described in section 3.4.6.C, p. 177.  With regard to the use of paper, suppliers have for many  years developed paper recycling routes in the main  countries where the Group operates, increasing the  recycled materials use rate to nearly 100%.  For some technical paper purchases, where it is more  difficult to integrate recycled material, Saint -Gobain  adopts a reasonable diligence approach and relies on  recognized certifications to verify the responsible  management of forests.  A COMMITTED GROUP MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS 148 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 3.3.5 2025 PERFORMANCE (RESOURCES) Saint-Gobain has adjusted its medium-term objectives to  align them with its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan,  published in October 2025. The “Virgin raw materials  avoided” indicator used until then was no longer  sufficiently representative of the Group’s activities or the  level of its ambitions. It was therefore replaced in 2025  by the “Rate of alternative materials” indicator, designed  to cover all of the Group’s activities and representing the  rate of integration of recycled (external) materials or co- products in material consumption (cf.  section 3.7.1.B.a,  p. 206). In 2025, raw material consumption is estimated at  53  million tonnes, 16% of which will be alternative  materials (cf. section 3.7.1.B.a, p. 206). An action plan is  underway to improve the quality and representativeness  of the indicator (cf. section 3.7.1.E, p. 214).  OBJECTIVES Deadline 2025 2024 2023 Baseline - 50% in water withdrawals between 2017 and  2030 (industrial sites) 2030  -25.8 %  -26.4 %  -24.0 % 41.6 M of m³ 41.3 M of m³ 42.7 M of m³ 56.1 M of m³ - 80% in liquid water discharge between 2010  and 2025 (at iso-production) 2025  -47.8 %  -35.6 %  -34.9 % Zero water discharge in area with extremely  high water risk 2030 0.4 M of m³ 0.3 M of m³ 0.4 M of m³ - 80% non-recovered waste between 2017 and  2030 2030  -27.1 %  -37.8 %  -45.5 % 0.4 Mt 0.4 Mt 0.3 Mt 0.6 Mt 100% of recyclable packaging 2030  91.3 %  86.6 %  94.6 % 100 % of packaging with &gt; 30% of bio-sourced  or recycled content 2030  84.7 %  82.0 %  81.9 % 100% of the active quarries with a biodiversity  management plan by 2025 2025  35.0 %  28.0 %  27.0 % - 20% in SOx emissions by between 2010 and  2025 (at iso-production) 2025  -68.2 %  -54.9 %  -86.1 % - 20% in NOx emissions between 2010 and  2025 (at iso-production) 