centers development centers 30+ 3,600 3,500 in 2025 by the end of 2025 by the end of 2025 agreements signed  with startups researchers employees in the  marketing field 1,170 2,653 12 by the end of 2025 by the end of 2025 by the end of 2025 manufacturing facilities  worldwide (a) sales outlets and  showrooms worldwide Sustainable Construction  Talks organized  worldwide (b) 162,000 80 132 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 26 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (a)   By the end of 2025 - cf. “Our global footprint”, pp. 12 et 13. (b)   Cf. section 3.1.4.B, p. 63. 1.1.2 MARKETS ON A STRUCTURAL GROWTH PATH Saint-Gobain’s business is structurally driven by  underlying trends (see section 1.2.1.A, p. 39) that provide  the Group with long-term growth drivers, not only in the  residential market but also in the non-residential and  infrastructure markets. Saint-Gobain relies on a robust  organizational model based on country platforms as  management units, grouped into five major regions:  Northern Europe, Southern Europe – Middle East – Africa,  North America, Latin America, and Asia-Pacific (see “Our  global footprint,” pp.  12 and 13). With an industrial  presence in 80 countries , the Group benefits from  growth opportunities in every major region of the world. A – Western Europe In Western Europe, a recovery is expected in the  residential markets, which have seen a contraction in  recent years due to the inflation surge of 2022-2024 and  the rise in real estate rates. Building permits and housing  starts are increasing in countries that  are  important to  the Group, such as France, the  United Kingdom,  Germany, and Sweden. As of late November 2025,  building permits were up 14% year-on-year in France  (1).  The decline in activity in  recent years has exacerbated  the housing shortage and the housing crisis in Europe,  degrading property purchasing power and encouraging  governments to support housing construction . In the  United Kingdom, for example, there is a shortfall of  2.5 million homes, and the current government aims to  build 1.5  million homes between 2025 and 2029, which  represents a clear acceleration from the construction  levels of the last decade.  The winds are also favorable for building renovation. In  addition to the ongoing recovery in real estate  transactions in most European countries, which are an  important driver of renovation, energy renovation is  supported by public measures , both at the European  and national levels. In addition to European regulatory  mechanisms, such as the Energy Performance of  Buildings Directive (EPBD), many European countries  have adopted regulatory arrangements (such as the  Climate and Resilience Act in France, which prohibits the  leasing of energy-inefficient residences), subsidies, and  market mechanisms (such as energy saving certificates  in Spain). Energy renovation also offers significant  financial benefits for households, through its ability to  reduce energy bills on the one hand, and also because of  the growing influence of energy efficiency on  property  prices. In many European countries, price differentials  between the most and least energy-efficient homes  exceed 15%, even comparing homes with equivalent  structural characteristics: this is the case in the  Netherlands, for example, as well as in Germany, France,  Italy and Spain. Non-residential and infrastructure  markets are also drivers in Europe.  The need for reindustrialization, particularly in the  defense sector, and the development of data centers  represent sources of growth for new construction, while  the energy renovation of tertiary buildings, both public  and private, maintains its growth level. In the  infrastructure sector, some European governments have  ambitious development projects planned; this is notably  the case in Germany, where the new €500  billion  Infrastructure Fund will significantly increase activity in  this sector over the next 10 years. The requirements of  renovating aging infrastructure on the continent,  including bridges and tunnels, offer complementary  opportunities. Finally, the increasing effects of climate  crises on buildings, both residential and non-residential,  imply significant adaptation needs . The solutions  offered by Saint-Gobain for renovation are perfectly  adapted to these new needs. For example, protecting  buildings against heat waves involves insulating external  walls or installing solar control glass. Similarly, the  Group’s construction chemicals solutions meet the need  for buildings to be watertight, against violent rains or  floods, for example.  B – North America  In North America, the housing shortage  is a very  important factor in the structural growth  of  Saint-Gobain’s business in the residential sector. In the  US, the pace of construction in the decade following the  2008 global financial crisis did not keep up with  population growth, and the shortfall is currently  estimated at 3.7  million homes  (2), which represents  nearly 3 years’ worth of construction at the current pace.  In Canada, strong population growth over the past  decade, fueled by dynamic immigration, has also led to  severe housing shortages, exacerbating the housing  affordability crisis: an additional 5  million homes would  be needed to meet the needs and restore the housing  purchasing power of Canadian households (3).  Furthermore, the frequency of hurricanes, fires and  storms, which is unfortunately accentuated by climate  change, implies non-discretionary renovation needs ,  which represent a source of sustainable growth for the  Group’s roofing and cladding business in the region. The  increase in such climatic events due to the climate  crisis will generate significant adaptation needs in the  coming years.  Finally, the non-residential and infrastructure markets  also benefit from solid structural fundamentals. The  reindustrialization policies of successive US  governments, as well as the trend towards reshoring  industries on US soil, are driving growth in the industrial  buildings market and the rise of data centers represents  a significant source of growth. As in Europe,  infrastructure needs remain high, particularly in terms  of renovation. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 27 (1) CGEDD. (2) Freddie Mac. (3) CMCH Canada Mortgage &amp; Housing Corporation. C – Asia and fast-growing regions Emerging countries are experiencing strong population  growth, combined with rapid urbanization. The global  population will increase from 8.2 billion in 2024 to nearly  10 billion by 2080 (1), and most of this increase will take  place in emerging countries. Similarly, the urban  population is expected to grow by almost 2  billion by  2050, particularly in Asia and Africa. In addition, around  1.1 billion people currently live in informal settlements and  are still waiting for decent housing (2). Based on this data,  the World Economic Forum estimates that  new  residential construction needs  worldwide will  reach  2 billion homes over the next 80 years.  Emerging countries are also experiencing economic  development trajectories favoring strong growth of the  middle classes , whose desire for quality housing  perfectly matches Saint-Gobain’s offer. In India, the  number of households entering the middle class will  increase by an average of 16% per year by 2030. This  growth has also reached 11% in Indonesia, and 21% in  Vietnam. Urbanization will also continue in the Middle  East and Latin America : in Brazil, for example,  the  middle class is expected to grow by nearly 4%  annually over the coming years  (3). This demographic  growth and its associated urbanization imply significant  needs for the expansion of  cities, with consequential  tertiary housing and  infrastructure needs, representing  complementary sources of business for the Group. Some  countries are  starting to build new cities or ambitious  urban development projects. In Egypt, 37 new cities are  under construction, and Saudi Arabia is currently  building 14 large-scale projects to support its economic  diversification program. D – Industrial markets For its industrial customers , Saint-Gobain provides  performance solutions and meets market expectations  through its ability to innovate , its research and  development potential, its closeness to its customers,  and its use of digital technology and data analysis.  Industrial markets are expected to continue to grow  steadily over the 2025-2030 period, driven by  reindustrialization and strategic autonomy efforts in  developed countries, ramped-up military spending, and  the development of new industrial sectors associated  with “green” technologies.  The mobility market, in which Saint-Gobain is present  through a range of insulation, acoustics, and technical  glazing solutions, is undergoing expansion fueled by the  natural turnover of car fleets, the transition to electric  vehicles, and the relocation of value chains by auto  manufacturers, trends that will structurally drive the  Group’s business in the coming years. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 28 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) United Nations. (2) Idem. (3) Oxford Economics. 1.1.3 A SOLUTIONS-BASED APPROACH,  ROLLED OUT COUNTRY BY COUNTRY  Saint-Gobain relies on the support of a robust organization that is well suited to a deglobalized world, as well as  local construction markets. It is based on country platforms as managerial units, which are grouped into five major  regions: Northern Europe, Southern Europe – Middle East – Africa, North America, Latin America, and Asia-Pacific.  Within each of these regions, the country CEOs are responsible for all Saint-Gobain product lines, to provide a  comprehensive response to the most complex needs of their customers, through a solutions-based approach.  Country by country, Saint-Gobain is deploying this approach in all its markets – residential as well as non-residential  and infrastructure. A – Construction and renovation markets a. Residential In all its countries, Saint-Gobain has successfully  deployed a solutions-based approach, enabling it to  position itself as a “one-stop shop,” a leading supplier of  integrated solutions both for renovation  and new  construction of single-family houses and multi- family housing. The Group provides a comprehensive range of solutions  dedicated to the global renovation of housing,  which  help improve their energy performance, acoustic and  visual comfort, as well as air quality. In France, for  example, a customer looking to undertake a  residential  renovation project can find an integrated offering from  Saint-Gobain that covers all their needs. First of all, this  offering includes comprehensive renovation systems,  particularly external thermal insulation solutions  combining insulating materials, plasterboard, profiles,  mortars, fiberglass reinforcement, and finish coats, as  well as solar-control insulated glazing units and blow-in  insulation for attics. When combined, these solutions  help cut a residence’s energy bill by 70%, and  significantly improve its energy performance diagnosis  (EPD), raising it from class G to class C. Saint-Gobain supplements this offering with digital  solutions available through its distribution businesses,  making it easier to conduct assessments and issue price  quotes for improving building energy performance. The  Group also provides services intended to improve  productivity for craftsmen on worksites , such as  logistics and kitting solutions. Aware of the  environmental issues, Saint -Gobain also provides  worksite waste collection and sorting services, relying  on a network of 810 collection and sorting points in  France, particularly at Point.P stores. Finally, the Group  invests heavily in training professionals: each year, over  100,000 craftsmen are trained and supported in France  on energy renovation issues.  In North America, and particularly in Canada in the past  few years, the Group has expanded its offering  dedicated to lightweight construction , in order to  provide a comprehensive solution for the building  envelope, helping improve the quality of buildings and  their resilience against climate change. Saint-Gobain stands out in particular by offering  complete systems for the customer . For example, the  Group markets integrated roofing systems under the  CertainTeed brand combining waterproofing  membranes, impact-resistant ClimateFlex shingles, and  their associated accessories. These solutions help  buildings better withstand storms and guarantee roof  insurability. The Group also develops siding systems that  are highly resistant to moisture and impacts, while  providing its customers with interactive digital tools that  make it easier to choose and customize solutions.  CertainTeed’s One Precision Assemblies system is  another example that helps significantly reduce project  completion times . Pre-assembled panels featuring  windows, doors, siding, roofing, insulation, storm- proofing and vapor barrier membranes are produced in  one week. Once delivered to a modular home worksite,  they can be installed in under three weeks, compared  with five months for a construction using traditional  methods.  To replace traditional, heavy-construction methods  which devour resources, time, and labor, Saint-Gobain  has developed lightweight façade solutions which are  both ultra-high performance and easy to install . In  Brazil, for example, such solutions combine Glasroc X  board, a moisture-resistant fiberglass-reinforced  plasterboard (Placo and Adfors), glass wool insulation  (Isover), fiber cement sheets (Brasilit), as well as  fiberglass reinforcement mesh and joint tape (Adfors),  and finish coats (Quartzolit) These systems are  supplemented by technical consulting and project  monitoring services.  They make it possible to  significantly improve productivity on worksites ,  particularly in multi-family housing, respond to labor  shortage issues, reduce waste, and offer superior  performance – in terms of quality, energy efficiency, and  occupant comfort – compared with traditional methods. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 29 Example: renovation of a multi-family residential building Example: construction of a single-family house in Canada  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 30 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com Eclaz Sun double glazing  with solar control Interactive digital solution for energy  renovation diagnostics External Thermal Insulation  Composite System Roof thermal  insulation Recycling  services Interior wall  insulation Multipurpose  screeds Acoustic  ceilings Reinforced partitions Heating and plumbing  installation Siding Waterproofing  membranes Ceilings Plasterboard Metal profiles Insulation Roof shingles Roofing  membranes  b. Non-residential Saint-Gobain is accelerating the deployment of its solutions-based approach in non-residential sectors by offering  ranges of solutions dedicated to each market segment (hospitals, schools, hotels, and data centers), meeting the high  demands of its customers in those markets, particularly in terms of energy and acoustic performance, visual comfort,  and air quality. Example of solutions for lightweight construction: school As an example, Saint-Gobain provides a comprehensive  range of high-performance solutions for the  construction and renovation of educational institutions:  ceilings, plaster-based solutions and acoustic flooring  systems help significantly improve the acoustic  performance of classrooms, contributing to better  learning and teaching conditions. To support the  specification of its solutions, Saint-Gobain also offers  augmented-reality digital tools through its Ecophone  acoustic ceilings brand, making it possible to test these  solutions in various acoustic environments recreating  classrooms. In another innovation, plasterboard and  ceilings equipped with Activ’Air technology help absorb  formaldehyde from the ambient air, thus helping  students concentrate better. The Eclaz glazing line  makes it possible to optimize interior comfort, with the  Sun solar-control line for façades that are heavily  exposed to sunlight, or alternatively, the Lumi range of  ultra-transparent glazing, maximizing natural light for  darker rooms. Finally, all of Saint-Gobain’s solutions for  energy renovation , such as its ventilated façade  systems and external thermal insulation systems, make  it possible to reduce heating expenses by 70% in  educational institutions and other public buildings.  Another example: Saint-Gobain is now recognized in  Europe and North America as one of the leaders  – in  terms of supplying construction materials – for data  centers. Saint-Gobain provides a comprehensive,  dedicated range of technical, innovative solutions for  meeting the sector’s strict requirements concerning  thermal performance, fire protection, and productivity.  On this note, there are a few examples we can mention,  such as stone wool-based sandwich panels which are  insulating, fire-resistant, and quick to install, its cool-roof  solutions, making it possible to reduce the temperature  of the building, its ceiling solutions that can support very  heavy loads, its range of solutions for  low-carbon  concrete, its special anti-static flooring, and its fire- resistant glazing.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 31 Customized kitting  service Acoustic  partitions Facade insulation Fire protection  glazing Self-leveling  screeds Suspended ceilings Low-carbon  delivery Solar control glazing  Example: construction of an office building c. Infrastructure Over the past five years, Saint-Gobain has built a place for  itself as a top global leader in construction chemicals. The  Group now offers solutions to meet the needs of  worksites and structures, particularly mortars, concrete  admixtures, cement additives, waterproofing solutions for  buildings and infrastructure, flooring, and façades,  concrete repair solutions, adhesives, joint tape and  adhesive systems. This offering is based on leading brands  like Weber, Chryso, Fosroc, and GCP. Construction  chemicals are essential for the infrastructure market –  bridges, tunnels, airports, hydroelectric power plants and  wind farms – a potentially significant growth driver.   Thanks to a broad range of technical products, the Group  is able to cover the various critical parts of any type  of infrastructure. In this context, Saint-Gobain offers, among others, highly  technical waterproofing solutions for bridges and  tunnels, concrete admixtures and cement additives that  help improve the mechanical performance and durability  of structures, as well as putties and anchoring systems  for fastening and connecting concrete structural  elements. The Group is also developing innovative digital  solutions, like Verifi and Maturix, which are used  to monitor concrete properties in real time.  Additionally, Saint-Gobain offers a comprehensive range  of ductile cast iron pipes  and accessories for water  transport and distribution through the PAM brand, and  with the Adfors brand, it designs fiberglass and technical  textiles used for the repair and reinforcement of roads  and airport runways. Thanks to this integrated, innovative offering,  Saint-Gobain sustainably supports infrastructure  development and modernization,  while meeting  increasing requirements concerning performance,  reliability, and durability. As an example, on the large  airport construction sites in high-growth countries like  Vietnam, Saint-Gobain stands out as the supplier of  integrated solutions  that address both the building  aspect – fire-resistant glazing and partitions, acoustic  ceiling and partition solutions, lightweight ventilated  façade systems, solar-control glazing, waterproofing  systems for flat roofs, or technical flooring for heavy- traffic areas – and the infrastructure aspect –  fiberglass for reinforcing runways and taxiways,  concrete admixtures, waterproofing solutions for  bridges and underground passages, and fireproofing  for steel structures.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 32 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com Ventilation duct  insulation “Cool roof”  waterproofing Highly energy-efficient  glazing Electrochromic  insulating glass Fire-resistant glass  partitions High-security glass Glass wool insulation Mortars for floor  covering Acoustic ceilings Perforated  plasterboard Low-carbon concrete Example: Saint-Gobain transport infrastructure solutions  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 33 Injections  for concrete repair Structural bonding Solutions for  low-carbon concrete Solar control glazing Impact-resistant  partitions Acoustic  plasterboard Fire protection  for structures Structural reinforcement  of taxiways Flooring Waterproofing solutions  for bridge decks In-transit concrete  quality control Protective coatings  for concrete Repair mortars for concrete  finishing and grouting Fiberglass  reinforcement veil Structural sealing  for concrete works Pipes  B – Industrial markets Country by country, Saint-Gobain deploys this same  solutions-based approach over its industrial application  lines. In a context marked by the energy transition,  decarbonization, and the search for performance, the  Group’s solutions make it possible to support the major  industrial players in their transformation. Saint-Gobain  is positioned as a key technological partner, offering high  value-added materials and systems. The Group offers a  comprehensive range of solutions for industry and  mobility, including technical and refractory ceramics for  glass furnaces, metallurgy, chemicals, and industrial  processes; abrasive solutions for cutting, polishing, and  assembly in complex environments; high-performance  polymers and films , offering thermal, chemical, and  dielectric properties; and glazing for mobility , for  automotive, rail and aerospace vehicles, with advanced  features (safety, comfort, design, connectivity). These  products are designed to meet the performance,  reliability and sustainability requirements of industrial  and transport markets. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 34 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.1.4 AN INSPIRING PURPOSE With its corporate purpose, “Making the World a Better Home”, the Group  took on its ambition to improve everyone’s lives by making the planet a fairer,  more inclusive, more harmonious, healthier and sustainable living space. Through its business model, generally, and with its solutions, specifically,  Saint-Gobain has a tangible impact on the life of every individual and on their  living, working, healthcare, educational and mobility environments. The  Group’s corporate purpose is the link between the infinitely small unit of each  person’s living space and the infinitely large one of our shared home: the  planet. It is fully aligned with Saint-Gobain’s vision – to be the worldwide  leader in light and sustainable construction – and with its strategic plan,  “Lead &amp; Grow” (see p. 16 as well as section 1.2.2, p. 42). Our corporate purpose sets the course for our shared future. It looks like us. It  represents a call to action, and is based on the values that guide us, the  Principles of Conduct and Action (see section 1.2.7.E.a, p.  64). This is the  profound ambition of our corporate purpose: to act every day to make the  world a more beautiful and more sustainable place in which to live.  1.1.5 360 YEARS OF HISTORY Saint-Gobain was founded in 1665 by Louis XIV, King of  France, under the name of “Royal Manufacture of  Mirrors”.  Compagnie de Saint-Gobain diversified into sectors such  as chemicals, glass products, and the automotive  industry, rapidly expanding into international markets,  establishing itself in the United States in 1829, Germany  in 1858, Italy in 1888 and Spain in 1905. The Group, which successfully overcame the major crises  of the century and invested heavily in research and  development, withdrew from its chemical activities and  merged with Pont-à-Mousson, created in 1856 and  specializing in cast iron pipes. In 1986, the privatization of Saint-Gobain – which had  been nationalized in 1982 – was a huge popular success:  1,500,000 shareholders subscribed to the transaction.  The acquisition of Norton in 1990, then Carborundum in  1996, enabled the Group to develop its know-how in  abrasives, plastics and ceramics.  The 2005 acquisition of British Plaster Board, the world  leader in plasterboard, combined with Saint-Gobain’s  insulation solutions, made the Group the world’s No. 1 in  interior design solutions. Saint-Gobain focused its strategy on sustainable  housing, while continuing to serve numerous industrial  markets, and pursued its expansion in emerging  countries. In late 2018, the Group launched its “Transform &amp; Grow”  plan aimed at establishing a new organization by country  and ensuring agile and value-creating portfolio  management.  In 2019, Saint-Gobain acquired Continental Building  Products, the first step in strengthening its positions  in the construction market in North America. Saint-Gobain established its corporate purpose,  “Making the world a better home”. The 2021 acquisition of Chryso made Saint-Gobain a  major player in construction chemicals. In October 2021,  Saint-Gobain launched its “Grow &amp; Impact” strategic  plan, setting out its vision to become the  worldwide  leader in light and sustainable construction.  In 2022, the Group finalized the acquisition of Kaycan – a  specialist in exterior building materials – and of GCP  Applied Technologies, a key step in establishing itself as  a leader in construction chemicals.  The following year, Saint-Gobain acquired Building  Products of Canada, a manufacturer of roofing shingles  for the residential sector, and wood fiber insulation  panels.  Saint-Gobain was certified as “Top Employer Global” for  its 2025 fiscal year, for the 11 th consecutive year. The  Group acquired CSR, a leader in construction materials in  Australia and New Zealand, as well as Bailey, a specialist  in metal frames for light construction in Canada, and  initiated the acquisitions of Fosroc, a leading global  player in construction chemicals, and Ovniver (Cemix  brand) in Mexico. These constitute new strategic steps in  establishing Saint-Gobain’s global presence in  construction chemicals, which, once the transactions are  complete, will bring its total sales in this sector to  €6.5 billion (pro forma).  In 2025, Saint-Gobain celebrates its 360th anniversary, a  figure that also represents its global reach and its circular  and comprehensive approach. In October 2025, the  Group launches its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan,  marking the beginning of a new chapter for  Saint-Gobain, based on a clear ambition: to accelerate its  profitable growth in a sustainable manner and  outperform its markets, thereby enhancing the value  created for all its stakeholders. At the end of 2025, the  Group is present in 80 countries (1). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 35 (1) Countries with active industrial presence as of Dec. 31, 2025.  1.1.6 LOOKING BACK ON THE YEAR 2025 January  Saint-Gobain certified as “Top Employer Global”  for the 10th consecutive year Saint-Gobain was certified “Top Employer Global” for the  tenth consecutive year, joining a small group of 17 global  companies recognized in 2025. This award highlights the  Group’s commitment to its 161,000 employees in  80  countries and its continued progress in diversity,  inclusion, sustainable development, and employee  development. Saint-Gobain completes the acquisition of Ovniver  in Mexico and Central America  Saint-Gobain completed the acquisition of Ovniver  Group (Cemix brand), a major player in construction  chemicals in Mexico and Central America. This strategic  transaction strengthens Saint-Gobain’s presence in  high-growth markets and strengthens its global  leadership in construction chemicals products. Saint-Gobain launches its first 100% recycled  plasterboard  Saint-Gobain announces the UK launch of its first  plasterboard entirely made from recycled materials, a  world first for the Group. Developed by British Gypsum,  this innovation represents an important step in  circular  construction and supports the Group’s commitment to  achieving carbon neutrality by 2050. February Saint-Gobain announces the launch of its first  low-carbon stone wool production line in Spain  Saint-Gobain will invest €40  million to expand and  decarbonize its insulation plant in Azuqueca, near  Madrid. The new electric furnace will replace fossil fuels,  reducing CO₂ emissions by 70% and increasing  production capacity by 40%, marking an important step  towards the Group’s goal of carbon neutrality by 2050. Saint-Gobain finalizes the acquisition of Fosroc Saint-Gobain completed the acquisition of Fosroc, a  leading construction chemicals company with a strong  presence in India, the Middle East and Asia-Pacific. This  acquisition strengthens the Group’s presence in  emerging markets and consolidates its worldwide  leadership in light and sustainable construction. March Saint-Gobain successfully carries out a €1.2 billion  double tranche bond issue  Saint-Gobain issued €1.2  billion in two tranches  –  €700 million (3 years, 2.75%) and €500 million (8 years,  3.50%) – taking advantage of favorable market  conditions to anticipate its refinancing needs. The bonds  were oversubscribed almost three times over, reflecting  investors’ confidence in the Group’s credit quality. April Saint-Gobain included in the CDP’s “List A”  For the second year in a row, Saint-Gobain is on the  CDP’s prestigious “Climate List,” which rewards its  leadership in environmental transparency and climate  action. Only 2% of the companies assessed receive this  distinction, highlighting the Group’s strong sustainability  strategy. Sustainable construction: global trends revealed by the third edition of the Saint-Gobain barometer  The third edition of the Sustainable Construction  Barometer reveals a growing global awareness and  urgency concerning sustainable construction practices.  Based on surveys conducted in 27 countries, the study  highlights the main regional differences and the need to  strengthen training and actions to accelerate the sector’s  transformation. May Saint-Gobain to launch its first low-carbon stone  wool insulation plant in the United Kingdom Saint-Gobain will build a new low-carbon stone wool  insulation plant in Melton Mowbray. Equipped with a fully  electric furnace powered by renewable energy, the plant  will produce 50,000 tonnes per year, meeting the United  Kingdom's growing demand for sustainable building  materials. June Saint-Gobain launches its world’s lowest-carbon  insulation plant in Finland  Saint-Gobain has unveiled the production of very low- carbon glass wool at its Forssa site in Finland. Fully  powered by biogas and hydropower, the plant will  reduce CO₂ emissions by 1,500 tonnes per year and  reduce the carbon footprint of its products by up to 40%. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 36 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com General Shareholders’ Meeting and Board of Directors  Meeting on June 5, 2025  Saint-Gobain shareholders reappointed Benoit Bazin as  Chief Executive Officer and approved a 5% dividend  increase, to €2.20 per share. Three new independent  Directors were appointed, reinforcing the diversity and  expertise of the Board of Directors: Maya Hari, a  Singaporean national, a partner at Modern Manifesto,  Antoine de Saint-Affrique, a French national, Chief  Executive Officer of Danone, and Hans Sohlström, a  Finnish national, Chairman and Chief Executive Officer of  Stora Enso. Strengthening of the local model and changes  to the Executive Committee Effective July 1, 2025 Saint-Gobain is strengthening its decentralized  organization to empower local leaders and accelerate  growth at the national level. A renewed Executive  Committee, led by Chief Executive Officer Benoit Bazin  and new Chief Operating Officer Patrick Dupin, will lead  the next phase of profitable growth to be presented at  the Capital Markets Day in October 2025. Saint-Gobain strengthens its digital platform  dedicated to construction chemicals,  with the acquisition of Maturix Saint-Gobain acquired Maturix, a Danish company  offering real-time concrete monitoring solutions. This  transaction expands the Group’s digital offering,  supplements its VERIFI® system, and enables smarter  and more sustainable construction processes. July Saint-Gobain sells Brüggemann Saint-Gobain signed an agreement to sell Brüggemann, a  German specialist in prefabrication solutions, to the  Köster group. This transaction is part of  the  Group’s  strategy to optimize its business portfolio. Saint-Gobain strengthens its construction chemicals  platform with three complementary acquisitions Saint-Gobain strengthened its construction chemicals  business with the acquisitions of Interstar Materials  (North America), Isoltech (Italy), and Soquimic (Peru).  These acquisitions expand its portfolio of sustainable  solutions and support its global growth, in line with its  “Grow &amp; Impact” strategy. August CSR enters into agreement for sale of major real estate  asset in Australia  CSR, an affiliate of Saint-Gobain, reached an agreement  to sell its Badgerys Creek property, near Sydney, for  AUD575  million (€320  million). Including previous  transactions, AUD900  million of CSR real estate assets  have now been monetized since its 2024 acquisition. September Saint-Gobain strengthens its organization  in the field of construction chemicals in Germany  Saint-Gobain is reorganizing its construction chemicals  business in Germany to strengthen its synergies and  market leadership. The Weber dry mortar business will  be integrated into the Franken Maxit joint venture,  creating one of the largest dry mortar manufacturers in  the country. Saint-Gobain unveils the first plasterboard plant  powered by 100% renewable electricity  in North America  Saint-Gobain’s CertainTeed plant in Sainte-Catherine,  near Montreal, has become the first zero-carbon  plasterboard plant in North America. Fully powered by  hydroelectricity, the plant has increased its capacity by  40% while reducing its energy consumption by 30% and  its CO₂ emissions by 44,000 tonnes per year. October Saint-Gobain enters into exclusive negotiations  for the sale of SFIC Belgium  Saint-Gobain is in exclusive talks with Ponsardin  Industries for the sale of SFIC Belgium, its building  materials distribution subsidiary. This sale, planned for  late 2025, is part of the Group’s portfolio optimization  strategy. Saint-Gobain signs an agreement for the sale  of the Tumelero retail network in Brazil  Saint-Gobain signed a definitive agreement with the  Brazilian group GG10, owner of the G-Haus brand, for the  sale of Tumelero, a retail chain specializing in construction  materials, with a strong presence in southern Brazil. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 37 New “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan: Saint-Gobain  leverages its leadership to accelerate  its profitable growth After the success of its previous plan (“Grow &amp; Impact”),  Saint-Gobain, the worldwide leader in light and  sustainable construction, launches its new strategic plan,  “Lead &amp; Grow”. With #1 positions in every region and its  country-based operating model, well-adapted to its local  markets and a deglobalized world, the Group is raising  its profitable growth trajectory, targeting  outperformance thanks to an unrivalled suite of  solutions: a strategy focused on growth and value  creation; an ambitious financial trajectory over the  2026-2030 period; and attractive shareholder returns. Saint-Gobain completes the divestment  of two distribution businesses: SFIC Belgium  and Tumelero in Brazil Saint-Gobain finalized its divestment of SFIC Belgium, a  specialist construction materials distribution business for  plasterboard, insulation and ceilings in the Belgian  market, on November 3, 2025. In addition, Saint-Gobain  finalized its divestment of Tumelero, a retail chain  specializing in construction materials with a strong  presence in southern Brazil, on December 1, 2025. Saint-Gobain cancels 4.2 million shares On December 3, 2025, Saint-Gobain cancelled 4,243,098  treasury shares purchased on the market. Following this  operation, the total number of shares composing the  capital is 495  million and the number of shares  outstanding is now 493 million compared to 497 million  at the end of December, 2024. The Group allocated  €402 million to buy back its own shares (net of employee  share creation) in 2025. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 38 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.2 Strategy and value creation 1.2.1 SEIZING OPPORTUNITIES GENERATED BY MEGATRENDS Saint-Gobain’s corporate purpose, “Making the World a  Better Home”, is directly linked to three megatrends:  global construction demand (in the residential,  non-residential and infrastructure markets) due to  urbanization, population growth, and social and  economic challenges; climate change (due to greenhouse  gas emissions), generating great unpredictability, as well  as more frequent extreme weather events; and the  scarcity of natural resources, reflected in the depletion of  non-renewable resources.  These three global megatrends generate key challenges  that the Group considers to be of importance (i.e., likely  to influence its performance) to its business model, and  which generate immense opportunities for Saint-Gobain:  population growth and urbanization, the aspiration of  the middle classes for quality buildings, energy  renovation, relocating business activities in the context  of deglobalization, adapting buildings and infrastructure  to climate change, and, lastly, the development of  circularity. In response to these major, closely related challenges,  Saint-Gobain promotes a series of relevant responses  throughout its value chain , involving its suppliers and  customers, as well as its competitors, and public and  regulatory authorities. These responses include  regulatory measures (e.g., promoting a stable legal and  financial framework focused on sustainable construction),  procurement measures (e.g., marketing reusable and  recyclable products), design measures (e.g., designing  buildings capable of withstanding more frequent extreme  weather events) and operational measures   (e.g., improving the recovery of materials when buildings  are demolished). The “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan   notably enables Saint-Gobain to contribute to these  responses. Therefore, it aligns directly with global  megatrends, the challenges that arise from them, and  the market opportunities they generate. A – Megatrends a. Global demand for construction The challenge  The urban population is expected to grow by almost  2  billion by 2050, particularly in Asia and Africa. In  addition, around 1.1  billion people currently live in  informal settlements and are still waiting for decent  housing  (1). Based on this data, the World Economic  Forum estimates that new residential construction  needs worldwide will reach 2 billion homes over the next  80 years, a sustainable source of  growth for all of the  Group’s solutions. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 39 (1) United Nations. MEGATRENDS GLOBAL DEMAND FOR  CONSTRUCTION CHALLENGES AND  OPPORTUNITIES (residential, non-residential, infrastructure) due to urbanization, population growth and social and economic challenges Growth and urbanization of global population INCREASING THE VOLUME OF NEW CONSTRUCTION Aspiration for quality buildings and infrastructure: BUILDING SAFE, DECENT, HEALTHY, COMFORTABLE, AND AFFORDABLE  BUILDINGS CLIMATE CHANGE greenhouse gas emissions, unpredictability,  extreme weather events SCARCITY OF  NATURAL RESOURCES depletion of non-renewable resources Energy renovation: ACCELERATE THE VOLUME OF RENOVATION AND REALLOCATION  OF EXISTING BUILDINGS TO INCREASE THE ENERGY PERFORMANCE  OF BUILDINGS AND REDUCE CARBON EMISSIONS Adapting to climate change: PROVIDING SOLUTIONS THAT INCREASE THE RESILIENCE  OF BUILDINGS AND INFRASTRUCTURE Resource scarcity and circularity: BUILDING AND RENOVATING USING FEWER RESOURCES De-globalization and relocation:  BUILDING LOCAL INDUSTRIES AND INFRASTRUCTURE DRAWING  ON SAINT-GOBAIN’S “LOCAL FOR LOCAL” MODEL  Although new construction needs are particularly acute  in emerging countries, developed countries are also  affected. Housing shortages can be seen in several  developed countries, including the United States,  Canada, the United Kingdom and Germany. The  deterioration in housing affordability over the past  decade implies, in particular, the need for quality but  affordable housing , an equation that lightweight  construction makes it possible to solve by reducing the  use of resources and construction time, thereby helping  to reduce the total cost of the building. Finally, urbanization and these global housing needs are  accompanied by requirements for local public services,  and more generally for improving the quality of life in  cities, thanks to quality service buildings  and  infrastructure.  How does this affect  the construction industry?  The sector is directly affected by all these challenges,  from building and renovating existing residential and  non-residential buildings, to building and renovating  infrastructure. The objective is, in particular, to increase  productivity in the building sector in order to meet this  quantitative challenge. b. Climate change  The challenge  Since the pre-industrial era (around 1850-1900), the  world’s average surface temperature has increased by  around 1.34  °C to 1.41  °C, with 2024 being the hottest  year on record, at 1.55  °C above the benchmark. It is  scientifically established that greenhouse gas emissions,  generated by human activity, are primarily responsible  for this increase in average temperatures. The  construction industry is particularly concerned , as it  accounts for nearly 34% of annual CO 2 emissions  worldwide. These emissions are generated primarily  by the operation of the buildings (around 75%), together  with the production and transport of materials  and construction equipment (around 25%). In addition, rising temperatures are accompanied by  extreme weather events such as heat waves, heavy rainfall  and floods, droughts, rires and hurricanes, which are  becoming more frequent and severe. Between 3 and  3.6 billion people live in areas that are highly vulnerable to  extreme weather events, including severe water stress and  drought. In 2024, 393 recorded disasters affected  167.2 million people, exceeding the annual average of 371  disasters recorded between 2004 and 2023. How does this affect  the construction industry?  Reducing the carbon footprint of construction requires  changes in construction standards: the more systematic  use of renovation over demolition-reconstruction, the  widespread use of lightweight construction methods  that reduce the use of cement and concrete, the  decarbonization of these two materials, and finally,  energy renovation that reduces the energy consumption  of buildings. Saint-Gobain offers solutions for all these  techniques and is therefore perfectly positioned on the  underlying trend of construction decarbonization. Furthermore, the need to adapt all residential and non- residential buildings to climate change creates immense  needs that the construction industry must face. The aim  is both to build new buildings that are resilient to climate  change, and to adapt existing buildings to exceptional  weather conditions, in order to ensure safety and  comfort for residents and to minimize losses and repair  and reconstruction costs, in a context where insurance  companies are reducing their coverage of natural  disasters. The Group’s solutions are particularly suited to  these needs, whether it involves strengthening the  insulation of buildings, installing solar-control glazing to  protect against heat waves, or protecting buildings using  waterproofing membranes. c. Scarcity of natural resources The challenge  Resource scarcity refers to the growing gap between  demand for essential natural resources (such as fresh  water, minerals, land, and energy) and the planet’s ability  to regenerate or provide them sustainably. This  imbalance is intensifying as population, consumption,  and environmental pressures converge.  Global water demand is growing fast: it is expected to  exceed supply by 40% and increase by another 20-30%  after 2040, driven by urbanization, agriculture, and  energy needs. At the same time, global commodities  extraction has tripled over the past 50 years and is  expected to almost double by 2050, exposing economies  to the risks of shortages and high price volatility.  How does this affect  the construction industry?  The construction industry accounts for 37% of solid  waste and nearly 50% of natural resource consumption  worldwide, leading to the extraction of 100 billion tonnes  of raw materials per year. Two resources are particularly  consumed by construction: sand, of  which 50  billion  tonnes are consumed per year, and water, 15% of which  is consumed by construction. Circularity ( limiting the  consumption of resources, extending product life, and  encouraging product recycling, taking into account  their entire life cycle) offers a way forward, but scaling  up requires overcoming major obstacles in  terms of  design, regulations and logistics. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 40 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com B – Challenges and opportunities a. Population growth and urbanization:  increasing the volume  of new construction The increase in the global population and the  acceleration of urbanization (see section 1.2.1.A.a  above) means accelerating both the production of  new buildings and the renovation and reallocation  of  existing buildings, in order to provide a sufficient  volume of adequate housing. The construction industry  faces an unprecedented challenge: meeting the  explosive demand for housing caused by population  growth, urbanization, and climate change, while  addressing labor shortages , stagnant productivity ,  and sustainability imperatives . This twofold issue  requires a fundamental transformation of traditional  construction and renovation methods towards  industrialized and  automated approaches. From this  point of view, Saint-Gobain’s lightweight construction  solutions, which  increase productivity, are perfectly  suited to these challenges. b. Aspiration for quality housing:  building safe, dignified, healthy,  comfortable, and affordable buildings This aspiration overlaps with two developments: on the  one hand, the middle classes are expanding in emerging  countries, as illustrated by the 16% average annual  growth of the Indian middle class by 2030; on the other  hand, the acceleration required in construction to meet  quantitative housing needs must not sacrifice quality: it  is a question of building safe, healthy, comfortable and  affordable housing.  The benefits of building comfort on occupant quality of  life have been demonstrated; for example, the thermal  and acoustic comfort of a classroom has beneficial  effects on student learning. Saint-Gobain offers  lightweight construction solutions that improve thermal  and acoustic comfort, as well as the air quality and light  of buildings.  Furthermore, in view of deteriorating housing  affordability over the past decade, in both emerging and  developed countries, the issue of affordability has also  become more important. Saint-Gobain lightweight  construction solutions optimize the cost of building  construction, thanks to productivity gains estimated at  between 20% and 70% during the construction phase, as  well as the recurring cost of building occupancy   (by  reducing energy bills); at the same time, these  solutions improve the economic value of buildings for  their owners: not only are rental yields improved, but real  estate prices increasingly reflect housing energy  efficiency. In many European countries, real estate price  differentials between the most and least energy-efficient  homes exceed 15%, even comparing homes with  equivalent structural characteristics: this is the case in  the Netherlands, for example, as well as in Germany,  France, Italy and Spain.  c. Adapting buildings and infrastructure  to climate change: providing solutions  that increase the resilience  of buildings and infrastructure The built environment faces unprecedented  vulnerabilities related to the climate crisis (see section  1.2.1.A.b above). The acceleration of extreme weather  events, such as heatwaves, fires, floods, and hurricanes,  requires cities, buildings, and infrastructure to adapt to  a new climatic situation. These adaptation efforts must  cover both new construction and existing buildings and  must be adapted to the specific nature of climate  change in each region of the world.  Saint-Gobain’s offering, in particular in the insulation of  partitions and roofs, glazing, shock-resistant shingles and  waterproofing and fire protection membranes, makes it  possible to meet the challenges of this necessary  adaptation. Furthermore, the Group’s organization by  country is particularly adapted to the need to determine  local solutions for adapting buildings.  d. Energy renovation of buildings:  accelerating the volume of renovation  and reallocation of existing buildings  to increase their energy performance  and reduce carbon emissions Achieving carbon neutrality by 2050 requires the  complete decarbonization of the construction industry,  addressing not only emissions during the construction  phase, but also during the use phase of buildings. These  represent around three quarters of the building’s carbon  emissions and can be drastically reduced by energy  renovation. To  date, only 5% of buildings are carbon  neutral, and  annual renovation rates remain low.  Saint-Gobain’s solutions are particularly suited to energy  renovation, whether for glazing, plasterboard solutions,  or insulation for façades and roofs. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 41 e. Relocation of activities  as part of deglobalization After decades of globalization and internationalization of  industrial value chains, companies have faced multiple  industrial and logistical challenges since the Covid  pandemic, including value chain disruptions  during  lockdowns, multiple climate hazards that have affected  global trade routes (e.g., the Panama Canal affected by  drought), and regional conflicts, e.g., in the Red Sea.  Moreover, growing global geopolitical tensions are also  affecting trade and encouraging central governments to  increase their strategic autonomy. All of these  developments are encouraging companies and central  governments to implement policies of reindustrialization  and the local development of strategic industries ,  including defense and the data centers necessary for the  artificial intelligence revolution. The needs for industrial  buildings are therefore increasing considerably in the  Group’s various countries. Saint-Gobain’s offering also targets these non-residential  segments. In the data center sector, for example,  solutions for technical insulation, waterproofing  membranes, technical ceilings, low-carbon admixtures,  and technical screeds meet the precise requirements of  manufacturers. f. Resource scarcity and circularity:  building and renovating,  using fewer resources Resource scarcity (see section 1.2.1.A.c above) involves  building and renovating while optimizing the use of raw  materials and promoting the modularity and reallocation  of buildings. As construction consumes 40% of the  world’s raw materials and generates one -third of the  world’s waste, circularity is both an environmental  imperative and a strategic sovereignty issue .  Saint-Gobain’s offering responds to this challenge. The  reduction in the use of raw materials can reach 50% in  lightweight construction methods: the substitution of  heavy materials and solid façades with wood, metal or  concrete structures naturally reduces the natural  resources used; in addition, site waste is significantly  reduced by the use of pre-fabricated and pre-assembled  modules; and lastly, lightweight construction facilitates  the deconstruction of buildings at the end of their life  and the re-use of construction materials, to the extent  that buildings are transformed into a “materials bank”,  which will ultimately lead to a drastic reduction in the  raw materials consumed by the construction industry. 1.2.2 DEFINING STRATEGIC OBJECTIVES The “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan marks the beginning of  a new chapter for Saint-Gobain, based on a clear  ambition: to sustainably accelerate profitable growth  and outperform its markets , thereby increasing the  value created for all its stakeholders. Over the  2026-2030 period, the Group is targeting “mid -single  digit” average annual sales growth (1), outperforming its  markets by 1 to 2 points, an EBTIDA margin of between  15% and 18%, ROCE above 13%, and a free cash flow  conversion ratio of more than 50%. Anchored in the Group’s long-term vision and corporate  purpose – “Making the World a Better Home” – this plan  is based on a proven model, local for local, and  recognized leadership in light and sustainable  construction, whether in mature or fast-growing markets.  The megatrends that are shaping the construction  industry today are creating an opportunity-rich  environment. A global population that continues to  grow and urbanize, the expansion of the middle class in  high-growth markets, the housing crisis in fully  developed economies, the growing need for local  infrastructure, and the climate emergency are  profoundly transforming demand. These developments  reinforce the need for efficient building and  infrastructure solutions that improve the daily lives of  occupants, while being resource-efficient, low-carbon,  and adapted to extreme weather conditions . In each  region, these trends are reflected in a specific way: in  Europe, energy renovation is becoming an imperative; in  North America, housing shortages and resilience to  climate change are structuring the market; in Asia and  emerging economies, urbanization and infrastructure  are generating increasing construction needs. To meet these challenges, Saint-Gobain stands out with  a unique offering of solutions covering the entire  building envelope and infrastructure, deployed on a  country-by-country basis, which constitutes a decisive  competitive advantage because it precisely meets the  complex needs of the Group’s customers. These solutions  make it possible to address all the construction markets  – residential as well as non-residential and infrastructure –  offering Saint-Gobain a vast addressable market for  sustainable, profitable growth. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 42 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) In local currencies, excluding any major economic slowdown and/or significant divestment. A – Strengthening a sustainable,  profitable growth profile The “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan aims to create  a sustainable, profitable growth profile for the Group. Continuing the geographical  rebalancing of the Group’s activities First, Saint-Gobain intends to fully capture the gradual  recovery in Europe, with the objective of growing by an  average of 3% to 5% per year (1). At the same time,  Saint-Gobain is pursuing a clear strategy of strengthening  its presence in higher-growth regions, including North  America, Asia, and other high-growth markets.  Accounting for around half of the Group’s sales, these  regions represent key drivers of future growth. The  Group aims to achieve, in the long term, nearly 60% of  global sales in these regions. This trajectory is based on  an average growth rate of 5%  to 8% (1), supported by  demographic dynamics, infrastructure demand, and the  upscaling of construction standards.  Broadening the coverage of all construction markets The “Lead &amp; Grow” plan also aims to significantly expand  the Group’s presence in all construction markets, and  more specifically in the non-residential and  infrastructure segments. Historically more focused on  residential, Saint-Gobain now enjoys a solid foundation  for acceleration in these new markets, which are still  under-penetrated, thanks to the expansion of its offering,  its mastery of complex technical solutions, and the  driving force of construction chemicals activities. In this  segment, which is a prime gateway to infrastructure  projects, the objective is to achieve over €9  billion in  sales by 2030, through a combination of capital  expenditure, product innovation and targeted external  growth operations.  Enhancing the Group’s profile in light and  sustainable construction More broadly, Saint -Gobain continuously enhances its  business profile by supplementing, in a logical sequence,  country by country, all its product lines – interior,  exterior, and construction chemicals solutions. Thus, the  Group is strengthening its ability to offer integrated  solutions adapted to the needs of its customers, and  which create value for the Group. The strategic plan is accompanied by active, selective  management of the business portfolio (see section  1.2.7, p. 60). Saint-Gobain plans to increase its scope by  about 20% between now and 2030, by combining  targeted acquisitions and disciplined disposals. Each  transaction is assessed according to strict criteria:  consolidation of leadership positions, increased  exposure to high growth countries, strengthening of  construction chemicals activities, creation of financial  value, with ROCE above 13%. B – Accelerating profitable growth  thanks to the solutions-based  approach  The roll-out of the “Lead &amp; Grow” strategy relies on a  central lever: accelerating the development of solutions.  For several years now, Saint-Gobain has undergone a  profound transformation of its model, moving from a  product portfolio to an integrated logic of solutions  capable of fully meeting customer expectations . This  approach, which was successfully initiated in the  previous plan, is being considerably strengthened under  “Lead &amp; Grow”. Meeting customers’ needs,  creating value for Saint-Gobain The Group’s solutions-based approach is above all a  response to, or even an anticipation of the needs of its  customers, who are facing increasing complexity on  construction sites and have greater needs for  productivity, performance and sustainability. The major  construction players are looking for integrated  approaches, capable of providing guarantees of quality  and performance with a single point of contact to  support them on their project. Saint-Gobain has a unique  breadth and depth of range, including the broadest low- carbon offering on the market, capable of addressing the  growing green building market. The Group thus stands  out on the market by providing a  comprehensive  response to its customers’ issues. The deployment of solutions increases the value created  for customers and, as a result, the Group’s financial  performance, through three main levers: ■ Cross-selling across the entire Saint-Gobain portfolio,  which enables the Group to increase its sales with its  customers and thus outperform its competitors; ■ The sale of innovative, differentiated, high value- added solutions  – such as complete EnveoVent  lightweight façade systems – that increase the  profit margin; ■ The services and specified sales that make it possible  to enhance the sold product mix on large projects  towards more value-added solutions. Profitable growth opportunities,  country by country The expansion of the solutions-based approach is  grounded in a rigorous, repeatable methodology,  deployed locally. This implementation is structured in  two ways: the gradual expansion of the offering in each  country, and the comprehensive coverage of all sales  channels. Country by country, Saint-Gobain is systematically  expanding and enhancing its offering to gain market  share across the entire connected suite of the Group’s  products, covering the entire building envelope and  infrastructure. This model, for example, has been  deployed in India over the last 10 years. Saint-Gobain has  grown from two product lines to a full range of  innovative solutions with high value-added, with leading  positions in each category, and strong growth in sales  and margins (see “Our strategy”, p. 19). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 43 (1) In local currencies, excluding any major economic slowdown and/or significant divestment. At the same time, the Group deploys its solutions across  all sales channels, from professional trading to individual  customers, from direct sales on major projects to digital  sales. This multi-channel coverage strengthens the  distribution of solutions, facilitates the commercial  relationship, and helps to increase market share in each  segment. To ensure the strategy’s effectiveness, Saint-Gobain  closely monitors, country by country, a set of indicators  related to the deployment of solutions: cross-selling  rates, share of value-added products and growth in  specified sales. Finally, the solutions-based approach also applies to  industrial markets . In these segments, the Group’s  activities are already characterized by a high level of  technical expertise, a high proportion of specified sales,  and a significant contribution to the Group’s  performance. The innovation resulting from these  businesses – whether coated glass in mobility, polymers  for construction chemicals, or ceramics for industrial  decarbonization – benefits all of the Group’s activities. C – Accelerated penetration  of non-residential and  infrastructure markets With €15  billion of sales already generated in the  non-residential and infrastructure segments – in a total  addressable market of nearly €250  billion –  Saint-Gobain intends to significantly increase  its penetration in these markets. This ambition is based  on a clear strategy: relying on highly technical “hero”  products, capable of opening access to complex  projects, and drawing on integrated offers adapted to  the requirements of these segments. Differentiating technical products,  real entry points for large  non-residential projects Saint-Gobain is able to differentiate itself in  non-residential projects thanks to its highly technical  solutions, essential to building performance, and which  act as strategic gateways for large-scale projects.  “X-Ray Protection” anti-radiation plasterboards, sold  under the Placo® brand, are one particularly illustrative  example: lighter, recyclable, simpler to install and  bringing real health benefits, compared with traditional  lead-based solutions, these boards have made it  possible to meet the technical and environmental  requirements of the Nantes University Hospital project,  in which Saint -Gobain sold a total of 26 solutions  (cf. section "Markets and activities", p. 9. Saint-Gobain is implementing this same approach in  the data center market, a fast-growing market  characterized by high needs for thermal performance,  fire protection and reliability guarantees. With a  comprehensive range of dedicated partitioning systems  – technical ceilings, floors, waterproofing membranes,  insulation, etc. – Saint-Gobain is now identified as one of  the leaders in this segment, used by  a significant  proportion of operators in Europe and North America (1). Construction chemicals  as a growth driver in infrastructure In just a few years, Saint-Gobain has built a global  leadership position in construction chemicals, with no  less than 43 new lines and plants built since 2021  and 37 acquisitions in this market, including Chryso, GCP,  Fosroc, and Cemix. This leadership plays a decisive role  in the Group’s ability to grow in the infrastructure  market, enabling it to address segments such as tunnels,  bridges, viaducts, energy networks, and hydro  structures, with high-performance solutions,  specification teams, and the combination of expertise  from several strong, globally recognized brands, offering  an integrated approach to the customer. A dedicated approach to capture the full  potential of under-penetrated markets To support its growth ambition in the non-residential  and infrastructure segments, Saint-Gobain has set up  a dedicated organization structured around  comprehensive offerings by market segment (data  centers, hospitals, schools, transport infrastructure,  etc.). It relies on specification and technical support  teams, with strong expertise in building sciences,  capable of supporting architects, design offices,  engineers, and project owners in the design and  execution of projects. This approach is also accompanied by a strengthened  model for managing key accounts, making it possible to  monitor major projects throughout their duration,  coordinate the Group’s brands, and propose coherent  and optimized solutions. The new Long Thanh airport in  Vietnam perfectly illustrates this method: by  mobilizing  15 complementary solutions from different Group  entities, Saint-Gobain has demonstrated its ability to  address the most demanding projects in a coherent,  integrated manner (cf. “Our strategy”, p. 18). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 44 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) Source: Morgan Stanley, July 2025. Lastly, Saint-Gobain’s lead in digital innovation is a key  competitive advantage in non-residential and  infrastructure projects by providing value to the Group’s  customers. In R&amp;D, artificial intelligence  allows  Saint-Gobain to develop new products quicker and thus  accelerate their marketing. Artificial intelligence also  improves the Group’s sales performance, for example by  automating the large -scale analysis of tenders.  Saint-Gobain also deploys a complete suite of digital  tools for its customers, for example in construction  chemicals with its Verifi and Maturix ranges, which  improve real-time monitoring of concrete and increase  product quality and productivity on construction sites. Based on these fundamentals – comprehensive offering,  technical expertise, dedicated organization, advanced  digital capabilities – Saint-Gobain aims to achieve mid to  high-single digit annual sales growth  (1) in the  non-residential and infrastructure markets. The Group  already has a solid presence in many applications,  in-depth knowledge of needs by country, and targeted  development plans to capture identified pockets  of growth. D – An organization structured  around country platforms  to catalyze growth To fully execute its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategy, Saint-Gobain  relies on a particularly robust organizational model   based on country platforms. Built gradually over the past  few years and proven in various economic contexts, this  model is now a major asset for capturing growth  opportunities, enabling rapid execution, and ensuring  consistency of decisions as close to the markets  as possible. These platforms, deeply rooted in their local ecosystem,  combine three essential dimensions to seize growth  opportunities: daily proximity to customers and teams,  access to the Group’s global expertise, and strong  autonomy to manage their resources, adapt their  priorities, and deploy the most relevant solutions at the  local level. This combination makes them real  commercial machines, capable of simultaneously  activating all product lines and sales channels. A bolt-on, proactive acquisition strategy Country platforms play a decisive role in the Group’s  acquisition momentum, which is one of the structuring  levers of the “Lead &amp; Grow” plan. They are often the  ones who initiate discussions with targets. Their  proximity to local players, their in-depth knowledge of  the markets, and their established reputation facilitate  bilateral discussions with family businesses or specialized  players. Over the past five years, 80% of acquisitions  made by Saint-Gobain have been concluded bilaterally,  sometimes after several years of dialog and trust  building. The country platforms are then responsible for  operational integration, talent retention, and generating  synergies. Recent successes – for example in India,  where the margin rate of insulation activities has  increased significantly in three years thanks to rigorous  integration – demonstrate the relevance of this model. Operational excellence supported  by the Group’s expertise Saint-Gobain’s country platforms ensure that they  deliver the best operational performance possible,  drawing on the Group’s expertise, whether in terms of  industrial excellence, innovation with the regional R&amp;D  centers, good sales practices in key account  management, customer loyalty or training programs, or  monitoring the deployment of Saint-Gobain’s solutions- based approach. This approach enables them to increase  their competitiveness, by accelerating and optimizing  execution. Environmental excellence is at the heart of the  performance model of the country platforms, which are  working to reduce the carbon footprint of their  operations, increase their share of recycled materials,  reduce their water consumption, and thus deploy  sustainable solutions for Saint-Gobain’s customers that  give the Group a leading competitive advantage. The progress made by Saint-Gobain in terms of  decarbonization and circularity is recognized by all  stakeholders. They help to increase the confidence of  customers, investors, and public partners, and contribute  to the Group’s sustainable growth. This environmental  excellence is now seen as a differentiating attribute  of Saint-Gobain in construction markets. A culture of performance  and accountability Lastly, the performance of the country platforms is based  on a culture of high standards and  accountability  embodied by their leaders. Nearly 90% of country  platform CEOs are native to the country they manage,  which guarantees a thorough understanding of local  issues and enhances the quality of operational leadership. This performance culture is supported by an incentive  scheme that is closely aligned with value creation.  Managers manage their P&amp;L independently, with specific  targets in terms of growth, margins, ROCE, and CSR. The  Group’s 3,000 key managers also benefit from  a  long- term incentive system directly indexed to  financial  performance, share-price trends and  environmental  objectives. This framework helps to unite the teams around a high  level of commitment and a strong collective ambition,  essential to ensuring the long-term success of the  “Lead &amp; Grow” plan. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 45 (1) In local currencies, excluding any major economic slowdown and/or significant divestment. 1.2.3 STAKEHOLDERS A – Principles of dialog with stakeholders Saint-Gobain considers the interests of all its  stakeholders when determining its long-term strategy.  First of all, this involves building a dialog, and also  ensuring that this  dialog is transparent and based on  mutual trust. Factors such as the size of the Group, its  global dimension, and the variety of its business lines  mean that dialog must, above all, be organized in a  decentralized manner, with each operational entity being  responsible for conducting it within its own scope. Saint-Gobain has mapped out its ecosystem, identifying  and grouping its stakeholders by category. For each  category, a Group position is responsible for organizing  the reporting of information on its expectations, at the  local or global levels, and producing a summary of them.  Thus, dialog is established with stakeholders on a regular  basis at both the Group and local levels. Each  stakeholder’s preferred dialog method has also been  noted. This dialog enables us to consider all the  expectations of Saint-Gobain’s stakeholders. In June 2025, the Group’s organization changed to  further accentuate coordination by country (see sections  1.2.2.D, p.  45 and 1.2.7.B, p.  62). Consequently, each  country is in charge of the dialog with its local  stakeholders and deploys a specific action plan to meet  expectations.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 46 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 47 KEY STAKEHOLDERS DIALOG MODALITY Customers: direct customers,  users and specifiers Suppliers and subcontractors,  partners Employees (full-time, part-time),  temporary workers, employee  representatives, apprentices,  interns, secondary and vocational  education Shareholders: employees,  institutional investors, SRI  (Socially Responsible Investment),  rating and ranking agencies Governments, regulators,  intergovernmental entities,  international organizations  (UN, ILO, etc.), inter-professional  associations, Green Building  Councils • Permanent contacts • Internal communication material • Meetings with Group managers • Bodies representing employees • Intranet  • Website • Global surveys or surveys on specific subjects Local populations and  communities close to sites • Group publications (brochures, etc.) • Website • Letters to shareholders • Shareholder guide • Public declarations • Meetings with investors • Meetings with individual shareholders • Public Affairs • Participation in working groups, in most cases led by inter- professional associations, on various construction method-related  technical issues, such as evaluating of building performance • Green Building Councils NGOs, foundations,  professional associations,  universities, schools, media • Regular meetings • Publications and magazines; company websites;  professional forums and trade fairs  • Publication of educational guides on energy efficiency and the  environment; charter of recommendations promoting the insulation  of existing buildings; participation in associations or groups • Customer and end-user training • Charter relating to suppliers; monitoring action plans  for the responsible purchasing policy • Test markets, commercial studies, focus groups, etc. • Meetings initiated by sites or country organizations • Solidarity initiatives • Regular meetings with elected officials and representatives  of local administrations • Experimental work in the regions in partnership with public entities  and elected officials • Group publications • Meetings • Participation in university training • Forums in schools • Support for youth development MARKET EMPLOYEES INVESTORS REGULAR  AUTHORITIES AND  PUBLIC AFFAIRS  PARTNERS LOCAL  COMMUNITIES CIVIL SOCIETY B – Incorporating stakeholders’ interests and opinions Dialog with stakeholders is therefore carried out on an ongoing basis at various levels of the Saint-Gobain  organization. The Group’s activities and the deployment of its strategy are mainly local. For this reason, country  platforms (see section 1.2.5, p. 53) play an essential role in the quality of this dialog.  ORGANIZATION OF STAKEHOLDER DIALOG AND SUMMARY OF THEIR INTERESTS AND POINTS OF VIEW: Main stakeholders Dialog and coordination  by countries  (Coordination) Summary  produced by the  functional divisions Interests  and points of view Market Customer: direct  customer, users,  and specifiers The Group’s upstream  and downstream value  chains are mainly local.  Group Operational  Performance Department Sustainable growth Improve productivity,  attractiveness for  the workforce  and incorporate  sustainability issues Trust and reliability Suppliers and  subcontractors, partners Employees Employees (full-time,  part-time), temporary  workers, employee  representatives,  apprentices, interns,  secondary and vocational  education The human resources  organization of each  country is in charge  of social dialog.  The Group HR  Department Working conditions Opportunities Development  and training Investors Shareholders: employees,  institutional and  individual shareholders.  SRI (Socially Responsible  Investment), rating and  ranking agencies Dialog with investors is  conducted only at Group  level. The financial  communications team  is responsible for this. Finance Department Results: financial  performance  and sustainability Trust: transparency  and meeting  commitments Reputation Regulatory  authorities and  public-sector  business  partners Governments, regulators,  intergovernmental  entities, international  organizations (UN, ILO,  etc.), inter-professional  associations, Green  Building Councils The dialog is facilitated  by the country teams  according to an  organization adapted  to local specificities, and  the need for technical  expertise. Support teams  can reinforce local teams  on strategic issues  such as sustainable  construction.  Institutional Affairs  Department  Compliance with  commitments Value sharing:  responsibility  and sustainability  (combating climate  change) Local  communities Local populations  and communities close  to sites The Senior Management  of the country is in  charge of dialog with  local communities and  in particular residents  in the vicinity of sites. Regional management Safety Trust: respecting values Solidarity Civil society NGOs, foundations,  professional associations,  universities, schools,  media Each country determines  the primary contacts in  its teams based on the  subject. In general, the  contacts are incorporated  into HR teams. Communications, HR,  CSR and Sustainable  Development  Departments Clarity in its  commitments  transparency on actions  and achievements Reliability in partnerships PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 48 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.2.4 SUSTAINABILITY CHALLENGES INTEGRATED  INTO STRATEGY A – The materiality of  sustainability challenges Since 2015, Saint-Gobain has regularly carried out  “double materiality” assessments to identify and  prioritize the impacts that are most significant for both  the company and its stakeholders, and  those  with a  major financial impact. The update of the double materiality analysis carried out  in 2024 is based on the ESRS (European Sustainability  Reporting Standards). This change in methodology  has not led to any major changes in the list of material  challenges for Saint-Gobain.  The matrix is a representation made based on the results  of the double materiality analysis (see section 3.1.3,  p.  106). Materiality challenges have been classified  according to the contributions of the CSR roadmap. Sustainability issues were identified based on: ■ Information and knowledge gained from previous  financial years;  ■ Sector-specific reference frameworks specifying the  priority issues linked to construction business lines; ■ The specific features of the Group’s business model  and value chain; ■ Compliance with EU regulations by incorporating  the detailed challenges listed by the ESRS. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 49 The methodology and detailed results  are presented in section 3.1.3, p. 106. B – Contributions  to sustainability issues  For each of the sustainability issues identified, there are  impacts, positive or negative, risks and  opportunities,  particularly growth opportunities. Through the three axes of its CSR roadmap, Saint-Gobain  defines its ambition, including its objectives, and its  action plans to seize opportunities and contribute to  positive impacts while controlling sustainability risks and  its footprint. Growth opportunities are particularly developed under  the “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan (see section 1.2.2, p. 42)  in particular climate change adaptation solutions and the  development of circularity. A DECARBONATED HOME GROWTH AND RESILIENCE Opportunities to outperform markets  and access new markets: ■ Increased competitiveness and an attractive offering,  thanks to the proposal of low-carbon and energy-efficiency  solutions; ■ Growing demand for solutions adapted to climate change  (regulations and user demands), particularly solutions for  adapting to the effects of climate change. Opportunities related to operational  and financial control: ■ Cost reduction and availability of supplies through  industrial processes, innovation in particular, and  operational excellence. CHALLENGES Reduce the negative impact on the environment and society:  ■ Reduce greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions (scope 1, 2 and 3) contributing to climate change and biodiversity loss;  ■ Reduce GHG emissions related to the construction sector.  Adapt resources to the needs of the transition plan: ■ Risk of increased energy costs; ■ Potential need for investment in GHG reduction initiatives. APPROACH Maximize Saint-Gobain’s contribution: Proposing innovative solutions that contribute  to reducing the carbon emissions of the construction  and industrial sectors, including those linked with  building use.  Minimize Saint-Gobain’s footprint:  Achieving net zero emissions (scope 1, 2 and 3)  by 2050. ACTIONS Proposing solutions: ■ Program for sustainable and high-performance solutions;  ■ Assessing the impact of products and solutions on the  value chain. Taking action for sustainability: ■ 2030 carbon roadmap;  ■ Aligning the carbon strategy with the net zero carbon  by 2050 target. OBJECTIVES ■ Net zero emissions (scope 1, 2 and 3) by 2050; ■ Scope 1 &amp; 2 GHG emissions: reduction between 40% and 45% by 2035 (vs. 2017); ■ Scope 3 GHG emissions: 16% reduction by 2030 (vs. 2017). ESRS E1 SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT GOALS: 7; 9; 11; 12; 13 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 50 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS GROWTH AND RESILIENCE Leadership opportunities: ■ Using resources from responsible sources  and limiting the use of non-renewable resources; ■ Thanks to country platforms, creating recycling  channels for construction materials. Opportunities to outperform markets  and access new markets: ■ Growing demand and expectations for products  and systems that are eco-designed and associated  with the development of circularity. Opportunities associated with operational  and financial control: ■ Cost reduction and availability of supplies through  circularity, including local waste collection  and sorting organization; ■ Cost reduction and availability of supplies through  industrial processes and innovation, in particular  the operational excellence program. CHALLENGES  Reduce negative impacts on the environment and society:  ■ Negative consequences of pollution; ■ Pressure related to water consumption; ■ Pressure related to the consumption of extracted resources; ■ Impacts of waste generated by construction, renovation, or demolition activities; ■ Impact of activities on biodiversity (deforestation, extraction, construction). APPROACH Maximize Saint-Gobain’s contribution: Reducing adverse effects on nature by optimizing circular  flows, using more recycled or bio-sourced materials,  and improving the use of buildings. Minimize Saint-Gobain’s footprint:  Limiting pollution, especially water pollution,  and preserving natural resources and biodiversity  along the value chain.  ACTIONS Proposing solutions: ■ Program for sustainable and high-performance solutions;  ■ Innovation program for sustainability; ■ Creation of or participation in the creation of recycling  networks (waste collection and recovery). Taking action for sustainability: ■ Program on the substitution of substances of concern; ■ Application of the Forest, Timber Purchasing and Water  policies and the “Responsible Purchasing” program. OBJECTIVES ■ 80% reduction in non-recovered waste by 2030 (vs. 2017); ■ 50% reduction in water withdrawals by 2030 (vs. 2017); ■ Zero water withdrawal in areas under extremely high water stress; ■ 100% of packaging recyclable by 2030. ESRS E2 to E5 SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT GOALS: 6; 7; 9; 12; 13 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 51 A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL GROWTH AND RESILIENCE  Leadership opportunities: ■ Engaging business partners in the implementation  of sustainable strategies; ■ Positive impacts of commitments to local communities  through local anchoring and the socio-economic  development of territories; ■ Employee well-being along the value chain through fair  treatment, social dialog, inclusion, and diversity. Opportunities associated with operational  and financial control:  ■ Improving employee performance across the value chain  through opportunities and skills development; ■ Developing long-term relationships with suppliers  in the value chain to ensure their stability. Regulation and compliance:  ■ Anticipating regulations and responding to growing market  demand for greater product transparency. CHALLENGES  Reduce the negative impact on society:  ■ Exposing employees and workers in the value chain to physical and psychological problems related to health and safety,  working conditions, inequalities, and human rights;  ■ Exposing customers and end users to health and safety issues; ■ Exposing affected communities to risks of industrial accidents.  Anticipate the impacts of legal and regulatory changes and their potential effects on the Group’s reputation: ■ Inappropriate product use by customers and end users; ■ Unethical governance practices (corruption and bribery, lobbying, lack of whistleblower protection). APPROACH Maximize Saint-Gobain’s contribution: Delivering solutions with health, comfort and safety benefits  across the value chain, and supporting communities  by accelerating the transition to sustainable construction. Minimize Saint-Gobain’s footprint:  Promoting ethical conduct, diversity and inclusion, health  and safety and social dialog by engaging all stakeholders,  including suppliers. ACTIONS Proposing solutions: ■ Program for sustainable and high-performance solutions;  ■ Innovation program for sustainability. Taking action for sustainability: ■ Employee engagement and diversity: HR policy, “Ethics and  Compliance” program; ■ Health and Safety across the value chain: Responsible  Purchasing and Human Rights policies, Health and Safety  Charter; ■ Stakeholder support: Saint-Gobain Foundation,  “Build Change” program. OBJECTIVES ■ Value of TF2 by 2030: 1.4; ■ 33% female managers by 2030; ■ Employee engagement rate above benchmark (&gt;76% in 2025) each year (annual); ■ 100% coverage of the CARE program (annual); ■ Diversity index above 90% (annual). ESRS S1 to S4 / G1 SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT GOALS: 1; 3; 4; 5; 8; 9; 10; 11; 16; 17 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 52 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.2.5 SAINT-GOBAIN'S VALUE CHAIN Saint-Gobain’s value chain extends from the extraction of  raw materials to the end-user, including the end-of-life of  products. Its four-part structure is identical, regardless of  country or market. This value chain is deployed locally,  i.e., raw materials come from suppliers located in the  same region as the site that will transform them. The  range of products and solutions is defined by the  country, i.e., as close as possible to the markets and  customers it serves. This is why the country platforms are  central to the deployment of the “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic  plan. Exports of the Group’s products are  marginal and  limited to specific situations, such as the supply of pipes  for infrastructure-related projects.  Similarly, while the principle of circularity (step 4) is  universal, its deployment and implementation are strictly  local. Its results are visible only if there is  complementarity at each stage of the value chain. This  implies effective collaboration between stakeholders in  each country.  (1) Life Cycle Assessment.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 53 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 54 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com See section 3.4.6, p. 175 See sections “Markets and activities”, p. 8,  1.2.2.B and D, p. 43 and p. 45, 1.2.7.A, p. 60 See sections 1.1.2, p. 27 and 1.1.3, p. 29 See section 3.3.3.D, p. 145 The synergies between the expertise of the central  purchasing teams and the country or regional  organizations make it possible to meet the needs of the  sites, whether industrial or tertiary, in terms of  quality,  quantity, regularity and service, and competitiveness.  Group procurement is organized to combine the  Group’s operational expertise and negotiation capacity  with the specific needs of local business lines and sites.  Thus, a central team, Saint-Gobain Purchasing,  coordinates the purchasing of the major purchasing  categories. For example, coordination is in place  for  energy purchases in order to secure supply  and  control energy costs and to identify more local  sources of supply of decarbonized energy that meet  both the requirements of site supply continuity  and  energy cost competitiveness. These synergies  between the central team and the local teams  are  essential for the deployment of the “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategy, including the achievement of the financial  and non-financial objectives by 2030.  Saint-Gobain’s purchasing policy favors the establishment  of medium- and long-term contracts and partnerships  with its suppliers. Given the diversity of  materials,  products, or services purchased and the number of active  suppliers, partner companies can be large companies,  mid-sized companies, or small and medium-sized  companies. The exposure to risks of suppliers’ employees is one of  the criteria taken into account in responsible purchasing  risk mapping (see section 3.4.6, p. 175). The supply chain of industrial sites is mostly regional.  This implies the management of a large number of  suppliers. Nearly 190,000 companies supply the sites,  with nearly 20,700 account for 90% of the Group’s non- trade purchases (by purchase value).  Purchases corresponding to the offering of the trading  business lines are managed directly by the country  platforms. They are concentrated around more than  10,850 suppliers. Each brand develops its offering  according to its positioning and the expectations of the  markets in the countries in which it operates. As such, it  manages relations with mainly local suppliers.  The environmental and social impacts related to the  Group’s procurement, including the exposure risks of  employees of suppliers and subcontractors, as well as  the responsible procurement policy, risk mapping, and  action plans are described in section 3.4.6, p. 175. They consist of industrial sites including quarries,  trading or service agencies including logistics sites and  storage areas, and other tertiary sites, such as offices or  R&amp;D centers. The geographic breakdown of sites and  workforce is shown in the “Our global footprint” section  (pp. 12 and 13). Thus, solutions for the interior, solutions  for the exterior, solutions for construction sites and  construction chemicals are developed, produced, and  sold in each country in order to meet the specific needs  of the construction market and customers. The impacts  associated with industrial sites are closely monitored  from an environmental perspective due to industrial  processes and stored materials (see section 1.2.7.D.b,  p. 64 and section 5.1.1, p. 290); from a social perspective  through team-based work organization (see section  3.4.2, p.  154); and in terms of relationships with local  communities (see section 3.4.3, p. 162). In general, Saint-Gobain employees have permanent  contracts (91.8% in 2025). Depending on operational  needs, employees with fixed-term contracts (8.2% in 2025)  or temporary workers may supplement the workforce.  Some countries make marginal use of independent  contractors (fewer than 5,000 people in 2024). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 55 Each country platform has a portfolio of sustainable and  high-performance solutions (see section 1.2.7.A, p.  60)  to  meet the needs of the residential construction,  non-residential construction, infrastructure, and other  industries markets. Saint-Gobain considers a customer to  be any company or person having an influence on the  purchasing decision. Depending on the market,  customers may be segmented into three categories: ■ Direct customers: companies that acquire products or  services for use in production or construction projects  or to resell them; ■ Users: companies or persons who use the products in  their industrial process, during the implementation of  a construction or renovation project or after their  installation. ■ Specifiers: companies or persons who do not buy  or use the Group’s products and services, but whose  opinion is vital to the purchasing decision process  of  direct customers due to their technical expertise,  reputation or any other capacity (architects,  for example).  At the various stages of construction projects,  interactions with customers help determine needs and  adapt solutions. Each country is committed to  deploying solutions adapted to all channels: direct sales  or through networks of distributors or e-commerce.  Saint-Gobain’s strategy does not directly target  consumers. Some distributors, who are direct customers  of the Group, specialize in or develop business activities  linked to the “do it yourself” trend. Saint-Gobain  is  committed to adapting its offering to specific needs  in  order to support the growth of this segment.  Depending on the country, direct customers and users  can be large international companies (e.g., for large new  construction projects), trading houses, craftsmen, or sole  proprietorships. Employees of companies, whether  customers or users, work mainly on construction sites.  Their working conditions depend on the country, local  regulations, country-specific construction methods,  the climate, and the local labor market.  To develop circularity in the construction market, it is not  enough to offer recyclable products or limit waste on  construction sites, it is a question of creating new local  ecosystems. Through their leadership and proximity to  customers, country platforms can make circularity  a real growth driver. Saint-Gobain integrates the circular  economy principles into its solutions placed  on the market right from the design stage: ■ By limiting the use of non-renewable resources and  developing the reusability of products, the proportion  of recycled or biosourced materials or byproducts  incorporated into production and product packaging  (1) + (2);  ■ By reducing the proportion of final waste throughout  the entire value chain; ■ By ensuring the recyclability of the solutions placed  on the market (2); ■ By collaborating with its customers to reduce the  impact on natural resources of the construction and  building industry in particular, to extend the lifespan  of buildings and facilitate the collection of recyclable  materials when buildings are demolished (3). As part of its program to develop sustainable and  high-performance solutions (see section 1.2.7.A, p.  60),  Saint-Gobain strives to develop solutions that optimize  the use of resources. Special attention is paid to the  recyclability of products and packaging placed on the  market. Some Saint-Gobain products, such as glass and  gypsum products, can be recycled indefinitely in a  closed-loop industrial process. Industrial processes are  then adapted to replace natural raw materials with  recycled materials derived from internal or  external  collection.  Depending on the markets addressed, Saint -Gobain’s  products have a lifespan that is variable but optimized  relative to their use. Some products, such as glazing,  have theoretical lifespans of up to 30 years; others, such  as additives in the construction chemicals market,  are designed for a specific use. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 56 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.2.6 THE GROUP'S VALUE CREATION MODEL The value creation model shows the consistency of the  Group’s strategy. The “Grow &amp; Impact” (2020 – 2025)  and then "Lead &amp; Grow" (2025 – 2030) strategic plans  are aligned both with the purpose of “Making the world a  better home” and with the vision of being the leader in  light and sustainable construction. Saint-Gobain guides  its efforts on the megatrends of global demand for  construction, the fight against climate change, and  resource scarcity (see section 1.2.1, p. 39). Based on the  challenges and opportunities associated with these  megatrends, the Group has defined strategic objectives  to accelerate sustainable profitable growth and  outperform the markets (see section 1.2.2, p. 42).  As part of its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan, Saint-Gobain  confirms its vision of a construction market that it  approaches in a decentralized and local manner through  its country platforms (see section 1.2.5, p.  53), its  commitment to meeting customer expectations by  adopting a solutions-based approach (see section 1.1.3,  p.  29) and its ambition to increase its presence in the  non-residential construction and infrastructure markets  (see section 1.2.2, p. 42). To this end, the country platforms will be able to draw  on the Group’s expertise (1.2.7.F, p.  67) in terms of  industrial excellence, innovation, best commercial  practices and environmental performance.  Saint-Gobain integrates its stakeholders into its model  through mapping (see section 1.2.3, p. 46). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 57 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 58 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com We deploy our solutions-based  approach country by country  and across all sales channels  to meet our customers’  expectations and create  value for Saint-Gobain We are increasing  our presence in  the non-residential and  infrastructure markets  thanks to an expanded,  adapted, and enhanced  offering We rely on highly effective  country platforms  with strong local roots,  which catalyze our growth PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 59 A CULTURE OF TRUST  WITH STAKEHOLDERS FINANCIAL STRENGTH FOSTERING GROWTH COUNTRY PLATFORMS (CF. PP. 12 AND 13) OPERATIONAL EXCELLENCE  Our values: the Principles of Conduct and Action 83% employee  engagement index  see section 1.2.7.E.b, p. 66 100% of partners are  signatories to the charter  see section 3.4.6, p. 175  2,653 sales outlets / showrooms 90% of country CEOs are  native to their region 1,170 manufacturing facilities 8 regional R&amp;D centers  Marketing and business expertise Brand name Product Innovation €3,752 M Free cash flow see section 2.1, p. 82 €213 M In CAPEX and R&amp;D  in 2025 for the  “Carbon 2030” roadmap see section 3.2, p. 116€3,309 M Current net income see section 2.1, p. 82 3,606 researchers  see section 1.2.7.D.a, p. 63 1.21 kg CO2e /€  Scope 1 and 2 carbon  intensity in 2025  (in € of EBITDA), representing  a 58% decrease since 2017 –  see section 3.2, p. 116 450+  patents filed in 2025 see section 1.2.7.D.a, p. 63 93% of employees feel health  and safety is a priority  on their team see section 3.4.2.A, p. 155 17 PPAs active in 2025 see section 3.7.1.B.a, p. 206 53 Mt of materials consumed  (estimation on a restricted  perimeter –  see section 3.3, p. 138 30+ investment or collaboration agreements signed in 2025 with start-ups see section 1.2.7.D.a, p. 63 2026-2030: SAINT-GOBAIN IS AIMING FOR (SEE. P. 17) A DECARBONATED HOME MORE PERFORMANCE WITH LESS MID-SINGLE-DIGIT: Average sales growth 15-18% EBITDA  margin &gt;50% Free cash flow  conversion ratio  &gt;13% ROCE -35% CO2 emissions over scope 1  and 2 between 2017 and  2024 in absolute value  see section 1.3.2.A, p. 74 +35% scope 3 emissions  between 2017 and 2024,  in absolute value see section 1.3.2.A, p. 74 -27% waste not recovered  between 2017 and 2025  in absolute value see section 1.3.2.B, p. 76  -26% Reduction in water  withdrawals between  2017 and 2025 see section 1.3.2.B, p. 76 1.4 Accident frequency rate*  in 2025 see section 1.3.2.C, p. 78 91%  Proportion of employees  who took part in training  during the year see section 1.3.2.C, p. 78 30% Percentage of female  managers in 2025 see section 1.3.2.C, p. 78 * With and without lost time  for 1 million hours worked by  our employees, temporary workers,  and permanent subcontractors. PERFORMANCE SEGMENTS A BETTER LIVING FOR ALL €1,166 M  Dividends paid €21 M   Investments  in local communities €5,178 M  Portion retained  for growth €1,234 M   Taxes and duties €342 M  Interest  repayment  including IFRS16  interest€9,458 M   Salaries and  other personnel  compensation €300 M  Retirement  contributions SHARING THE VALUE CREATED €46,483 M  Sales €17,699 M Value added €29,376 M  Operational  costs/payments  to suppliers VALUE SHARING (SEE. P. 73) €592 M   Other miscel-  laneous income  1.2.7 CROSS-FUNCTIONAL STRENGTHS AND LEVERS A – Offering solutions that  combine performance  and sustainability Saint-Gobain adopts a customer-centric approach with  a view to developing complete, innovative solutions  (see “Solutions-based approach”, p.  6). In each of the  countries in which it operates, the teams offer their  customers solutions combining performance and  contribution to sustainable development, in particular  promoting the acceleration towards a decarbonized  and more circular economy.  As well as providing high-performance solutions, the  Group’s offering must enable its customers to make  progress on their own sustainable development goals,  thereby accelerating the transition of the construction,  mobility and industry sectors to a “low-carbon” economy  that consumes fewer natural resources, while improving  the health, safety, and well-being of users.  To measure its progress in this respect, since 2020  Saint-Gobain has implemented a methodology that takes  into account local markets and contexts to estimate the  share of its sales generated by solutions offering  benefits in terms of performance and sustainability. Beyond the traditional approach of meeting a specific  need with a suitable product, the aim is to offer clients  comprehensive, end-to-end support in addressing  complex challenges, modeled as a series of “use cases” – illustrations of particular scenarios such as building a  single-family home or renovating a hospital. Examples of  how these solutions are implemented in different  countries are presented in section 1.1.3, p. 29. To measure the performance of solutions , the Group  assesses the increase in economic value for the customer  based on criteria such as productivity and financial  benefits, as well as the user experience.  To measure the sustainability impacts of solutions, the  methodology relies on performance assessments of  solutions in terms of environmental or health  and well-being impacts.  The performance of a solution is assessed across the  entire value chain and for the main stakeholders  involved, right through to the end user.  In addition to measuring its overall effort, this approach –  which covers more than 90% of Saint-Gobain’s sales –  aims to align the sustainability benefit assessment  methods, and guide the work of the Group’s teams,  particularly in terms of innovation, customer relations,  sales, marketing, and the design of solutions. The share of sales generated with sustainable solutions  was 73% in 2025. a. Product innovation  and customer proximity Creating more value for its customers, strengthening its  offering of more sustainable solutions, taking full  advantage of its local and global presence and expertise,  and reducing time-to-market: these are the objectives to  which Saint-Gobain’s teams are committed.  The offering of solutions is defined by the country  platforms (see section 1.2.5, p.  53) as close as possible  to the needs of customers and local markets.  The in-depth knowledge of the markets, the proximity of  the teams with its customers (see pp. 6 and 7), the design  of a residential, non-residential, or infrastructure project  (see value chain 1.2.5, p. 53), allows the teams to propose  innovations developed locally or adapted from solutions  that have proven their worth in other countries; possibly  with the technical support and expertise of the central  teams by business line – “performance segments”. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 60 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com ECONOMIC VALUE  INCREASE IMPROVED QUALITY ENVIRONMENTAL  IMPACTS HEALTH AND  WELL-BEING IMPACTS PERFORMANCE SUSTAINABILITY ■ Productivity ■ Other financial benefits ■ Reliability ■ Creativity, adaptability in design ■ Energy efficiency and carbon footprint ■ Circularity et water resources preservation ■ Products and services that support the  environmental performance of other sectors ■ Security and safety, including professional  risks reduction on value chain ■ Ergonomics for installers ■ Interior air quality ■ Acoustic, thermal and visual comfort Regulatory and normative requirements are integrated  into the innovation process from the earliest stage of the  marketing brief, and marketing teams in each country of  commercialization ensure product compliance with  regulations. Lastly, the “World Class Manufacturing” (WCM)  industrial excellence program  (see section 1.2.7.F.a,  p. 67) ensures the deployment of best practices in terms  of quality and product compliance at industrial sites,  based on the ISO 9001 standard. Technical products are launched after sales force  training and reinforced support by product managers  and technical trainers. In several countries, they  intervene directly at worksites when installers use a  differentiating innovation for the first time, throughout  the launch phases until the sales organization reaches a  sufficient level of maturity.  In addition, a monitoring procedure incorporating every  step in the innovation process allows for the rapid  identification and consideration of potential difficulties  prompting attention to potential problems. Close  monitoring of the progress achieved and product  performance means that the process of innovation is  faster and more secure. Saint-Gobain University’s “Unicampus” program  (see  section 1.2.7.F.b, p.  70) offers five training courses  associated with the innovation process. These training  courses are primarily intended for marketing and R&amp;D  functions but also aim to raise awareness across  all functions. A product compliance training module has  also been incorporated into the program. Practical  courses combining theory, practice, and experience  sharing are offered widely to the principal offices  involved in the innovation process, such as marketing,  R&amp;D, purchasing, production, and industrial efficiency. The process for launching new products, systems and  services is verified as part of internal control (see section  1.2.7.F.a, p.  67). It is regularly updated to improve the  quality management system procedures and associated  monitoring indicators.  b. Toward more sustainable  products and solutions Saint-Gobain has incorporated an environmental, health  and safety impact verification dimension into the  innovation process since 2008 in the form of a checklist.  Since 2023, a new standard has been developed and a  tool for integrating sustainability issues into the  innovation process is available to the teams.  The main objectives of the new standard for innovation are: ■ Eliminating or limiting the presence of substances of  very high concern in new products marketed by  Saint-Gobain and used in the downstream value chain; ■ Eliminating or limiting and controlling potential health  and environmental risks during production processes  at Saint-Gobain sites. ■ select relevant sustainability indicators to measure  performance throughout the innovation process,  based on product categories and market needs. At each stage of the innovation process, this tool makes  it possible to define and monitor objectives for reducing  the environmental footprint and maximizing health and  well-being benefits in accordance with the approach to  propose efficient and sustainable solutions (see section  1.2.7.A, p. 60).  c. Informing customers  through to end users To provide clients with information on the environmental  benefits of products and solutions, Saint -Gobain is  committed to the development and  promotion of life  cycle analyses (LCAs). These analyses are useful internally to guide innovation,  and to meet growing market demand for greater  transparency. Saint-Gobain is committed to ensuring  that, by 2030, 100% of the Group’s sales, excluding  distribution activities, will be generated by products  covered by verified LCAs (life cycle analyses) or EPDs  (environmental product declarations).  As of late 2025, 57% (stable vs 2024) of sales were  generated by products covered by verified LCAs or  EPDs, either specific (produced by Saint -Gobain) or  generic (produced by professional associations). This represents more than 5.000 EPDs (2,700 in 2024)  verified EPDs published in close to 40 countries; the  Group is positioned as the world’s leading provider of  verified EPDs – by number – in the construction industry. Saint-Gobain’s customers also rely on sustainable  construction certifications, such as BREEAM and LEED,  to assess and enhance the overall performance of their  buildings. They seek products whose features help  achieve the desired levels of performance. To support country platform teams in leveraging this  opportunity, dedicated tools have been made available  for three segments – gypsum, insulation, and glass.  These include checklists to identify the potential  contributions of products to the main sustainability  standards, as well as evidence that can be provided to  customers (documents, data). These tools are  supplemented by locally adaptable presentations to  share this information with customers.  For example, the contribution of Saint-Gobain glazing is  available on the Saint -Gobain Glass website: https:// www.saint-gobain-glass.com/green-building-labelling- schemes.  According to Group’s estimates, the number of projects  supported by a sustainable construction certification is  rising in the main countries where it operates.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 61 With regard to information on users, the Group’s  products comply with current regulations, such as CE  marking in Europe, or the requirement for chemical  products to have labels and safety data sheets (SDS).  Saint-Gobain also provides non-mandatory information  specific to some of its products, such as: ■ Voluntary safety data declaration sheets for  unclassified articles or substances; ■ The declaration of the composition of components  and materials via the IMDS (International Material Data  System) database in the case of the automotive  industry; ■ Environmental Product Declarations (EPDs) for  construction products (or Environmental and  Health  Declaration Forms (FDES) in France); ■ Voluntary certifications obtained through  independent national laboratories – or, failing that,  international laboratories close to these markets –  when innovative products or systems are not covered  by international or national standards. Specific measures relating to the product compliance  program are presented in section 1.2.7.F.a, p. 67. B – Creating the conditions  for success: organization  for performance Established in June 2025 to create the conditions  required for the deployment of the “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategic plan, Saint-Gobain’s new organization is part of  a strategy aimed at strengthening profitable growth,  simplifying the structure, and asserting the Group’s  worldwide leadership in light and sustainable  construction. This development is not a break, but a  natural step that draws on the strengths of the existing  model in order to go further. It aims to  strengthen  Saint-Gobain’s ability to efficiently execute its strategic  plan by combining proximity, agility, and expertise. For several years, the multi-local model has enabled  the Group to get closer to the markets and customers.  Today, this model is being expanded : each country is  becoming fully responsible for all business lines, with  greater autonomy to coordinate commercial and  operational performance. This empowerment makes it  possible to act faster, seize opportunities in markets that  are still partially addressed, and expand Saint -Gobain’s  offering to create more value. It also promotes a  solutions-based approach, tailored to the needs of  customers, whether for key accounts, distribution  networks, or direct sales. To support this change, Saint-Gobain has created a  Group Operational Performance Department. Its role is  to guide, challenge and support countries in the  implementation of their growth plans. This Department  relies on two complementary levers. On the one hand,  the Performance Segments provide business expertise  to enrich the offering , maximize synergies and ensure  strategic consistency. On the other hand, the shared  Expertise Platforms provide innovation, digital,  purchasing and industrial and commercial excellence  solutions. This ensures that each country has  the  resources and skills to succeed. This organization also simplifies the Group’s structure.  This integration promotes strategic coherence, the  pooling of resources, and the achievement of economies  of scale, which will be reinvested in  growth. This is an  operational lever to aim for significant cost reduction.  This organization also strengthens the perception of the  consistency of offers and stimulates the culture of  collaboration within Saint-Gobain.  The expected benefits are concrete and measurable. The  Group will be able to offer a more comprehensive and  better-deployed offering, improve its operational  efficiency, strengthen its proximity to customers and act  quickly in an economic environment marked by  regionalization. This organization is designed to  accelerate profitable growth , simplify processes to  become more competitive, and assert Saint-Gobain as a  worldwide leader in light and sustainable construction  across its entire scope. This change comes at a key moment. The current  organization has reached maturity: its strengths  are known and its areas for improvement are identified.  The global context is shifting towards deglobalization,  which requires more agility and flexibility. By deepening  the multi-local model and mobilizing 100% of the Group’s  scope in the countries, Saint-Gobain is giving itself the  means to respond to these challenges and seize  opportunities. This organization is designed as a  powerful lever to execute the new “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategic plan. C – Rigorously allocating  financial resources Saint-Gobain rigorously applies its dynamic capital  allocation approach, adhering to strict criteria in terms of  value creation potential. As part of its “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategic plan, the Group plans to deploy approximately  €20 billion of capital between now and 2030, combining  growth and attractive returns for shareholders, while  maintaining a solid balance sheet with a net debt to  EBITDA ratio of between 1.5x and 2.0x. Of this total,  Saint-Gobain plans to allocate $12  billion in growth  investments  (1) over the 2025-2030 period , giving  priority to high-growth markets (North America, Asia- Pacific, growth countries, construction chemicals) as well  as consolidating its leading positions and enriching its  offering for each regional platform, country by country.  The Group also plans to increase shareholder returns to  around €8 billion: approximately €6 billion distributed in  dividends, targeting steady growth in the dividend per  share; and €2 billion in share buybacks. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 62 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) Growth investment: growth capex + net M&amp;A (acquisitions net of divestments). a. Supporting the Group’s growth  and commitments In 2025, Saint-Gobain continued its investment policy,  allocating resources to strengthen its industrial base   with 24 new lines or plants coming on stream during the  year. These investments cover all continents (Americas,  Europe, Asia-Pacific, Middle East, and Africa) and a very  large part of the Group’s activities, from plaster to  insulation, construction chemicals, mortars, and solutions  for industry and mobility. In addition, in 2025, the Group  committed €213  million in investments (CAPEX and  R&amp;D) to support its carbon roadmap, with the goal of  spending at least €100  million per year on these  expenses until 2030. b. Ensuring agile, value-creating  portfolio management Saint-Gobain is consistently pursuing a strategy to  optimize the Group’s profile in order to continue to  strengthen growth, profitability, and value creation.  From 2018 through December 31, 2025, Saint-Gobain has  made acquisitions representing approximately €7 billion  in revenue and divestments representing around  €10  billion in revenue. As part of its “Lead &amp; Grow”  strategic plan, the Group is targeting an asset rotation of  more than 20% of revenue over the 2026-2030 period. Continuing to pursue a strategy of value-creating acquisitions In 2025, Saint-Gobain completed 17 acquisitions,  generating additional sales of €862  million. These  acquisitions consolidate Saint-Gobain’s position as the  worldwide leader in light and sustainable construction  and accelerate its growth. In the construction chemicals sector, the Group  completed the acquisition of the Ovniver Group (Cemix  brand), a major player in the construction chemicals  sector in Mexico and Central America. Saint-Gobain also  completed the acquisition of Fosroc, a leading company  in construction chemicals with a strong presence in India,  the Middle East, and Asia-Pacific. This acquisition  strengthens the Group’s presence in high-growth  emerging markets and consolidates its worldwide  leadership in light and sustainable construction.  Saint-Gobain also strengthened its digital platform  dedicated to construction chemicals with the acquisition  of Maturix, a Danish company offering digital solutions  for real-time monitoring of concrete manufacturing. This  transaction expands the Group’s digital offering,  complements its VERIFI® system, and enables smarter  and more sustainable construction processes. Conducting regular reviews of the portfolio  in order to concentrate the Group’s strengths In 2025, the Group completed 6 divestments, generating  €352  million in revenue. Saint-Gobain signed an  agreement to sell Brüggemann, a German specialist in  prefabrication solutions, to the Köster Group. The Group  also finalized the sale of SFIC Belgium, a distributor of  building materials specializing in plaster, insulation, and  ceilings on the Belgian market, as well as the sale of  Tumelero, a building materials distribution chain in Brazil  with a strong presence in the south of the country. D – Creating a culture  of innovation, serving  industrial performance a. Developing a culture of innovation  Innovation is the lever on which Saint-Gobain relies to  develop new products and solutions. It is both a  condition and an accelerator of growth. Innovation  makes it possible: ■ to meet the evolving customer expectations (in  particular for more performance and sustainability) in  order to continuously adjust the Group’s offering;  ■ to stand out from its competitors;  ■ to take advantage of opportunities created by global  megatrends (see section 1.2.1, p. 39);  ■ to anticipate the rapid evolution of the role of digital  and artificial intelligence , which are transforming  Saint-Gobain’s markets;  ■ to stay ahead of increased competition;  ■ to respond effectively to the rapid growth and issues  of certain markets  (for example, the need for  increased productivity for installers, due to a growing  shortage of skilled construction and renovation  workers).  In this context, innovation is a cross-functional asset  that permeates all the Group’s activities and enables  the success of its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan   (see  section 1.2.2, p.  42). To make the most of it,  Saint-Gobain defines priorities and aims to find the right  balance between the different innovation horizons; links  innovation to the expectations of its markets,  maintaining the greatest proximity to its customers (see  above, section 1.2.7.A.a, p.  60); and tends to make  progress to reduce the time-to-market for innovative  solutions.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 63 For the Group, innovation is an open process, involving  both resources and research and development teams  present around the world (8 transversal R&amp;D centers,  3,600 researchers and 450+ patents filed in 2025) as well  as external partners such as start-ups (30+ agreements  signed in 2025 via its NOVA entity), universities,  customers and suppliers of the Group. Saint -Gobain  implements innovation through a structured, Group-wide  system, based on clear strategic priorities, a unified  Innovation Framework, and strong coordination between  central and regional teams.  Innovation is driven by  customer centricity, sustainability, and the transition of  products to complete solutions, supported by methods,  tools, and learning loops that improve success rates and  shorten time-to-market. A central Innovation  Development team maintains and deploys this  framework, while the regions gradually take responsibility  in order to maximize the impact as close as possible to  the activities. Skills are strengthened through extensive  training programs, including the network of Innovation  Catalysts – employees formally trained to stimulate  innovation locally – as well as through digital tools such  as the Innovation Project Companion, which offers  secure, personalized support. Together, these elements  create a shared mindset, harmonized practices, and  execution discipline that enable Saint-Gobain to innovate  consistently and effectively across the Group.  Saint-Gobain uses AI and digital tools to accelerate  innovation, facilitating faster experimentation, more  informed decision-making, and better access to  knowledge. Generative AI contributes to the  development of new materials, while secure internal tools  increase productivity and support teams. Together, these  technologies optimize workflows, reduce time-to-market,  and drive data-driven innovation.  b. Integrating innovation  into industrial processes  Innovation is central to Saint-Gobain’s industrial  operations. By relying on innovation the Group makes  progress in terms of operational excellence,  decarbonization, and circularity . Year after year,  investments in industrial facilities have increased  productivity and safety, reduced carbon emissions, raw  material consumption and waste production.  This modernization involves the integration of digital  technologies (in particular via IoT sensors for real-time  monitoring of energy production and consumption);  predictive maintenance through data analysis  (vibrations, temperatures, pressures) in order to reduce  unplanned shutdowns; and digital twinning to simulate  and optimize production lines. This also involves  replacing energy sources , such as biogas or  hydroelectricity, rather than fossil fuels. It also involves  automation and robotification , deploying robots for  repetitive, dangerous or delicate tasks (glass handling,  packaging) and automated quality control systems to  immediately detect defects. It is also a question of  reducing the carbon footprint , through the energy  optimization of glass furnaces (new geometries, heat  recovery); the use of alternative fuels (hydrogen,  electricity) to replace natural gas; or by integrating  waste or end-of-life products into the production  process. Saint-Gobain is also working on deploying tools  based on artificial intelligence , for example in its  automotive glazing plants – to improve product quality –  or to optimize the operation of its fiberglass furnaces, in  order to reduce costs, the quantity of resources and  energy used, and CO 2 emissions. Finally, the Group is  making progress thanks to new materials  and the  optimization of formulations, through more efficient and  sustainable materials (insulation, smart glazing), less  energy-intensive melting and transformation processes,  as well as additives and formulations that improve  quality, while accelerating rates. E – Developing a culture of trust a. Building trust with stakeholders Values shared with Saint-Gobain’s  stakeholders The Group bases its development on its corporate  purpose and draws on strong values firmly rooted in  Saint-Gobain’s culture: the Principles of Conduct and  Action. They constitute the Group’s code of ethics and  guide Saint-Gobain’s decisions. This code of ethics is the foundation of all Saint-Gobain  policies and commitments, specifically the compliance  policies, the human rights policy, the Environmental,  Health and Safety Charter, and the “Buyer” and “Supplier”  Charters of the Purchasing Department. This code of  ethics applies to all Group entities and employees, as well  as subcontractors in their work on Saint-Gobain sites, and  suppliers (see section 1.2.3.A, p. 46).  The Principles of Conduct and Action are translated into  31 languages and available on the Group’s website for  wide distribution to external as well as internal  stakeholders. During the recruitment process or at the  time of onboarding, they are communicated to each new  employee, including those with fixed-term contracts and  temporary workers, in the local language of the country.  This communication may take various forms, depending  on the country: ■ A welcome brochure with comments from the Human  Resources representative or the manager; ■ The code of ethics directly incorporated into the  employment contract or the job offer letter. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 64 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com Online training on the Principles of Conduct and Action  is available to all employees and is mandatory for new  hires. The Saint -Gobain University Management School  offers an introduction to business ethics and compliance  to allow managers to discuss these issues and share  their operational experience.  The Group trains each new manager from their first year  at the Group on the code of ethics, the fight against  corruption, and compliance with antitrust law  (“Principles of Conduct and Action, ACT and Comply”).  Participation in public debate &gt; Advocacy Saint-Gobain participates in public debates on the  strategic challenges  for its business activity and  environment. This participation takes place directly, via  professional associations, and via global, regional, or local  alliances. The Group’s advocacy activities aim to  constructively feed into discussions and bring together  value chain players and stakeholders. Inspired by its  corporate purpose “Making the world a better home ,”  Saint-Gobain regularly works on the priority themes of  decarbonization of the economy, circularity, and well- being in the construction sector (see section 1.3.2, p. 74).  Saint-Gobain and the majority of its subsidiaries belong  to professional associations representing their industry  at the national or supranational levels. For example, in  France, the Group is involved in professional associations  representing companies, such as Afep and Medef.  Saint-Gobain’s subsidiaries also cooperate with various  local associations or organizations involved in normative  or regulatory issues, or related to  environmental, social,  societal or economic issues. The subsidiaries have internal  procedures in place to ensure that their participation in  associations is recognized and referenced, and that  employees who represent them in associations are  trained in the rules of antitrust law. In 2025, Saint-Gobain  published a Lobbying Policy.  Policy Lobbying Policy Purpose Provide a framework for  responsible lobbying activities Scope of application Employees, corporate officers,  Group managers in contact with  managers holding political office,  administrations or governments Under the supervision of Director of Institutional Affairs Link to the policy Lobbying Policy Saint-Gobain ensures that its advocacy initiatives are  aligned with its commitment to contribute to carbon  neutrality by 2050. Its actions and positions therefore  take into account the ambition to limit the rise  in temperatures to below 1.5 °C.  The Group ensures the proper coordination of the  positions taken locally and that these institutional  commitments are well-known and respected by  the  countries. Saint -Gobain complies with the  transparency obligations applicable to relations between  companies and public authorities in all the  countries  where it operates. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 65 &gt; Multi-stakeholder partnerships Saint-Gobain has been a member of the United Nations  Global Compact since 2003 and regularly reports on its  progress in the areas covered by the Compact.  Saint-Gobain also encourages its partners, particularly its  suppliers, to commit to the Global Compact’s ten  principles. Likewise, the Group pays special attention to the United  Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), so as to  make progress in evaluating the real impacts that  companies can have in working towards a fairer, more  sustainable world. These goals also make possible new  multi-stakeholder collaborations, in which companies are  engaged locally and globally.  Finally, the Group is a member of the forum of committed  companies with the organization Transparency  International France. In this respect, Saint -Gobain has  committed to rejecting and combating corruption, in all  its forms. The Group undertakes to make its best efforts  to implement a solid prevention mechanism, inspired by  the current best practices of the business world.  A list of the Group’s main partnerships and investments is  available on the website (https://www.saint-gobain.com/ en/corporate-responsibility/our-responsibility/our- commitments-partnerships-and-recognitions). The Group refrains from financing any political party. b. Committed employees Building a culture of trust,  responsibility and collaboration Saint-Gobain’s new organization, aiming to empower  countries and at the local level, entails building trust, as a  condition for the effective delegation of authority and  fostering cross-functional collaboration between all the  Group’s functions and business lines at the global level.  The Trust, Empowerment and Collaboration (TEC)  approach represents a significant change in corporate  culture within the Group , which primarily concerns  managerial culture: the aim is to establish, by default,  the  principle of trust and thus move from a vertical,  traditional structure, to an open, learning organization,  leaving the freedom of action at the local management  level in the 80 countries (1) where Saint-Gobain operates.  It also responds to the underlying trends affecting the  world in which the Group operates, and in particular to  the disintermediation brought about by new  technologies, which makes managerial methods obsolete  and promotes profound changes in the relationship  between individuals and work. The successive lockdowns  associated with Covid-19, the development of  teleworking, and the adaptation of working hours at the  production sites have called for greater autonomy on  the part of employees and required them to be more  flexible and adaptable. In this respect, the TEC approach  is a way of aligning Saint-Gobain’s strategy with changes  in the expectations of employees and strongly supports  the development of its employer brand. The TEC culture also helps to make the innovation  process more efficient, to align it with customer needs  by fostering collaboration between countries and  business lines (for example, to provide an offering of  complete solutions to Saint-Gobain’s customers) and  between functions (for example, between researchers,  marketing specialists and technicians).  To implement this approach on the ground, the Group is  relying increasingly on its training program (see section  1.2.7.F.b, p. 70) and on approaches such as collective and  individual coaching, made accessible to a greater  number of employees, as well as the gradual roll-out of  360° assessments. The development of feedback is  encouraged to strengthen collaboration and trust, both  at the Group level (for example, via the annual  me@Saint-Gobain survey), as well as in project  management, and at the individual level (by developing  the ability to request and receive feedback). Each manager is therefore encouraged to promote  the TEC culture within their scope and monitor progress  on its implementation.  Employee satisfaction Increasing employees’ involvement in a context of  change, both generational and technological, is an  essential challenge for the Group. To meet this challenge,  the Group places managerial attitude and involvement at  the heart of this approach: managers motivate and  develop the loyalty of employees by giving more  meaning to their everyday work and by favoring a spirit  of initiative. Since 2019, a unique tool to measure employee  commitment has been developed. It makes it possible to  conduct surveys targeted to particular populations and/ or themes, for example, such as the specific  responsiveness of managers during transformation  phases. This platform is also used to conduct the annual  global survey sent to all employees: “me@Saint-Gobain”.  Employees are asked questions covering the seven main  focus areas of the HR policy and the current  transformation programs: ■ Team: a positive and collaborative working environment; ■ Actor: an organization that values employees; ■ Talent: an HR organization focused on upskilling; ■ Management: a participatory management style; ■ Inspiration: a clear focus on the future; ■ CSR: a responsible company; ■ Innovation, excellence, and customer focus. With a participation rate of 88% in 2025,  127,311  employees took part. In particular, 85% of them  would recommend Saint-Gobain as a good place to work. The engagement index reached a score of 83%.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 66 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) This figure refers to countries with active industrial presence. This index groups the results into four themes: ■ Pride in working for Saint-Gobain; ■ Short-term retention capacity; ■ Recommendation; ■ The feeling of being appreciated and valued for their  work. In accordance with the HR organization, each country is  able to obtain results for its own scope and is  responsible for circulating the results and initiating  dialog with employees to define action plans to work  on areas requiring improvement. F – Deploying an expertise  platform a. Ensuring the Group’s operational  performance The "WCM" Program for industrial excellence Saint-Gobain’s “World Class Manufacturing” (WCM)  program, deployed at industrial sites, combines the  standardization of methods, tools, and best practices   with the modularity required to adapt to a wide variety  of industrial processes and site sizes. It is based on  continuous performance improvement methods such as  Lean, Six Sigma, TPM (Total Productive Maintenance),  and 5S. This program defines the logic, the objective of  rigor, and the actions to achieve continuous  improvements in terms of quality, performance, and  sustainability by integrating a high level of service and  customer satisfaction.  The “WCM” program is based on certification procedures  such as ISO 9001 for quality, ISO 14001 and 50001 for the  environment, ILO OSH 2001 and ISO 45001 for health and  safety. This is a program of cultural transformation of  sites, aimed at involving as many people as possible in  improving performance. Digital tools are made available  to help this transformation. Its management, monitoring  its execution, and improving the performance of the sites  are managed across the businesses in coordination with  the Management of the sites. The deployment of the “WCM” program at a site is  adapted to its priorities, size, strengths, and weaknesses  and its objectives in terms of financial and environmental  performance, quality, and customer satisfaction. Thus,  each site establishes its own roadmap, in accordance  with international guidelines, standards specific to its  business line, the expectations of its customers and the  improvement objectives set. A “WCM” expertise network  has been set up to better disseminate the program,  optimize resources by country, region, or business line,  and share expertise on each of the program pillars. A  central team heads up the WCM network, trains the  teams, creates and applies the program standards,  circulates the tools, and provides site-specific assistance  on request. They rely on local teams to increase the  maturity of the sites. Saint-Gobain’s “WCM” program is based on eight pillars,  each one representing an area of excellence. The foundations of the program define the methods  and tools: ■ Analyzing losses to determine the priority of actions; ■ Resolving problems with logic, rigor and precision for  lasting improvement; ■ Involving and engaging employees in a proactive  approach; ■ Improving standards to make progress easier to  deploy and more robust over time. Achieving industrial excellence is a demanding process  that requires gradual, methodical and constant  implementation. To support the sites in this process,  training programs are rolled out during sessions led  either by the central teams or by local trainers. These  trainers are trained and certified by the central team. In  2025, more than 2,200 employees were trained. The  methods developed under the “people development”  pillar to ensure the management of  Saint -Gobain’s  technical skills by adapting training programs, employee  career paths, and, when necessary, the search for external  skills. This pillar also plays a vital role in strengthening the  TEC culture (see section 1.2.7.E.b, p. 66).  The benefits in terms of competitiveness, improvement  of customer service, stability of performance, and  employee commitment can be measured at each stage  of the site path. Stringency levels have been established  to set principles shared by the entire organization. Each  of these levels corresponds both to a performance  objective set by the business and to a maturity milestone  in the approach. Audits verify the robustness of the  actions implemented and the sustainability of the results  obtained. As of late 2025, 45 “Foundations” sites,  181 sites were certified “Bronze”, 60 “Silver” and 6 “Gold”. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 67 The “WCM” program accelerates the digital  transformation of the sites , a significant reduction in  production costs, while minimizing risks in terms of  health, safety, environmental, and industrial risks. The  “Quality”, “Industrial Performance” and “Environment”  pillars contribute significantly to reducing Saint-Gobain’s  environmental footprint by reducing production waste  and water consumption and by improving energy  efficiency. The “WCM” program and its extension to the  supply chain represent a change of culture and  management system, with the aim of bringing a high  level of service to customers, better competitiveness,  and greater efficiency, while continuously improving the  health and safety of all Group employees and also of its  partners. It fosters and promotes employee commitment  and mobility. Finally, it contributes to the success of the  digital transformation of plants and to the adoption  of its methods and tools by user. In the Industry 5.0 era, the Group’s operational  performance is based on the ability of Saint-Gobain’s  sites to integrate digital technology across the entire  value chain. To meet this challenge, a strategic program  has been launched to accelerate the digital  transformation of all the Group’s sites. Its goal is to  increase operational efficiency, reduce costs, and drive  product innovation through a unified governance  framework and state-of-the-art technology assets. This  initiative is part of a global ambition, in line with the  “Lead &amp; Grow” strategy: to make each site, by 2030, a  model of performance and sustainable development on  a global scale, based on an integrated, lean, and resilient  digital ecosystem capable of supporting competitiveness  and operational excellence. To achieve this ambition, it is essential to establish  a  Group baseline to assess the digital maturity of each  site and incorporate it into the existing WCM program.  The objective assessment of this digital maturity on five  levels makes it possible to develop personalized  digitalization roadmaps, taking into account the specific  needs and potential of each site, in order to support  them step by step in their progress. Saint-Gobain is embarking on a bold transformation to  reinvent the future of its operations. By combining  innovation and standardization, Saint-Gobain’s initial  priorities focus on key areas: streamlining solutions,  consolidating data platforms, expanding IoT  connectivity, integrating AI (classic, generative, agentic),  and deploying computer vision and digital twinning.  These levers will allow the Group to rethink its processes  and increase operational excellence to a new level, but  also to continuously improve the performance of its  products in terms of  quality, reliability, and efficiency.  This ambition is also driven by a Digital Transformation  Accelerator, which ensures strategic consistency and  catalyzes synergies. Training programs organized by technical business line  ensure the management and updating of the skills  required to offer customers high-performance,  competitive, and innovative products and services. These  programs are generally developed and implemented by  training teams organized by business line: glass, gypsum,  insulation, mortars, pipe, etc. The Glass Technical  Academy thus incorporated all glass manufacturing  training, as well as technical glass transformation training  for construction and automotive applications. Designed  and delivered by around 70 experts, the training sessions  provide in-depth technical content, practical exercises,  and site visits. This constitution of a center of excellence  around the businesses enables the transfer of knowledge,  sharing of best practices, and the alignment of methods  within the business lines. Optimizing the supply chain Ensuring optimized management of the supply chain,  from supplier to customer, is an essential part of both  industrial efficiency and customer service. By helping  optimize inventory levels, supply chain efficiency is also a  crucial factor in cash management. Lastly, the Supply  Chain makes it possible to develop innovation in logistics  services, the shared management of products, and their  sales forecasts (Sales &amp; Operations Planning), which are  levers of profitable growth in Saint-Gobain’s existing and  future markets of Saint-Gobain. All of these missions  make the supply chain a major contributor to the  company’s sustainable development. The Group’s supply chains may be local or global  and are managed by the entities’ internal organizations.  Global coordination and a team of experts allow  common logistics optimization tools, dedicated training,  and customized supply chain improvement programs to  be provided. These teams of experts also work directly in  the countries with the support of the general  management bodies to raise awareness of all functions,  identify potential for improvement and draw up action  plans to be implemented by the country teams.  Depending on the maturity of local supply chains,  actions may involve optimizing the management of  product lines by the marketing teams, implementing  collaborative processes, developing the customer culture  at the sites, adapting planning (customer forecasts such  as plant scheduling) or optimizing transport. The  objective is to maximize customer satisfaction while  optimizing financial and environmental impacts. Saint-Gobain has also developed “Supply Chain 4.0”  digital programs to optimize distribution networks,  measure transport CO₂ emissions, and then take action to  reduce them, and reduce the lead times in order or  procurement processes. Whether it is for its purchases or  customer delivery, the Group has programs for the  optimization of processes, routes, and vehicle loading  as well as more efficient travel planning by coordinating  inbound and outbound transport to prevent empty miles. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 68 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com The supply chain support teams also work with the  internal data, AI, digital, marketing, purchasing, and  finance teams to test, implement and improve innovative  solutions or choose market tools so as to best meet the  needs of the entities.  In competitive markets, the Group’s supply chains work  constantly to optimize, innovate, and strike the best  balance between delivery times and reliability, costs,  inventories, and carbon footprint. This is in order to best  meet the growing expectations of Saint-Gobain’s  customers, and to serve the objectives of its “Lead &amp;  Grow” strategic plan. The impact of these scope  3 reduction efforts  (categories 4 and 9) is described in section 3.2.4.C, p. 131.  Quality and the product compliance program The Group’s Quality Excellence Department supports  countries and local teams in order to boost customer  satisfaction by improving the quality culture and Group  product compliance and reducing the costs stemming  from poor quality.  It relies on the ISO  9001 or IATF  16494 certification  standards, the “Quality &amp; Process Control” pillar of the  “WCM” program, and the “Internal Quality Control”  guidelines (see section 5.2.5, p. 313). Training programs  on the tools relating to product quality and compliance  are also organized as part of “UniCampus” (see section  1.2.7.F.b, p. 70) or via e-learning courses available on the  Group’s training platform. The purpose of the product compliance program is to  challenge and improve organizations’ processes in order  to ensure that products: ■ comply with applicable regulations and product  standards; ■ meet the customer promise. The customer promise includes the functionalities or  performances announced and communicated to the  customer.  Product compliance is a fundamental requirement .  Procedures and processes are adapted in step with  changes in the organization of the Group, which  supplemented the measures already in place by  launching a program in 2021 to further strengthen the  culture of quality and resilience of organizations  concerning product compliance. The program was  designed to remind countries and local teams of the key  principles and processes related to product quality and  compliance throughout the product life cycle, from  design to production, marketing, use, and end of life. This program includes four steps: ■ Awareness campaigns at all Group levels; ■ The definition of a roadmap by local management  teams; ■ An assessment of the effectiveness of organizations  and processes in the countries for each business line; ■ The definition and implementation of continuous  improvement plans where necessary. A network of local facilitators and supports comprising  over 200 people at end-2025 has been set up and  trained to support local teams in the implementation of  the program and ensure the sharing of best practices,  thus fostering the continuous improvement of  procedures and processes.  Country and regional CEOs demonstrate their  commitment to promoting and implementing the  program through the annual self-assessment process  and job offer letters implemented at the Group level by  internal audit.  Lastly, the smooth running of the program is monitored  by a committee created for this purpose consisting of  two members of the Group’s General Management, and  the results are presented regularly to the members of the  Executive Committee. The Group’s internal control teams  also assess the effective implementation of the program  through the general audit process and  annual targeted  audit campaigns. Industrial and distribution risk prevention The aim of the industrial and distribution risk prevention  policy is to reduce the likelihood of accidental events,  minimize their severity if  they do occur, and preserve  the continuity of business operations. This policy applies  to all Saint-Gobain sites. Defined and led by the Risk and Insurance Department,  this policy is rolled out within the organization to the sites  by a network of  prevention coordinators. The Group’s  reference framework is a risk prevention manual. It  includes the applicable standards, procedures, and  technical rules, methods for identifying and reducing risks,  including natural risks, and the drafting of emergency and  business continuity plans. This  manual may be shared  with the relevant stakeholders, such as permanent on-site  subcontractors. The risk assessment tool is used by all  operational sites and provides an objective assessment of  the protection and prevention level. It takes into account  both human factors (organization, procedures,  communication, training, etc.) and physical facilities. This  means that each site is able to identify its areas for  improvement, develop action plans including investments,  and use this tool to measure progress. The tool is  accompanied by periodic site visits by the risk prevention  engineering team: a total of 387 sites were visited in 2025.  A total of 119 sites have the HPR (Highly Protected Risk)  label, 47% of which are priority sites. The Group is rolling out business continuity plans for  each site according to the risk assessment and the  vulnerability of processes to unforeseen interruptions  including an anticipation of the impacts of an accident, so  as to limit its effects. The aim is to fully or partially ensure  customer service and recover operational capabilities  as quickly as possible. The subcontractors working on the  sites are involved in the process and must commit to a  prevention plan. In 2025, 21 sites were visited by the  prevention engineering team, specifically to verify the  quality of their continuity plans with regard to the  Group’s guidelines in this area. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 69 Saint-Gobain constantly adapts its risk management  system to better anticipate the potential risks associated  with the effects of climate change. Action plans to  further increase prevention, particularly in the face of  natural disaster risks, are being rolled out. In particular,  they include changes in the design of certain sites to  reduce, where necessary, the impacts of these natural  events, especially flooding. In 2025, 14 at-risk sites were  visited to specifically check their exposure to  natural  disasters, especially flooding. Environmental management system,  industrial hygiene, occupational health  and safety (EHS) The EHS management system is based on an internal  reference framework that describes the process for  identifying and managing environmental and  occupational health and safety impacts and risks to be  implemented by each of the Saint-Gobain sites. The  description of this system is available in chapter 5 “Risks  and Control” in section 5.2.5, p. 313. Each Group site conducts its own assessment of  environmental, health and safety risks  under the Site  Manager’s responsibility. To do this, a standardized  method has been deployed and is regularly updated.  Training courses are organized for site teams, and a  digital application has been developed incorporating risk  analysis, assessment, and management actions. This tool  facilitates the deployment, reliability and monitoring of  risk management action plans at all the Group’s sites. Each site has tools to facilitate the continuous  improvement of its performance by reducing negative  impacts and anticipating risks: ■ Thematic policies accompanied by standards  and application guides; ■ Thematic training and webinars on the main EHS  issues; ■ Tools for monitoring action plans and reporting  to drive performance improvement. A support team of experts, in the regions and centrally,  support the sites in this process. The action plans of the sites are part of the “WCM”  approach (see section 1.2.7.F.a, p.  67), in particular the  “Environment and risk prevention” and “Health &amp; Safety”  pillars.  Audits of the environmental, health, and safety  management system are conducted by the functional  division concerned. These audits are conducted as a  priority at sites with ISO or equivalent certification. In  addition to such audits, a version of the audit  methodology has been developed for smaller sites  for which ISO certification is less suitable. Lastly, a training matrix specifies the training courses on  managing environmental, health, and safety risks for the  teams according to the position held. It is a particularly  relevant tool to use to define employee training courses. b. Developing human capital Saint-Gobain’s development depends first and foremost  on the quality and expertise of the women and men who  comprise the Group. This is reflected first and foremost in  the attention paid to recruiting the best talents in all  specialties, including engineers, digital &amp; data scientists,  sales and marketing staff, product managers, supply  chain, and sustainable development specialists. Beyond  the recruitment process and the attention paid to  its  brand as an employer, it is Saint -Gobain’s collective  ability to train, nurture, and constantly develop these  talents that will enable it to stand out. With digital  technology and refined data processing, career paths can  be more easily individualized and the HR support policy  customized (see section 1.2.7.E.b, p. 66). Training provided by Saint -Gobain must ensure the  employability and success of all employees throughout  their working lives. The objective is to facilitate access to  training through formats that correspond to their needs  and expectations. Training is part of the Group’s major transformations, as  it provides support for employees and availability of the  skills necessary for the success of operational teams. Particular emphasis is placed on the skills that are  the most critical for the future of the Group and that will  therefore strengthen the employability of  employees in  the medium and long term. The training policy is structured as follows: ■ Supporting the transformation of leadership based on  trust, empowerment and collaboration, and  promoting ways of working that generate greater  efficiency and flexibility. This includes managerial  training and talent development; ■ Fostering growth and focus on customers: sales and  marketing performance, product innovation, services,  and business models; ■ Ensuring efficiency and operational excellence:  industrial performance, and programs dedicated to  Saint-Gobain’s major support functions (finance, HR,  purchasing, marketing, etc.) in order to improve and  adapt skills; ■ Implementing mandatory training (on compliance,  ethics, cybersecurity, etc.). All programs are in digital or hybrid format (in-person  and digital) and the majority of in-person programs are  regional, in order to maximize inclusion for all the  employees worldwide, and to reduce the number  of  business trips and minimize the CO 2 emissions and  carbon footprint. This applies in particular to the  “Unicampus” program, the objective of which is to  enable the  dissemination of a common culture of  operational excellence focused on the expectations  and  needs of  customers. To that end, the training  sessions focus mainly on marketing, sales, and customer  services or  innovation. The only global in-person  programs are  reserved for employees identified as  having high potential. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 70 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com The level of satisfaction measured in 2025 was strong  and improved compared with 2024: ■ 91% of employees confirm that they are satisfied with  their training (“I feel adequately trained to do my  work”); ■ 82% of employees are satisfied with the training and  development proposals (“I have opportunities for  learning and development within my company”); ■ 88% of employees believe they have improved their  knowledge and skills (“I feel I have improved my skills  and abilities”); On the other hand, “Learning” was ranked third best,  with a score of 97.32% in the Top Employer certification,  after “Ethics &amp; Integrity” and “Purpose &amp; Value”. New programs and modules were created in 2025 to  support the Group’s growth strategy in the areas of  Commercial Excellence, Customer Focus, and Value  Creation. These programs aim in particular to increase  skills in the areas of Key Account Management and  Specification Sales. Unicampus programs continued to be  updated and enhanced to accelerate the development of  skills in these same areas.  The Group has launched two online webinars to teach  the basics of artificial intelligence: ■ Understanding Generative AI; ■ Using Generative AI Everyday.  In total, nearly 11,000 employees have been trained,  and these two training courses will be recorded to make  them accessible to everyone at any time. Workshops  are also held on request according to specific needs. c. Striving for commercial excellence Striving for commercial excellence is a priority for  Saint-Gobain, and is one of the priority objectives of  its  “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan (see section 1.2.2.C,  p.  42). The idea is to foster a winning mindset in the  Group’s customer approach, to be the best commercially  and  operationally on every line in Saint -Gobain’s  portfolio, leveraging innovation and digital platforms.  The aim is to outperform the Group’s competitors and  underlying end markets. For several years, Saint-Gobain has developed a  structured customer approach, with an organization that  places knowledge and listening to customers at the heart  of its strategy. This requirement translates into an  approach based on detailed segmentation of its markets  and customization of its offerings, in a “go-to-partner”  logic. The Group provides its teams with effective  methods and tools to strengthen their operational  efficiency over the long term. It also makes a special  effort to intervene as early as possible in the value chain,  in particular by recommending comprehensive solutions  and working closely with architects and specifiers. This  integrated approach enables Saint-Gobain to consistently  outperform its competitors across all of its markets. Identifying customer expectations and needs makes it  possible to improve the existing product and service  offering, drive innovation (see section 1.2.7.A.a, p. 60), and  adapt sales approaches to generate customer loyalty. The development of this corporate culture – in which  every employee focuses squarely on making customers’  every lives easier – is one of the fundamental levers for  generating sustainable growth. Knowledge of current and future market expectations is  essential in order to achieve Saint-Gobain’s medium- and  long-term targets. For this reason, the satisfaction of its  customers is a priority for the Group, whose objective is  to be an organization focused on their expectations.  To measure customer satisfaction, several practices have  been extended to all Group operations: ■ On the one hand, the provision of tools and  methodologies to detect the main points of  satisfaction and dissatisfaction among actual and  potential customers, both direct and indirect, and to  determine the net promoter score, the only measure  common to all; this refined measure will eventually  make all customers the Group’s primary specifiers; ■ On the other hand, the regular deployment of  operational action plans to visibly improve the  customer experience throughout the customer  journey.  Thus, customer satisfaction measurements are carried  out on a regular basis for all operations. In the field, the brands are increasingly collecting  feedback from customers on an interpersonal and  transactional level, with the dissemination, internally and  across functions, of best practices in the area. These data  are cross-referenced with the context of the relationship,  as well as with the type of customers, in order to assess  the degree of satisfaction throughout their journey, i.e.,  across the entire chain of interactions between them and  the Group’s brands. In this context, the satisfaction  questionnaires already implemented have shown that  compliance and punctuality are  the  most frequently  cited negative points. Every piece of customer feedback  is useful in improving the  quality of Saint -Gobain’s  performance in concrete, operational ways. Expertise developed at the central level enables local  teams to accelerate the implementation of best practices  while adapting them to the specificities of their markets,  thus creating an ability to know and respond rapidly to  customer needs which, among other things, enables  Saint-Gobain to deliver commercial excellence and  outperform markets. The aim is to generate growth by creating the conditions  that facilitate the experience and satisfaction of  customers. In addition to tools, it is essential to involve all  employees in this approach. A network of “Customer  Experience Champions” facilitates the deployment of  this customer culture at the local level. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 71 To go even further, Saint-Gobain invests in tools, training  and programs to help its employees better understand  its customers’ needs. The aim is to implement a  widespread culture of commercial excellence and a spirit  of conquest, covering various aspects of the customer  relationship, from devising offers and  setting prices to  digitalizing the customer journey, analyzing margins, and  seizing opportunities specific to certain markets, such as  energy renovation. The aim is to develop a corporate  culture in which the obsession of each employee is to  make the day-to-day lives of  customers easier and  create the conditions for greater proximity, so as to  detect all the customer’s needs and  respond to them  through offers that mobilize the  Group’s full range of  expertise. One of the Group’s areas of excellence and  progress is training, in order to  build a growing  community of customer experience experts, through  training in “customer experience” methods and concepts  through the “Unicampus” program. In addition, a  platform called “Pulse” has been created to centralize  and make available to Saint -Gobain countries and  entities methodologies and tools for  managing the  customer experience, such as  “personas”, i.e., the  definition of groups of customers whose behaviors and  preferences are merged into  a  single predictive model  and interview guides to  encourage each employee to  develop a culture of customer feedback. All the aspects of this approach have the virtue of  positioning Saint-Gobain as a provider of complete  solutions to its customers , and constitute a powerful  lever for development, differentiation and growth  for the Group.  Looking beyond the measurement of satisfaction in the  strictest sense, all these systems create the basis for a  co-development approach; based on customer opinions,  a shared innovation process can be implemented. Actions aimed at specifiers are also carried out. In  construction markets, architects play a key role in the  choice of solutions chosen by direct customers. In  addition, local and central teams work regularly with  them to analyze existing products, identify avenues for  innovation, and develop construction techniques. For  nearly 20 years, Saint -Gobain has organized an  architecture competition for students, the aim being to  develop a project that incorporates the principles of  sustainable construction. Every year, Saint-Gobain selects  a country with an architectural challenge and works  closely with the local municipality to develop a task that  will respond to real social and cultural issues. Students  from universities around the world submit their best ideas  to compete and win one of the prizes in this international  competition. The 2025 competition was organized in  close collaboration with the city of Villefontaine, France,  Les Grands Ateliers, the village of Chimilin, and the  Alliance HQE-GBC. The challenge was to develop a  residential area (for students and visiting professors) near  Les Grands Ateliers, and to renovate a former school  building in Chimilin into a multipurpose building for local  associations and organizations. More than 1,300 students  from over 200 universities in 33 countries submitted  projects. Thirty-three projects were selected for the final  phase and the awarding of five prizes. To meet the specific expectations of specifiers   (architects, project owners, or project managers),  dedicated teams are set up in numerous countries.  They  support customers and facilitate the optimization  of construction projects. These teams also participate in  the development of technical Business Units such as the  “Façades” Business Units or pre-manufacturing. This  collaboration, essential to generating innovation and  developing more sustainable, high-performance  products, solutions and construction methods, is built  right from the start of architects’ training. To help the Group stand out more and increase the value  provided on the market, Saint-Gobain aims to adopt a  one-stop-shop approach enabling them to find all the  solutions they need in one place. This approach simplifies  the customer journey and improves their  overall  experience. In this respect, Saint-Gobain’s organization,  which places the priority on the local level, constitutes a  high-value operational driver, as it enables greater agility  and in-depth knowledge of each region, along with its  regulatory context and economic fabric. The  adjustments made in the summer of 2025 to  Saint-Gobain’s organization reinforce this dynamic,  placing all business lines under the responsibility of the  country and regional CEOs and setting up specialized  central teams to support them.  The Group also uses artificial intelligence to improve its  sales performance, for example by automating the  large-scale analysis of tenders. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN STRATEGY AND VALUE CREATION 72 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.3 Sharing created value and contributions 1.3.1 SHARING THE VALUE CREATED PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SHARING CREATED VALUE AND CONTRIBUTIONS SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 73 €46,483 M €29,376 M SALES OPERATIONAL COSTS –  PAYMENTS TO SUPPLIERS €9,458 M €5,178 M €1,234 M Salaries and other  personnel compensation:  53.44% Taxes and  duties: 6.97% Share retained for  growth: 29.26% €300 M Retirement  contributions: 1.70% €342 M Interest repayment  including IFRS16  interest: 1.93% €1,166 M Dividends paid: 6.59% €21 M Investments in local  communities: 0.12% ADDED VALUE €17,699 M Other miscellaneous  income: €592 M 1.3.2 SAINT-GOBAIN'S CONTRIBUTIONS Saint-Gobain’s CSR strategy (cf.  section 3.1.4 B, p.  114)  relies on two levers for action: ■ deploying high-performant and sustainable solutions  that deliver benefits to stakeholders by maximizing  its contribution in terms of environmental impact,  health, and well-being; ■ mobilizing for sustainability by minimizing the  environmental and social footprint of its operations  across its value chain and with potentially affected  local communities. A – A decarbonated home Combating climate change and advancing the transition  toward a decarbonated world require the collective  mobilization and collaboration of all stakeholders:  customers and suppliers, citizens and employees,  governments and major institutions. Through solutions  that help reduce the construction sector’s environmental  footprint and its commitment to achieving net-zero  emissions by 2050, Saint-Gobain is making a significant  contribution to the fight against global warming. Solutions to reduce the carbon  impact of construction Saint-Gobain is deploying a global strategy aimed at  accelerating the reduction of the construction sector’s  carbon footprint throughout the life cycle of buildings  and infrastructure by developing a growing range of  sustainable solutions. This involves designing and  distributing solutions that are low in carbon emissions  during production, but that also facilitate energy  efficiency during the use phase (reducing heating, air  conditioning, and energy consumption requirements).  In addition to the greenhouse gas emissions avoided by  integrating these solutions into buildings, they also  reduce energy consumption and associated costs for  occupants, as well as reducing overall energy demand  both during peak periods and on average.  These solutions reduce CO₂ emissions, optimize the use  of natural resources, and improve the comfort, safety  and quality of life of occupants. Adaptation and resilience solutions Through its adaptation solutions, Saint-Gobain also  contributes to the resilience of its customers and end  users by responding to the growing needs of  construction markets facing more intense climatic  phenomena.  Thanks to the diversity of its businesses and expertise, its  partnerships and innovations, Saint-Gobain offers  solutions to address the dangers associated with the  effects of climate change (droughts, floods, fires, storms  and rising temperatures). Country platforms play an essential role in offering  adaptation solutions specific to the effects of climate  change in their territory. In this way, in countries  experiencing an increase in the number of heatwave  days, demand is shifting towards insulation solutions  and, more broadly, changes in building design to better  protect occupants from the effects of the sun. For  countries more exposed to the risk of storms, solutions  to reinforce roofs in particular can be offered. In addition to the need to adapt buildings and  infrastructure, Saint-Gobain’s approach also takes into  account the potential impact of climatic phenomena on  its customers’ construction sites. Solutions to improve  productivity or prefabrication can be proposed to better  organize construction sites and improve productivity. 2030 roadmap towards  net zero emissions in 2050 Saint-Gobain is committed to a long-term trajectory  aimed at achieving net zero emissions by 2050,  structured around ambitious intermediate milestones.  This roadmap relies on several complementary levers to  reduce scope  1 and 2 emissions: actions on products  (eco-design, weight reduction, composition, etc.),  industrial excellence (productivity, energy efficiency,  etc.), innovation (creating sites of zero scope  1 and 2  emissions in particular), and decarbonated energy  (electricity or other decarbonated energy sources).  Through its effective actions on these four levers, the  Group has reduced its emissions by 35% in 2025  compared to 2017, in line with its target of -33%  emissions in 2030 compared to 2017, in a context where  Saint-Gobain is developing a growth strategy for 2030  with the rollout of its “Lead &amp; Grow” plan. In this context, a new target has been set and the Group  is now aiming for a reduction of -40% to -45% by 2035. For scope  3, decarbonization is progressing more  gradually, as it involves a large ecosystem of  stakeholders. The most significant categories are  purchasing and transportation, with three key levers for  reduction: reformulating product compositions,  improving logistics performance, and decarbonizing  purchases linked to actions taken directly by suppliers. Saint-Gobain has also implemented a plan to adapt its  industrial sites, which strengthens the Group’s resilience  to the physical risks associated with climate change:  adapting industrial sites and the supply chain to climate  events, but also protecting its employees from extreme  working conditions. The Group’s efforts and the quality of its actions have  once again been rewarded with its inclusion in the CDP’s  “Climate A List.” PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SHARING CREATED VALUE AND CONTRIBUTIONS 74 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) Life Cycle Assessment. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SHARING CREATED VALUE AND CONTRIBUTIONS SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 75 The categories referred to are the most significant ones. Scope 3 cat. 4  and 9: 5 Mt CO2e Scope 3 cat. 1:  10.9 Mt CO2e Scope 3 cat. 3:  5 Mt CO2e Scope 1 and 2: 8.7 Mt CO2e B – More performance with less The construction sector has a major impact on the  environment, accounting for 40% of solid waste and  almost 50% of the consumption of natural resources.  Circular models contribute to environmental protection  and the preservation of resources and nature. Proposing solutions that promote  the development of circular flows  Saint-Gobain invests in innovation and eco-design in  order to offer increasingly sustainable products ,  particularly in the field of lightweight construction. The  Group is therefore continuing its efforts in terms of both  its products and solutions, while also working with its  customers to anticipate the use and demolition phases  of residential and non-residential buildings: optimizing  formulations to incorporate more recycled or renewable  raw materials, simulating the optimal deconstruction of a  building to predict which materials can be recycled and  facilitate their recovery, increasing the modularity of  buildings to facilitate changes in use and thus extend  their occupancy time… Developing circularity drawing on the  Group’s country platforms and expertise Saint-Gobain draws on both the Group’s expertise, which  drives a global vision, know-how and operational  excellence, and on country platforms with in-depth  knowledge of markets and customers, to respond locally  to the challenges on the ground. This combination of  global expertise and local presence enables Saint-Gobain  to implement concrete solutions tailored to customer  expectations and the specific characteristics of each  market (such as distribution channels, regulations,  customer expectations, and construction market  characteristics). This is essential for developing circularity,  which by its very nature can only be achieved at the local  level. Local production, local markets and customers and  leadership, that brings stakeholders together, are all  assets that country platforms can leverage to seize  growth opportunities linked to circularity. Optimizing resources through  the creation of recycling channels To optimize the use of raw materials, Saint-Gobain takes  action on several fronts. The Group first seeks to reduce  waste generation, whether from internal industrial  processes, construction sites, or demolition. Organizing  waste recovery along the value chain, setting up  collection channels (reuse, repair, recycling, or energy  recovery), and initiating waste treatment solutions for  reuse in production are all examples of partnerships and  solutions offered by country platforms. These local  partnerships enable us to work in synergy with other  players in the construction sector to recover and  reintroduce reused or recycled materials or products,  which also requires close dialog with stakeholders. In  parallel with this hands-on management, the Group’s  expertise plays an essential role in product innovation,  the promotion of the circular economy, and R&amp;D aimed  at accelerating the development of recycling channels  and the capacity for reuse or recycling in the  construction sector. Reducing the use  of non-renewable raw materials To reduce the use of non-renewable raw materials,  Saint-Gobain is taking action by reducing the natural  resource intensity of its products through eco-design:  making products and solutions lighter, changing  formulations and substituting non-renewable materials  with recycled or renewable materials, facilitating  recyclability, etc.  Beyond this reduction, Saint-Gobain’s approach is part of  the goal to reduce the intensity of non-renewable raw  materials in the construction sector: developing  modularity that limits waste at source and facilitates  waste management, seeking to increase the lifespan of  materials such as buildings and infrastructure, or  promoting lightweight construction that halves the  amount of materials required compared to traditional  methods. These resources, which are recyclable, recycled,  renewable, biosourced, or derived from co-products, are  directly integrated into the Group’s solutions. Thanks to  this dual approach, Saint-Gobain designs solutions that  are more economical, lighter, and incorporate a  growing proportion of alternative materials , thereby  helping to preserve natural resources and reduce the  environmental impact of the construction sector. Preserving water Faced with increasing water stress and rising demand for  water, Saint-Gobain is strengthening its responsible  water management, particularly with regard to  withdrawals and discharges in areas under water stress  and taking into account the water needs of local  stakeholders. The Group has set ambitious targets,  aiming to achieve zero liquid discharge by 2030 in areas  of extremely high water risk and to minimize water  withdrawals at all its sites, particularly those located in  regions subject to water stress. As a result, 86% of the  water consumed is reused thanks to closed-loop water  reuse systems. An internal water price has been in place  since 2024 and differentiates investments made in areas  under water stress. Protecting biodiversity  and combating deforestation Saint-Gobain is committed to Act4Nature International  (commitment renewed in 2025), reaffirming its  willingness to integrate biodiversity issues into all its  activities. Saint-Gobain has made a commitment to non- deforestation and is implementing a “Forest” policy,  supplemented by two specific policies published or  updated in 2025 (Timber Policy and Wood Pulp Policy).  In 2025, the Group published its Sustainability Briefs  entitled “Forests, Our Commitment,” which presents the  new Forest Policy and the concrete actions Saint-Gobain  is implementing to preserve forest ecosystems. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SHARING CREATED VALUE AND CONTRIBUTIONS 76 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) Life Cycle Assessment. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SHARING CREATED VALUE AND CONTRIBUTIONS SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 77 C – A better living for all  Saint-Gobain bases its development on its purpose –  Making the world a better home – and on strong values.  Through its business model and, in particular, thanks to  the solutions it provides, Saint-Gobain contributes to  having a tangible positive impact on all its stakeholders,  including individuals, local communities, and the  environment. In practice, this translates into respect for  human rights, a responsible purchasing policy, and the  preservation of people’s health and safety throughout  the value chain. Well-being and comfort solutions Saint-Gobain is developing a range of solutions  designed to improve comfort, convenience, and well- being for end users. The Group’s innovations and choice  of suitable materials can help improve cleanliness,  hygiene, air quality, and comfort in living spaces while  limiting health risks. These solutions are particularly  relevant in non-residential buildings such as schools and  hospitals, where indoor environmental quality is  essential. Through this offering, Saint-Gobain provides a  concrete and sustainable response to users’ needs.  When designing products, Saint-Gobain also takes into  account the working conditions of users. Improving  ergonomics for installers and anticipating risks on  construction sites are two key aspects of Saint-Gobain’s  product innovation approach. Employee commitments  For the 11 th consecutive year, Saint-Gobain has been  awarded the “Top Employer Global” certification, which  highlights the quality of the company’s commitment to  its employees, particularly in terms of diversity, inclusion,  and development of opportunities and skills. The high  participation rate in the annual me@Saint-Gobain survey  (88%) and the high level of recommendation of the  company as a good place to work (85%) reflect a  relationship of mutual trust, based on listening and the  involvement of everyone.  In addition, the Saint-Gobain Foundation supports Group  employees in their personal commitments to local  communities. Since its creation in 2008, the Foundation  has supported more than 400 projects sponsored by  employees in 44 countries promoting social housing and  the professional integration of excluded people.  Preserving employees’ health  and security The health and safety of employees are top priorities,  and Saint-Gobain places them at the heart of its  corporate culture. While the frequency rate of accidents  with and without lost time among employees, temporary  workers and permanent subcontractors has been halved  since 2017, Saint-Gobain is strengthening its measures  for managing critical situations at all levels of the  organization.  Eliminating all workplace accidents, everywhere, for  everyone, at all times, is Saint-Gobain’s daily priority. In  this context, the Group reiterates its strong commitment  to making the long-term absence of fatal accidents a  priority objective and is rolling out numerous awareness- raising and prevention initiatives targeting work  situations that could lead to serious accidents on its  sites, for its employees as well as temporary workers and  subcontractors. Responsible purchasing Saint-Gobain is continuing the sustainable transformation  of its supply chain by strengthening the management of  risks related to natural raw materials, in particular through  its “Forest” policy. In addition, Saint-Gobain is mobilizing  its suppliers around ambitious environmental and social  requirements, supported by the development of  recycling channels, such as those for paper, which in  many countries achieve very high rates of recycled  material integration. The Group regularly conducts CSR  audits, particularly in the most sensitive regions, in  accordance with international standards, and implements  corrective action plans with suppliers when necessary. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SHARING CREATED VALUE AND CONTRIBUTIONS 78 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) Life Cycle Assessment. PRESENTIN