  for products  containing recycled  materials; ■ Development of local  recycling channels  in response to the  scarcity of resources. Wind of  Change ST ++ ++ +++ ++ Resources  and the  circular  economy  (see section  3.3.3, p. 143) Goods  and  services Development  of solutions  to meet  customers’  adaptation  and resilience  needs ■ Taking into account  local climate  specificities in  the development  of sustainable  construction solutions; ■ Cooperation with local  actors to improve  the resilience and  adaptability of cities  in relation to their  exposure to physical  risks; ■ Development  of new materials  and construction  techniques for better  energy efficiency. ST + + ++ Sustainable  solutions  (see section  3.2.1.B.b,  p.119) and  transition  plan/ adaptation  plan Goods  and  services Development/ expansion  of low-carbon  products ■ Development of a  low-carbon solutions  offering; ■ Design of products  whose use has little  impact, in particular  energy efficiency  solutions. ST +++ +++ +++ ++ Sustainable  solutions  (see section  3.2.1.B.b,  p.119) and  transition  plan Political  and legal Regulatory  changes in  the composition  of products ■ Substitution of the raw  materials currently  used; ■ Demand for  transparency and  choice of more  sustainable solutions; ■ Implementation of new  compositions. ST + + ++ Adaptation  plan (see section  3.2.3, p. 127) Climate  change  mitigation  transition  plan  (see section  3.2.4, p. 129) Reputa- tional Perception  in civil  society ■ Increasing stakeholder  attention and  responsiveness on  climate change issues,  including the use  of fossil fuels; ■ Growing impact  of public opinion. ST ++ ++ Adaptation  plan (see section  3.2.3, p. 127) Short Medium Long  terms Priority on the  value chain* Risk  management  strategy  and growth  opportunitiesType Title Impact description Scenario *  Very low ( ), Low (+), Moderate (++), High (+++) A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 126 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 3.2.3 ADAPTATION PLAN A – Adaptation to physical risks In addition to developing an offering linked to market  adaptation needs, Saint -Gobain is preparing its  operations for the risks associated with climate change  and is developing: ■ programs to adapt its operations, particularly its  industrial sites, to climatic events (see section 1.2.7.F.a  “Industrial and distribution risk prevention policy”,  p. 67); ■ programs to adapt working conditions to rising  temperatures; ■ adaptation programs linked to the potential impact of  climate change on the supply chain. The “World Class Manufacturing” (WCM) industrial  excellence program (see section 1.2.7.F.a, p. 67) ensures  the implementation of best practices in terms of  methods and tools at industrial sites and facilitates the  implementation of adaptation measures. In 2022, a specific study of the exposure of the main  sites was conducted. These risks are managed by the  sites concerned, which draw up and implement action  plans to reduce their risk exposure, updating business  continuity plans to take account of climatic risks. The  technical and industrial performance department, in  particular the industrial and R&amp;D departments of each  activity, coordinates actions involving changes to  industrial processes or plant design. However, as  specified in paragraph 3.2.2.B, the risks identified do not  present significant financial materiality for the Group  (even over the long-term, in the worst-case scenario  based on current knowledge).  Saint-Gobain is implementing concrete measures to  respond to physical risks at its sites. For example, the  new furnaces installed on the sites are designed to  better take into account the risk of flooding. All  Saint-Gobain industrial sites exceed local seismic  standards. To mitigate the risk of drought, particularly in  water-stressed areas, Saint-Gobain is working to adapt  its most water-intensive sites to operate them in a closed  loop and potentially invest in rainwater capture systems.  These measures are accompanied by training programs  for site teams that enable the rapid implementation of  preventive measures in the event of climate events such  as rising water levels or floods.  The HR and Environment, Health and Safety (EHS) teams  have worked on prevention tools to anticipate the  deterioration of working conditions during heat waves  and periods of intense cold.  B – Adaptation to transition risks All of Saint-Gobain’s activities are compatible with  climate transition and the goal of achieving net zero  emissions by 2025. a. Political and legal At the local level, monitoring and compliance programs  have been implemented in the countries coordinated by  the EHS Department, while the Legal Department  monitors new environmental regulations. In addition, the  marketing teams ensure that the Group’s products  comply with local environmental regulations. Similarly,  the risks of climate change disputes are assessed as part  of the existing risk management process. Saint -Gobain  monitors changes in climate reporting requirements in  order to meet the expectations of its stakeholders,  particularly regulatory requirements and investor  expectations. b. Technological The Group anticipates the technological risk linked to the  substitution of existing products with low-carbon  options by investing in the development of disruptive  technologies and eco-innovative solutions to meet the  expectations of its customers. Two internal carbon prices  were set up in 2016 to support the viability of  Saint-Gobain’s projects and strategy (see section  3.2.1.B.c, p. 120).  c. Market Saint-Gobain anticipates the risk of raw materials  scarcity by actively promoting the transition towards a  circular economy (see section 3.3.3, p.  143) and by  reducing its water consumption (see section 3.3.2,  p.  140). The substitution of non-renewable virgin raw  materials with renewable or recycled raw materials, the  extension of the lifespan or use of Saint-Gobain’s  products or systems, and the reduction of the intensity  of materials are at the heart of the Group’s innovation  process and enable it to ensure the competitiveness of  its solutions while anticipating changes in the  preferences of its end users and legislation. Saint-Gobain is also working to optimize and reduce its  energy consumption through such means as investing in  digital tools to adjust energy consumption as closely as  possible to needs. A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 127 Anticipation of the increase in the cost of high-emission  products, which would follow the tightening of  regulations and the change in preferences expected by  end users and customers, encourages Saint -Gobain to  support the development of low-carbon materials and  methods throughout its value chain. In addition to its  efforts to contribute to carbon neutrality in its  operations, the Group promotes renewable energies  and develops solutions to improve the energy efficiency  of its customers to enable them to meet the climate  and  environmental challenges they face (see section  3.2.2.B, p. 124).  Lastly, the Purchasing Department factors the risk of an  increase in the price of raw materials or of energy and  greenhouse gas emissions into its purchasing strategies.  As part of its Responsible Purchasing program,  Saint-Gobain relies on a diversity of suppliers and supply  sources to reduce the risk of transportation difficulties  and supply chain disruptions. d. Reputational Mindful of the impact of a bad reputation on  environmental matters, the Group fully integrates  reputational risk into its risk management policy.  Saint-Gobain is demonstrating its commitment to the  climate through its climate policy and concrete targets  for reducing its CO2 emissions and energy consumption.  Lastly, the needs and risks related to access to water  for  local populations are also taken into account in its  action plans. C – Opportunities related  to adaptation  Regardless of the predictive scenarios on the effects of  climate change (see section 3.2.2.A, p.  122), the  combined effects of temperature rises and climate  events (droughts, floods, fires, storms, etc.) will have a  strong impact on construction market growth: ■ destruction of homes caused by the increase in  climatic phenomena, resulting in increased demand  for reconstruction in the geographical areas affected; ■ buildings damaged by drought and shrinkage in clay  soils, for example in France the percentage of  buildings reckoned to be exposed or highly exposed  to risks varies from 35% to 78% for temperature  increases between 1.8 °C and 4.4 °C (source I4CE). At the same time, construction methods will have to  change to incorporate new constraints to preserve the  value of buildings and/or to ensure the comfort of  building users, both residential and non-residential, in  response to rising temperatures and the growing  number of days of heatwave.  Saint-Gobain is thus orienting its offering towards  sustainable construction markets. Saint-Gobain’s portfolio  of businesses and expertise in insulation, materials  reinforcement with construction chemicals, and building  science, ensures that it is particularly well positioned to  meet the adaptation needs of construction markets. By  leveraging existing expertise and building on its culture  of innovation (see section 1.2.7.D.a, p. 63), Saint-Gobain’s  range of solutions can be extended to meet the perils  associated with the effects of climate change, in  particular adapting housing to rising temperatures or  protecting buildings from storms and flooding. Thanks to its strong exposure to the renovation market,  the Group is ideally situated to play a decisive role in the  national and European “green recovery” plans for the  energy transition, which should support Saint -Gobain’s  structural growth. Saint-Gobain’s portfolio of expertise  and solutions provides it with a particularly favorable  positioning in the face of the need to adapt construction  markets, and resource scarcity. The program to develop  the range of sustainable and high-performance solutions  also includes a component on improving customer  productivity. The organization structure based on country platforms  (see section 1.2.5, p.  53) makes it possible to develop  solutions that meet the specific needs of the region  concerned and to be part of local building resilience  programs. Specific offers for adapting buildings are being  developed in many countries, for example, in the US  CertainTeed has partnered with the Insurance Institute  for Business &amp; Home Safety (IBHS) to offer roofing  solutions that meet their FORTIFIED™ Roof standard.  FORTIFIED™ Roof is a nationally recognized standard  designed to strengthen homes against extreme weather  conditions, including high winds, hail, and heavy rain. It  goes beyond typical building codes and offers a unified  system of products and installation methods that have  been proven to increase the resilience of homes and  reduce potential damage.  In some parts of the world, rising temperatures combined  with high humidity levels will have a significant effect on  working conditions at construction sites, which may even  lead to a shutdown of construction sites for an increasing  number of days. Saint -Gobain’s customers will have to  change their methods and find productivity drivers. Since  its launch in 2021, the program to develop the range of  sustainable and high-performance solutions (see  section  1.2.7.A, p.  60) has included a customer  productivity improvement component through  digitalization service proposals designed to improve  anticipation of worksite organization or prefabrication,  optimizing assembly time on the worksite. A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 128 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 3.2.4 TRANSITION PLAN  A – 2030 to 2050 trajectory Saint-Gobain’s medium- and long-term growth ambitions  require a reduction in the carbon intensity of its  operations and solutions. The Group has set a target of  net zero emissions by 2050 in scope 1, 2 and 3, validated  by the Science Based Targets initiative (SBTi) and  aligned with the Paris Agreement and a 1.5 °C trajectory. In 2020, SBTi validated Saint -Gobain’s 2030 target:  8.9 million tonnes of CO2 in scope 1 and 2 in 2030, i.e., a  33% reduction in absolute terms between 2017 and 2030,  and a 16% reduction in scope 3 over the same period.  While the Group has rotated its business portfolio, the  cumulative effects on the original baseline are not  significant, at under 5%. The Group has invested in new  business activities, mainly in the construction chemicals  sector, and has sold trading and glass processing  activities. Saint-Gobain has also made acquisitions to  step up growth in new regions, investing in the  Australian company CSR in 2024 as well as in Canada, in  Kaycan, Bailey and BP Canada.  In addition, Saint-Gobain has implemented a set of  indicators to monitor its performance in terms of climate  change. Emissions reduction performance is therefore an  operational performance indicator in the same way as  financial performance indicators. By the end of 2025, the Group had reduced its emissions  in scope 1 and 2 by 35% since 2017. Assessing scope 3 emissions remains a challenge for all  companies due to methodological changes, the  complexity of value chains, and disparities in the quality  of data provided by partners. Since 2021, Saint-Gobain  has implemented a program to improve the quality of  data collected and expand the scope and categories of  scope 3 emissions considered.  This is why the update of Saint-Gobain’s scope  3  emissions assessment in 2021 was accompanied by a  more specific assessment of emission factors and a more  granular analysis of the impacts on the three most  significant categories, representing more than 80% of  total scope 3 emissions. The calculation was updated in  2023 to more accurately reflect the impact of end-of-life  products and based on more recent emission factors.  The methodology used to measure Saint-Gobain’s  scope  3 emissions is available in the resource center  section of its website ( https://www.saint-gobain.com/ en/corporate-responsibility/resource-center). A database has also been created to track the emission  factors of materials and products purchased by the  Group. The ideal and most reliable approach is to obtain  a life cycle analysis from suppliers for the materials and  products they have delivered to the Group. If this  information is not available, the assessment is based on  values from recognized databases, such as Gabi or  EcoInvent. This collective effort to improve data results in an  automatic increase in the Group’s scope  3 emissions  assessment, but it is an essential prerequisite for  ensuring the effectiveness of the collective action plans  implemented.  At the end of 2025, scope 3 emissions were estimated at  23.4 million tonnes for the scope considered in the 2030  target validated by SBTi (24.4 million tonnes at the end  of 2023), as detailed in section 3.2.4.A, p.  129. This  increase is largely due to improved coverage of  estimates, particularly the inclusion of end-of-life  emissions from products. The scope 3 reduction target between 2017 and 2030 is  measured on a reduced scope in accordance with the  methodology validated by SBTi. Nevertheless,  Saint-Gobain assesses all categories and action plans are  implemented across all of its operations. scope  3  reporting tables for all categories and for the scope of  the target validated by SBTi are available at section  3.5.3, p. 197. The scope  3 impact of Saint -Gobain’s operations is  concentrated on the three most significant categories:  ■ purchases of raw materials (category 1) and energy  (category 3);  ■ transport and logistics (categories 4 and 9);  ■ purchases related to trading operations (category 1).  Each country is responsible for its own roadmap  incorporating emissions from scope  1, 2 and 3. Each  year’s financial budgets include an assessment of carbon  emission reductions based on planned investments. The  carbon roadmap is supported by a CAPEX and R&amp;D  investment plan of at least €100  million per year until  2030. In 2025, €213 million had been invested in capital  expenditure and R&amp;D related to the carbon roadmap.  Under European Taxonomy Regulation 2020/852, a  limited number of the Group’s activities have been  assessed. As a result, only 16.4% of its revenue is eligible  in 2025. Furthermore, the methods used to calculate  indicators, particularly Capex and Opex as defined by  the regulation, differ from the operational rules for  monitoring the carbon roadmap. The Capex amounts  published in accordance with the European Taxonomy  Regulation are available in section 3.6, p. 200. The carbon roadmap, and the action plans in general,  addressing the commitment to achieve net zero carbon  emissions by 2050 are regularly monitored by the  governance bodies (cf. section 3.2.1.A, p. 116). A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 129 B – Scope 1 and 2 carbon emissions  and decarbonization levers The 2030 roadmap relies on four main levers to reduce  scope 1 and 2 emissions: ■ actions on products  (A): product optimization and  eco-design, including recycling efforts and the  integration of recycled materials;  ■ industrial excellence  (B): industrial process  improvements and productivity efforts; ■ innovation  (C): new technologies and new  compositions; ■ the use of carbon-free energies (D). The quantification  of the importance of each of these levers in achieving  the 2030 target is estimated and excludes the effect  of production volume.  The CO₂ emissions reduction target is calculated by  absolute value and not by intensity. In addition,  variations in production volume to adapt to market  demand, and changes in scope  related to acquisitions  and disposals, have a significant impact on the  measurement of progress towards the 2030 target.  The success of this roadmap requires the involvement of  all employees and a change of mentality. Carbon  reduction objectives are included in all operational  processes: the WCM program for ongoing productivity  efforts, energy purchases that include securing supplies  of carbon-free energies (renewable electricity or biogas,  for example), and increased use of recycled materials,  which involves the development of collection channels in  all countries or the evolution of products through eco- design (such as lightening and optimizing packaging to  reduce the impact of transport). The future availability of decarbonized and economically  viable energy in sufficient quantities is an indispensable  factor in achieving zero net emissions by 2050. However,  access to this energy remains uncertain owing to  challenges in the production, distribution and growing  demand for low-carbon energy. Energy transport and  the connection of sites also remain key factors to be  included in the roadmap, certainly since the security of  supply is not fully guaranteed. 2017 2025 Volume Optimization  &amp; eco-design Process &amp;  Performance  Improvement Innovation Decarbonated  Energy  Purchase 2030 Growth 2035 The levers identified by Saint-Gobain to achieve its scope 1 and 2 decarbonization targets are detailed in the table  below. The percentage corresponds to the contribution of each lever to achieving the 2030 target (excluding scope  and volume effects). This assessment remains an estimate.  A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 130 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com - 33% A B C D 13.4 Mt 8.7 Mt 9 Mt 8 Mt  Name  of lever Position in supply chain Description  of action Contribution  to the  objective Structuring  assumptions A – Product  optimization  and eco-design Purchases of raw materials Operations Development and optimization  of the solution portfolio (lighter products,  optimized packaging, integration  of recycled materials) 15% Excluding  volume and  scope effects Application case: ■ BANTAM program (weight of plasterboard reduced by up to 20%); ■ SLIMWOOL program (optimized weight of glass wool for equivalent performance). B – Improving  processes and  performance Operations Improving the productivity and energy  efficiency of Saint-Gobain’s industrial  processes 30% Excluding  volume and  scope effects Application case: ■ ISO 50001 certification for 26.5% of Saint-Gobain sites; ■ Deployment of digital tools and energy audits to improve and better control industrial performance. C – Innovation Research and development Research and development of sustainable  and efficient solutions for new decarbonized  industrial processes (low-carbon glass,  hydrogen) 5% Excluding  volume and  scope effects Application case: ■ First low-carbon gypsum plants under investment in Norway and Canada; ■ Oraé® low-carbon glass offer (40% reduction compared with the European market average) and Lanaé® recyclable glass  wool gradually rolled out. D – Purchase  of decarbonated  energy Operations Transition to energy supply from  decarbonized sources 50% Excluding  volume and  scope effects Application case: ■ Action plans for site procurement and development of renewable energy projects; ■ Launch in March 2024 of very low-carbon production at three US façade cladding production sites. C – Scope 3 emissions and  decarbonization levers The deployment of the 2030 carbon roadmap also  includes the scope 3 reduction target. The first step is to  improve the identification and measurement of impacts,  while raising awareness among internal players, in  particular buyers and logistics experts, and external  partners, primarily suppliers.  Actions are taken in each of the 15 categories according  to the materiality of their impact. Three categories are crucial to achieving the reduction  target: ■ category 1: purchased goods and services; ■ categories 4 and 9: transport. While scope  3 decarbonization levers are clearly  identified, the Group’s ability to reduce its scope  3  emissions largely depends on the ability of the Group’s  partners to decarbonize their own operations.  The action plan addresses three main areas: ■ product reformulation, which will contribute  approximately 30% to achieving the target; ■ innovation and logistics performance, which will  contribute approximately 10% to achieving the target. ■ the decarbonization of purchases through supplier-led  initiatives, which will contribute approximately 60% to  achieving the target. These figures exclude any changes in methodology,  changes in scope, improvements in information systems  and the quality of information about emission factors.  SBTi commitment  and presentation of scope 3  Category 11 of scope  3, “Use of products sold”, is not  material for Saint-Gobain according to SBTi criteria and  therefore does not need to be reported. The main  components of this category are the use of boilers and  windshields sold by the Group. For each of these activities, the Group’s direct influence  on reducing this category is limited and does not prevent  the transition of these industries. Nevertheless, Mobility  Business Unit teams are developing specific products for  electric vehicles and are involved in their customers’  action plans for sustainable mobility. Similarly, the  purchasing teams of Saint-Gobain’s distribution  businesses work with their suppliers to reduce the carbon  impact of heating during the use phase of buildings. The  brands are also contributing to efforts to transform the  construction industry by training trade professionals and  informing users (see section 3.4.3.B, p. 163). A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 131  Other scope 3 categories The impact of category 11, on the use of sold products, is  concentrated on two operations: the sale of automotive  glazing and the sale of air-conditioning and heating  equipment by the heating equipment building trading  brands.  With regard to category 3 (fuel and energy-related  emissions not included in scope  1 and 2), efforts to  reduce fossil fuel consumption and promote the use of  decarbonated energy sources will have a positive impact  on the reduction of scope 3 emissions. Regarding category 6 on business travel, the travel  policy was updated in 2023 to: ■ check and significantly reduce the number of trips,  including international trips; ■ systematically review the advisability of postponing or  switching seminars and other meetings into virtual  meetings; ■ travel by train is mandatory for all journeys of less  than 4 hours.  Methodology, Volume Saint-Gobain expects an increase in scope 3 emissions as  a result of the increase in production, and also as a result  of actions to improve the reliability of the scope  3  assessment carried out with suppliers. Five principles are implemented by the purchasing  teams and environmental experts:  ■ a digital tool available to teams at local level and by  category to enable them to estimate scope  3  emissions;  ■ communication kits to share the challenges and  targets of the fight against climate change with  suppliers; ■ the collection of information by suppliers on their  emissions on the basis of life cycle analyses verified  by independent third parties; ■ the integration of the maturity of suppliers regarding  their climate commitment into the overall assessment  of their performance;  ■ the creation of joint action plans with suppliers to  reduce their carbon impact. This systematization will continue to make scope  3  assessments more reliable. It also structures action plans  to accelerate the reduction of emissions.  Best practices have been identified and shared with all  Group buyers. 2017 2025 Methodology,  Volume Reformulation Decarbonization  of the supply  chain Innovation &amp;  logistics  performance 2030 The levers identified by Saint-Gobain to achieve its scope 3 decarbonization targets are detailed in the table below.  The percentage corresponds to the contribution of each lever to achieving the 2030 target (excluding scope and  volume effects). This assessment remains an estimate.  A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 132 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com - 16% 17.4 Mt 23.4 Mt 14,6 Mt A B C  Name  of lever Position in supply  chain Description  of action Contribution  to the  objective Structuring assumptions E –  Reformulation  of products Operations Reformulation of products  aimed at substituting the  highest-impact raw materials,  either with recycled materials  or with new low-carbon  compositions 30% Excluding any changes in methodology  and scope, improvements in information  systems, and the quality of information  on emission factors Application case: ■ Integration of recycled glass in glass wool production (see section 3.3.3.C.b, p. 144). F – Logistics  optimization The entire  value chain      Optimization of logistics  upstream and downstream  of the value chain 10% Excluding any changes in methodology  and scope, improvements in information  systems, and the quality of information  on emission factors Application case: ■ Choice of multimodal and lower-emission modes of transport in partnership with the Group’s logistics providers. G –  Decarbonization  of purchasing Purchases of  raw materials Decarbonization of purchases  through measures carried out  by suppliers (exogenous  driver) 60% Excluding any changes in methodology  and scope, improvements in information  systems, and the quality of information  on emission factors Application case: ■ Assessment questionnaires and on-site audits of the CSR performance of suppliers according to risk level; ■ Specific action plans on critical raw materials such as sand, wood and high-risk minerals (see section 3.4.6, p. 175). D – The objectives beyond 2030 a. Objective 2035 In October 2025, for Capital Markets Day and the launch  of its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan, Saint-Gobain  announced new targets for 2035, with an absolute  reduction in scope  1 and 2 emissions of between 40%  and 45% (2017 basis). This objective was determined by taking into account  the Group’s growth objectives, the effects of measures  and investments linked to the “Carbon 2030” roadmap,  and the uncertainties linked to the availability of low- carbon and competitive energy in the countries where  the Group operates.  This objective was set on a voluntary basis. SBTi  extended the validity of scope  1 and 2 and scope  3  objectives through to 2030. A process to approve new  objectives beyond 2030 has been initiated as part of the  revision of methodologies, which impacts trajectory  calculations in particular.  At the same time, the standards and methodologies for  assessing scope  3 are being revised by the reference  bodies. In addition, pending an assessment of the effect  of these changes, Saint-Gobain is moving ahead with its  in-depth work to improve data quality and the reliability  of the methodology implemented.  b. Objective 2050 A new roadmap for 2050 will be established following  the “Carbon 2030” roadmap. It will incorporate the  technical advances identified thanks to the innovation  efforts made over the 2020-2030 period. While in the  2020-2030 period, innovation efforts will not produce  much by way of tangible results in terms of reducing  scope 1 and 2 emissions, their implementation will be the  most significant contributor to reducing emissions in the  2030-2050 period.  The development of a circular economy in all countries  where the Group is present and is also a determining  factor in achieving the reduction target for scope  1, 2  and 3. The availability of decarbonized and economically viable  energy in sufficient quantities will be an indispensable  factor in achieving zero net emissions by 2050. The associated financial resources will also be assessed. A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME   SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 133 3.2.5 2025 PERFORMANCE (CLIMATE) OBJECTIVES Deadline 2025 data 2024 data (2) 2023 data Baseline -40 to-45% in scope 1 and 2 GHG emissions  between 2017 and 2035 2035  -35.1%  -36.8 %  -34.3 % 8.7 Mt 8.5 Mt 8.8 Mt 13.4 Mt  -16% in scope 3 GHG emissions between 2017  and 2030 (1) 2030  +34.8 %  +36.5 %  +40.8 % 23.4 Mt 23.7 Mt 24.4 Mt 17.4 Mt - 15% in energy consumption between 2010  and 2025 (at iso-production) 2025  -5.9 %  -5.6 %  -6.7 % - 20% in scope 1 and 2 CO2e emissions  between 2010 and 2025 (at iso-production) 2025  -35.4 %  -32.9 %  -30.8 % 100% of revenue generated with products  covered by verified LCA or EPD, excluding  distribution activity 2030  57.4 %  57.7 %  53.5 % (1) Methodology and scope of calculation for scope 3 aligned with the Science Based Targets 2030 (SBTi) initiative. Scope has been expanded compared to 2017 thanks to improved data collection. (2) Data for scope 1 and 2 in 2024 exclude recent acquisitions. 2024 CO2e is estimated at 8.9 Mt, including the full-year effect of acquisitions. The 2010-2025 targets for iso-production were  established in 2014, prior to the implementation of the  “Transform &amp; Grow” and “Grow &amp; Impact” strategic plans.  Given the rotation of the asset portfolio and changes in  the geographical footprint, they no longer reflect the  Group’s performance. As a result, medium-term targets  in absolute terms have been gradually introduced.  Nevertheless, by the end of 2025, most of the targets set  had been achieved:  ■ CO2 emissions and energy: exceeded CO2e emission  reduction targets for scope  1 and 2 (-35% vs. -20%)  thanks to the use of carbon-free energy, offsetting the  shortfall in energy consumption reduction targets  (-6% vs. -15%). The Group has significantly changed its  energy mix, favoring the transition from high-carbon  energies such as fuel oil to lower-carbon energies  such as natural gas and decarbonized energies such  as decarbonized electricity. The choice of energy type  has an impact on the consumption measured for the  same use. ■ Other emissions: Sox and Nox emission reduction  targets have been significantly exceeded. The dust  emission reduction target, although regularly  exceeded in recent years, has not been achieved in  2025. ■ Water discharges: the effect of changes in the asset  portfolio is particularly significant for this indicator.  The reduction achieved remains substantial, even if it  is far from the target of -48% vs. -80%. ■ Waste: with a reduction of 47%, the result achieved is  close to the target of 50%. A COMMITTED GROUP A DECARBONATED HOME 134 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com GHG EMISSIONS 2025 2024 (2) 2023 ESRS GRI SDG Market based Gross direct GHG emissions (scope 1) 7,907,492 t 7,628,642 t 7,738,985 t E1-6 305-1 12; 13 Change in direct GHG emissions (scope 1) 278,850 t -110,343 t -657,341 t E1-6 305-5 12; 13  3.66%  -1.43%  -7.83 % E1-6 305-5 12; 13 Percentage of direct GHG emissions (scope 1)  from regulated emission trading schemes  EU-ETS  35.9%  34.4% E1-6 305-5 12; 13 Gross indirect GHG emissions (scope 2) -  market-based 802,233 t 856,282 t 1,088,365 t E1-6 305-5 12; 13 Change in indirect GHG emissions (scope 2) -  market based -54,049 t -232,083 t -317,677 t E1-6 305-5 12; 13 Percentage of indirect GHG emissions (scope  2) from contractual instruments  69.7%  68.2% E1-6 305-5 12; 13 GHG emissions (scope 1+2) - market based 8,709,725 t 8,484,924 t 8,827,350 t E1-6 305-1 &amp; 2 12; 13 Change in total GHG emissions (scope 1+2) -  market based 224,801 t -342,426 t -975,018 t E1-6 305-5 12; 13 Other indirect GHG emissions (scope 3) -  Group (1) 78,371,616 t 79,403,867 t 81,859,656 t E1-6 30