centers development centers 30+ 3,600 3,500 in 2025 by the end of 2025 by the end of 2025 agreements signed  with startups researchers employees in the  marketing field 1,170 2,653 12 by the end of 2025 by the end of 2025 by the end of 2025 manufacturing facilities  worldwide (a) sales outlets and  showrooms worldwide Sustainable Construction  Talks organized  worldwide (b) 162,000 80 132 PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 26 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (a)   By the end of 2025 - cf. “Our global footprint”, pp. 12 et 13. (b)   Cf. section 3.1.4.B, p. 63. 1.1.2 MARKETS ON A STRUCTURAL GROWTH PATH Saint-Gobain’s business is structurally driven by  underlying trends (see section 1.2.1.A, p. 39) that provide  the Group with long-term growth drivers, not only in the  residential market but also in the non-residential and  infrastructure markets. Saint-Gobain relies on a robust  organizational model based on country platforms as  management units, grouped into five major regions:  Northern Europe, Southern Europe – Middle East – Africa,  North America, Latin America, and Asia-Pacific (see “Our  global footprint,” pp.  12 and 13). With an industrial  presence in 80 countries , the Group benefits from  growth opportunities in every major region of the world. A – Western Europe In Western Europe, a recovery is expected in the  residential markets, which have seen a contraction in  recent years due to the inflation surge of 2022-2024 and  the rise in real estate rates. Building permits and housing  starts are increasing in countries that  are  important to  the Group, such as France, the  United Kingdom,  Germany, and Sweden. As of late November 2025,  building permits were up 14% year-on-year in France  (1).  The decline in activity in  recent years has exacerbated  the housing shortage and the housing crisis in Europe,  degrading property purchasing power and encouraging  governments to support housing construction . In the  United Kingdom, for example, there is a shortfall of  2.5 million homes, and the current government aims to  build 1.5  million homes between 2025 and 2029, which  represents a clear acceleration from the construction  levels of the last decade.  The winds are also favorable for building renovation. In  addition to the ongoing recovery in real estate  transactions in most European countries, which are an  important driver of renovation, energy renovation is  supported by public measures , both at the European  and national levels. In addition to European regulatory  mechanisms, such as the Energy Performance of  Buildings Directive (EPBD), many European countries  have adopted regulatory arrangements (such as the  Climate and Resilience Act in France, which prohibits the  leasing of energy-inefficient residences), subsidies, and  market mechanisms (such as energy saving certificates  in Spain). Energy renovation also offers significant  financial benefits for households, through its ability to  reduce energy bills on the one hand, and also because of  the growing influence of energy efficiency on  property  prices. In many European countries, price differentials  between the most and least energy-efficient homes  exceed 15%, even comparing homes with equivalent  structural characteristics: this is the case in the  Netherlands, for example, as well as in Germany, France,  Italy and Spain. Non-residential and infrastructure  markets are also drivers in Europe.  The need for reindustrialization, particularly in the  defense sector, and the development of data centers  represent sources of growth for new construction, while  the energy renovation of tertiary buildings, both public  and private, maintains its growth level. In the  infrastructure sector, some European governments have  ambitious development projects planned; this is notably  the case in Germany, where the new €500  billion  Infrastructure Fund will significantly increase activity in  this sector over the next 10 years. The requirements of  renovating aging infrastructure on the continent,  including bridges and tunnels, offer complementary  opportunities. Finally, the increasing effects of climate  crises on buildings, both residential and non-residential,  imply significant adaptation needs . The solutions  offered by Saint-Gobain for renovation are perfectly  adapted to these new needs. For example, protecting  buildings against heat waves involves insulating external  walls or installing solar control glass. Similarly, the  Group’s construction chemicals solutions meet the need  for buildings to be watertight, against violent rains or  floods, for example.  B – North America  In North America, the housing shortage  is a very  important factor in the structural growth  of  Saint-Gobain’s business in the residential sector. In the  US, the pace of construction in the decade following the  2008 global financial crisis did not keep up with  population growth, and the shortfall is currently  estimated at 3.7  million homes  (2), which represents  nearly 3 years’ worth of construction at the current pace.  In Canada, strong population growth over the past  decade, fueled by dynamic immigration, has also led to  severe housing shortages, exacerbating the housing  affordability crisis: an additional 5  million homes would  be needed to meet the needs and restore the housing  purchasing power of Canadian households (3).  Furthermore, the frequency of hurricanes, fires and  storms, which is unfortunately accentuated by climate  change, implies non-discretionary renovation needs ,  which represent a source of sustainable growth for the  Group’s roofing and cladding business in the region. The  increase in such climatic events due to the climate  crisis will generate significant adaptation needs in the  coming years.  Finally, the non-residential and infrastructure markets  also benefit from solid structural fundamentals. The  reindustrialization policies of successive US  governments, as well as the trend towards reshoring  industries on US soil, are driving growth in the industrial  buildings market and the rise of data centers represents  a significant source of growth. As in Europe,  infrastructure needs remain high, particularly in terms  of renovation. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 27 (1) CGEDD. (2) Freddie Mac. (3) CMCH Canada Mortgage &amp; Housing Corporation. C – Asia and fast-growing regions Emerging countries are experiencing strong population  growth, combined with rapid urbanization. The global  population will increase from 8.2 billion in 2024 to nearly  10 billion by 2080 (1), and most of this increase will take  place in emerging countries. Similarly, the urban  population is expected to grow by almost 2  billion by  2050, particularly in Asia and Africa. In addition, around  1.1 billion people currently live in informal settlements and  are still waiting for decent housing (2). Based on this data,  the World Economic Forum estimates that  new  residential construction needs  worldwide will  reach  2 billion homes over the next 80 years.  Emerging countries are also experiencing economic  development trajectories favoring strong growth of the  middle classes , whose desire for quality housing  perfectly matches Saint-Gobain’s offer. In India, the  number of households entering the middle class will  increase by an average of 16% per year by 2030. This  growth has also reached 11% in Indonesia, and 21% in  Vietnam. Urbanization will also continue in the Middle  East and Latin America : in Brazil, for example,  the  middle class is expected to grow by nearly 4%  annually over the coming years  (3). This demographic  growth and its associated urbanization imply significant  needs for the expansion of  cities, with consequential  tertiary housing and  infrastructure needs, representing  complementary sources of business for the Group. Some  countries are  starting to build new cities or ambitious  urban development projects. In Egypt, 37 new cities are  under construction, and Saudi Arabia is currently  building 14 large-scale projects to support its economic  diversification program. D – Industrial markets For its industrial customers , Saint-Gobain provides  performance solutions and meets market expectations  through its ability to innovate , its research and  development potential, its closeness to its customers,  and its use of digital technology and data analysis.  Industrial markets are expected to continue to grow  steadily over the 2025-2030 period, driven by  reindustrialization and strategic autonomy efforts in  developed countries, ramped-up military spending, and  the development of new industrial sectors associated  with “green” technologies.  The mobility market, in which Saint-Gobain is present  through a range of insulation, acoustics, and technical  glazing solutions, is undergoing expansion fueled by the  natural turnover of car fleets, the transition to electric  vehicles, and the relocation of value chains by auto  manufacturers, trends that will structurally drive the  Group’s business in the coming years. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 28 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com (1) United Nations. (2) Idem. (3) Oxford Economics. 1.1.3 A SOLUTIONS-BASED APPROACH,  ROLLED OUT COUNTRY BY COUNTRY  Saint-Gobain relies on the support of a robust organization that is well suited to a deglobalized world, as well as  local construction markets. It is based on country platforms as managerial units, which are grouped into five major  regions: Northern Europe, Southern Europe – Middle East – Africa, North America, Latin America, and Asia-Pacific.  Within each of these regions, the country CEOs are responsible for all Saint-Gobain product lines, to provide a  comprehensive response to the most complex needs of their customers, through a solutions-based approach.  Country by country, Saint-Gobain is deploying this approach in all its markets – residential as well as non-residential  and infrastructure. A – Construction and renovation markets a. Residential In all its countries, Saint-Gobain has successfully  deployed a solutions-based approach, enabling it to  position itself as a “one-stop shop,” a leading supplier of  integrated solutions both for renovation  and new  construction of single-family houses and multi- family housing. The Group provides a comprehensive range of solutions  dedicated to the global renovation of housing,  which  help improve their energy performance, acoustic and  visual comfort, as well as air quality. In France, for  example, a customer looking to undertake a  residential  renovation project can find an integrated offering from  Saint-Gobain that covers all their needs. First of all, this  offering includes comprehensive renovation systems,  particularly external thermal insulation solutions  combining insulating materials, plasterboard, profiles,  mortars, fiberglass reinforcement, and finish coats, as  well as solar-control insulated glazing units and blow-in  insulation for attics. When combined, these solutions  help cut a residence’s energy bill by 70%, and  significantly improve its energy performance diagnosis  (EPD), raising it from class G to class C. Saint-Gobain supplements this offering with digital  solutions available through its distribution businesses,  making it easier to conduct assessments and issue price  quotes for improving building energy performance. The  Group also provides services intended to improve  productivity for craftsmen on worksites , such as  logistics and kitting solutions. Aware of the  environmental issues, Saint -Gobain also provides  worksite waste collection and sorting services, relying  on a network of 810 collection and sorting points in  France, particularly at Point.P stores. Finally, the Group  invests heavily in training professionals: each year, over  100,000 craftsmen are trained and supported in France  on energy renovation issues.  In North America, and particularly in Canada in the past  few years, the Group has expanded its offering  dedicated to lightweight construction , in order to  provide a comprehensive solution for the building  envelope, helping improve the quality of buildings and  their resilience against climate change. Saint-Gobain stands out in particular by offering  complete systems for the customer . For example, the  Group markets integrated roofing systems under the  CertainTeed brand combining waterproofing  membranes, impact-resistant ClimateFlex shingles, and  their associated accessories. These solutions help  buildings better withstand storms and guarantee roof  insurability. The Group also develops siding systems that  are highly resistant to moisture and impacts, while  providing its customers with interactive digital tools that  make it easier to choose and customize solutions.  CertainTeed’s One Precision Assemblies system is  another example that helps significantly reduce project  completion times . Pre-assembled panels featuring  windows, doors, siding, roofing, insulation, storm- proofing and vapor barrier membranes are produced in  one week. Once delivered to a modular home worksite,  they can be installed in under three weeks, compared  with five months for a construction using traditional  methods.  To replace traditional, heavy-construction methods  which devour resources, time, and labor, Saint-Gobain  has developed lightweight façade solutions which are  both ultra-high performance and easy to install . In  Brazil, for example, such solutions combine Glasroc X  board, a moisture-resistant fiberglass-reinforced  plasterboard (Placo and Adfors), glass wool insulation  (Isover), fiber cement sheets (Brasilit), as well as  fiberglass reinforcement mesh and joint tape (Adfors),  and finish coats (Quartzolit) These systems are  supplemented by technical consulting and project  monitoring services.  They make it possible to  significantly improve productivity on worksites ,  particularly in multi-family housing, respond to labor  shortage issues, reduce waste, and offer superior  performance – in terms of quality, energy efficiency, and  occupant comfort – compared with traditional methods. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 29 Example: renovation of a multi-family residential building Example: construction of a single-family house in Canada  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 30 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com Eclaz Sun double glazing  with solar control Interactive digital solution for energy  renovation diagnostics External Thermal Insulation  Composite System Roof thermal  insulation Recycling  services Interior wall  insulation Multipurpose  screeds Acoustic  ceilings Reinforced partitions Heating and plumbing  installation Siding Waterproofing  membranes Ceilings Plasterboard Metal profiles Insulation Roof shingles Roofing  membranes  b. Non-residential Saint-Gobain is accelerating the deployment of its solutions-based approach in non-residential sectors by offering  ranges of solutions dedicated to each market segment (hospitals, schools, hotels, and data centers), meeting the high  demands of its customers in those markets, particularly in terms of energy and acoustic performance, visual comfort,  and air quality. Example of solutions for lightweight construction: school As an example, Saint-Gobain provides a comprehensive  range of high-performance solutions for the  construction and renovation of educational institutions:  ceilings, plaster-based solutions and acoustic flooring  systems help significantly improve the acoustic  performance of classrooms, contributing to better  learning and teaching conditions. To support the  specification of its solutions, Saint-Gobain also offers  augmented-reality digital tools through its Ecophone  acoustic ceilings brand, making it possible to test these  solutions in various acoustic environments recreating  classrooms. In another innovation, plasterboard and  ceilings equipped with Activ’Air technology help absorb  formaldehyde from the ambient air, thus helping  students concentrate better. The Eclaz glazing line  makes it possible to optimize interior comfort, with the  Sun solar-control line for façades that are heavily  exposed to sunlight, or alternatively, the Lumi range of  ultra-transparent glazing, maximizing natural light for  darker rooms. Finally, all of Saint-Gobain’s solutions for  energy renovation , such as its ventilated façade  systems and external thermal insulation systems, make  it possible to reduce heating expenses by 70% in  educational institutions and other public buildings.  Another example: Saint-Gobain is now recognized in  Europe and North America as one of the leaders  – in  terms of supplying construction materials – for data  centers. Saint-Gobain provides a comprehensive,  dedicated range of technical, innovative solutions for  meeting the sector’s strict requirements concerning  thermal performance, fire protection, and productivity.  On this note, there are a few examples we can mention,  such as stone wool-based sandwich panels which are  insulating, fire-resistant, and quick to install, its cool-roof  solutions, making it possible to reduce the temperature  of the building, its ceiling solutions that can support very  heavy loads, its range of solutions for  low-carbon  concrete, its special anti-static flooring, and its fire- resistant glazing.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 31 Customized kitting  service Acoustic  partitions Facade insulation Fire protection  glazing Self-leveling  screeds Suspended ceilings Low-carbon  delivery Solar control glazing  Example: construction of an office building c. Infrastructure Over the past five years, Saint-Gobain has built a place for  itself as a top global leader in construction chemicals. The  Group now offers solutions to meet the needs of  worksites and structures, particularly mortars, concrete  admixtures, cement additives, waterproofing solutions for  buildings and infrastructure, flooring, and façades,  concrete repair solutions, adhesives, joint tape and  adhesive systems. This offering is based on leading brands  like Weber, Chryso, Fosroc, and GCP. Construction  chemicals are essential for the infrastructure market –  bridges, tunnels, airports, hydroelectric power plants and  wind farms – a potentially significant growth driver.   Thanks to a broad range of technical products, the Group  is able to cover the various critical parts of any type  of infrastructure. In this context, Saint-Gobain offers, among others, highly  technical waterproofing solutions for bridges and  tunnels, concrete admixtures and cement additives that  help improve the mechanical performance and durability  of structures, as well as putties and anchoring systems  for fastening and connecting concrete structural  elements. The Group is also developing innovative digital  solutions, like Verifi and Maturix, which are used  to monitor concrete properties in real time.  Additionally, Saint-Gobain offers a comprehensive range  of ductile cast iron pipes  and accessories for water  transport and distribution through the PAM brand, and  with the Adfors brand, it designs fiberglass and technical  textiles used for the repair and reinforcement of roads  and airport runways. Thanks to this integrated, innovative offering,  Saint-Gobain sustainably supports infrastructure  development and modernization,  while meeting  increasing requirements concerning performance,  reliability, and durability. As an example, on the large  airport construction sites in high-growth countries like  Vietnam, Saint-Gobain stands out as the supplier of  integrated solutions  that address both the building  aspect – fire-resistant glazing and partitions, acoustic  ceiling and partition solutions, lightweight ventilated  façade systems, solar-control glazing, waterproofing  systems for flat roofs, or technical flooring for heavy- traffic areas – and the infrastructure aspect –  fiberglass for reinforcing runways and taxiways,  concrete admixtures, waterproofing solutions for  bridges and underground passages, and fireproofing  for steel structures.  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 32 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com Ventilation duct  insulation “Cool roof”  waterproofing Highly energy-efficient  glazing Electrochromic  insulating glass Fire-resistant glass  partitions High-security glass Glass wool insulation Mortars for floor  covering Acoustic ceilings Perforated  plasterboard Low-carbon concrete Example: Saint-Gobain transport infrastructure solutions  PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 33 Injections  for concrete repair Structural bonding Solutions for  low-carbon concrete Solar control glazing Impact-resistant  partitions Acoustic  plasterboard Fire protection  for structures Structural reinforcement  of taxiways Flooring Waterproofing solutions  for bridge decks In-transit concrete  quality control Protective coatings  for concrete Repair mortars for concrete  finishing and grouting Fiberglass  reinforcement veil Structural sealing  for concrete works Pipes  B – Industrial markets Country by country, Saint-Gobain deploys this same  solutions-based approach over its industrial application  lines. In a context marked by the energy transition,  decarbonization, and the search for performance, the  Group’s solutions make it possible to support the major  industrial players in their transformation. Saint-Gobain  is positioned as a key technological partner, offering high  value-added materials and systems. The Group offers a  comprehensive range of solutions for industry and  mobility, including technical and refractory ceramics for  glass furnaces, metallurgy, chemicals, and industrial  processes; abrasive solutions for cutting, polishing, and  assembly in complex environments; high-performance  polymers and films , offering thermal, chemical, and  dielectric properties; and glazing for mobility , for  automotive, rail and aerospace vehicles, with advanced  features (safety, comfort, design, connectivity). These  products are designed to meet the performance,  reliability and sustainability requirements of industrial  and transport markets. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 34 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com 1.1.4 AN INSPIRING PURPOSE With its corporate purpose, “Making the World a Better Home”, the Group  took on its ambition to improve everyone’s lives by making the planet a fairer,  more inclusive, more harmonious, healthier and sustainable living space. Through its business model, generally, and with its solutions, specifically,  Saint-Gobain has a tangible impact on the life of every individual and on their  living, working, healthcare, educational and mobility environments. The  Group’s corporate purpose is the link between the infinitely small unit of each  person’s living space and the infinitely large one of our shared home: the  planet. It is fully aligned with Saint-Gobain’s vision – to be the worldwide  leader in light and sustainable construction – and with its strategic plan,  “Lead &amp; Grow” (see p. 16 as well as section 1.2.2, p. 42). Our corporate purpose sets the course for our shared future. It looks like us. It  represents a call to action, and is based on the values that guide us, the  Principles of Conduct and Action (see section 1.2.7.E.a, p.  64). This is the  profound ambition of our corporate purpose: to act every day to make the  world a more beautiful and more sustainable place in which to live.  1.1.5 360 YEARS OF HISTORY Saint-Gobain was founded in 1665 by Louis XIV, King of  France, under the name of “Royal Manufacture of  Mirrors”.  Compagnie de Saint-Gobain diversified into sectors such  as chemicals, glass products, and the automotive  industry, rapidly expanding into international markets,  establishing itself in the United States in 1829, Germany  in 1858, Italy in 1888 and Spain in 1905. The Group, which successfully overcame the major crises  of the century and invested heavily in research and  development, withdrew from its chemical activities and  merged with Pont-à-Mousson, created in 1856 and  specializing in cast iron pipes. In 1986, the privatization of Saint-Gobain – which had  been nationalized in 1982 – was a huge popular success:  1,500,000 shareholders subscribed to the transaction.  The acquisition of Norton in 1990, then Carborundum in  1996, enabled the Group to develop its know-how in  abrasives, plastics and ceramics.  The 2005 acquisition of British Plaster Board, the world  leader in plasterboard, combined with Saint-Gobain’s  insulation solutions, made the Group the world’s No. 1 in  interior design solutions. Saint-Gobain focused its strategy on sustainable  housing, while continuing to serve numerous industrial  markets, and pursued its expansion in emerging  countries. In late 2018, the Group launched its “Transform &amp; Grow”  plan aimed at establishing a new organization by country  and ensuring agile and value-creating portfolio  management.  In 2019, Saint-Gobain acquired Continental Building  Products, the first step in strengthening its positions  in the construction market in North America. Saint-Gobain established its corporate purpose,  “Making the world a better home”. The 2021 acquisition of Chryso made Saint-Gobain a  major player in construction chemicals. In October 2021,  Saint-Gobain launched its “Grow &amp; Impact” strategic  plan, setting out its vision to become the  worldwide  leader in light and sustainable construction.  In 2022, the Group finalized the acquisition of Kaycan – a  specialist in exterior building materials – and of GCP  Applied Technologies, a key step in establishing itself as  a leader in construction chemicals.  The following year, Saint-Gobain acquired Building  Products of Canada, a manufacturer of roofing shingles  for the residential sector, and wood fiber insulation  panels.  Saint-Gobain was certified as “Top Employer Global” for  its 2025 fiscal year, for the 11 th consecutive year. The  Group acquired CSR, a leader in construction materials in  Australia and New Zealand, as well as Bailey, a specialist  in metal frames for light construction in Canada, and  initiated the acquisitions of Fosroc, a leading global  player in construction chemicals, and Ovniver (Cemix  brand) in Mexico. These constitute new strategic steps in  establishing Saint-Gobain’s global presence in  construction chemicals, which, once the transactions are  complete, will bring its total sales in this sector to  €6.5 billion (pro forma).  In 2025, Saint-Gobain celebrates its 360th anniversary, a  figure that also represents its global reach and its circular  and comprehensive approach. In October 2025, the  Group launches its “Lead &amp; Grow” strategic plan,  marking the beginning of a new chapter for  Saint-Gobain, based on a clear ambition: to accelerate its  profitable growth in a sustainable manner and  outperform its markets, thereby enhancing the value  created for all its stakeholders. At the end of 2025, the  Group is present in 80 countries (1). PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 35 (1) Countries with active industrial presence as of Dec. 31, 2025.  1.1.6 LOOKING BACK ON THE YEAR 2025 January  Saint-Gobain certified as “Top Employer Global”  for the 10th consecutive year Saint-Gobain was certified “Top Employer Global” for the  tenth consecutive year, joining a small group of 17 global  companies recognized in 2025. This award highlights the  Group’s commitment to its 161,000 employees in  80  countries and its continued progress in diversity,  inclusion, sustainable development, and employee  development. Saint-Gobain completes the acquisition of Ovniver  in Mexico and Central America  Saint-Gobain completed the acquisition of Ovniver  Group (Cemix brand), a major player in construction  chemicals in Mexico and Central America. This strategic  transaction strengthens Saint-Gobain’s presence in  high-growth markets and strengthens its global  leadership in construction chemicals products. Saint-Gobain launches its first 100% recycled  plasterboard  Saint-Gobain announces the UK launch of its first  plasterboard entirely made from recycled materials, a  world first for the Group. Developed by British Gypsum,  this innovation represents an important step in  circular  construction and supports the Group’s commitment to  achieving carbon neutrality by 2050. February Saint-Gobain announces the launch of its first  low-carbon stone wool production line in Spain  Saint-Gobain will invest €40  million to expand and  decarbonize its insulation plant in Azuqueca, near  Madrid. The new electric furnace will replace fossil fuels,  reducing CO₂ emissions by 70% and increasing  production capacity by 40%, marking an important step  towards the Group’s goal of carbon neutrality by 2050. Saint-Gobain finalizes the acquisition of Fosroc Saint-Gobain completed the acquisition of Fosroc, a  leading construction chemicals company with a strong  presence in India, the Middle East and Asia-Pacific. This  acquisition strengthens the Group’s presence in  emerging markets and consolidates its worldwide  leadership in light and sustainable construction. March Saint-Gobain successfully carries out a €1.2 billion  double tranche bond issue  Saint-Gobain issued €1.2  billion in two tranches  –  €700 million (3 years, 2.75%) and €500 million (8 years,  3.50%) – taking advantage of favorable market  conditions to anticipate its refinancing needs. The bonds  were oversubscribed almost three times over, reflecting  investors’ confidence in the Group’s credit quality. April Saint-Gobain included in the CDP’s “List A”  For the second year in a row, Saint-Gobain is on the  CDP’s prestigious “Climate List,” which rewards its  leadership in environmental transparency and climate  action. Only 2% of the companies assessed receive this  distinction, highlighting the Group’s strong sustainability  strategy. Sustainable construction: global trends revealed by the third edition of the Saint-Gobain barometer  The third edition of the Sustainable Construction  Barometer reveals a growing global awareness and  urgency concerning sustainable construction practices.  Based on surveys conducted in 27 countries, the study  highlights the main regional differences and the need to  strengthen training and actions to accelerate the sector’s  transformation. May Saint-Gobain to launch its first low-carbon stone  wool insulation plant in the United Kingdom Saint-Gobain will build a new low-carbon stone wool  insulation plant in Melton Mowbray. Equipped with a fully  electric furnace powered by renewable energy, the plant  will produce 50,000 tonnes per year, meeting the United  Kingdom's growing demand for sustainable building  materials. June Saint-Gobain launches its world’s lowest-carbon  insulation plant in Finland  Saint-Gobain has unveiled the production of very low- carbon glass wool at its Forssa site in Finland. Fully  powered by biogas and hydropower, the plant will  reduce CO₂ emissions by 1,500 tonnes per year and  reduce the carbon footprint of its products by up to 40%. PRESENTING SAINT-GOBAIN SAINT-GOBAIN TODAY 36 SAINT-GOBAIN • UNIVERSAL REGISTRATION DOCUMENT 2025 Ë www.saint-gobain.com General Shareholders’ Meeting and Board of Directors  Meeting on June 5, 2025  Saint-Gobain shareholders reappointed Benoit Bazin as  Chief Executive Officer and approved a 5% dividend  increase, to €2.20 per share. Three new independent  Directors were appointed, reinforcing the diversity and  expertise of the Board of Directors: Maya Hari, a  Singaporean national, a partner at Modern Manifesto,  Antoine de Saint-Affrique, a French national, Chief  Executive Officer of Danone, and Hans Sohlström, a  Finnish national, Chairman and Chief Executive Officer</data>
    </edge>
